/* BeyondVAs ISA Certification Program: curriculum content. Generated from the module files; edit the JSON and rebuild. */
window.BV_CURRICULUM = {"modules":[{"id":"F1","part":"foundations","title":"Welcome to BeyondVAs","summary":"How BeyondVAs works, how this certification program works, who supports you, how you work with your client, and how you get paid.","objectives":["Explain in your own words what BeyondVAs does, what it believes and what a client expects from you.","Plan your study of this program: the modules, the practice assignments, the quizzes and how to send work to your coach.","Name the people around you (coordinator, coach, client) and know which one to contact for each kind of problem.","Describe your first 30 days with a client: the kickoff, the check-ins on days 5, 14 and 30, the daily report and the process videos.","Explain how you are paid (USD, the 5th and the 20th, tracked hours) and what keeps you in good standing."],"lessons":[{"id":"F1-1","title":"Who we are and what we believe","minutes":6,"body":[{"t":"p","text":"Welcome. You were approved because you showed us three things: clear English, a professional attitude and the wish to build a long career. This program turns those three things into the skills a US real estate team pays for. Read every lesson. Do every practice assignment. Ask every question you have. Nobody here expects you to know US real estate already. We expect you to learn it well."},{"t":"p","text":"**What BeyondVAs does.** BeyondVAs finds, trains, tracks and pays bilingual virtual assistants (VAs) and inside sales agents (ISAs) from Honduras and Latin America. We place them with real estate agents and teams in the United States. The agent or team you work for is **your client**. The people your client wants to sell homes to, or buy homes from, are **leads** or **prospects**. You never call them “customers” of BeyondVAs: they belong to your client."},{"t":"p","text":"Clients hire us for three reasons, and you are the center of all three. They want an assistant who is **trained** before day one, **tracked** so they know the work is real, and **managed** by someone who backs the assistant up. Most bad VA stories in the US have the same three gaps: nobody trained the VA, nobody tracked the work, nobody managed the relationship. Our company is built to close those gaps. When you do your job well, you prove that the model works."},{"t":"callout","title":"The two roles in this program","text":"A **VA** (virtual assistant) handles operations: email, calendars, data entry, CRM updates, listing paperwork, marketing tasks. An **ISA** (inside sales agent) talks to leads all day by phone and text, qualifies them and sets appointments for the agents. The Foundations modules (F1 to F6) are for everyone. The ISA track (I1 to I11) is for anyone who will make calls."},{"t":"p","text":"**What we believe.** Our culture fits in three short ideas. You will hear them often."},{"t":"list","items":["**People first.** You are a colleague and a professional, never a “resource”. We treat you with respect, and we expect you to treat your client, their leads and your teammates the same way.","**Over-communicate.** A US client cannot see you. Silence feels like a problem, even when everything is fine. Say what you are doing, when it will be done and what is blocking you. Too much clear information is better than too little.","**Grow together.** You start with this program, then you get monthly training and coaching for as long as you work with us. When you grow, your client grows, and so do we."]},{"t":"p","text":"**What a client expects.** US real estate agents are busy, fast and practical. Most of them run a small business. They pay for results, and they notice small things. Here is what a good client relationship looks like from their side:"},{"t":"dodont","do":["Start your shift on time, every day, already clocked in and ready.","Reply to a request quickly, even if the answer is “I am on it, done by 2 PM ET”.","Follow their process exactly, and write it down so you never ask twice.","Tell them about a problem early, with a suggestion.","Keep their leads’ information private and safe."],"dont":["Disappear for an hour without a word.","Say “yes” to a task you do not understand, then guess.","Change their process because you think yours is better, without asking.","Hide a mistake and hope nobody sees it.","Copy any client data to your own phone, computer or accounts."]},{"t":"p","text":"**An example.** Ana works as a VA for a two-agent team in Phoenix. On her third day, the team lead, Mark, asks her to update 40 contacts in the CRM before his 3 PM meeting. At 1:30 PM she sees that 12 contacts have no email address. A weak VA would update the 28 she can and say nothing. Ana writes in the client channel: “Mark, 28 of 40 are done. 12 have no email in the CRM. I listed them in this note. Do you want me to skip them or text them to ask?” Mark answers in two minutes. He remembers this message for months. That is over-communication, and it is the habit that keeps clients for years."},{"t":"p","text":"**What this means for you.** Your client will judge BeyondVAs by you. Your coordinator will defend you, coach you and help you when something goes wrong. In return we ask for honesty, reliability and effort. If you give those three, the rest can be taught, and this program is where we teach it."},{"t":"callout","title":"Remember","text":"Trained, tracked, managed. People first, over-communicate, grow together. If you are ever unsure what to do, ask yourself: what would a reliable professional do here, and what would my client want to know right now?"}]},{"id":"F1-2","title":"How this certification program works","minutes":7,"body":[{"t":"p","text":"This is a real training program, not a short video. Plan for about 20 to 26 hours in total, which is roughly three full working days. About half of that time is reading. The other half is hands-on practice: role-plays you record, notes you write, exercises in a practice CRM and calls you shadow. The practice is where you really learn, so do not skip it to finish faster."},{"t":"p","text":"**The two parts.** Every VA takes the Foundations. Anyone who will call or text leads also takes the ISA track."},{"t":"table","head":["Part","Modules","What you learn"],"rows":[["Foundations","F1 Welcome to BeyondVAs","How the company, your coordinator, your client and your pay work"],["Foundations","F2 Working with US clients","Culture, time zones, phone English, email and text, reliability"],["Foundations","F3 Your tools","Hubstaff, Follow Up Boss, Google Workspace, password vault, backups"],["Foundations","F4 Real estate basics","The buyer and seller journeys, who does what, the vocabulary"],["Foundations","F5 Professionalism and scope","Confidentiality and what an unlicensed assistant can and cannot say"],["Foundations","F6 Compliance essentials","Calling hours, Do Not Call, consent, opt-outs, recording, fair housing"],["ISA track","I1 to I11","A.L.M., speed to lead, conversations, financing, objections, sellers, CRM discipline, appointments, nurture, recovery, QA and KPIs"]]},{"t":"p","text":"**Inside every module.** Each module has the same parts, so you always know what comes next:"},{"t":"steps","items":["**Objectives.** Three to five things you will be able to do at the end. Read them first, and read them again at the end to check yourself.","**Lessons.** Four to six lessons with explanations, examples, scripts and common mistakes. Each lesson shows how many minutes it takes to read.","**Practice.** One real assignment, usually 45 to 120 minutes. It ends with a deliverable: something you send to your coach, such as a recording, a written note or screenshots.","**Quiz.** Eight to ten questions. The wrong options are realistic on purpose. Each question has an explanation, so read it even when you answer correctly."]},{"t":"p","text":"**Your coach.** A BeyondVAs coach reviews your practice deliverables and gives you feedback. Sometimes your coach asks you to repeat a role-play. That is normal. It does not mean you failed; it means the coach wants you to sound confident before a real lead hears you. On the ISA track, the program ends with supervised calls scored on our 100-point quality rubric, where the standard is 90 or more. You will learn the rubric in module I11."},{"t":"callout","title":"How to send a deliverable","text":"Name every file with your name, the module and the date, for example **Lopez_F2_practice_2026-11-03.mp3**. Send it the way your coach tells you during onboarding. Never put client data or real lead information in a practice file: use the fictional people from the lessons."},{"t":"p","text":"**How to study in English.** You read English well, or you would not be here. Still, reading three days of training in your second language is tiring. These habits help:"},{"t":"list","items":["Study in blocks of 50 minutes with a 10-minute break. Your brain keeps more that way.","Read the scripts **out loud**. On the phone you will speak, not read silently, so train your mouth as well as your eyes.","Keep a personal vocabulary list. When you meet a new word, write the word, the meaning in English and an example sentence. Do not only write the Spanish translation.","When a sentence is confusing, read the example after it. Every rule in this program comes with an example.","If something is still unclear, write down the question and ask your coach. A question you ask now saves a mistake with a real client later."]},{"t":"p","text":"**A sample three-day plan.** You can study at your own pace, but many VAs follow a plan like this one:"},{"t":"table","head":["Day","Morning","Afternoon"],"rows":[["Day 1","F1, F2 and F3 lessons","F1 to F3 practice, F4 lessons"],["Day 2","F4 practice, F5 and F6","I1 to I4 lessons and practice"],["Day 3","I5 to I8","I9 to I11, then schedule supervised calls"]]},{"t":"p","text":"A VA who only does Foundations will finish sooner. A future ISA will often need a fourth day for role-plays and recordings. That is fine. We prefer you finish well rather than fast."},{"t":"dodont","do":["Do the practice assignments honestly, in your own words and your own voice.","Re-read a lesson before its quiz if you are not sure.","Ask your coach about anything that sounds different from what a client told you."],"dont":["Click through lessons just to see the quiz.","Copy answers or recordings from another VA. Coaches compare, and it ends your application.","Use real client or lead information in any practice file."]},{"t":"p","text":"When you finish, you will know more about US real estate operations than many new agents do. That is the goal. Our clients choose BeyondVAs because our assistants arrive ready."}]},{"id":"F1-3","title":"The people around you: coordinator, coach and client","minutes":7,"body":[{"t":"p","text":"You never work alone at BeyondVAs. Three kinds of people are around you, and each one has a different job. Knowing who to contact for each problem saves time and protects you."},{"t":"table","head":["Person","Who they are","Contact them for"],"rows":[["Your client","The US agent or team you work for. They direct your daily work.","Tasks, priorities, their processes, questions about their leads and tools"],["Your coordinator (manager)","A BeyondVAs manager who backs you up and manages the relationship with the client.","Schedule, pay, time tracking, outages, days off, conflicts, anything that feels wrong"],["Your coach","A BeyondVAs trainer, sometimes the same person as your coordinator.","Training questions, practice feedback, call quality, scripts and role-plays"]]},{"t":"p","text":"**The client directs the work.** You are an independent professional engaged through BeyondVAs, and the client decides what you work on each day: which leads to call, which tasks come first, how they like their CRM. Follow their process. If you think a process could be better, suggest it politely, but keep following the current process until they agree to change it."},{"t":"p","text":"**The coordinator carries the relationship.** BeyondVAs holds the agreements, the monitoring and the payments. Your coordinator runs the kickoff with your client, checks in with them during your first month and reviews your time before the client is billed. Your coordinator is on your side. When something goes wrong, the coordinator wants to hear it from you first, not from the client."},{"t":"callout","title":"The client channel","text":"Your client talks to you **only inside the client channel** that BeyondVAs sets up. No private direct messages, no personal WhatsApp, no personal email. If a client sends you a private message, answer politely in the channel: “Thanks, Mark! Moving this to our channel so everything stays in one place.” This protects you, the client and the record of what was agreed."},{"t":"p","text":"Why so strict? Because a channel is a shared memory. If the client says on Tuesday “call the Garcia lead on Friday”, and on Friday a teammate asks why you called, the message is there. Private messages disappear, get misread or create disagreements nobody can check. Your coordinator can also see the channel, which means you always have backup."},{"t":"p","text":"**When to go to your coordinator.** Contact your coordinator the same day, and before you act, when any of these happen:"},{"t":"list","items":["Your internet or power fails, or you will be late or absent. Tell your coordinator first, then the client in the channel.","The client asks you to do something outside your scope, such as negotiating a price, giving an opinion of value, explaining a contract or calling a list that was not checked against Do Not Call. You will learn exactly why in F5 and F6.","The client asks you to work off the clock, to share a password in chat, or to download or export client data.","The client is rude, pressures you, or crosses a personal line. You do not have to handle that alone.","You made a mistake that a lead or the client will notice. Tell your coordinator, then fix it with the client."]},{"t":"script","title":"Saying no to an out-of-scope request, politely","lines":[{"who":"Client","text":"Hey Lucia, can you call the Smiths and tell them we can probably get them $20K off the asking price?"},{"who":"You","text":"Thanks, Mark. Price and negotiation have to come from a licensed agent, so I can’t say that part. I can call the Smiths now and book a time for you to talk with them today. Does 4:00 or 5:30 PM your time work?"},{"who":"Client","text":"Right, good catch. Book 5:30."}]},{"t":"p","text":"Notice the pattern: thank the client, say clearly what you cannot do in one sentence, and offer what you can do right away. Most clients respect this. If a client insists, do not argue. Say “Let me check with my coordinator so we do this the right way,” and message your coordinator at once."},{"t":"p","text":"**When to go to your coach.** Your coach helps you get better. Ask your coach when a lead raised an objection you did not know how to answer, when you want feedback on a recorded call, or when a script from your client sounds unclear in English. Coaches like specific questions: “On the call with the Lee lead, she said she wanted to wait for rates. What could I have said?” is better than “How do I handle objections?”"},{"t":"dodont","do":["Keep every client conversation in the client channel.","Tell your coordinator about problems the same day, before the client hears it from someone else.","Follow the client’s process, and suggest improvements politely."],"dont":["Accept private messages or personal phone numbers from a client.","Try to solve a serious problem alone because you are embarrassed.","Complain about the client to other VAs or in any public place."]},{"t":"p","text":"In short: the client directs the work, the coordinator backs you up, the coach helps you grow, and the channel keeps everyone on the same page."}]},{"id":"F1-4","title":"Your first 30 days with a client","minutes":7,"body":[{"t":"p","text":"The first month decides most client relationships. A client who feels confident in week one becomes a client for years. A client who feels confused in week one starts to doubt. This lesson shows you what happens in the first 30 days and how to make each step go well."},{"t":"steps","items":["**Interviews.** After a client signs, they meet two or three trained VAs on short video interviews and choose one. Camera on, quiet room, good light, a clean background. Prepare two sentences about yourself and one question about their business.","**Setup week.** BeyondVAs sets up your Hubstaff tracking account, the client’s access to your hours and screenshots, and secure access to the client’s tools through the password vault. You check that every login works before day one.","**Day one: the kickoff.** A BeyondVAs manager runs a kickoff call with you and the client. Together you set week-one priorities, your schedule in the client’s time zone and how the client wants to be updated.","**Week one: learn their business.** The client screen-shares their top five tasks. You record each process as a short video and write a checklist for it. There is a short daily check-in, usually about ten minutes.","**Days 5, 14 and 30: check-ins.** Your manager checks in with the client, and with you, to see what is working and what needs to change. After day 30, weekly reports continue."]},{"t":"p","text":"**The kickoff call.** Come with a notebook and these questions. Write down every answer:"},{"t":"list","items":["What are the three most important things you want me to take off your plate in week one?","What are my hours, in your time zone? Who covers when I am offline?","How do you want updates: one daily report, or also messages during the day? Which things are urgent enough to interrupt you?","Which tools will I use, and which ones should I never touch?","Are there words, phrases or scripts you want me to use with your leads, and anything I should never say?","Who else on your team will send me tasks?"]},{"t":"p","text":"**Recording processes (your SOP library).** When the client shows you a task, ask permission to record the screen-share. Then turn each recording into a short checklist, called an **SOP** (standard operating procedure). Example title: “How to add a new open-house lead”. Write numbered steps, one action per step, with the tool and the button names. Save it where the client tells you, inside the client’s systems, never on your own computer. A good SOP library means you never ask the same question twice, the client sees your progress, and if you are ever sick, a backup can follow your steps."},{"t":"callout","title":"The question rule","text":"In week one, collect your non-urgent questions and ask them together at the daily check-in, instead of sending ten separate messages. Urgent questions (a lead waiting, a deadline today) go in the channel right away. This shows respect for the client’s time."},{"t":"p","text":"**The daily report.** At the end of every shift, you post a daily report in the client channel. It is short, structured and the same every day, so the client can read it in 30 seconds. Here is a general format for a VA. ISAs add their call numbers, which you will learn in I11."},{"t":"script","title":"Example daily report (VA)","lines":[{"who":"You","text":"**Daily report, Tue 11/03, 9:00 AM to 5:00 PM PT**"},{"who":"You","text":"**Done:** 42 contacts updated in the CRM; 3 showing requests confirmed for Thursday; listing photos for 1820 Elm St uploaded to the marketing folder."},{"who":"You","text":"**In progress:** open-house sign-in sheet entry, 18 of 30, finishing tomorrow by 11 AM PT."},{"who":"You","text":"**Blocked / need from you:** login for the showing app is not working; I sent the error to my coordinator too."},{"who":"You","text":"**Tomorrow:** finish sign-in sheets, prepare Friday’s newsletter draft."}]},{"t":"p","text":"**How to win the first month.** These habits make clients say “I should have done this years ago”:"},{"t":"dodont","do":["Be online five minutes before your start time, every day.","Repeat a task back before you start it: “So I will update all 40 contacts and flag the ones without email. Right?”","Finish small tasks fast and well. Trust starts with small things.","Send the daily report every single day, even on slow days.","Ask your coordinator for feedback after the day-5 and day-14 check-ins."],"dont":["Wait for the client to tell you every step. Use your SOPs.","Say “I understand” when you do not. Ask again; it is professional.","Promise big improvements in week one. Show them with results instead.","Skip the report because “nothing happened”. Say what you did and what is next."]},{"t":"p","text":"**An example.** In her first week, Daniela notices her client, a team in Dallas, answers messages mostly at 7:30 AM and 6:00 PM Central. She adjusts: she posts her questions at 7:00 AM so they are waiting, and her report at 5:45 PM. By day 14, the client tells the manager, “She just runs things.” Daniela did not work harder; she worked in the client’s rhythm."},{"t":"p","text":"Expect some confusion in the first two weeks. Every client is different, and nobody knows a new business on day one. What the client wants to see is not perfection; it is steady progress, clear communication and no surprises."}]},{"id":"F1-5","title":"Getting paid and staying in good standing","minutes":7,"body":[{"t":"p","text":"You deserve to understand exactly how you are paid, and what you need to do so that every hour you work is counted. This lesson explains it."},{"t":"p","text":"**Your status.** You are an **independent contractor** engaged and paid through BeyondVAs. You are not an employee of your client. The client never pays you directly, and you never send invoices to the client. BeyondVAs bills the client and pays you. This protects you: you never have to chase a client for money. As an independent contractor, you are responsible for your own taxes and registrations in your country; if you are not sure what applies to you, ask a local accountant."},{"t":"table","head":["Topic","How it works"],"rows":[["Currency","US dollars (USD)"],["Pay dates","Twice a month: the 5th and the 20th"],["What is paid","Your tracked hours in Hubstaff for your client"],["Who pays","BeyondVAs, using the payment method you set up during onboarding"],["Pay calendar","Your coordinator gives you the calendar that shows which dates each payment covers"]]},{"t":"p","text":"**Tracked hours are your pay.** Your hours come from Hubstaff, the time tracker you will learn in F3. While you are clocked in, Hubstaff takes screenshots at random moments, about every two minutes, and measures activity from your keyboard and mouse. The client can see your hours and your screenshots. This is not because anyone thinks you are dishonest. It is how the client knows the work is real, and it is the reason clients trust a VA in another country."},{"t":"p","text":"**Idle time.** If Hubstaff shows a long period with no keyboard or mouse activity and no explanation, that time is flagged. Your manager reviews flagged time before the client is billed, and may ask you about it. There is often a good reason. An ISA on a 20-minute phone call looks idle to a tracker. A VA reading a long contract scrolls slowly. The solution is simple: when you do work with little typing, write a short note in the tracker, such as “Call with lead, Garcia” or “Reading client SOP for listings”. A clear note turns a question into a non-issue."},{"t":"callout","title":"Clock in only when you work","text":"Clock in when you start working for the client. Clock out for lunch, breaks, personal calls and errands. Never leave the tracker running while you are away, and never use any tool or trick that fakes activity. Fake activity is fraud. It ends the placement and the relationship with BeyondVAs."},{"t":"p","text":"**The daily report.** At the end of every shift, post your daily report in the client channel. The report, the tracked hours and the screenshots tell the same story. When the three agree, nobody has questions. When your report says “called 85 leads” and your tracker shows two hours idle, someone will ask why."},{"t":"p","text":"**What keeps you in good standing.** These are the habits your coordinator watches. They matter as much as your skills."},{"t":"list","items":["**Punctuality.** Clocked in and ready at your start time. If you will be late, message your coordinator and the client channel **before** your start time, not after.","**Honest time.** Every tracked minute is client work, with a note when activity is low.","**Daily report.** Every shift, no exceptions.","**Communication.** Requests acknowledged quickly, problems reported early, everything inside the client channel.","**Security.** Passwords only through the password vault, client data never downloaded or exported, a lost or suspicious login reported at once.","**Outages handled.** If your internet or power fails, tell your manager immediately and switch to your backup internet or power. You will set this up in F3."]},{"t":"p","text":"**What puts you at risk.** Missing shifts without notice, tracking time while doing personal things, sharing client information, talking to a client outside the channel to make private arrangements, or ignoring a compliance rule on calls. Any of these can end a placement. Most VAs who lose a client do not lack skill; they lose trust through small, repeated habits."},{"t":"script","title":"Messaging before your start time","lines":[{"who":"You","text":"Hi Carla (coordinator), power is out in my area since 7:40 AM. I am on my phone hotspot and laptop battery, clocking in on time at 8:00 AM CT. I will update you at 10."},{"who":"Coordinator","text":"Thanks for the heads-up. Tell the client channel too, and let me know if the hotspot drops."},{"who":"You","text":"(in the client channel) Good morning, Mark! Quick note: power outage here, I am on backup and working normally. If calls drop, I will switch to tasks and message you."}]},{"t":"p","text":"**Questions about your pay.** If a payment looks wrong, or hours seem missing, ask your coordinator in writing, with the dates. Never raise pay questions with the client. Your coordinator checks Hubstaff, the approved hours and the pay calendar, and answers you. Most issues are a missing clock-out or a time-zone mix-up, and they are easy to fix when you report them quickly."},{"t":"p","text":"Honest time, a daily report and clear communication: do those three every day and you will never have to worry about your pay or your place."}]}],"practice":{"title":"Your welcome plan","minutes":60,"intro":"Prepare for your first client the way a professional does. You will write your introduction, plan your questions, practice a daily report and check that you understand how your time and pay work. Use fictional details from the lessons only.","steps":["Write a two-sentence introduction you could say at a client interview (who you are, what you are good at). Record yourself saying it on camera, in under 30 seconds, in a quiet room with good light.","Write the first message you would post in a new client channel on day one (4 to 6 lines): greeting, your hours in the client’s time zone, how you will send updates, and one question.","From F1-4, choose the six kickoff questions you think matter most for a team that sells homes in Phoenix, Arizona, and add one question of your own.","Write a sample daily report in the Done / In progress / Blocked / Tomorrow format for this fictional day: you confirmed 4 showings, updated 30 contacts, could not log in to the showing app, and will prepare a newsletter tomorrow.","Write the message you would send to your coordinator and the client channel if your power fails 20 minutes before your shift.","Answer in writing, in 2 or 3 sentences each: When do you clock out? What do you do if Hubstaff will show low activity during a long call? Who do you ask if a payment looks wrong?","Read the three beliefs (people first, over-communicate, grow together) and write one sentence for each about how you will show it in your first week."],"deliverable":"One document with your channel message, kickoff questions, daily report, outage message and written answers, plus your 30-second video introduction. Send both to your coach, named Lastname_F1_practice_date."},"quiz":[{"q":"A client is a US real estate team. In BeyondVAs language, what are the people that team wants to help buy or sell homes?","options":["BeyondVAs customers","Leads or prospects of your client","Your clients","Contractors"],"answer":1,"explain":"Your client is the agent or team you work for. The people they want to work with are their leads or prospects. They belong to your client, not to BeyondVAs."},{"q":"Your client sends you a private direct message asking you to call a lead. What do you do?","options":["Answer privately; it is faster","Ignore it until they post in the channel","Thank them and move the conversation to the client channel, then do the task","Give them your personal WhatsApp number instead"],"answer":2,"explain":"Clients talk to VAs only inside the client channel. Move it there politely and do the work, so the record stays in one place and your coordinator can back you up."},{"q":"Your client asks you to tell a lead the seller will “probably take $20K less”. What is the best reply?","options":["Do it, because the client directs your work","Say price and negotiation must come from a licensed agent, and offer to book a time for the agent to talk with the lead","Refuse and stop working until your coordinator calls","Tell the lead a smaller number to be safe"],"answer":1,"explain":"Negotiation and price are outside an unlicensed assistant's scope. Say what you cannot do in one sentence and offer what you can do: book the agent. If the client insists, message your coordinator."},{"q":"Which of these is the main reason the client channel rule exists?","options":["It keeps a shared record of what was agreed, and your coordinator can see it","It makes messages slower so clients send fewer tasks","Clients prefer not to talk to VAs","It replaces the daily report"],"answer":0,"explain":"A channel is a shared memory. Private messages get lost or misread; the channel shows what was agreed and gives you backup."},{"q":"When are BeyondVAs VAs paid?","options":["Every Friday, by the client","Once a month in local currency","When the client approves each task","Twice a month, on the 5th and the 20th, in US dollars, by BeyondVAs"],"answer":3,"explain":"You are an independent contractor paid in USD by BeyondVAs on the 5th and the 20th. The client never pays you directly."},{"q":"You are an ISA on a 20-minute call with a lead, and you barely touch the keyboard. What should you do in Hubstaff?","options":["Move the mouse every minute so it does not look idle","Clock out during calls","Add a short note, such as “Call with lead, Garcia”, so the low activity makes sense","Nothing; idle time is never reviewed"],"answer":2,"explain":"Low activity during calls or reading is normal. A short note explains it. Faking activity is fraud, and unexplained idle time is flagged and reviewed before billing."},{"q":"You will be 20 minutes late because of a power outage. When do you tell your coordinator and client?","options":["Before your start time, and you switch to your backup power or internet","After you arrive, with an apology","Only if the client asks","At the end of the day in the report"],"answer":0,"explain":"Message before your start time, not after. Tell your manager at once and switch to backup internet or power."},{"q":"In your first week, a client screen-shares how to add an open-house lead. What is the best habit?","options":["Watch once and trust your memory","Ask them to show you again every time","Record it with permission and turn it into a step-by-step SOP saved in the client's systems","Save a copy of their CRM on your computer to practice"],"answer":2,"explain":"Recording each process and writing a checklist (SOP) means you never ask twice. Save it inside the client's systems, never on your own computer."},{"q":"Who should you contact first if a payment looks wrong?","options":["The client, in the channel","Another VA","Your coordinator, in writing, with the dates","Hubstaff support"],"answer":2,"explain":"Pay questions go to your coordinator, never to the client. Your coordinator checks the tracked hours and the pay calendar."},{"q":"Which belief means “tell people what you are doing, when it will be done and what is blocking you”?","options":["People first","Over-communicate","Grow together","Trained, tracked, managed"],"answer":1,"explain":"A US client cannot see you, so silence feels like a problem. Over-communicating keeps them confident."}]},{"id":"F2","part":"foundations","title":"Working with US clients","summary":"The culture, time zones, phone English, writing habits and reliability that make a US real estate client trust you from week one.","objectives":["Communicate in the direct, friendly style US clients expect, and understand the workplace phrases and abbreviations they use.","Convert any time between your clock, your client's clock and a lead's clock, including the daylight saving changes in March and November.","Speak clearly on the phone: open a call, spell names, say numbers, prices and addresses, and confirm details without confusion.","Write short, clear emails, texts and channel messages using US formats for dates, times and money.","Apply the reliability habits that keep a client: acknowledge within 15 minutes, commit to a time with a time zone, and report problems early."],"lessons":[{"id":"F2-1","title":"How US clients think about work","minutes":6,"body":[{"t":"p","text":"Every culture has its own rules about work. None is better than another, but if you do not know the rules, good work can look bad. This lesson explains the habits most US real estate clients share, so that your good work is seen as good work."},{"t":"p","text":"**1. Direct is polite.** In many Latin American workplaces, people soften bad news, or say “yes” to avoid disappointing the boss. In the US, clients usually prefer a clear answer, even when it is “no” or “not yet”. A clear “I can’t finish all 40 today; I can finish 25 by 5 PM. Which ones first?” is respected. A soft “Yes, no problem” followed by 25 done is a broken promise."},{"t":"p","text":"**2. Questions are a sign of quality.** Some VAs worry that asking questions makes them look less capable. In the US it is the opposite. A question before the work shows care; a mistake after the work shows guessing. Ask short, specific questions: “Should the follow-up text go out today or tomorrow morning?”"},{"t":"p","text":"**3. Time is money.** Most agents are paid only when a home sells. Every hour they spend fixing a problem is an hour they are not selling. Fast replies, short messages and work that does not need correcting save them money, and they notice."},{"t":"p","text":"**4. First names and friendly tone.** Clients usually want you to use their first name: “Hi Mark”, not “Dear Mr. Johnson” or “Sir”. The tone is friendly, but still professional. A short greeting is enough; you do not need long formal openings."},{"t":"p","text":"**5. Small talk is short.** A client may ask “How was your weekend?” on a Monday call. Answer in one or two sentences and ask back: “Good, thanks! I went to the beach with my family. How about you?” Then move to work. Avoid politics, religion and personal problems with clients."},{"t":"p","text":"**6. Real estate is a weekend business.** Agents show homes on evenings and weekends, when buyers are free. Your client may send messages on Saturday or late at night. You only work your agreed hours, but understand that their schedule is not nine to five. A message sent at 9 PM their time does not mean they expect an answer at 9 PM."},{"t":"callout","title":"US holidays to know","text":"On these days many clients slow down or take time off, but buyers may tour more: New Year’s Day (January 1), Memorial Day (last Monday in May), Independence Day (July 4), Labor Day (first Monday in September), Thanksgiving (fourth Thursday in November) and Christmas (December 25). Ask your client in advance whether you work on each one. Some states also limit sales calls on holidays; you will see this in F6."},{"t":"p","text":"**Workplace phrases you will hear.** US clients use many short phrases and abbreviations. Learn these now so you are never confused."},{"t":"glossary","terms":[{"term":"Heads up","def":"A warning or early notice. “Heads up: the Garcia showing moved to 4 PM.”"},{"term":"Circle back","def":"Return to a topic later. “Let’s circle back on this Friday.”"},{"term":"Touch base","def":"Have a short contact or check-in. “Let’s touch base tomorrow morning.”"},{"term":"On my plate","def":"Tasks I am responsible for. “What’s on your plate today?”"},{"term":"ASAP","def":"As soon as possible. Ask what that means in hours if it matters."},{"term":"EOD / EOW","def":"End of day / end of week, in the client’s time zone."},{"term":"ETA","def":"Estimated time of arrival or completion. “What’s your ETA on the report?”"},{"term":"FYI","def":"For your information. No action needed unless they say so."},{"term":"OOO","def":"Out of office."},{"term":"Sounds good / Will do","def":"Short ways to say “I agree” and “I will do it”."},{"term":"Loop me in","def":"Include me in the messages about this."},{"term":"Ballpark","def":"A rough estimate. Careful: never give a ballpark price or home value; that is for the agent."}]},{"t":"p","text":"**False friends for Spanish speakers.** Some English words look like Spanish words but mean something different. These cause real confusion:"},{"t":"table","head":["English word","What it really means","Spanish look-alike"],"rows":[["Actually","In fact, really","Not “actualmente” (currently = “currently”)"],["Eventually","In the end, finally","Not “eventualmente” (possibly = “possibly”)"],["Assist","Help","Not “asistir” (attend = “attend”)"],["Realize","Understand suddenly","Not “realizar” (carry out = “do”, “complete”)"],["Embarrassed","Ashamed, uncomfortable","Not “embarazada” (pregnant)"],["Sensible","Reasonable, practical","Not “sensible” (sensitive = “sensitive”)"]]},{"t":"dodont","do":["Give a clear answer, including “no” or “not yet”, with an alternative.","Ask a specific question before you start a task you do not fully understand.","Keep greetings short and friendly, then get to the point."],"dont":["Say “yes” to everything to seem helpful.","Use very formal phrases like “Dear Sir, I hope this message finds you well” in a quick channel message.","Share opinions about politics or religion with clients or leads."]}]},{"id":"F2-2","title":"Time zones: your clock, your client's clock and the lead's clock","minutes":7,"body":[{"t":"p","text":"Time zones cause more VA mistakes than English does. A message that says “I’ll call at 3” can mean three different times. In this lesson you learn to think in three clocks and to never write a time without its zone."},{"t":"list","items":["**Your clock:** where you live. Honduras uses Central Standard Time (UTC minus 6) all year and does **not** change its clocks.","**Your client’s clock:** where the agent or team works. Your schedule, your deadlines and your daily report use this clock.","**The lead’s clock:** where the lead lives. For calls and texts, **this** is the clock that counts. A client in Florida can have a lead who is moving from California."]},{"t":"p","text":"**The four main US time zones.** Eastern (ET), Central (CT), Mountain (MT) and Pacific (PT). Most US states change their clocks for daylight saving time: clocks move **one hour forward on the second Sunday in March** and **one hour back on the first Sunday in November**. Honduras does not move, so the difference between you and your client changes twice a year. Most of Arizona and all of Hawaii do not change either."},{"t":"table","head":["When it is 10:00 AM in Honduras","March to November (daylight time)","November to March (standard time)"],"rows":[["Eastern (New York, Florida, Georgia)","12:00 PM","11:00 AM"],["Central (Texas, Illinois, Tennessee)","11:00 AM","10:00 AM"],["Mountain (Colorado, Utah)","10:00 AM","9:00 AM"],["Arizona (most of the state)","9:00 AM","9:00 AM"],["Pacific (California, Washington, Nevada)","9:00 AM","8:00 AM"]]},{"t":"callout","title":"If you do not live in Honduras","text":"Build this same table for your own country. Colombia and Peru, for example, use UTC minus 5 all year, so they are one hour ahead of Honduras. Check whether your country changes its clocks, and save your table where you can see it during every shift."},{"t":"p","text":"**Your schedule is set in the client’s time zone.** If you work 9:00 AM to 5:00 PM Pacific, that means 10:00 AM to 6:00 PM in Honduras in summer, but 11:00 AM to 7:00 PM in winter. When the US changes its clocks, your hours in Honduras change too. Put a reminder in your calendar for both Sundays every year, and confirm your hours with your coordinator the week before."},{"t":"p","text":"**Calls follow the lead’s clock.** Our house calling window is 9:00 AM to 8:00 PM in the **lead’s** local time, Monday to Saturday, and noon to 8:00 PM on Sunday. Texts follow the same window. Some states are stricter, and your manager tells you their rules before you dial. You will study the full rules in F6. For now, practice the math."},{"t":"steps","items":["Find the lead’s location: the address in the CRM, the area of the home they asked about, or the area code of their phone. If these disagree, call only at a time that is inside the window in every possible location and ask your manager.","Convert the window to your clock. Example: a lead in Los Angeles in December. 9:00 AM PT is 11:00 AM in Honduras, and 8:00 PM PT is 10:00 PM in Honduras.","Check the day. On Sunday the window starts at noon: noon PT is 2:00 PM in Honduras in December.","Before every call, ask yourself: what time is it **for them** right now?"]},{"t":"p","text":"**Always write the zone.** Never write “I’ll send it by 3.” Write “I’ll send it by 3:00 PM ET.” When you write to a lead, use **their** time zone and do not add the abbreviation if it sounds strange in a friendly text: “Does tomorrow at 4 PM work for you?” is fine when you already converted it to their time."},{"t":"script","title":"Confirming a time across zones","lines":[{"who":"Client","text":"Can you book the Patel consult for Thursday at 10?"},{"who":"You","text":"Sure! Just to confirm: Thursday 11/12 at 10:00 AM Central, your time? The Patels are in Denver, so for them that is 9:00 AM Mountain. Does that still work?"},{"who":"Client","text":"Good catch. Make it 11 Central so it’s 10 for them."}]},{"t":"p","text":"**A real mistake to avoid.** In November, a VA in Honduras kept calling a lead at what she thought was 9:15 AM Eastern. But the clocks had changed the Sunday before, and she had not updated her table. She was calling at 8:15 AM Eastern, before the house calling window. The lead complained. The fix is simple: update your table on the clock-change Sundays, and use a world clock on your screen that shows your client’s zone and the zones of your leads."},{"t":"dodont","do":["Keep a world clock with your zone, your client’s zone and the four US zones.","Write every time with its zone in messages to your client.","Check the lead’s local time before every call and text."],"dont":["Assume the lead lives in the same zone as your client.","Forget that your hours in your country change in March and November.","Call or text because “it is already 10 AM here”."]}]},{"id":"F2-3","title":"Phone English that sounds confident","minutes":6,"body":[{"t":"p","text":"On the phone, the other person cannot see your face, your smile or your hands. They only hear your voice. Clarity matters more than accent. Many successful US agents speak English with an accent; what makes a caller sound professional is a clear structure, a calm pace and correct details. This lesson gives you all three."},{"t":"p","text":"**Pace and voice.** When we are nervous, we speak fast. Slow down to about 80 percent of your natural speed. Pause after your name and after every question. Smile while you speak; people can hear it. Stand up or sit straight for important calls. Use a good headset in a quiet room, and close windows and doors."},{"t":"p","text":"**Open every call the same way.** Say who you are, who you work for and why you are calling, in the first sentences. Our house rule is to also say that the call may be recorded, on every call. Your client may give you a specific script; use theirs."},{"t":"script","title":"A clear call opening","lines":[{"who":"You","text":"Hi, is this Jennifer?"},{"who":"Lead","text":"Yes, who’s this?"},{"who":"You","text":"Hi Jennifer, this is Sofia with the Parker Home Team. Just so you know, this call may be recorded. You asked about the house on 1820 Elm Street this morning, so I wanted to call you right away. Do you have two minutes?"}]},{"t":"p","text":"**Numbers.** Numbers are where most phone mistakes happen. Learn how Americans say them:"},{"t":"table","head":["Written","Said","Tip"],"rows":[["(661) 555-0142","six-six-one, five-five-five, zero-one-four-two","Say phone numbers in groups of 3, 3 and 4, with a pause between groups."],["1820 Elm St","eighteen-twenty Elm Street","House numbers are often said in pairs."],["405 Oak Ave","four-oh-five Oak Avenue","Zero is often “oh” in addresses and phone numbers."],["$450,000","four hundred fifty thousand (or “four-fifty”)","Agents often say “four-fifty” or “450K”."],["$1.2M","one point two million","M means million; K means thousand."],["3 bd / 2 ba","three bedrooms, two bathrooms","Agents say “a three-two”."],["6:30 PM","six-thirty in the evening","Add “in the morning” or “in the evening” when you confirm."]]},{"t":"callout","title":"Fifteen or fifty?","text":"13 and 30, 14 and 40, 15 and 50 sound almost the same on the phone. When a number matters, say it a second way: “fifteen, one-five”. When the lead says it, repeat it back: “Was that one-five or five-zero?”"},{"t":"p","text":"**Spelling names and emails.** Never guess a spelling. Ask, and spell it back using simple words. You can use the international alphabet (Alpha, Bravo, Charlie) or common words (“B as in boy, M as in Mary”). Both are fine."},{"t":"script","title":"Confirming an email","lines":[{"who":"You","text":"What’s the best email to send the listings to?"},{"who":"Lead","text":"It’s jen dot walsh at example dot com."},{"who":"You","text":"Great. Let me read it back: J-E-N, dot, W-A-L-S-H, at example dot com. Is Walsh with an S-H at the end?"},{"who":"Lead","text":"Yes, that’s right."}]},{"t":"p","text":"**Useful phrases.** Keep these on a card near your screen until they are automatic:"},{"t":"list","items":["When you did not hear: “Sorry, I didn’t catch that. Could you say it one more time?”","When the line is bad: “You’re breaking up a little. Can I call you right back?”","When you need a moment: “Let me check that for you. One moment, please.”","To confirm: “Just to confirm, you’re looking for three bedrooms in Mesa, under 450, and you’d like to move by March?”","When you do not know: “That’s a great question for the agent. I’ll make sure they cover it with you.”","To close: “Perfect. You’ll get a text confirmation in a minute. Thanks, Jennifer, have a great afternoon!”"]},{"t":"p","text":"**Sounds to practice.** Spanish speakers often find a few English sounds hard. Practice these pairs out loud for five minutes a day: **ship / sheep**, **live / leave** (short and long i), **very / berry** (v and b), **chair / share** (ch and sh), **think / sink** (th). Also pronounce the last consonant: “tour**s**”, “walk**ed**”, “hou**se**”. You do not need to sound American; you need to be understood the first time."},{"t":"dodont","do":["Slow down, pause and smile.","Repeat back every number, name, email and time.","Say your name, your client’s team and the recording notice at the start of the call."],"dont":["Fill silences with “ehh”, “este” or “okay, okay”. A short pause is professional.","Pretend you understood when you did not.","Read a script like a robot. Learn it well enough to say it naturally."]}]},{"id":"F2-4","title":"Writing emails, texts and channel messages","minutes":6,"body":[{"t":"p","text":"Most of your communication with your client is written. Clear writing saves the client time and protects you from misunderstandings. The rule for everything you write: **answer first, details second, short always**."},{"t":"p","text":"**Channel messages to your client.** Start with the most important information. Use one message per topic, not five messages in a row. Use a short list when you have more than two items."},{"t":"table","head":["Weak message","Strong message"],"rows":[["“Hi Mark, good morning, how are you? I was working on the contacts and I saw something, some of them don’t have emails, I don’t know if you want me to do something.”","“Mark, 28 of 40 contacts are updated. 12 have no email (list in the CRM note). Skip them, or text them to ask? I’ll keep going on the rest meanwhile.”"],["“Done.” (about which task?)","“Done: Friday’s three showings are confirmed and in your calendar.”"],["“I’ll send it later.”","“I’ll send the newsletter draft by 3:00 PM ET.”"]]},{"t":"p","text":"**US formats.** These are different from many Latin American countries, and mistakes here look careless to a US client:"},{"t":"list","items":["**Dates:** month first. 11/03/2026 means **November 3**, not March 11. When it matters, write the month as a word: “Tue, Nov 3”.","**Times:** 12-hour clock with AM or PM and the zone: “4:30 PM CT”. Avoid 16:30.","**Money:** dollar sign first, commas for thousands, a period for cents: **$1,250.50**. In many Latin countries it is written 1.250,50; in the US that is wrong.","**Phone numbers:** (661) 555-0142.","**Addresses:** number, street, city, state abbreviation, ZIP: 1820 Elm St, Lancaster, CA 93534."]},{"t":"p","text":"**Emails.** Use a clear subject line that says what the email is about and whether action is needed: “Action needed: confirm Saturday open house details” or “FYI: 3 new leads from the Oak Ave open house”. Start with “Hi Mark,” and end with a short line like “Thanks, Sofia”. Keep emails to five short paragraphs or fewer. Read every email once before sending, and check names, dates, times and attachments."},{"t":"p","text":"**Texts to leads.** Texts are short, friendly and personal. Every first text says your name and your client’s team. Ask one question at a time, and make it easy to answer."},{"t":"script","title":"A good first text to a lead","lines":[{"who":"You","text":"Hi Jennifer, this is Sofia with the Parker Home Team. You asked about 1820 Elm St. It’s still available! Would you like to see it tomorrow at 4 PM or Saturday at 11 AM?"},{"who":"Lead","text":"Saturday works!"},{"who":"You","text":"Great, Saturday at 11 AM it is. I’ll text you the agent’s name and the address the day before. Thanks, Jennifer!"}]},{"t":"callout","title":"Texting rules (preview of F6)","text":"Text only leads who agreed to receive texts. Texts follow the same window as calls: 9:00 AM to 8:00 PM in the lead’s time, noon to 8:00 PM on Sunday. If a lead replies “STOP”, “remove me” or “don’t text me”, you stop at once and follow the opt-out steps you will learn in F6. Never argue."},{"t":"p","text":"**Tone and style.** Friendly, simple and correct. A few rules:"},{"t":"dodont","do":["Use short sentences and simple words.","Use the lead’s first name and your client’s team name.","Run a spell check before sending anything long.","Write CRM notes in English, always, even when the call was in Spanish.","Match your client’s style: if they write short and casual, you can too."],"dont":["Write in ALL CAPITAL LETTERS. It reads as shouting.","Use many emojis with clients, and none with leads unless your client’s templates use them.","Use slang like “u”, “thx”, “pls” in client or lead messages.","Send long blocks of text with no line breaks.","Translate word by word from Spanish. Write the idea in simple English instead."]},{"t":"p","text":"**Spanish-speaking leads.** One of your strengths is that you are bilingual. If a lead prefers Spanish, follow your client’s rule. Many clients are happy for you to continue in Spanish. Either way, your notes, reports and messages to the client are always in English, so the agent can read them."},{"t":"p","text":"**Before you press send, check:** Is the most important point first? Is every time written with its zone? Are the date, money and phone formats correct? Is the name spelled right? Would I understand this message if I read it on my phone in ten seconds? If yes, send it."}]},{"id":"F2-5","title":"Reliability: the habits that keep a client","minutes":6,"body":[{"t":"p","text":"Clients rarely end a relationship because of one mistake. They end it because they stop trusting that things will happen without them checking. Reliability means the client never has to wonder. These are the habits that build it."},{"t":"p","text":"**1. Acknowledge every request within 15 minutes.** During your shift, reply to every request from your client within 15 minutes, even if you cannot do the task yet. The reply tells them three things: you saw it, you understood it, and when it will be done."},{"t":"script","title":"Acknowledging a request","lines":[{"who":"Client","text":"Can you pull all the leads from last weekend’s open house and send them a thank-you text?"},{"who":"You","text":"On it! 14 leads from the Oak Ave open house. I’ll check which ones agreed to texts, send the thank-you within the calling hours, and confirm here by 2:00 PM ET."}]},{"t":"p","text":"**2. Commit to a time, with a time zone.** “Soon” and “later” mean nothing. “By 2:00 PM ET” is a promise the client can plan around. Choose a time you are sure you can meet, with a little extra margin."},{"t":"p","text":"**3. Close the loop.** When the task is done, say **“Done”** and what was done. If part of it could not be done, say what is missing and why: “Done: 11 of 14 texted. 3 had not agreed to texts, so I added a call task for each instead.” Never leave a request without a final answer."},{"t":"p","text":"**4. Bad news early.** If you see you will miss a deadline, tell the client as soon as you know, not at the deadline. A good rule: if half the time is gone and less than half the work is done, send a message. Bring a suggestion with the problem."},{"t":"table","head":["Too late","Early and helpful"],"rows":[["At 5:00 PM: “Sorry, I couldn’t finish the 40 contacts.”","At 2:00 PM: “Heads up: the contacts are taking longer because many have duplicates. I’ll finish 25 today. Should I prioritize the newest leads?”"],["The day after: “I forgot to confirm the showing.”","Before the showing: “I missed the confirmation text last night. I just called the buyer and confirmed 11 AM. The process fix: I set reminders the moment I book.”"]]},{"t":"p","text":"**5. Be there when you said you would be.** Be online five minutes before your start time. Keep the same schedule every day. If you will be late or absent, message your coordinator and the client channel **before** your start time. For planned days off, tell your coordinator as early as possible so they can arrange coverage; you will learn the full policy in F5."},{"t":"p","text":"**6. Keep your own task list.** Do not depend on memory. Every request goes into a list with the due time in the client’s zone. Before you end your shift, check it: every item is done, in progress with a new time, or handed over. Memory has no alarm button."},{"t":"p","text":"**7. Confirm before you start.** For any task that is new or unclear, repeat it back in one sentence before you begin. It takes 20 seconds and prevents hours of rework."},{"t":"callout","title":"The reliability test","text":"At the end of every shift, ask: Did I answer every request within 15 minutes? Did every promise have a time and a zone? Did I close every loop? Did I warn early about anything late? Did I post my daily report? Five yes answers is a reliable day."},{"t":"p","text":"**An example of reliability in action.** Kevin is an ISA for a team in Atlanta. At 1:00 PM his client asks him to call 30 old leads before 5:00 PM ET. Kevin replies at 1:04 PM: “On it, 30 leads by 5:00 PM ET. I’ll check each one is cleared for calling first.” At 3:00 PM he has called 12, because several leads talked for a long time. He writes: “Heads up: 12 of 30 done, 2 appointments set! Long conversations are slowing me down. I can finish about 22 by 5. Should I continue tomorrow morning with the rest?” The client answers: “Great work. Yes, finish tomorrow.” Kevin did not finish the task, and the client is happier than if he had rushed all 30."},{"t":"dodont","do":["Reply to client requests within 15 minutes during your shift.","Say “Done” with details, or say exactly what is missing.","Warn early and bring a suggestion."],"dont":["Go silent while you work on a long task.","Promise a time you are not sure about.","Wait until the deadline to say there is a problem."]}]}],"practice":{"title":"Clocks, calls and messages drill","minutes":75,"intro":"Three short drills that train the skills from this module: time-zone math, clear phone English and strong written messages. Work alone, then record and write your answers for your coach.","steps":["Build your personal time-zone table: your country against ET, CT, MT, Arizona and PT, for both March to November and November to March. Add the dates of the next two US clock changes.","Solve these in writing, showing your clock and the other clock: (a) Your client is in Florida; a lead lives in Seattle. It is Sunday in January. What is the earliest time in your clock you may call the lead? (b) You work 9:00 AM to 5:00 PM CT. What are your hours in your country in July and in December? (c) A lead in Phoenix asks for a call “at 7 PM” in June. What time is that for your client in Dallas, and for you?","Record yourself (audio) reading the call opening script from F2-3 three times: once fast, once at 80 percent speed, once at 80 percent speed while smiling. Listen and choose the best one.","Record yourself saying these out loud: (661) 555-0142; (480) 555-0199; 1820 Elm St; 405 Oak Ave; $450,000; $1.2M; 3 bd / 2 ba; 6:30 PM; the email jen.walsh@example.com spelled letter by letter.","Record a 2-minute mock call with a friend or family member playing a lead named Jennifer: open the call with the recording notice, confirm her email by spelling it back, confirm a date and time, and close politely.","Rewrite these three weak messages into strong ones: (a) “hi, the thing you asked is not done yet sorry”; (b) “I’ll call them later”; (c) “ok” in reply to “Can you confirm the 3 showings for Saturday?”","Write one subject line and a five-line email to your client reporting that Saturday’s open house sign-in sheets are entered (22 visitors, 9 agreed to texts, 4 want a call back).","Write your answers to the reliability test from F2-5 for an imaginary day where you missed one deadline, and write the early warning message you should have sent."],"deliverable":"One document with your time-zone table, the three time calculations, the three rewritten messages, the email and the reliability answers, plus three audio files: the best call opening, the numbers drill and the 2-minute mock call. Send them to your coach, named Lastname_F2_practice_date."},"quiz":[{"q":"Your client asks for 40 contacts by 5 PM, and you know you can only finish 25. What is the best answer?","options":["“Yes, no problem!” and do your best","“I can finish 25 by 5:00 PM ET. Which ones should come first?”","Say nothing and finish what you can","“That’s impossible.”"],"answer":1,"explain":"In US workplaces, direct is polite. A clear answer with an alternative is respected; a “yes” followed by a missed promise breaks trust."},{"q":"It is 10:00 AM in Honduras in July. What time is it in Los Angeles (Pacific)?","options":["8:00 AM","10:00 AM","9:00 AM","12:00 PM"],"answer":2,"explain":"From March to November, Pacific Daylight Time is one hour behind Honduras. In winter it is two hours behind."},{"q":"Your client is in Florida. The lead lives in California. Whose clock decides when you may call the lead?","options":["The lead's clock","Your clock","Your client's clock","Whichever is earliest"],"answer":0,"explain":"The calling window is always in the lead's local time. A Florida client can have California leads."},{"q":"When do most US states change their clocks?","options":["The first Sunday in January and July","The last Sunday in March and October","The US does not change its clocks","Forward on the second Sunday in March, back on the first Sunday in November"],"answer":3,"explain":"Daylight saving time starts on the second Sunday in March and ends on the first Sunday in November. Honduras does not change, so your hours there move."},{"q":"A lead says their budget is “fifteen hundred a month”, but the line is bad. What do you do?","options":["Write 1,500 and move on","Repeat it back and confirm a second way: “Just to confirm, fifteen hundred, one-five-zero-zero?”","Ask them to email it","Guess from the price of homes they viewed"],"answer":1,"explain":"Teen and -ty numbers (15 and 50) sound alike on the phone. Always repeat important numbers back, and say them a second way."},{"q":"How do you write November 3, 2026 for a US client?","options":["3/11/2026","2026/3/11","11/03/2026, or “Tue, Nov 3”","3 Nov 26"],"answer":2,"explain":"US dates put the month first. When it matters, write the month as a word to avoid confusion."},{"q":"Which money format is correct in the US?","options":["$1.250,50","1250,50 USD","$ 1 250.50","$1,250.50"],"answer":3,"explain":"In the US, commas separate thousands and a period separates cents, with the dollar sign first."},{"q":"Your client posts a new request while you are busy on another task. What is the reliability standard?","options":["Acknowledge within 15 minutes with what you will do and by when","Reply when the first task is done","Reply at the end of the day in the report","Only reply if it is urgent"],"answer":0,"explain":"Acknowledge every request within 15 minutes during your shift: you saw it, you understood it, and when it will be done."},{"q":"Halfway through the time for a task, you have done less than a third of it. What should you do?","options":["Work faster and hope","Wait until the deadline to explain","Tell the client now, with a suggestion","Ask another VA to do it secretly"],"answer":2,"explain":"Bad news early. If half the time is gone and less than half the work is done, send a heads-up with a suggestion."},{"q":"Which word is a false friend that can confuse a Spanish speaker?","options":["Actually, which means “in fact”, not “currently”","Lead, which means a prospect","Showing, which means a home tour","Closing, which means the end of a sale"],"answer":0,"explain":"“Actually” means “in fact” or “really”. To say “actualmente”, use “currently”."}]},{"id":"F3","part":"foundations","title":"Your tools","summary":"Set up and use the tools every BeyondVAs assistant works with: your workstation and backups, the outage protocol, Hubstaff, the password vault, Google Workspace and Follow Up Boss.","objectives":["Prepare a workstation with backup internet and backup power, and follow the outage protocol without losing client time or trust.","Track your time honestly in Hubstaff: clock in and out correctly, write task notes, and explain low activity before it becomes a question.","Protect client accounts and data: use the password vault, recognize phishing and fraud attempts, and never download or export client data.","Use Gmail, Google Calendar, Docs, Sheets and video calls the way a US client expects, with every time in the right time zone.","Find your way around Follow Up Boss: people, profiles, stages, tags, tasks, notes, Smart Lists and action plans."],"lessons":[{"id":"F3-1","title":"Your workstation, internet and power","minutes":6,"body":[{"t":"p","text":"A US client cannot see your office, but they feel it on every call and every task. A dropped call, a frozen screen or barking in the background costs trust. Before your first client, your workstation must be ready and tested. BeyondVAs verified your computer, internet and backup during your application. This lesson explains what “ready” means every single day."},{"t":"table","head":["Item","What you need","Why"],"rows":[["Computer","A modern laptop or desktop with webcam, updated operating system and antivirus, used only by you for client work","Client tools run in the browser; slow or shared machines cause errors and security risks"],["Headset","A wired or reliable headset with a microphone that reduces noise","Leads must hear you clearly; laptop microphones pick up the room"],["Main internet","A stable home connection, with the computer connected by cable when possible","Cable is more stable than Wi-Fi for calls and video"],["Backup internet","A mobile hotspot with enough data, ideally from a different provider than your home internet","If your main internet fails, you switch in minutes"],["Backup power","A laptop with several hours of battery, or a battery backup (UPS) for the computer and router","Power outages happen; your router needs power too"],["Work space","A quiet room with a door, good light, a neutral background","Clients and leads should hear only you"]]},{"t":"p","text":"**Test your internet.** Run a speed test at the start of your first week and whenever calls sound bad. Look at three numbers: download speed, upload speed and ping (also called latency). For voice and video calls, upload and ping matter most. Your coordinator gives you the minimum for your role; as a general guide, a stable connection with a low ping (under about 100 milliseconds) is more important than a very high download speed. Take a screenshot of the result for your main and your backup connection; you will send both in the practice."},{"t":"p","text":"**Test your backup before you need it.** Many VAs have a backup that does not work when the moment comes: the hotspot has no data, the phone battery is low, or the UPS battery is old. Once a week, switch to your backup for five minutes and make a test call. Keep your phone charged during your shift, and keep enough data on the plan for at least half a day of work."},{"t":"callout","title":"Your personal checklist, every morning","text":"Fifteen minutes before your shift: computer charged and updated, headset connected and tested, internet working, phone charged with hotspot data, battery backup on, client tools open and logged in through the vault, world clock showing your client’s time zone. Then clock in."},{"t":"p","text":"**Noise and privacy.** Tell the people you live with your working hours. Close the door. Put pets in another room during calls. Turn off notifications on your phone and computer that are not work related. Remember that Hubstaff screenshots show your whole screen while you are clocked in: keep only client work open, and never open personal email, social media or banking during your shift."},{"t":"p","text":"**Separate work and personal.** Use a separate browser profile for client work, so client tabs, logins and history never mix with your personal ones. Do not let anyone else use the computer you work on. If you share a computer at home, talk to your coordinator before your first shift."},{"t":"dodont","do":["Connect by cable when you can, and keep Wi-Fi as a second option.","Test your backup internet and power once a week.","Keep your workspace quiet and your screen showing only client work."],"dont":["Work from a café, a car or any public Wi-Fi with client data.","Let family members use your work computer.","Wait for an outage to find out your hotspot has no data."]},{"t":"p","text":"**An example.** Luis works for a team in Houston. One afternoon a storm hits his city and the power goes out. His laptop keeps running on battery, but his router turns off. Because he tested his hotspot that Monday, he switches in under two minutes, tells his manager and posts a short note in the client channel. The client barely notices. A month later, the client asks BeyondVAs for a second VA, “someone like Luis”. Preparation is invisible when it works, and that is the point."}]},{"id":"F3-2","title":"The outage protocol","minutes":6,"body":[{"t":"p","text":"Internet and power outages are not your fault. How you handle them is your responsibility. A VA who disappears for two hours during an outage loses trust. A VA who follows the protocol keeps it, even if work stops for a while. Learn these steps until they are automatic."},{"t":"steps","items":["**Tell your manager at once.** Message your BeyondVAs manager from your phone the moment the outage starts: what happened, when, and what you are doing.","**Switch to your backup.** Turn on your hotspot or battery backup and reconnect. Most switches take a few minutes.","**Tell the client channel.** Post a short, calm note: you are on backup and working, or you are offline and when you will update them.","**Handle what was interrupted.** If a call dropped, call or text the lead back as soon as you are connected (inside the calling window). If you were in the middle of a task, check that nothing was saved half done.","**Fix your time.** If you could not work for a period, clock out in Hubstaff for that time, or tell your manager so the record is correct. Never leave the timer running while you cannot work.","**Update and close.** When the outage ends, tell your manager and the client channel that you are back to normal, and add a line to your daily report."]},{"t":"script","title":"Outage messages that build trust","lines":[{"who":"You","text":"(to your manager) Hi Carla, power outage in my area since 2:10 PM my time. Laptop on battery, switching to my phone hotspot now. Will confirm in 5 minutes."},{"who":"You","text":"(to your manager, 5 minutes later) Back online on hotspot. Calls sound clear. I’ll keep you posted if anything changes."},{"who":"You","text":"(in the client channel) Hi Mark, quick heads-up: power outage here, I’m on backup and working normally. If my connection drops, I’ll switch to tasks and update you within 15 minutes."}]},{"t":"p","text":"**If the backup also fails.** Sometimes both connections are down, for example during a big storm. Then your manager decides what happens next: they may tell the client, ask a teammate to cover urgent work like new leads, or agree on a time to check in again. Your job is to keep your manager informed with honest updates, for example every 30 minutes by phone, and to not make promises to the client that you cannot keep."},{"t":"callout","title":"Speed to lead does not stop","text":"If you are an ISA, new leads keep arriving during your outage, and the first team to call usually wins them. That is why your manager must know immediately: they can make sure someone covers new leads while you reconnect. Five minutes of silence from you can cost your client a buyer."},{"t":"p","text":"**What you must never do during an outage.** You may want to “keep working” by saving client files on your computer or phone to work offline. Do not. You never download or export client data, not even during an outage. You also never use a public Wi-Fi network in a café to continue client work; it is not secure. If you cannot work safely, you stop, clock out and tell your manager. A safe pause is always better than a data risk."},{"t":"table","head":["Situation","Right action"],"rows":[["Internet drops for 30 seconds during a call","Reconnect, call the lead back right away, apologize briefly: “Sorry, my line dropped.”"],["Power out, laptop on battery, router off","Switch to hotspot, tell your manager, note in client channel."],["Hotspot and power both down for an hour","Tell your manager by phone, clock out, update every 30 minutes, follow their plan."],["You expect a planned outage tomorrow (your power company announced it)","Tell your manager today, plan to work on backup, confirm with the client channel."],["Your computer breaks","Tell your manager at once. Never move client work to a borrowed or family computer without approval."]]},{"t":"p","text":"**Planned outages.** In many areas, power companies announce cuts in advance. If you know about one, tell your manager the day before and make sure your backup battery and hotspot are ready. Your client will appreciate the warning more than any explanation after the fact."},{"t":"dodont","do":["Message your manager first, within minutes.","Keep the client informed with short, calm notes.","Correct your tracked time for any period you could not work."],"dont":["Disappear and explain later.","Download client files to work offline, or use public Wi-Fi.","Blame the client’s tools or the weather in a long message. Short and factual is best."]}]},{"id":"F3-3","title":"Hubstaff: time tracking and screenshots","minutes":6,"body":[{"t":"p","text":"Hubstaff is the time tracker BeyondVAs uses. It records your hours, takes screenshots and measures activity while you are clocked in. Your hours in Hubstaff are what the client is billed and what you are paid for. The client can see your hours and screenshots. Used well, Hubstaff is your best friend: it proves your work without you having to explain it."},{"t":"p","text":"**How it works.** You install the Hubstaff desktop app, sign in with the account BeyondVAs creates for you, and choose your client’s project. When you press start, the timer runs. While it runs:"},{"t":"list","items":["Hubstaff takes **screenshots at random moments, about every two minutes**. You do not know exactly when. If you use two monitors, it captures both.","It measures **activity**: how much of the time your keyboard or mouse was used. It does not record what you type.","If you stop using the keyboard and mouse for a while, the time may be marked as **idle**, and the app may ask you whether you were working.","Your manager and your client see the hours, the activity levels and the screenshots."]},{"t":"callout","title":"Unexplained idle time is reviewed","text":"Long periods with no activity and no explanation are flagged. Your manager reviews flagged time before the client is billed and may ask you about it. A short task note usually answers the question before anyone asks."},{"t":"p","text":"**The four habits of clean time.**"},{"t":"steps","items":["**Start when you start.** Clock in when you begin client work, not when you wake up or while you make coffee.","**Stop for every break.** Lunch, personal calls, errands, a child who needs you: stop the timer. Start it again when you return.","**Write task notes.** Choose the task if your manager created tasks in Hubstaff, and add a short note when you change activity, especially work with little typing: “Calls: new leads” or “Reading client SOP for listings”.","**Answer idle prompts honestly.** If Hubstaff asks about idle time and you were away, choose to remove that time. If you were on a call, keep it and add a note."]},{"t":"p","text":"**Low activity is normal for some work.** Calls, video meetings, reading long documents and watching training videos all have little typing. That is real work. The problem is only low activity with no explanation. Compare these two days:"},{"t":"table","head":["Day A (questions)","Day B (no questions)"],"rows":[["10:00 to 11:30, activity 8%, no note","10:00 to 11:30, activity 8%, note: “Calls with new leads (12 dials, 3 conversations)”"],["Screenshots show the dialer only","Screenshots show the dialer and the CRM, matching the note"],["Daily report: “made calls”","Daily report: “85 dials, 13 conversations, 2 appointments”"]]},{"t":"p","text":"The work was the same; Day B tells a clear story. For ISAs, managers also compare activity with the call log, because a VA on a 20-minute call looks idle to a tracker and is not."},{"t":"p","text":"**Screenshots and privacy.** Screenshots show your whole screen. That is why you keep only client work open while you are clocked in. Never open personal email, social media, banking or private chats during your shift. If you need to do something personal, stop the timer first. Screenshots may also capture client information, which is why they are visible only to your client and BeyondVAs."},{"t":"p","text":"**Manual time and corrections.** If you forgot to start the timer, do not add time on your own. Tell your manager the same day with the exact times and what you worked on, and follow their instructions. If you forgot to stop the timer, tell your manager so the extra time is removed. Honest corrections are normal; hidden ones are not."},{"t":"callout","title":"Zero tolerance","text":"Never use any device, app or trick that fakes keyboard or mouse activity, and never let someone else work under your account. Hubstaff and your manager can detect these patterns. Fake activity is fraud and ends the placement and your relationship with BeyondVAs."},{"t":"dodont","do":["Start and stop the timer exactly with your work.","Write a note for calls, meetings and reading.","Report any forgotten clock-in or clock-out the same day."],"dont":["Leave the timer running during breaks.","Open personal sites or chats while clocked in.","Add or edit time without telling your manager."]}]},{"id":"F3-4","title":"Passwords, the vault and security habits","minutes":6,"body":[{"t":"p","text":"Your client trusts you with their CRM, their email and their leads’ names, phone numbers, addresses and sometimes finances. One stolen password can expose all of it. Real estate is also a favorite target for fraud, because large amounts of money move between buyers, sellers and banks. This lesson gives you the habits that keep that information safe."},{"t":"p","text":"**The password vault.** BeyondVAs gives you access to your client’s tools through a **password vault**: a secure app that stores logins and fills them in for you. You may never even see some passwords. The rules are simple:"},{"t":"list","items":["Passwords travel **only through the vault**. Never in chat, email, text, a document or a photo.","If a client tries to send you a password in the channel, reply: “Thanks! Could you add it to the vault instead? We never share passwords in chat.” If they already sent it, tell your manager so the password can be changed.","Never save client passwords in your browser, a notebook or a file.","Use a strong, unique password for your own vault account and your Hubstaff account, and turn on two-step verification wherever you can.","When you leave a client, your access is removed the same day. That protects you as much as the client."]},{"t":"p","text":"**Verification codes.** Many tools send a one-time code to the client’s phone when you sign in. Ask for it in the client channel, use it right away, and never forward a code to anyone else. If you receive a code you did not request, someone may be trying to sign in to that account: tell your manager and the client at once."},{"t":"callout","title":"Client data never leaves the client’s systems","text":"You work inside your client’s tools. You never download, export, copy, print or photograph client data, and you never move it to your own computer, phone, email or cloud storage. This is part of your contract. If a client asks you to export a list, tell your coordinator before you do anything."},{"t":"p","text":"**Phishing: the fake message.** Phishing is a message that pretends to be someone you trust in order to steal a password or money. Watch for these signs:"},{"t":"list","items":["**Urgency and pressure:** “Your account will be closed in 1 hour, log in now.”","**A sender that is almost right:** mark@parkerhometeam-mail.example.com instead of the client’s real address.","**A link to a login page** you did not ask for. Open your tools only from the vault or your saved bookmarks, never from a link in an email.","**Unusual requests from “the boss”:** buy gift cards, change payment details, send a list of contacts, keep it secret.","**Attachments you did not expect,** especially files that ask you to enable something."]},{"t":"script","title":"Checking a suspicious request","lines":[{"who":"Email","text":"From: “Mark Parker”. Hi, I’m in a showing and can’t talk. I need you to buy four $100 gift cards for a client gift and send me the codes. Urgent, don’t tell anyone."},{"who":"You","text":"(in the client channel, not replying to the email) Hi Mark, I received an email from an address that looks like yours, asking me to buy gift cards. I haven’t replied or clicked anything. Can you confirm it’s not from you? I’m also letting my manager know."},{"who":"Client","text":"Definitely not me. Thanks for checking!"}]},{"t":"p","text":"**Wire fraud in real estate.** Criminals send fake emails to buyers with new bank instructions for the down payment. You will rarely touch this, but remember: never send, change or confirm any bank or payment details for anyone. If a lead or client mentions new wiring instructions, tell your client right away so the agent can warn the buyer by phone."},{"t":"dodont","do":["Lock your screen every time you stand up.","Keep your computer and browser updated.","Report a lost device, a strange login or a clicked bad link to your manager within the hour.","Check any unusual request in the client channel before you act."],"dont":["Install browser extensions or software on client accounts without approval.","Use public Wi-Fi or a shared computer for client work.","Feel embarrassed to report a mistake. Fast reporting limits the damage; hiding it multiplies it."]},{"t":"p","text":"Security is not about being afraid. It is about a few calm habits every day: vault for passwords, client data stays inside, check before you click, and report quickly."}]},{"id":"F3-5","title":"Google Workspace and video calls","minutes":6,"body":[{"t":"p","text":"Most US real estate teams run their office on Google Workspace: Gmail, Calendar, Drive, Docs and Sheets. Some use Microsoft 365 instead; the habits are the same. Your client usually gives you an email address on their own domain, such as sofia@parkerhometeam.example.com. Everything you do there represents your client."},{"t":"p","text":"**Gmail.** Your client may ask you to manage their inbox or your own team address. Keep it organized:"},{"t":"list","items":["Use **labels** to sort mail the way your client decides, for example “Leads”, “Listings”, “Showings”, “Vendors”, “Waiting for reply”.","Use **stars** or a label for anything that needs action, and clear them when done.","Read each email fully before replying. Reply all only when everyone needs the answer.","Use templates the client approves for common replies, and personalize the name and details every time.","Never delete emails unless your client asks. Archive instead."]},{"t":"p","text":"**Google Calendar.** In real estate, the calendar is where money is made: showings, listing appointments and consultations. One wrong time zone can make an agent miss a buyer. Set up your calendar like this: set the **primary time zone to your client’s zone**, and add your own zone as a **secondary time zone**, so you see both clocks on the side of the calendar."},{"t":"steps","items":["Create the event at the correct time in the right zone, and check it twice.","Use a clear title: “Showing: Jennifer Walsh, 1820 Elm St”.","Add the address in the location field, and the details the agent needs in the description (your client will tell you what to include; you will learn the full booking rules in I8).","Invite the right people only, with the right email addresses.","Set reminders the way your client wants.","Never move or delete an event on an agent’s calendar without permission."]},{"t":"p","text":"**Drive, Docs and Sheets.** Your client’s documents live in their Drive. Follow these rules:"},{"t":"dodont","do":["Save work in the folder your client chooses, with clear names: “2026-11-03 Open house sign-ins, Oak Ave”.","Share a file only with the specific people who need it, by email address.","Use comments to ask questions in a document, and suggestion mode to propose changes to someone else’s text.","Use version history if something goes wrong, instead of trying to rebuild a file.","Keep spreadsheets clean: one header row, one record per row, no merged cells in data."],"dont":["Set any client file to public or “anyone can view” sharing.","Download client files to your computer, or save them in your personal account.","Rename, move or delete files that other people use without asking.","Rewrite someone else’s document directly when suggestion mode is available."]},{"t":"p","text":"**Video calls (Google Meet, Zoom or similar).** Your kickoff, check-ins and team meetings are video calls. Treat them like an in-person meeting."},{"t":"list","items":["Join one or two minutes early, with your camera on unless told otherwise.","Test your headset and microphone before the meeting starts.","Use a neutral background or a simple blur. Good light in front of you, not behind you.","Mute yourself when you are not speaking in a group call.","Keep a notebook open, and write action items with owners and due times.","Close other tabs and notifications; you may need to share your screen."]},{"t":"callout","title":"After every meeting","text":"Within an hour, post a short summary in the client channel: what was decided, what you will do and by when, and any questions. “Summary of today’s check-in: 1) I take over showing confirmations starting Monday. 2) Open-house leads go in the CRM the same day. 3) Question: should I text weekend leads on Sunday afternoon?” This turns a conversation into a record."},{"t":"p","text":"**An example of a calendar mistake.** A VA created a listing appointment for “Thursday at 5” in her own calendar zone, Honduras, during the summer. Her client was in Los Angeles. The event showed at 4:00 PM Pacific, and the agent arrived an hour early to an empty house. Setting the primary calendar zone to the client’s zone prevents this. Always look at the event in the client’s zone before you send the invite."}]},{"id":"F3-6","title":"A tour of Follow Up Boss","minutes":6,"body":[{"t":"p","text":"Follow Up Boss (often called FUB) is a CRM: a customer relationship manager. It is the system where your client keeps every lead, every call, every text, every note and every task. Many US real estate teams use it. Others use different CRMs; the ideas in this lesson work in all of them. Your coach gives you a practice account so you can click through everything before you touch a real client’s data."},{"t":"callout","title":"The most important rule","text":"**If it is not in the CRM, it did not happen.** Your client’s dashboards, your agents and your manager only see what you log. Every call, text, note, stage change and task is logged the same day, inside the CRM. All lead conversations happen through the client’s systems: never from your personal phone, WhatsApp or personal email."},{"t":"p","text":"**The main parts of Follow Up Boss.**"},{"t":"glossary","terms":[{"term":"People","def":"The list of all contacts (leads, past clients, sphere). You can search, filter and sort it."},{"term":"Person profile","def":"One contact’s page: name, phones, emails, address, source (where the lead came from), assigned agent, stage, tags, and the timeline."},{"term":"Timeline","def":"The history on the profile: every call, text, email, note, task and stage change, newest first. Read it before every call."},{"term":"Stage","def":"Where the person is in the process. Each client names its own stages; in this program you will see stages like New, Attempted Contact, Hot Active, Nurture, Pending Handoff and Archived / DNC."},{"term":"Tags","def":"Short labels that describe a person, for example “Open House Oak Ave”, “Spanish”, “Seller”, or “DNC”. Tags drive lists and automations, so use only the tags your client approves."},{"term":"Source","def":"Where the lead came from: a website form, a portal, an ad, an open house, a referral."},{"term":"Task","def":"A to-do with a type (call, text, email, follow up), a due date and an owner. Tasks due today appear on your task list."},{"term":"Note","def":"What you write after each contact. ISAs use a fixed format: Date/Time, Call/Text Disposition, A, L, M, Next Step (you will master it in I7)."},{"term":"Smart List","def":"A saved filter that shows the people who match certain rules, such as “new leads in the last 15 minutes”. You work your day from Smart Lists."},{"term":"Action plan","def":"An automatic sequence of emails, texts or tasks that runs over days or weeks. It must be paused when a person opts out or books an appointment."},{"term":"Deals","def":"The pipeline of transactions (under contract, closing). VAs often update it for the client."}]},{"t":"p","text":"**Calling and texting from the CRM.** Follow Up Boss can place calls and send texts from inside the profile, and it logs them on the timeline automatically. Your client may also use a separate dialer connected to the CRM. Either way, you dial by hand from the client’s system. You will learn the rules for who you may call and text, and when, in F6."},{"t":"p","text":"**How an ISA uses the CRM in a shift.** You will learn each step in depth in the ISA track. Here is the overview, so the pieces make sense:"},{"t":"steps","items":["Open your Smart Lists in order: New Speed-to-Lead (0 to 15 minutes old), Hot Active (ready within 30 days), Scheduled Follow-Up Tasks (due today), then 30 to 60 Day Nurtures.","Open the person’s profile and read the timeline before every call: what did they ask, who talked to them last, what was promised?","Call or text from the profile, inside the lead’s calling window.","Log the note right after the contact, never at the end of the day from memory.","Update the stage, the tags and the next task before you move to the next person.","Before you end the shift, check that today’s tasks are done or rescheduled with a reason."]},{"t":"p","text":"**How an operations VA uses it.** Entering open-house sign-ins as new people with the right source and tag, cleaning duplicates, updating contact details, adding tasks for agents, updating deals and making sure every new lead is assigned. The rule is the same: accurate, same day, inside the system."},{"t":"dodont","do":["Search before you create a person, to avoid duplicates.","Read the timeline before every contact.","Log every contact right away, in English, in the client’s format.","Pause action plans when a lead opts out or books an appointment."],"dont":["Delete a contact. Opted-out contacts especially must stay, tagged DNC, so nobody contacts them again.","Invent new tags or stages without your client’s approval.","Export lists or copy contacts outside the CRM.","Write opinions about a lead in notes. Write facts and what they want."]},{"t":"p","text":"Spend time clicking around your practice account. The more natural the CRM feels, the more attention you can give to the person on the phone."}]}],"practice":{"title":"Set up and prove your toolkit","minutes":100,"intro":"In this practice you prepare your real workstation and show your coach that every tool works, including your backups. Then you complete a short exercise in the Follow Up Boss practice account your coach gives you. Use only fictional leads.","steps":["Run a speed test on your main connection (by cable if possible) and on your backup hotspot. Take a screenshot of each result showing download, upload and ping.","Do a backup drill: during a test call with a friend or family member, turn off your main internet and switch to your hotspot. Time how long the switch takes. Then unplug your laptop charger and write down how many hours of battery the system estimates.","Write your personal outage plan (half a page): your backup internet, your backup power, your manager's contact method, and the three messages you would send (manager, manager update, client channel).","In Hubstaff, with your coach's permission, run a 20-minute practice session on the training project: 10 minutes of typing work and 10 minutes reading a lesson. Add a task note for each part. Take a screenshot of the session summary.","Set up your Google Calendar with a client time zone of Pacific as primary and your own zone as secondary. Create a practice event: “Showing: Jennifer Walsh, 1820 Elm St” on next Saturday at 11:00 AM Pacific. Screenshot the event showing both time zones.","Phishing check: read these three messages and write for each whether it is safe and what you would do: (a) an email from your client's real address asking you to call a lead; (b) an email from “Mark Parker” at an unknown domain asking for the CRM password; (c) a text with a link saying your Hubstaff account will be closed today.","In the Follow Up Boss practice account: search for “Jennifer Walsh” to check for duplicates, then create her as a new person (phone (661) 555-0142, email jen.walsh@example.com, source “Website”, tag “Practice”). Add a note “Practice note: first contact attempt, left voicemail”, set her stage to Attempted Contact (or the closest stage in the account), and create a call task for tomorrow at 10:00 AM in her time zone.","Find one Smart List in the practice account and write two sentences about which people it shows and why an ISA would work it."],"deliverable":"Send your coach: the two speed-test screenshots, your backup drill results (switch time and battery hours), your written outage plan, the Hubstaff session screenshot, the calendar screenshot, your phishing answers, and a screenshot of Jennifer Walsh's profile showing the note, stage, tag and task, plus your Smart List sentences. Name the files Lastname_F3_practice_date."},"quiz":[{"q":"The power goes out during your shift. What is the first thing you do?","options":["Wait 30 minutes to see if it comes back","Message your manager at once, then switch to your backup and tell the client channel","Clock out and go offline until the next day","Tell the client in tomorrow's report"],"answer":1,"explain":"Tell your manager immediately and switch to backup internet or power. Then post a short note in the client channel."},{"q":"Your internet and backup are both down for a long time. A coworker suggests working from a café's Wi-Fi with client files saved on your phone. What do you do?","options":["Do it so the client does not lose time","Use the café Wi-Fi but do not save files","Save the files on a USB drive to work at home later","Stop, clock out, keep your manager informed and follow their plan"],"answer":3,"explain":"You never download or export client data and never use public Wi-Fi for client work. A safe pause is better than a data risk."},{"q":"How often does Hubstaff take screenshots while you are clocked in?","options":["At random moments, about every two minutes","Once an hour at a fixed time","Only when the client requests one","Never; it only counts hours"],"answer":0,"explain":"Screenshots are taken at random moments, about every two minutes, and the client can see them along with your hours."},{"q":"You spend 40 minutes on calls with leads and barely use the keyboard. What is the right Hubstaff habit?","options":["Use a tool that moves the mouse","Stop the timer during calls","Add a task note such as “Calls: new leads” so the low activity is explained","Type random text between calls"],"answer":2,"explain":"Low activity during calls is normal work. A note explains it. Any trick that fakes activity is fraud."},{"q":"You forgot to start the timer for the first hour of your shift. What do you do?","options":["Add the hour yourself in Hubstaff","Tell your manager the same day with the exact times and what you did","Work an extra hour at the end without telling anyone","Leave the timer running tonight to make up for it"],"answer":1,"explain":"Never add or edit time on your own. Report forgotten clock-ins or clock-outs to your manager the same day and follow their instructions."},{"q":"Your client posts the CRM password in the channel. What is the best response?","options":["Thank them and save it in your browser","Copy it into your notes document","Ask them to put it in the vault, and tell your manager so the password can be changed","Delete the message and use the password"],"answer":2,"explain":"Passwords travel only through the password vault. A password posted in chat should be changed, so tell your manager."},{"q":"An email from “your client” at a slightly different address asks you to buy gift cards urgently and keep it secret. What do you do?","options":["Buy them; the client is busy","Reply to the email to ask if it is real","Click the link to verify the sender","Do not reply or click; check with the client in the client channel and tell your manager"],"answer":3,"explain":"Urgency, secrecy, gift cards and an almost-right address are classic phishing signs. Verify through a channel you trust, never by replying to the email."},{"q":"What is the best way to set up Google Calendar to avoid booking mistakes?","options":["Primary time zone set to your client's zone, your own zone as secondary","Primary time zone set to your own zone only","Use UTC for everything","Write the times in the event title instead"],"answer":0,"explain":"Seeing both clocks, with the client's zone as primary, prevents appointments from landing an hour off."},{"q":"A lead opted out and asked never to be contacted. What do you do with their contact in Follow Up Boss?","options":["Delete the contact so nobody calls them","Keep the contact, tag it DNC, set the stage to Archived / DNC and pause action plans","Export it to a separate spreadsheet","Leave it as it is and remember not to call"],"answer":1,"explain":"Never delete an opted-out contact: the DNC record is what stops future calls and texts. Tag it, archive it and pause automations. Full rules are in F6."},{"q":"Which statement best describes the CRM rule for BeyondVAs assistants?","options":["Log calls at the end of the week","Personal WhatsApp is fine for leads who prefer it","If it is not in the CRM, it did not happen: log every contact the same day inside the client's system","Notes are optional if the call was short"],"answer":2,"explain":"Your client's dashboards and agents only see what you log. Every contact is logged the same day, and lead conversations stay inside the client's systems."}]},{"id":"F4","part":"foundations","title":"Real estate basics and vocabulary","summary":"How a US home is bought and sold, who does each job, where leads come from, and the 50 words you will hear every day on a real estate team.","objectives":["Explain the buyer journey and the seller journey step by step, and say where an ISA or VA helps in each one","Name the people in a US transaction (agent, broker, lender, escrow and title, inspector, appraiser, transaction coordinator) and what each one does","Read a listing on the MLS or a portal and turn it into a clear summary for your agent","Recognize the most common lead sources and listing statuses and what they mean for your next call","Use and pronounce 50 core real estate terms correctly in calls, texts and Follow Up Boss notes"],"lessons":[{"id":"F4-1","title":"Who does what on a US real estate team","minutes":7,"body":[{"t":"p","text":"Buying a home in the United States is the largest purchase most families ever make. It takes many people, many documents and usually 30 to 60 days from accepted offer to keys. Your client, the agent or team you work for, sits in the middle of all of it. Before you can help them, you need to know who the other players are, what each one is responsible for, and where your job begins and ends."},{"t":"p","text":"In this lesson you meet the cast. Learn the names well: you will hear them in every team meeting, every lead note and every call."},{"t":"table","head":["Role","What they do","How you interact with them"],"rows":[["**Real estate agent** (also “salesperson” or “licensee”)","Holds a state license. Represents a buyer or a seller, gives advice, shows homes, writes and negotiates offers.","Your client, or an agent on your client’s team. You book appointments on their calendar and hand leads to them."],["**REALTOR®**","An agent who is a member of the National Association of REALTORS® and follows its Code of Ethics. Not every agent is a REALTOR®.","Same as an agent. Always write it with a capital R."],["**Broker**","Holds a higher license. Supervises agents and is legally responsible for their work. Every agent works under a broker.","Rarely. The broker’s rules decide what you may do."],["**Team lead**","A senior agent who runs a team of agents, sets the lead rules and often owns the CRM.","Often your day-to-day client contact."],["**ISA** (inside sales agent)","Calls and texts leads, qualifies them with A.L.M. and books appointments for agents.","This may be you."],["**VA** (virtual assistant)","Handles admin: inbox, calendar, CRM updates, listing paperwork, showing confirmations.","This may be you, or a teammate."],["**Lender / loan officer**","Checks the buyer’s income, credit and savings and issues a pre-approval, then the actual loan.","You never give loan advice. You connect a lead with the client’s preferred lender only when the agent says so."],["**Escrow officer / title company**","A neutral party that holds the money, checks that the seller really owns the home, and runs the closing.","You may send them documents or confirm dates if your client asks."],["**Home inspector**","Examines the home’s condition (roof, plumbing, electrical) and writes a report for the buyer.","You may schedule the inspection. You never explain or interpret the report."],["**Appraiser**","Gives the lender an independent opinion of the home’s value so the bank does not lend more than the home is worth.","Usually none. The lender orders the appraisal."],["**Transaction coordinator** (TC)","Manages the paperwork and deadlines from accepted offer to closing.","On some teams a VA does this job. You keep them informed of what you hear from leads."]]},{"t":"callout","title":"Listing agent and buyer’s agent","text":"The agent who represents the **seller** is the **listing agent** (or seller’s agent). The agent who represents the **buyer** is the **buyer’s agent**. In most deals there are two agents, one on each side. When a lead calls about a home your client has listed, they are calling the listing agent’s team. When a lead wants to buy, your client becomes their buyer’s agent."},{"t":"p","text":"**Where you fit.** Agents earn money when a home closes, and they close more homes when they spend their time in front of serious clients: at showings, listing consultations and negotiations. Every hour an agent spends dialing leads who do not answer is an hour not spent with a client. That is why teams hire ISAs and VAs. You protect the agent’s time. You find the serious people, learn what they need, and put them on the agent’s calendar with a note so complete that the agent never has to ask the same question twice."},{"t":"p","text":"**A day on a real estate team.** Teams run on a rhythm. Learn it so you know what your client expects at each hour."},{"t":"list","items":["**Morning:** new leads from the night and the early hours are called first, inside the calling window, and new leads during the day are called within five minutes. Agents prepare for showings.","**Midday:** follow-up calls and texts. Every lead you touch gets a next action and a date in the CRM.","**Afternoon:** agents are out at appointments, showings and listing consultations. You confirm tomorrow’s appointments and update notes.","**Evening (still inside the window):** many working people answer after 5 PM their time. This is often your best hour for live conversations.","**Weekly:** the team meets. Bring your numbers: dials, conversations, appointments set, appointments that happened."]},{"t":"dodont","do":["Learn the name and role of every person on your client’s team in your first week","Say “your agent” or the agent’s name when you talk to leads","Ask your coordinator when you are not sure whether a task belongs to you"],"dont":["Call yourself an agent, a realtor or a loan officer","Contact a lender, inspector or escrow officer for a lead unless your client asked you to","Assume every team works the same way: the roles above are common, but each client sets its own"]},{"t":"p","text":"**Common mistake:** new assistants sometimes answer a lead’s question about the loan, the inspection or the price because they want to be helpful. Being helpful here means connecting the lead to the right professional fast, not answering for them. You will practice the exact words in F5."}]},{"id":"F4-2","title":"The buyer journey, from first click to keys","minutes":7,"body":[{"t":"p","text":"Most of the leads you call are buyers, or people who might become buyers. To have a good conversation with them, you need to know where they are in the journey and what comes next. A buyer who is “just looking” online is at step one. A buyer who already has a pre-approval and wants to see a home on Saturday is at step four. You ask different questions and offer different next steps for each."},{"t":"steps","items":["**Dreaming and browsing.** The buyer looks at homes on portals and social media, often 6 to 12 months before buying. Many of your leads start here.","**First contact.** They click “request a tour” or “contact agent”, call a sign or fill a form. A lead is created in the CRM. This is where speed to lead matters most.","**Discovery with the ISA.** You learn A.L.M.: when they could meet (Appointment), where and what they want (Location), and why they are moving and by when (Motivation).","**Consultation with the agent and the buyer agreement.** The agent meets the buyer, explains how they work and signs a written buyer agreement before any tour. Since August 2024 this agreement is required before touring, and it states what the agent will be paid. The agent sends and explains it, never you.","**Pre-approval.** A lender reviews income, credit and savings and says how much the buyer can borrow. Sellers usually want to see this letter with any offer.","**Home search and showings.** The agent tours homes with the buyer, in person or by video.","**Offer and negotiation.** The agent writes an offer with a price, an earnest money deposit, a closing date and contingencies. The seller can accept, reject or counter.","**Under contract (in escrow).** The offer is accepted. The earnest money goes to escrow. Deadlines start.","**Inspection and appraisal.** The inspector checks the home’s condition; the appraiser checks its value for the lender. Problems may lead to repair requests or a new price.","**Loan approval and final walk-through.** The lender gives final approval (“clear to close”). The buyer walks through the home one last time.","**Closing.** Documents are signed, money moves, the deed is recorded, and the buyer gets the keys. The agent is paid at closing."]},{"t":"callout","title":"The buyer agreement rule","text":"An agent working with a buyer needs a **written buyer agreement before any tour**. When you book a tour, flag it in the calendar invite so the agent sends it first. If the lead asks what the agreement says or how much the agent is paid, say: “Your agent will walk you through that agreement before the tour.”"},{"t":"p","text":"**Contingencies, explained simply.** A contingency is a condition in the offer that lets the buyer cancel without losing their deposit if something goes wrong. The three most common are the **inspection contingency** (the home has serious problems), the **appraisal contingency** (the home is valued below the price) and the **financing contingency** (the loan is not approved). You do not need to explain them to leads. You need to understand them so you know why a deal can fall apart in escrow and why an agent may suddenly ask you to call back a lead who was “under contract”."},{"t":"p","text":"**How long it takes.** From the first click to closing can be a few weeks or more than a year. Once an offer is accepted, a financed purchase usually closes in 30 to 45 days. A cash purchase can close in one to two weeks."},{"t":"table","head":["What the lead says","Where they probably are","Your best next step"],"rows":[["“I’m just looking at what’s out there.”","Step 1, browsing","Ask what caught their eye, learn their timeline, offer a call with the agent or set a nurture follow-up."],["“Can I see 123 Oak Street on Saturday?”","Step 2, first contact, ready to tour","Qualify with A.L.M., book the agent, flag the buyer agreement in the invite."],["“We’re pre-approved up to $450,000.”","Step 5, serious","Treat as hot. Book an appointment within days, not weeks."],["“Our lease ends in March.”","Motivation and a deadline","Note the date. Plan the follow-up backwards from it."],["“We’re already under contract on a house.”","Step 8, in escrow with another agent or your client","Ask if they are working with an agent; note it; do not pitch."]]},{"t":"dodont","do":["Figure out the step before you choose your questions","Write the step and the timeline in your Follow Up Boss note","Treat pre-approved buyers with a deadline as your top priority"],"dont":["Tell a buyer how much they can afford or what rate they will get","Promise that an offer will be accepted","Book a tour without flagging the buyer agreement"]},{"t":"p","text":"**Mistake to avoid:** treating every lead as if they want to buy this month. Most online leads are months away. If you push them too hard, they stop answering. If you ignore them, another agent wins them. The skill is matching your next step to their step in the journey, which you will practice in the ISA track."}]},{"id":"F4-3","title":"The seller journey and seller leads","minutes":5,"body":[{"t":"p","text":"Sellers are fewer than buyers, but each seller is very valuable to your client. A listing brings marketing, sign calls and new buyer leads, and the agent gets paid when it closes. Many teams care more about one good seller appointment than about three buyer appointments. Learn the seller journey so you can recognize a seller lead and treat it with care."},{"t":"steps","items":["**Thinking about selling.** The owner wonders what the home is worth. They often request a home-value estimate online. This creates a lead.","**First contact.** You call. You learn their motivation (why sell, by when), the property (beds, baths, condition) and when they could meet the agent.","**Listing consultation (listing appointment).** The agent visits or meets by video, looks at the home and presents a **CMA**, a comparative market analysis that compares recently sold similar homes.","**Listing agreement.** The seller signs a contract that gives the agent the right to market the home for a period of time. It states the list price and what the agent is paid. Only the agent discusses and signs it.","**Preparation.** Repairs, cleaning, staging, professional photos, a description, sometimes a video or 3D tour.","**On the market.** The agent enters the listing in the MLS. It spreads to portals. Showings and open houses start.","**Offers and negotiation.** The agent presents offers to the seller and negotiates.","**Under contract to closing.** Inspection, appraisal, the buyer’s loan approval, then closing. The seller hands over the keys and receives the money."]},{"t":"p","text":"**Types of seller leads you will meet:**"},{"t":"list","items":["**Home-value request:** the owner typed their address into a “What is my home worth?” page. Curious, not always ready.","**FSBO** (For Sale By Owner): the owner is selling without an agent. Calling them is allowed only where your client’s state allows it, only with the client’s approved script, and only after the list is scrubbed against the Do Not Call lists.","**Expired listing:** the home was listed with an agent but did not sell before the agreement ended. Same rule as FSBO.","**Past client or sphere:** someone the agent already knows. Usually warm and friendly.","**Buyer who must sell first:** a buyer lead who owns a home. This is two leads in one: write both in the note."]},{"t":"callout","title":"Never give a value","text":"The most common seller question is “What is my home worth?” You never give a number, a range or an opinion, not even “around $400,000”. An opinion of value is the agent’s job. You say: “That is exactly what your agent will prepare for you, a full analysis of recent sales near you. Would Tuesday at 4 or Wednesday at 10 work for a quick visit?” You will practice this in the ISA track."},{"t":"script","title":"Recognizing a hidden seller","lines":[{"who":"You","text":"And are you renting right now, or do you own your current home?"},{"who":"Lead","text":"We own. We’d have to sell it to buy the bigger place."},{"who":"You","text":"That makes sense. Have you thought about timing, selling first or buying first?"},{"who":"Lead","text":"Not really. That’s what worries me."},{"who":"You","text":"That’s a very common question, and your agent can map out both options with you. Could you meet Thursday at 6 PM to talk through selling and buying together?"}]},{"t":"dodont","do":["Ask every buyer whether they own or rent","Note the property address, beds, baths and condition for every seller lead","Tell your agent right away about a seller lead with a deadline"],"dont":["Give a price, range or opinion of value","Promise how fast a home will sell","Call FSBO or expired listings unless your manager confirmed your state allows it and the list is scrubbed"]}]},{"id":"F4-4","title":"The MLS, portals, listing statuses and lead sources","minutes":6,"body":[{"t":"p","text":"Every lead you call saw a home somewhere. To speak with confidence, you need to know where listings live, how they spread, what their status means and where your leads come from."},{"t":"p","text":"**The MLS.** The Multiple Listing Service is a private database where licensed agents in an area share their listings. There are hundreds of MLSs in the US, each covering a region. Agents put listings in the MLS so that other agents can bring buyers. The MLS holds details the public does not see, such as showing instructions and agent remarks. Some clients will give you MLS access; many will not. Never share MLS-only information, such as private remarks or lockbox details, with a lead."},{"t":"p","text":"**IDX and portals.** IDX (Internet Data Exchange) lets agents show MLS listings on their own websites. Large portals such as Zillow, Realtor.com and Redfin also display listings to the public. When a buyer clicks “contact an agent” on a portal or a team website, a lead lands in your client’s CRM. Portal information can be out of date: a home that shows as active may already be under contract. That is why you never promise a lead that a home is available. You say: “Let me have your agent confirm that it’s still available.”"},{"t":"table","head":["Status","What it means","What you say to an interested lead"],"rows":[["**Coming soon**","Listed but not yet open for showings.","“It isn’t open for tours yet. I can have your agent let you know the moment it is.”"],["**Active**","On the market, accepting showings and offers.","“Let’s get you in to see it. Saturday at 11 or 2?”"],["**Active under contract / contingent**","An offer was accepted, but conditions are still open. Some sellers accept backup offers.","“It has an accepted offer with conditions. Your agent can check if backup offers are possible and show you similar homes.”"],["**Pending**","Under contract, conditions mostly cleared, waiting to close.","“That one is pending, so it will likely close. Let’s find you similar homes.”"],["**Sold / closed**","The sale is complete.","Use it to learn what they like: “What did you love about that one?”"],["**Withdrawn / canceled**","Taken off the market before it sold.","Note it for the agent."],["**Expired**","The listing agreement ended without a sale.","A possible seller lead, only where your state and client allow calling."]]},{"t":"p","text":"Status names vary a little from one MLS to another, so learn the exact names your client’s MLS uses. **Reading a listing.** Agents use short forms. “4 bd / 2.5 ba, 2,150 sqft, 0.2 ac, built 1998, HOA $85/mo, DOM 12” means: four bedrooms, two full bathrooms and one half bathroom (a toilet and sink but no shower), 2,150 square feet of living space, a lot of one fifth of an acre, built in 1998, a monthly homeowners association fee of 85 dollars, and 12 days on the market. Learn these short forms; you will write them in every note."},{"t":"p","text":"**Where leads come from.** Your conversation changes with the source, so always check the lead source field in Follow Up Boss before you dial."},{"t":"table","head":["Lead source","Typical mindset","Opening angle"],"rows":[["Portal inquiry","Saw one specific home, often early in the journey","Ask about that home first, then what else they want"],["Paid ad (social or search)","Clicked an offer like a home list or a value estimate; may not remember","Remind them gently what they requested"],["Team website (IDX)","Registered to save searches; browsing","Ask what areas and price they are watching"],["Open house sign-in","Met the agent or saw the home in person","Mention the home and the date they visited"],["Sign call","Drove by a listing; local and curious","Ask what they liked about the home and the area they live in now"],["Past client or sphere","Knows and likes the agent","Warm, personal, ask how they are and what is changing"],["Referral","Recommended by someone they trust","Mention who referred them; treat as hot"]]},{"t":"callout","title":"Why this matters","text":"A lead who saw a specific home wants to hear about that home in the first 10 seconds. Opening with “Are you thinking of buying?” when they asked about 123 Oak Street sounds like you did not read their inquiry. Check the source and the property, then open with it."},{"t":"p","text":"**Common mistake:** confusing the MLS with the portals. The MLS is the agents’ database; portals are public websites that show listings. Another mistake is telling a lead the price or condition of a home from memory. Read the listing, relay only what is published, and send every question about value, negotiation or condition to the agent."}]},{"id":"F4-5","title":"Glossary part 1: people, properties and listings","minutes":6,"body":[{"t":"p","text":"These terms are the vocabulary of your daily work. Read each one aloud. If a word is new, write your own example sentence. In the practice assignment you will use 20 of these terms in notes and a recorded explanation. Pronunciation tips are in brackets where English speakers often hear mistakes."},{"t":"glossary","terms":[{"term":"Lead","def":"A person who showed interest (a form, a call, a sign-in) and may buy or sell. Your client’s leads are your work list."},{"term":"Prospect","def":"Another word for a lead, often one you are actively working."},{"term":"Sphere (sphere of influence)","def":"People the agent already knows: friends, family, past clients. Often the warmest leads."},{"term":"Past client","def":"Someone who already bought or sold with the agent."},{"term":"CRM","def":"Customer relationship management software. Most of our clients use Follow Up Boss, where every lead, note and task lives."},{"term":"ISA","def":"Inside sales agent: calls and texts leads, qualifies them and books appointments for agents."},{"term":"Listing","def":"A home for sale under a listing agreement, or the online page that describes it. [LIS-ting]"},{"term":"Listing agent / seller’s agent","def":"The agent who represents the seller."},{"term":"Buyer’s agent","def":"The agent who represents the buyer."},{"term":"Single-family home (SFH)","def":"A detached house for one household on its own lot."},{"term":"Condo (condominium)","def":"A unit the buyer owns inside a building or complex; shared areas are owned together and managed by an HOA."},{"term":"Townhouse / townhome","def":"A home that shares one or two walls with neighbors, usually on several floors, often with an HOA."},{"term":"Multi-family","def":"A building with 2 to 4 units (duplex, triplex, fourplex), often bought by investors."},{"term":"New construction","def":"A home just built or still being built, often sold by a builder with its own sales agent."},{"term":"bd / ba","def":"Bedrooms / bathrooms. “2.5 ba” means two full baths and one half bath (toilet and sink, no shower)."},{"term":"Square footage (sqft)","def":"The living area of the home in square feet. 1,000 sqft is about 93 square meters."},{"term":"Lot","def":"The land the home sits on. Often in acres: 1 acre is about 4,047 square meters."},{"term":"HOA","def":"Homeowners association: manages shared areas and rules and charges a monthly or yearly fee. Say each letter: H-O-A."},{"term":"MLS","def":"Multiple Listing Service: the private listing database agents share in an area."},{"term":"IDX","def":"The data feed that lets agents show MLS listings on their own websites."},{"term":"Portal","def":"A large public home-search website that shows listings and sends inquiries to agents."},{"term":"DOM (days on market)","def":"How long a home has been listed. A high number can mean the price is too high."},{"term":"List price","def":"The price the seller asks for. Not the same as the sale price."},{"term":"Price reduction","def":"The seller lowered the list price. A good reason to call back interested buyers."},{"term":"Showing","def":"A visit to a home for sale with an agent. Also called a tour."},{"term":"Open house","def":"A set time when the home is open for anyone to visit, hosted by an agent."},{"term":"Coming soon / Active / Pending / Sold","def":"Listing statuses: not yet showing / on the market / under contract / closed."},{"term":"FSBO","def":"For Sale By Owner: a seller with no agent. Say “FIZZ-bo”."},{"term":"Expired","def":"A listing whose agreement ended without a sale."},{"term":"CMA","def":"Comparative market analysis: the agent’s report comparing recent nearby sales to estimate a price. Only the agent prepares and presents it."},{"term":"Comps (comparables)","def":"Recently sold homes similar to the one being priced."},{"term":"Staging","def":"Furnishing and decorating a home to look its best for photos and showings."}]},{"t":"callout","title":"Words that sound alike","text":"**Realtor** is said “REEL-ter” (two syllables), not “real-a-tor”. **Escrow** is “ES-kro”. **Lien** sounds like “lean”. **Suite** (a unit) sounds like “sweet”. If you say a word wrong, a lead may not trust you; practice them aloud."},{"t":"p","text":"**How to study.** Make flashcards with the term on one side and your own example on the other. Ten minutes a day for a week is better than one long session. Use the words in your Follow Up Boss notes: “Wants SFH, 3 bd / 2 ba, no HOA, under $400K.” Correct, short notes are a sign of a professional."}]},{"id":"F4-6","title":"Glossary part 2: money, contracts and closing","minutes":6,"body":[{"t":"p","text":"These terms describe the money and the contract side of a sale. You will hear them from agents and leads all the time. Understanding them is required. Explaining or advising on them is not your job: when a lead asks what one of these means for them, you send the question to the agent or, if the agent says so, to the lender."},{"t":"glossary","terms":[{"term":"Pre-qualification","def":"A quick, informal estimate from a lender of what a buyer might borrow, based on what the buyer says. Weak."},{"term":"Pre-approval","def":"A lender reviewed documents and credit and states how much the buyer can borrow. Sellers expect a pre-approval letter with an offer."},{"term":"Mortgage","def":"The loan used to buy a home, with the home as security."},{"term":"Down payment","def":"The part of the price the buyer pays in cash; the loan covers the rest."},{"term":"Cash buyer","def":"A buyer who pays without a loan. Usually closes faster."},{"term":"FHA / VA / conventional loan","def":"Common loan types. FHA is government-insured with a lower down payment; VA is for eligible veterans and service members; conventional is a regular bank loan. Never advise on which is better."},{"term":"Interest rate","def":"The cost of borrowing, as a yearly percentage. You never quote or predict rates."},{"term":"Closing costs","def":"Fees paid at closing for the loan, title, escrow, taxes and more, beyond the price."},{"term":"Buyer agreement (buyer representation agreement)","def":"A written contract between a buyer and their agent. Required before any tour since August 2024. It states what the agent will be paid. Only the agent sends and explains it."},{"term":"Listing agreement","def":"A contract that gives the listing agent the right to market and sell a home for a set period."},{"term":"Commission","def":"What an agent is paid, usually at closing. You never discuss it with leads."},{"term":"Offer","def":"A written proposal to buy: price, deposit, closing date and conditions."},{"term":"Counteroffer","def":"The other side’s answer with different terms."},{"term":"Multiple offers","def":"More than one buyer made an offer on the same home."},{"term":"Earnest money (EMD)","def":"A deposit the buyer pays when the offer is accepted, held by escrow, to show they are serious."},{"term":"Contingency","def":"A condition that lets the buyer cancel without losing the deposit, such as inspection, appraisal or financing."},{"term":"Under contract","def":"An offer was accepted and signed; the sale is in progress."},{"term":"Escrow","def":"The period from accepted offer to closing, and the neutral company that holds money and documents. “In escrow” means under contract."},{"term":"Title / title insurance","def":"Title is legal ownership. The title company checks it and insures it against problems such as an old lien."},{"term":"Lien","def":"A legal claim on a property for an unpaid debt."},{"term":"Home inspection","def":"A professional review of the home’s condition for the buyer. You never interpret the report."},{"term":"Appraisal","def":"An independent value estimate ordered by the lender."},{"term":"Clear to close (CTC)","def":"The lender has approved everything; closing can happen."},{"term":"Final walk-through","def":"The buyer’s last visit to check the home before closing."},{"term":"Closing / settlement","def":"The day documents are signed, money moves and ownership transfers."},{"term":"Closing date (COE)","def":"The planned date of closing (close of escrow)."},{"term":"Deed","def":"The document that transfers ownership to the buyer."},{"term":"Investor","def":"A buyer who purchases to rent out or resell, not to live in."},{"term":"Relocation","def":"Moving to a new city, often for a job. A strong motivation with a clear deadline."},{"term":"Contingent on sale","def":"A buyer who must sell their current home before they can buy."}]},{"t":"script","title":"Using the vocabulary without giving advice","lines":[{"who":"Lead","text":"Should I do FHA or conventional? And what’s the rate right now?"},{"who":"You","text":"Those are really important questions, and I want you to get the right answer for your situation, not a guess from me. That’s the lender’s specialty. Would you like your agent to connect you with someone, or are you already working with a lender?"},{"who":"Lead","text":"I haven’t talked to anyone yet."},{"who":"You","text":"Got it, I’ll note that for your agent. While I have you, which areas are you looking at?"}]},{"t":"callout","title":"Know the word, send the question","text":"Knowing the vocabulary makes you sound professional and helps you write clear notes. It does not make you an advisor. Every question about loans, rates, credit, contract terms, inspections, value or commission goes to the right professional."},{"t":"p","text":"**Writing notes with these terms.** Compare two notes. Weak: “Lead has money, wants to buy soon.” Strong: “Pre-approved (says $425K, lender not yet named), 10% down, wants to close before lease ends Mar 31, no home to sell first.” The strong note tells the agent exactly where the lead stands and uses the right words."}]}],"practice":{"title":"Read the market like a pro: listings, journeys and vocabulary","minutes":75,"intro":"In this assignment you use the vocabulary and the journeys on real public listings. You will not contact anyone. Use a public portal in a US city your coordinator gives you (or any large US city if you do not have a client yet).","steps":["Find five public listings in the city: one single-family home, one condo, one townhouse, one pending listing and one with a recent price reduction. For each, write a one-line summary in note short forms (for example: “SFH, 4 bd / 2.5 ba, 2,150 sqft, HOA $85/mo, DOM 12, list $489K, Active”).","For each listing, write which question you would ask first if a lead had inquired about it, and why, based on its status.","Write the buyer journey from memory in 11 steps, then check it against lesson F4-2 and correct it. Do the same for the 8-step seller journey.","For each of these four leads, write which journey step they are in and your next step: (a) “Just looking, maybe next year.” (b) “Pre-approved, lease ends in 60 days.” (c) “What is my house worth?” (d) “We’re under contract, but the inspection was bad.”","Make flashcards for all glossary terms. Study them for 10 minutes, then cover the definitions and write 20 terms in your own words without looking.","Record a 3-minute audio or video of yourself explaining, in English, the buyer journey to a new teammate, using at least 10 glossary terms. Listen to it once and re-record if you mispronounce Realtor, escrow, lien, FSBO or HOA.","Write three Follow Up Boss-style notes for imaginary leads that each use at least four glossary terms correctly."],"deliverable":"Send your coach one document with the five listing summaries, the four journey answers, your 20 written definitions and the three notes, plus your 3-minute recording. Name the files Lastname_F4_practice_date."},"quiz":[{"q":"A lead asks you, “Who actually holds my deposit after the seller accepts my offer?” Which role are they asking about?","options":["The appraiser","The home inspector","The escrow officer or title company","The listing agent"],"answer":2,"explain":"Escrow and title are the neutral party that holds the earnest money and documents until closing. You can name the role, but the agent explains the details."},{"q":"Since August 2024, what must be in place before an agent tours a home with a buyer?","options":["A written buyer agreement, sent and explained by the agent","A pre-approval letter from the client’s preferred lender","A signed offer on at least one home","A verbal promise from the buyer to work only with that agent"],"answer":0,"explain":"A written buyer agreement is required before any tour. The agent sends and explains it; the ISA only flags it in the calendar invite."},{"q":"What is the difference between the MLS and a portal?","options":["They are two names for the same database","The MLS is the private database agents share; a portal is a public website that displays listings and sends inquiries","A portal is only for sellers; the MLS is only for buyers","The MLS is run by lenders; portals are run by brokers"],"answer":1,"explain":"The MLS is the agents’ private database with details the public cannot see. Portals show listings to the public and can be out of date, so you never promise availability."},{"q":"A lead says they love a home that shows as “Pending.” What is the best response?","options":["Tell them to make a higher offer right away","Promise that the deal will probably fall apart","Say it is under contract and will likely close, then learn what they liked so the agent can find similar homes","Tell them the portal is wrong and book a showing"],"answer":2,"explain":"Pending means under contract with most conditions cleared. Use their interest to learn what they want, and never discuss offers or negotiation yourself."},{"q":"“3 bd / 2.5 ba” in a listing means:","options":["Three bedrooms, two full bathrooms and one half bathroom","Three bedrooms and two and a half floors","Three buildings and 2.5 acres","Three bedrooms, 2.5 miles from downtown"],"answer":0,"explain":"A half bath has a toilet and sink but no shower or tub. Use these short forms in your notes."},{"q":"A seller lead asks, “Just give me a rough number. What’s my house worth, $400,000?” What do you do?","options":["Give a range so they stay interested","Look up a portal estimate and read it to them","Agree that $400,000 sounds right","Explain that the agent prepares a full analysis of recent nearby sales, and offer two times for the consultation"],"answer":3,"explain":"Any number, range or agreement is an opinion of value, which only a licensed agent gives. Your job is to book the listing consultation where the agent presents the CMA."},{"q":"During discovery a buyer says, “We own our place and would need to sell it first.” What should your note show?","options":["Nothing extra; they are a buyer lead","That this is both a buyer lead and a possible seller lead, with the property details and timing","That they are not qualified because they must sell first","That they should get a bridge loan"],"answer":1,"explain":"A buyer who must sell is two opportunities. Record both, and never give financing suggestions like bridge loans."},{"q":"Which of these is a contingency?","options":["The earnest money deposit","The listing agreement","The right to cancel without losing the deposit if the home appraises below the price","The commission paid at closing"],"answer":2,"explain":"An appraisal contingency protects the buyer if the appraisal comes in low. Inspection and financing are the other common contingencies."},{"q":"Why do real estate teams hire ISAs?","options":["To negotiate offers so agents can rest","To protect the agents’ time by finding serious leads, qualifying them and booking them on the agents’ calendars","To replace the lender’s pre-approval","To write CMAs for sellers"],"answer":1,"explain":"Agents earn by spending time with serious clients. ISAs do discovery, A.L.M. qualification and scheduling, never licensed work."}]},{"id":"F5","part":"foundations","title":"Professionalism, confidentiality and your scope","summary":"What an unlicensed assistant may and may not do or say, how to protect your client’s data, and how to stay reliable and professional when real life happens.","objectives":["List what ISAs handle and what they never do, and explain why your scope depends on your client’s state","Redirect any out-of-scope question (price, value, contract, loan, inspection, commission) with a professional phrase that keeps the conversation moving toward an appointment","Protect client data: work only inside client systems, use the password vault, never download or export, and report a possible leak within the hour","Communicate absences, outages and problems early and through the right channel","Handle hard situations (a client asking for out-of-scope work, a private DM, an unscrubbed list) by pausing and escalating to your manager"],"lessons":[{"id":"F5-1","title":"Your scope as an unlicensed assistant","minutes":6,"body":[{"t":"p","text":"In the United States, helping someone buy, sell or lease a home for pay is licensed work. Each state decides what needs a license, and each state licenses its own agents and brokers. You do not hold a US real estate license. That is completely normal for an ISA or VA, and it does not make you less valuable. It does mean there is a clear line around what you may do. Crossing that line can cost your client their license, expose them to fines and lawsuits, and end your contract. Staying inside it, every time, is what makes you trustworthy."},{"t":"p","text":"Here is the boundary in one picture. In the box below, “Do” lists what ISAs handle and “Don’t” lists what ISAs never do. Memorize it."},{"t":"dodont","do":["Discovery","A.L.M. Qualification","Appointment Scheduling"],"dont":["Give pricing promises","Quote legal clauses","Interpret inspection reports","Sign agency agreements","Negotiate, or discuss price, terms or commission","Act as an agent or give an opinion of value"]},{"t":"callout","title":"Your scope depends on your client’s state","text":"In some states (Texas, for example) an unlicensed assistant may not call to find out whether someone wants to buy, sell or lease. Your manager sets your scope before you dial."},{"t":"p","text":"**What each “never” means in practice:**"},{"t":"list","items":["**Pricing promises:** “You’ll definitely get it under asking” or “This will sell in a week” are promises only an agent may make, and even agents avoid them.","**Legal clauses:** you do not read, quote or explain a contract, an addendum or a disclosure, even if the lead sends it to you.","**Inspection reports:** you can schedule an inspection. You never say whether a crack, a leak or a roof is serious.","**Agency agreements:** buyer agreements and listing agreements are sent, explained and signed by the agent only.","**Negotiation, price, terms, commission:** you never suggest an offer amount, a counteroffer, a closing date, a repair credit or what the agent charges.","**Acting as an agent or giving an opinion of value:** you never call yourself an agent, show a home, host an open house, or say what a home is worth, not even a range."]},{"t":"p","text":"**What you can usually do (your manager confirms it for your state):** answer calls, confirm and arrange appointments, relay facts your client has already advertised (the list price shown on the listing, the number of bedrooms, the open house time), call your client’s past clients, and do admin work. A few states allow more under the broker’s supervision. Some allow less. That is why the manager gives you a **scope card** for your client’s state before you start. Read it on your first days until you know it by heart, and ask when anything is unclear."},{"t":"table","head":["Example state rule (your manager confirms the current rules)","What it means for you"],"rows":[["Texas: an unlicensed assistant may not call anyone to find out whether they want to buy, sell or lease.","Your work for a Texas client may be limited to inbound calls, confirming appointments, past clients and admin. Your manager tells you exactly."],["Every state: outbound prospecting is set up only where license law permits it and after the client signs the BeyondVAs telemarketing compliance addendum. Florida, for example, has its own strict telemarketing law on top of federal rules.","No outbound dialing until the manager confirms the addendum is signed and tells you the state’s rules."],["California (example): outbound calling is possible when every compliance step is in place: scrubbed lists, the house calling window, identifying yourself and honoring opt-outs.","Outbound is possible with all compliance steps. You still never negotiate or give value."]]},{"t":"p","text":"**Why the line protects you too.** If a lead later says “The assistant told me the house was worth $500,000” or “The assistant said the inspection was fine”, your client is responsible for what you said. A clear, consistent habit of sending these questions to the agent means nobody can ever say you gave advice. It also makes the agent look good: the lead hears that there is a professional who will answer properly."},{"t":"callout","title":"When in doubt","text":"If you are not sure whether something is in scope, it is not, until your manager says it is. Pause, tell the lead the agent will follow up, and ask your manager in the client channel or your BeyondVAs channel."}]},{"id":"F5-2","title":"What you can and cannot say: redirect without losing the lead","minutes":6,"body":[{"t":"p","text":"Leads will ask you out-of-scope questions every single day. They are not trying to trick you. They want answers, and you are the person on the phone. If you say “I can’t answer that” and stop, the lead feels blocked and may hang up. If you answer, you break the rules. The skill is the third option: **acknowledge, redirect to the agent, and move toward the appointment.**"},{"t":"steps","items":["**Acknowledge:** show the question is important. “Great question.” “That’s smart to ask.”","**Redirect:** name the right professional and why. “That’s exactly what your agent will go over with you, with real numbers for your situation.”","**Move forward:** ask a discovery question or offer two appointment times. “Could you meet Thursday at 5 or Saturday at 10?”"]},{"t":"table","head":["The lead asks","Do not say","Say instead"],"rows":[["“What’s my house worth?”","“Probably around $380K.”","“Your agent will prepare a full analysis of recent sales near you. Would Tuesday at 4 or Wednesday at 10 work for a quick visit?”"],["“Will they take $20K less?”","“Sellers usually accept 5% less.”","“That’s a negotiation question, and your agent is the expert there. Let’s get you in front of them. Are you free this weekend?”"],["“What does this clause in the contract mean?”","“It means you can cancel within 10 days.”","“I want you to get an exact answer on that, so I’ll have your agent call you today. What time works?”"],["“The inspector found a crack in the foundation. Is that bad?”","“That’s usually nothing.”","“Your agent will review the report with you and can help you decide next steps. I’ll ask them to call you this afternoon.”"],["“What rate can I get? Is my credit good enough?”","“Rates are about 6% and you should be fine.”","“The lender is the right person for that. Would you like your agent to connect you with one?” (only if the agent has said to)"],["“How much does your agent charge?”","“Usually around 2.5%.”","“Your agent explains that clearly in the agreement before any tour. Want me to set up that conversation?”"],["“Is this a good neighborhood? Is it safe?”","“Yes, very safe, great schools.”","“Great question for your agent. I can also send you public sources.”"],["“Can you show me the house tomorrow?”","“Sure, I’ll meet you there.”","“I’ll book you with your agent. Would 11 or 2 tomorrow work?”"]]},{"t":"script","title":"A price question handled well","lines":[{"who":"Lead","text":"The house on Maple is listed at $415,000. Do you think they’d take $390,000?"},{"who":"You","text":"I love that you’re already thinking about an offer. Pricing strategy is exactly what your agent does best, and they’ll look at recent sales to guide you. What do you like most about the Maple house?"},{"who":"Lead","text":"The backyard, and it’s close to my work."},{"who":"You","text":"That’s a great combination. Let’s get you inside it. Your agent has Saturday at 11 or 2. Which is better?"},{"who":"Lead","text":"Eleven."},{"who":"You","text":"Perfect, Saturday at 11. Your agent will send you a short agreement to sign before the tour, which is standard now. I’ll note that you’re interested in an offer strategy."}]},{"t":"p","text":"**Relaying advertised facts is fine.** If the listing says 3 bedrooms, a list price of $415,000 and an open house Sunday 1 to 4 PM, you can repeat those facts. Add “as listed” when the information could change: “It’s listed at $415,000.” Never add your opinion: not “That’s a great price” and not “That’s overpriced”."},{"t":"dodont","do":["Use the same redirect phrases every time so they become automatic","Write the out-of-scope question in your note so the agent can answer it","Tell the lead when the agent will follow up, and make sure it happens"],"dont":["Say “I’m not allowed to say” and stop talking","Guess, even with “I think” or “maybe” in front","Share private MLS remarks, lockbox or showing instructions, the seller’s reasons or the seller’s lowest price"]},{"t":"callout","title":"Never say you are the agent","text":"Introduce yourself with your name and your client’s team: “Hi, this is Ana with the Riverside Homes Team.” If a lead asks “Are you the agent?”, say: “I’m Ana, I work with the team and help schedule time with your agent.” Never pretend, and never let a lead believe it."}]},{"id":"F5-3","title":"Confidentiality and client data","minutes":6,"body":[{"t":"p","text":"Every day you will see names, phone numbers, home addresses, family situations, incomes, divorces, job losses and debts. Real estate leads tell ISAs very personal things because they trust the team. Treat every piece of that information the way you would want your own family’s information treated. This is a legal duty under the confidentiality agreement you signed with BeyondVAs, and it is the reason clients trust us with their business."},{"t":"p","text":"**Every lead belongs to your client.** The leads, the notes, the scripts, the Smart Lists and the way the team works all belong to your client. You never contact a client’s lead outside the client’s systems, never keep a copy, never take a lead or a contact list to another client or another job, and never share how the team operates. This is true while you work for the client and after you finish."},{"t":"list","items":["**Your own computer, a private network.** Work only on your own computer with an up-to-date system and a screen lock. Never use a public computer, an internet café or open public Wi-Fi for client work.","**Inside the client’s systems only.** Work in the client’s Follow Up Boss, email and calendar. Never download, export, print or copy client data out of them, not even “to work offline”. Clients contract with us on this rule.","**Passwords only through the password vault.** Never send or accept a password by text, chat, email or a screenshot. If a client sends you a password in chat, do not use it; ask them to share it through the vault, and tell your manager so the password can be changed.","**No photos of the screen.** Do not take pictures of client screens with your phone or keep lead information on your personal phone.","**No personal tools.** Do not paste lead names, phone numbers or notes into personal apps, personal AI chat tools, translation websites or personal notes. Use only the tools your client and BeyondVAs approved.","**Lock your screen** every time you step away, even for one minute. Family members should not see client screens.","**Talk to the client only in the client channel.** No private DMs, personal WhatsApp or personal email with your client or their leads."]},{"t":"callout","title":"Report a possible leak within the hour","text":"If something goes wrong (you sent a text to the wrong lead, you clicked a suspicious link, your laptop was stolen, someone saw your screen), tell your BeyondVAs manager **within one hour**. Do not hide it and do not try to fix it alone. A fast report lets us change passwords and protect the client. A hidden mistake is much worse than a reported one."},{"t":"p","text":"**Phishing.** People pretending to be your client, a portal or a bank will email or text you asking you to “confirm your login” or open an attachment. Check the sender’s address, never log in from a link in a message, and when in doubt ask in the client channel. A real client will never be upset that you checked."},{"t":"script","title":"When a client asks for something unsafe","lines":[{"who":"Client","text":"Hey, can you export all my leads to a spreadsheet and email it to me? I want to look at them tonight."},{"who":"You","text":"Happy to help you review them. For data security I don’t download or export lead data, but I can build a Smart List in Follow Up Boss so you can see all of them in one view tonight. Would that work? I’ll also mention it to my manager in case you need a different option."}]},{"t":"p","text":"**What you may share, and with whom.** Inside the client’s systems, you share lead information with the agents who need it, through notes and the client channel. You never share it with friends, family, other VAs who do not work for that client, or other clients. When you talk with your coach about a call, describe the situation without full names, numbers or addresses unless your coach is reviewing it inside the client’s system."},{"t":"p","text":"**When you finish with a client,** your access is removed the same day. You do not keep files, screenshots, notes or contacts. If you notice you still have access somewhere, tell your manager."},{"t":"dodont","do":["Use the password vault for every login","Report a mistake or a suspicious message within the hour","Keep lead information inside the client’s CRM"],"dont":["Export, download or print lead lists","Save leads in your personal phone or personal tools","Talk about a client’s leads or business with anyone outside the client’s team and BeyondVAs"]}]},{"id":"F5-4","title":"Reliability: honest time, early messages and when life happens","minutes":5,"body":[{"t":"p","text":"US clients often say the most valuable quality in an assistant is not talent but reliability: being there when you said you would, doing what you said you would, and telling them early when something changes. You can be the best caller on the team, but if you disappear without warning once, the client will remember that more than your appointments."},{"t":"p","text":"**Honest time.** You are an independent contractor paid in USD for the hours you work. Hubstaff tracks your time and takes random screenshots about every two minutes while you are clocked in. Your client can see your hours and screenshots. Unexplained idle time is flagged and reviewed by your manager before the client is billed. This protects you and the client: hours worked are proven, and hours not worked are never billed."},{"t":"list","items":["Clock in when you start working and clock out for every break, lunch and personal task.","If you are working away from the computer (for example, a call on the headset while you look at a printed script), add a short note so the idle time is explained.","Never use tools or tricks to look active. It is dishonest, it is easy to detect, and it ends contracts."]},{"t":"p","text":"**When life happens.** Everyone gets sick, loses power or has a family emergency. What matters is how you communicate."},{"t":"table","head":["Situation","What you do"],"rows":[["You are sick or cannot start on time","Message **before** your start time, not after, in the client channel and to your coordinator. Say when you expect to be back."],["Internet or power goes out","Tell your manager at once and switch to your backup internet or power. If you cannot switch within a few minutes, tell the client channel too."],["You need a day off","Declare days off at least **10 business days** ahead, so the client and your coordinator can plan coverage. You work your client’s holiday calendar, not your country’s, unless you declare the day off."],["A client crosses a line (shouts, insults, asks for something illegal or out of scope, sends personal messages)","Stay polite, do not argue, and tell your BeyondVAs manager **the same day**. Your manager handles it with the client."],["You made a mistake","Tell your client and coordinator the same day, with what happened and what you did to fix it."]]},{"t":"script","title":"An early, professional message","lines":[{"who":"You","text":"Good morning, team. My power went out in my area at 7:40 AM. I switched to my backup and I’m online now, but the connection may be slower. If it drops, I’ll update you here within 10 minutes. Today’s new leads are being called first."}]},{"t":"p","text":"**Your daily report.** At the end of every shift, post a short report in the client channel: dials, live conversations, appointments set, important notes, and anything the agent must do tomorrow. A clear daily report shows your value without the client having to ask. You will learn the exact format in the ISA track."},{"t":"p","text":"**Tone.** Be warm, short and positive in writing. Answer client messages within 15 minutes during your shift. When you do not know, say “Let me check and get back to you by 2 PM” and do it. Use complete sentences, check spelling, and never send a message when you are angry: wait ten minutes, then write."},{"t":"callout","title":"Your commitment","text":"I will protect client information, communicate early and clearly, track my time honestly, and ask for help when I need it."}]},{"id":"F5-5","title":"Hard situations: pause, protect, escalate","minutes":5,"body":[{"t":"p","text":"Most days are routine. But sooner or later someone will ask you to do something that feels wrong. It might be a lead, an agent on the team or even your client. In those moments you do not need to win an argument or decide alone. You need a simple habit: **pause, protect, escalate.**"},{"t":"steps","items":["**Pause:** do not act yet. Say something polite and neutral, like “Let me confirm that and get right back to you.”","**Protect:** do nothing that breaks a rule while you wait: no out-of-scope answers, no unscrubbed calls, no data leaving the system.","**Escalate:** tell your BeyondVAs manager the same day, with the facts. Your manager talks with the client. That is their job, and they have your back."]},{"t":"p","text":"Work through these real situations. Read each one and decide what you would do before you read the answer."},{"t":"table","head":["Situation","What a professional does"],"rows":[["An agent asks you to “just tell the seller lead their house is worth about $450K so they’ll take the appointment.”","Pause. You never give an opinion of value, even when an agent asks. Offer the appointment instead and tell your manager."],["Your client uploads an old list of 2,000 phone numbers and asks you to start calling today.","Pause. Old databases and purchased lists can be called only after a Do Not Call scrub. Ask your manager to confirm the list was scrubbed before you dial."],["Your client asks you to use an autodialer “to go faster.”","Pause. You dial by hand. An autodialer needs written consent that your manager must confirm. Escalate."],["A lead asks for your personal WhatsApp “to make it easier.”","Keep the conversation in the client’s systems. “I’ll text you from our team line, that way your agent sees everything too.”"],["An agent sends you a private DM asking you to work extra hours off the clock.","Answer in the client channel politely and tell your manager. Every hour you work is tracked and billed through BeyondVAs."],["A lead says, “Don’t ever call me again.”","Stop. Tag DNC, set the stage Archived / DNC, pause automations, log it. You learn this fully in F6."],["A lead is angry and insulting.","Stay calm, do not argue, end the call politely, note it and tell your manager if it is serious."],["You realize you told a lead a wrong open-house time.","Fix it right away with a correction text (if they agreed to texts) or call, note it, and tell your client the same day."]]},{"t":"script","title":"Saying no to your client, politely","lines":[{"who":"Agent","text":"Just tell her the inspection stuff is minor. She’s nervous and I don’t want to lose the deal."},{"who":"You","text":"I understand, and I want to help keep her calm. I can’t comment on the inspection myself, but I can tell her you’ll call her today to walk through it. Would 4 PM work for you to call her? I’ll book it now."}]},{"t":"p","text":"Notice the pattern: you never say “That’s illegal” or lecture the client. You offer what you **can** do, right away. Most of the time the client is busy, not dishonest, and they will appreciate that you protected them."},{"t":"callout","title":"You are never alone","text":"Your BeyondVAs coordinator and manager are your backup. You are never expected to break a rule to keep a client happy. If a client keeps pushing you outside your scope, your manager handles that conversation, not you."},{"t":"dodont","do":["Offer the in-scope alternative immediately","Write down the facts (who, what, when) before you escalate","Escalate the same day"],"dont":["Do the out-of-scope task “just this once”","Argue with or lecture a client or lead","Wait days to tell your manager, hoping it goes away"]}]}],"practice":{"title":"Scope card, redirect drill and workstation security check","minutes":60,"intro":"This practice makes your scope automatic. You will build your own scope card, drill the redirects out loud until they feel natural, check that your workstation is secure, and sign your commitment.","steps":["Write your scope card on one page: the three things ISAs handle, the six things ISAs never do, five things you may usually do, and the state example your coordinator gives you (or Texas, if you have no client yet).","Write a redirect for each of these 10 questions using acknowledge, redirect, move forward: home value, offer amount, contract clause, inspection crack, interest rate, credit score, commission, “Is it a safe area?”, “Can you show me the house?”, “Are you the agent?”.","Record yourself reading all 10 redirects as if on a live call (audio is fine). Listen back. Re-record any that sound robotic, unsure, or that slip into an opinion.","Role-play 5 of them with a teammate or your coach: they ask, you answer without reading. Each must end with a discovery question or two appointment times.","Complete a workstation self-check and write yes or no for each: own computer with screen lock; system updates on; private home network with a password; backup internet tested this week; backup power tested; password vault installed; no client data in personal phone, personal apps or downloads folder.","Write the message you would send if your internet went out 20 minutes before your shift, and the message you would send if you texted a lead’s information to the wrong person.","Copy and sign the commitment: “I will protect client information, communicate early and clearly, track my time honestly, and ask for help when I need it.”"],"deliverable":"Send your coach your scope card, the 10 written redirects with the audio recording, the workstation self-check (with a plan for any “no”), your two messages and your signed commitment. Name the files Lastname_F5_practice_date."},"quiz":[{"q":"Which three things do ISAs handle?","options":["Discovery, A.L.M. qualification and appointment scheduling","Pricing, negotiation and contracts","Showings, open houses and inspections","Loan advice, appraisals and closings"],"answer":0,"explain":"ISAs do discovery, A.L.M. qualification and appointment scheduling. Everything licensed stays with the agent."},{"q":"A lead asks, “What does the financing contingency in my contract mean?” What is the best reply?","options":["Explain that it lets them cancel if the loan is not approved","Say “I can’t talk about that” and end the call","Acknowledge the question, say the agent will give an exact answer, and offer a time for the agent to call today","Tell them to search online for the meaning"],"answer":2,"explain":"Quoting or explaining contract terms is out of scope. Acknowledge, redirect to the agent and move forward with a time."},{"q":"Why does your scope depend on your client’s state?","options":["Because time zones change what you may say","Because each state’s license law decides what an unlicensed assistant may do; some, like Texas, do not allow an unlicensed assistant to call to find out whether someone wants to buy, sell or lease","Because only California allows ISAs","It does not; scope is the same in every state"],"answer":1,"explain":"License law is state law. Your manager sets your scope for your client’s state before you dial."},{"q":"Your client sends you their Follow Up Boss password in the team chat. What do you do?","options":["Use it and delete the message","Save it in your phone notes so you don’t forget","Forward it to your coordinator by email","Don’t use it; ask the client to share it through the password vault and tell your manager so it can be changed"],"answer":3,"explain":"Passwords travel only through the password vault. A password shown in chat should be changed."},{"q":"You accidentally texted one lead’s address to a different lead. When should you tell your manager?","options":["Within one hour","At the end of the week in your report","Only if the lead complains","Never, if you deleted the message"],"answer":0,"explain":"A possible leak is reported within the hour so the team can respond. Hidden mistakes cause much bigger problems."},{"q":"A lead asks if you are the agent. What is the right answer?","options":["“Yes, I handle everything for you.”","“I’m with the team and I help schedule time with your agent.”","Avoid the question and change the subject","“Kind of, I’m almost an agent.”"],"answer":1,"explain":"Never pretend or let a lead believe you are the agent. Give your name and your role clearly."},{"q":"You wake up with a fever and cannot work today. What do you do?","options":["Log in late when you feel better","Message the client channel and your coordinator after your shift should have started","Message the client channel and your coordinator before your start time and say when you expect to be back","Ask a friend to log in for you"],"answer":2,"explain":"Message before your start time, not after, through the right channels. Never let anyone else use your access."},{"q":"A listing shows a price of $415,000. A lead asks, “Is that a good price?” What may you say?","options":["“Yes, it’s below market.”","“It’s overpriced; offer less.”","“Homes there usually sell for more.”","“It’s listed at $415,000. Your agent can show you recent sales so you can judge the price. Could you meet Saturday?”"],"answer":3,"explain":"You may relay advertised facts, never an opinion about price or value."},{"q":"Your client asks you to start calling an old list of 2,000 numbers today. What is the professional first step?","options":["Pause and ask your manager to confirm the list was scrubbed against the National Do Not Call Registry and the client’s DNC list","Start dialing right away to impress the client","Refuse and stop working for the client","Load the list into an autodialer"],"answer":0,"explain":"Pause, protect, escalate. Lists other than inbound leads who asked to be contacted are called only after a DNC scrub."},{"q":"An agent asks you to tell a nervous buyer that the inspection problems are “minor.” What do you do?","options":["Do it, because the agent is licensed","Tell the buyer the problems are serious","Offer to book a call where the agent explains the report, and do not comment on the inspection yourself","Ignore the request and say nothing to anyone"],"answer":2,"explain":"You never interpret inspection reports, even when asked. Offer the in-scope alternative right away and escalate if pressure continues."}]},{"id":"F6","part":"foundations","title":"Compliance essentials: calling rules and fair housing","summary":"The calling window, Do Not Call lists, consent, opt-outs, call recording and fair housing: the rules that protect every lead, your client and you on every call and text.","objectives":["Decide whether you may call or text a lead right now, using the lead’s local time, day of the week and state","Check consent and Do Not Call status before you dial, and dial by hand","Identify yourself, announce recording, and handle any opt-out the same moment it happens, with the correct Follow Up Boss steps","Answer questions about schools, safety or “what kind of people live there” without steering, using the fair housing phrase","Explain why compliance is a shared job between you, your client and your BeyondVAs manager"],"lessons":[{"id":"F6-1","title":"Why compliance matters: the rules in one page","minutes":5,"body":[{"t":"p","text":"Every call and text you make goes out in your client’s name. US law sets strict rules for calls and texts to consumers, and the penalties are large. Under the federal Telephone Consumer Protection Act (TCPA), a person can sue for **$500 for each illegal call or text**, and up to **$1,500 each** if the violation was knowing or willful. One careless afternoon of 100 wrong calls could cost a client $50,000 or more. Real estate teams have been sued for exactly this."},{"t":"p","text":"You do not need to be a lawyer. You need to follow a short set of house rules that are stricter than the law in most places, so that if you follow them, you are safe almost everywhere. Here is the map of the rules that apply to your work."},{"t":"table","head":["Rule","What it covers","Your habit"],"rows":[["**TCPA** (federal law)","Calling hours, consent for autodialed and prerecorded calls and texts, Do Not Call, opt-outs","Call and text only in the window, dial by hand, honor every opt-out"],["**Telemarketing Sales Rule** and the **National Do Not Call Registry**","Consumers can register their number to stop sales calls. Callers must check lists against the registry at least every 31 days and identify themselves.","Call non-inbound lists only after a fresh scrub; say your name and your client’s team in the first 30 seconds"],["**State telemarketing laws** (“mini-TCPAs”)","Stricter hours, limits on repeat calls, registration rules. Texas, Florida and others.","Your manager gives you the state rules before you dial"],["**Call recording laws**","Some states require everyone on the call to agree to recording","Always announce that the call may be recorded"],["**Fair Housing Act**","No discrimination or steering in housing","Treat every caller the same; describe homes, not people"],["**Real estate license law**","What an unlicensed assistant may do (F5)","Stay inside your scope card"]]},{"t":"callout","title":"Compliance is a shared job","text":"Your client supplies scrubbed lists and approves every script. You dial by hand, inside legal hours, and honor every opt-out. Your BeyondVAs manager confirms your client’s state rules with them before any outbound work. If any part is missing, you pause and ask. (This is general training, not legal advice; your manager confirms the rules for your client.)"},{"t":"p","text":"**The six house rules you will learn in this module:**"},{"t":"steps","items":["Call and text only from **9:00 AM to 8:00 PM** in the lead’s local time Monday to Saturday, and **noon to 8:00 PM** on Sunday.","Call inbound leads who asked to be contacted. Call any other list only after it is scrubbed against the National Do Not Call Registry and the client’s own DNC list.","Text only leads who agreed to texts.","Dial by hand. No autodialer or prerecorded message unless your manager confirms written consent.","Identify yourself, announce that the call may be recorded, and honor every opt-out immediately.","Treat every caller the same and never steer."]},{"t":"p","text":"**Why a stricter house rule is easier.** Federal law allows calls from 8 AM to 9 PM. But Texas, Florida and other states are stricter, and leads live in many states. Remembering one safe window that works almost everywhere is easier and safer than remembering 50 different rules during a busy shift. The same idea applies to call recording: instead of memorizing which states require everyone’s consent, we treat every state as if it does."},{"t":"dodont","do":["Check the lead’s state and local time before every dial","Ask your manager when a rule is unclear","Follow the house rule even when the law seems to allow more"],"dont":["Assume a rule from one client applies to another client in a different state","Make “just one more call” after the window closes","Let a client or agent talk you out of a compliance rule; escalate instead"]}]},{"id":"F6-2","title":"The calling window and time zones","minutes":6,"body":[{"t":"p","text":"The calling window is the rule you will use most often, dozens of times per shift. Learn it until it is automatic."},{"t":"callout","title":"Calling window","text":"**9:00 AM to 8:00 PM** in the prospect’s local time zone, Monday to Saturday, and **noon to 8:00 PM** on Sunday. Texts follow the same window."},{"t":"p","text":"Federal rules allow 8 AM to 9 PM. Texas allows no solicitation calls before 9 AM (noon on Sunday) and Florida none after 8 PM, so this window is our default everywhere. Some states are stricter still: Rhode Island, for example, allows unsolicited sales calls only 9 AM to 6 PM on weekdays and 10 AM to 5 PM on Saturday, never on Sundays or holidays. The lead’s state is what counts, and your manager tells you its rules before you dial."},{"t":"p","text":"**The lead’s time, not yours, and not your client’s.** You are in Honduras or elsewhere in Latin America. Your client may be in California. The lead may be in Texas. The time that matters is the lead’s local time. Honduras does not change its clocks, but most of the US moves clocks forward one hour in March and back in November (daylight saving time), so the difference between you and your leads changes twice a year. Arizona (most of it) and Hawaii do not change."},{"t":"table","head":["US time zone","Example states","Hours from Honduras (US summer, Mar-Nov)","Hours from Honduras (US winter, Nov-Mar)"],"rows":[["Eastern (ET)","New York, Florida (most), Georgia","2 hours ahead","1 hour ahead"],["Central (CT)","Texas (most), Illinois","1 hour ahead","Same time"],["Mountain (MT)","Colorado, Utah; Arizona (no daylight saving)","Same time (Arizona: 1 hour behind)","1 hour behind (Arizona: 1 hour behind)"],["Pacific (PT)","California, Washington, Nevada","1 hour behind","2 hours behind"]]},{"t":"p","text":"**Which local time?** Use the lead’s address or the state of the property they asked about, as shown in Follow Up Boss. Be careful with the area code: people keep their mobile numbers when they move, so a 305 (Miami) number might belong to someone living in California. When the address and the area code point to different time zones, call inside the window that is safe in **both** places. When you have no location at all, wait until it is inside the window everywhere in the continental US: 9:00 AM Pacific is noon Eastern, and 8:00 PM Eastern is 5:00 PM Pacific, so Monday to Saturday that means about noon to 8:00 PM Eastern."},{"t":"table","head":["Situation","May you call now?"],"rows":[["Tuesday, 8:40 AM for a lead in Dallas, Texas","No. Wait until 9:00 AM Central. Texas allows no solicitation calls before 9 AM."],["Saturday, 7:50 PM for a lead in Miami, Florida","Yes, but finish the attempt before 8:00 PM. Do not start a voicemail at 7:59."],["Sunday, 10:30 AM for a lead in Phoenix, Arizona","No. Sunday starts at noon."],["Wednesday, 8:15 PM for a lead in Los Angeles who just filled a form","No. Schedule the first call for 9:00 AM tomorrow, Pacific time."],["Saturday, 5:30 PM for a lead in Providence, Rhode Island","No. Rhode Island allows unsolicited sales calls only 10 AM to 5 PM on Saturday. Your manager confirms how this applies to your client."],["Monday, 9:05 AM for a California lead whose number has a New York area code","Yes: it is 9:05 AM where they live (12:05 PM in New York), inside the window in both places."]]},{"t":"p","text":"**After-hours leads.** Leads arrive at night and on Sunday mornings. Speed to lead never overrides the window. A lead that arrives after hours gets its first call as soon as the window opens in the lead’s time zone, and texts wait too. Set a task in Follow Up Boss for the first minute of the window so it is the first thing you do."},{"t":"p","text":"**Holidays.** Some states ban sales calls on certain holidays, and many people do not want calls on Thanksgiving, Christmas or July 4th. Your manager will tell you the client’s holiday rules. If you are unsure, do not dial non-inbound lists on a US federal holiday until you ask."},{"t":"dodont","do":["Keep a world clock with ET, CT, MT and PT visible all shift","Check the lead’s address before the area code","Move any task that would land outside the window to the next morning"],"dont":["Use your own local time to decide","Send texts outside the window because “texts are different”","Forget the daylight saving changes in March and November"]}]},{"id":"F6-3","title":"Before you dial: consent, Do Not Call and manual dialing","minutes":6,"body":[{"t":"p","text":"Before your finger touches the dial button, answer one question: **does this person expect or allow a call from us?** The answer depends on where the number came from."},{"t":"list","items":["Inbound leads who asked to be contacted can be called inside the calling window.","Any other list (an old database, a farm list, a purchased list): call only numbers your client has checked against the National Do Not Call Registry and their own DNC list.","Text only leads who agreed to texts. Portal and ad forms usually include that consent; ask your manager if you are not sure.","Dial by hand. Never use an autodialer or a prerecorded message unless your manager confirms written consent."]},{"t":"p","text":"**What is an inbound lead?** A person who contacted your client or asked to be contacted: they filled a form on a portal or the team website, called a sign, signed in at an open house and agreed to follow-up, or replied to the team. They gave permission for that conversation. That permission is not unlimited: if they say stop, it ends at once, and an inquiry from long ago may not count anymore. Your manager decides when an old lead must be treated like a cold list and scrubbed."},{"t":"p","text":"**Scrubbed lists.** The National Do Not Call Registry is the federal list of numbers whose owners do not want sales calls. Your client must check (“scrub”) any non-inbound list against it, and against the client’s own internal DNC list, before you call. A scrub stays valid for **31 days**; after that the list must be scrubbed again, because people add their numbers every day. Outbound calls are made only on a list scrubbed within the last 31 days. If you do not know when a list was scrubbed, you do not dial it. Ask."},{"t":"callout","title":"Signs a list may not be safe","text":"A spreadsheet with no source, a list called “old leads 2019”, numbers copied from a website, or a client saying “just call them all, they won’t mind”. In each case: pause and ask your manager to confirm the scrub and the consent before dialing."},{"t":"p","text":"**Texting consent.** Texts are treated like calls under the law, and marketing texts need clear consent. Most portal and website forms include a consent box for calls and texts. A lead who called the team or met an agent at an open house may not have agreed to texts. Check the consent field or tag your client uses in Follow Up Boss. No consent, no text: call instead, inside the window."},{"t":"p","text":"**Dial by hand.** An autodialer is any system that dials numbers automatically, in bulk or in sequence, and a prerecorded or artificial voice message is a recording played to the person. Both need prior express written consent, and the penalties are where most lawsuits come from. You click to call each number yourself, one at a time, and leave voicemails in your own live voice. If a tool offers “power dialing”, “ringless voicemail” or “voice drops”, do not turn it on unless your manager confirms in writing that the consent is in place."},{"t":"p","text":"**Identify yourself.** Within the first 30 seconds of every call, say your name and your client’s team. Your caller ID must show a real number that the lead can call back. If the lead asks, give the team’s phone number or address."},{"t":"script","title":"A compliant opening","lines":[{"who":"You","text":"Hi, is this Maria? This is Daniel with the Lakeside Realty Team. Just so you know, this call may be recorded for quality. You asked about the townhouse on Birch Street this morning. Do you have two minutes?"},{"who":"Lead","text":"Oh yes, I did. Sure."},{"who":"You","text":"Great. What caught your eye about that one?"}]},{"t":"steps","items":["Check the lead source: inbound, or a list?","If a list: was it scrubbed in the last 31 days? If you do not know, stop and ask.","Check the lead’s local time, day and state against the window.","Check the DNC tag and stage. Archived / DNC means never.","For a text: check text consent.","Click to dial by hand. Identify yourself and announce recording."]},{"t":"dodont","do":["Run the six-step check until it is automatic","Leave voicemails in your own voice","Write in your note where the lead came from if the source field is empty"],"dont":["Dial a list you cannot confirm was scrubbed","Text a lead who has not agreed to texts","Turn on dialer or voicemail-drop features without written confirmation from your manager"]}]},{"id":"F6-4","title":"Opt-outs and call recording","minutes":6,"body":[{"t":"p","text":"An opt-out is a person telling you they do not want more calls or texts. When it happens, you stop. Not after one more try, not after a nice explanation: you stop, and you make sure nobody on the team or any automation contacts them again."},{"t":"callout","title":"Opt-outs","text":"If a prospect says **“STOP”**, **“Remove me”**, or **“Don’t call”**, immediately tag them as DNC and move their stage to **Archived / DNC**. Pause every automation and log the opt-out in the notes. Never delete an opted-out contact: the DNC record is what stops future calls and texts."},{"t":"steps","items":["On the call: say one polite sentence and end the call. Do not argue or try to change their mind.","Add the **DNC** tag in Follow Up Boss.","Change the stage to **Archived / DNC**.","Pause every action plan, email drip and automated text for the contact.","Write a note: date, time, channel, the lead’s words and “Opt-out honored.”","Do it right away, before your next dial. The law gives companies a short deadline; our rule is immediately."]},{"t":"script","title":"Honoring an opt-out on a call","lines":[{"who":"Lead","text":"Look, I don’t want any more calls. Take me off your list."},{"who":"You","text":"Of course. I’m sorry for the interruption. I’ll remove you right now, and you won’t hear from us again. Have a good day."}]},{"t":"p","text":"**Opt-outs come in many forms.** People rarely use the exact words. Treat all of these as opt-outs: “STOP”, “Unsubscribe”, “Quit”, “End”, “Cancel”, “Leave me alone”, “Take me off your list”, “Wrong number, don’t text me”, “Stop calling me”, and the same ideas in Spanish (“no me llames”, “ya no me escriban”). The law says people may opt out in any reasonable way, so do not wait for a magic word."},{"t":"table","head":["What the lead says","Is it an opt-out?","What you do"],"rows":[["“STOP”","Yes","Full opt-out steps."],["“Please don’t call me anymore, just email.”","Yes, for calls and texts","Tag DNC for phone and text, follow your client’s rule for email, log exactly what they said, and tell your manager."],["“Not interested right now.”","Not by itself","Respect it: “No problem.” Ask if a check-in later is OK. If they say no or sound annoyed, treat it as an opt-out."],["“I bought already, thanks.”","No","Congratulate them, update the stage per your client’s rules, stop buyer follow-up."],["“Wrong number.”","Yes, for that number","Apologize, note it, and mark the number so it is never called again for that contact."],["“How did you get my number? Never call again!”","Yes","Apologize, give the team name, full opt-out steps, tell your manager."]]},{"t":"p","text":"**Why never delete?** If you delete the contact, the opt-out disappears with it. Next month the same person may come in again from an old list or an import, and someone will call them. The DNC record in Follow Up Boss is the memory that protects them and your client."},{"t":"p","text":"**Call recording.** Many teams record calls for quality and coaching. Recording laws differ by state: some require only one person on the call to agree, but others (for example California, Florida, Pennsylvania, Washington, Illinois, Maryland and Massachusetts) require **everyone** on the call to agree. Our rule: **always announce that the call may be recorded, on every call, and treat every state as requiring everyone’s consent.** Say it at the start, in the opening: “Just so you know, this call may be recorded for quality.” If your client has its own wording in the script, use the client’s wording."},{"t":"p","text":"If a lead says “I don’t want to be recorded”, respect it: tell them you understand, and if your system lets you stop the recording, stop it; if it does not, offer to have the agent call them back from an unrecorded line. Note it. In a 3-way handoff, announce the recording again so the agent and the lead both hear it."},{"t":"dodont","do":["Announce recording in your first sentences, every call","Complete all opt-out steps before your next dial","Treat Spanish opt-outs exactly like English ones"],"dont":["Argue, explain or ask “Are you sure?” after an opt-out","Delete an opted-out contact","Call back from a different number after a lead asked you to stop"]}]},{"id":"F6-5","title":"Fair housing: treat every caller the same","minutes":6,"body":[{"t":"p","text":"The federal Fair Housing Act makes it illegal to discriminate in housing because of **race, color, religion, sex, national origin, familial status (families with children under 18, pregnancy) or disability**. Many states and cities protect more groups, such as age, marital status, sexual orientation, gender identity, source of income (for example, housing vouchers) or military status. Everyone on your client’s team, including you, must follow these laws on every call, text and note."},{"t":"p","text":"Treat every caller the same. Never steer anyone toward or away from an area, and never describe a neighborhood by who lives there, its schools, crime or “safety”, religion, or how “family-friendly” it is."},{"t":"callout","title":"The phrase to remember","text":"If they ask, say: **“Great question for your agent. I can also send you public sources.”** Log what the lead wants in the home, not who they are."},{"t":"p","text":"**What is steering?** Steering means guiding people toward or away from areas or homes because of who they are, or who lives there. It is often accidental and friendly: “You’ll feel at home in that area, there are lots of Hispanic families”, or “That area isn’t great for kids.” Both comments are steering, even if you meant to help. Your job is to let the lead choose based on facts about homes, prices and locations, and to send questions about the community to public sources."},{"t":"table","head":["The lead asks","Do not say","Say instead"],"rows":[["“Is it a safe neighborhood?”","“Yes, very safe.” / “I wouldn’t go there.”","“Great question for your agent. I can also send you public sources, like the local police department’s crime map.”"],["“How are the schools?”","“The schools are excellent, perfect for families.”","“Great question for your agent. I can also send you public sources, like the school district’s website and state school reports.”"],["“What kind of people live there?”","“Mostly young professionals.” / “Lots of families like yours.”","“Great question for your agent. I can also send you public sources, like census information. What matters most to you in the home itself?”"],["“Is there a church or mosque nearby?”","“It’s a very Christian area.”","“Great question for your agent. I can also send you public sources, like a map search for places that matter to you.”"],["“Is it family-friendly?”","“Totally, lots of kids.”","“Great question for your agent. What do you need in the home: how many bedrooms, a yard?”"]]},{"t":"p","text":"**Describe homes, not people.** You may talk about the home and its features: bedrooms, yard, garage, square footage, distance to a highway or a park, the price. You may relay facts from the listing. You may not describe neighbors, the “type” of buyers who like an area, or whether a place is “good” or “bad”."},{"t":"p","text":"**Notes follow the same rule.** Write what the lead wants in the home: “3 bd, yard, near I-35, under $350K, move by June.” Never write who they are: not their race, religion, accent, nationality, age guess, disability or whether they “seem like” anything. Notes can be read in a lawsuit. Write every note as if a judge will read it."},{"t":"p","text":"**Language and accessibility.** As a bilingual ISA, you are a huge asset to Spanish-speaking leads. Offer the same service in either language, with the same questions, the same homes and the same urgency. Never give one group different information or less attention. If a caller uses a relay service for deaf or hard-of-hearing people, or needs more time or a different way to communicate because of a disability, treat it as a normal call and give them the same service. If a lead asks for an accommodation, note it and tell the agent."},{"t":"script","title":"A fair housing question handled well","lines":[{"who":"Lead","text":"We have two little kids. Which of these areas has the best schools and the fewest problems?"},{"who":"You","text":"Great question for your agent. I can also send you public sources, like the school district websites and the city crime maps, so you can compare yourselves. In the home itself, how many bedrooms do you need?"},{"who":"Lead","text":"At least three, and a fenced yard."},{"who":"You","text":"Got it, three bedrooms and a fenced yard. Your agent can show you options in every area you’re considering. Would Saturday at 10 or 1 work?"}]},{"t":"dodont","do":["Use the fair housing phrase word for word","Ask about the home: rooms, yard, price, commute, timeline","Give every lead the same service in English or Spanish"],"dont":["Describe an area by who lives there, schools, crime, safety, religion or “family-friendly”","Suggest or discourage an area for any lead","Write anything about a lead’s identity in a note"]}]}],"practice":{"title":"Compliance drills and supervised dialing","minutes":120,"intro":"Compliance must be automatic, so this practice has two parts. First you drill the decisions on paper and in a Follow Up Boss practice account. Then you make supervised calls on your coach’s line, where every call is scored on compliance before you ever call a real lead.","steps":["Calling-window drill: your coach sends you 15 situations (day, your time, lead’s city). For each, write the lead’s local time and “call now” or “call at …”. Your target is 15 out of 15.","Before-you-dial drill: for 10 sample leads (source, scrub date, consent field, tags), write whether you may call, text, both or neither, and why.","Opt-out drill in the practice account: for three sample contacts who opted out by call, by text (“STOP”) and in Spanish (“ya no me escriban”), add the DNC tag, set the stage to Archived / DNC, pause all action plans and write the opt-out note. Take one screenshot of each finished contact (practice account only).","Write your compliant opening (name, client’s team, recording announcement, reason for the call) and say it aloud ten times until you can say it naturally without reading.","Fair housing drill: record yourself answering five questions your coach sends (schools, safety, “what kind of people”, religion, family-friendly) with the fair housing phrase and a question about the home.","Supervised dialing: join your coach for live role-play calls on the coach’s line. Each call is scored on identification, script, Do Not Call and opt-out handling, recording announcement and the appointment handoff. At least one caller will opt out and one will ask a fair housing question, and one call will be set at a time outside the window, where the right answer is not to dial.","Write a short reflection: the one compliance habit you found hardest and how you will make it automatic."],"deliverable":"Send your coach your window and before-you-dial answers, the three opt-out screenshots from the practice account, your written opening, the fair housing recording and your reflection. Your coach adds the scores from the supervised calls. Name the files Lastname_F6_practice_date."},"quiz":[{"q":"A prospect texts back: “STOP CALLING ME”. What is your mandatory immediate action?","options":["Call them immediately to convince them to stay subscribed.","Change their Follow Up Boss stage to Archived / DNC, pause all automations, and log the opt-out.","Ignore the text and try calling from a different number.","Delete the entire CRM database."],"answer":1,"explain":"TCPA compliance requires immediate cessation of all calls and texts upon an opt-out request."},{"q":"It is Sunday at 10:30 AM in Phoenix, Arizona. A lead there asked for a call yesterday. What do you do?","options":["Call now, because the lead asked for it","Text now, because texts are not calls","Wait until noon Phoenix time, then call","Wait until Monday at 8 AM"],"answer":2,"explain":"On Sunday the window is noon to 8:00 PM in the lead’s local time, and texts follow the same window."},{"q":"A lead’s address is in Los Angeles, but the phone number has a Miami area code. Which time do you use?","options":["Miami time only, because of the area code","Your own local time","Your client’s office time","A time that is inside the window in both places"],"answer":3,"explain":"People keep their numbers when they move. Use the address first, and when the two disagree, call when it is safe in both time zones."},{"q":"Your client gives you a farm list with no scrub date. What may you do?","options":["Nothing on that list until your manager confirms it was scrubbed against the National Do Not Call Registry and the client’s DNC list within the last 31 days","Call it, because farm lists are always allowed","Text it instead of calling","Call only the numbers with local area codes"],"answer":0,"explain":"Non-inbound lists are called only after a scrub, and a scrub stays valid for 31 days."},{"q":"Which opening is compliant?","options":["“Hi, I’m calling about homes. Are you buying?”","“Hi Maria, this is Daniel with the Lakeside Realty Team. This call may be recorded for quality. You asked about the townhouse on Birch Street.”","“Hi, this is your agent. Do you want to see homes?”","A prerecorded message about new listings"],"answer":1,"explain":"Identify yourself and your client’s team, announce the recording, and give the reason for the call. Never claim to be the agent, and never use prerecorded messages without confirmed written consent."},{"q":"Why do we always announce that a call may be recorded?","options":["Because it makes the lead talk faster","Because only Florida requires it","Because some states require everyone on the call to agree, and we treat every state as requiring everyone’s consent","Because the recording replaces the Follow Up Boss note"],"answer":2,"explain":"Recording laws vary, and several states require all-party consent. One habit on every call keeps you safe everywhere."},{"q":"A lead asks, “Is that neighborhood safe for my kids?” What do you say?","options":["“Great question for your agent. I can also send you public sources.”","“Yes, it’s very safe and family-friendly.”","“I would avoid that area.”","“Most of the families there are like yours.”"],"answer":0,"explain":"Describing an area by safety, schools, who lives there or how family-friendly it is can be steering. Use the fair housing phrase and ask about the home."},{"q":"Which Follow Up Boss note follows fair housing rules?","options":["“Nice young Hispanic couple, expecting a baby.”","“Older man, seems to have a disability, wants a quiet area.”","“Religious family, wants to be near their church.”","“3 bd / 2 ba, fenced yard, near I-35, under $350K, move by June.”"],"answer":3,"explain":"Log what the lead wants in the home, not who they are. Notes can be read in a lawsuit."},{"q":"Your dialing software offers a “voice drop” that leaves a prerecorded voicemail. When may you use it?","options":["Any time, because it saves time","Only after 6 PM","Only if your manager confirms written consent is in place","Only for leads in your own time zone"],"answer":2,"explain":"Dial by hand and leave voicemails in your own voice. Autodialers and prerecorded messages need prior express written consent confirmed by your manager."},{"q":"Why must you never delete a contact who opted out?","options":["Because the DNC record is what stops future calls and texts if the number comes in again","Because deleting contacts slows down Follow Up Boss","Because the agent may want to call them later","Because it is fine to delete them after 30 days"],"answer":0,"explain":"Deleting the contact erases the opt-out. Keep it tagged DNC in Archived / DNC so nobody contacts them again."}]},{"id":"I1","part":"isa","title":"In the buyer's shoes and A.L.M.","summary":"Understand the person behind every inquiry and run every call on Appointment, Location and Motivation.","objectives":["Describe what a typical buyer feels when they send an inquiry, and adjust your tone to it.","Explain the five buyer truths and use them to choose what to say when a lead is nervous, quiet or doubtful.","Run A.L.M. (Appointment, Location, Motivation) on a live call, with at least two good questions for each letter.","Answer “Can we even afford it?” and “I already have an agent” without pressure and inside your unlicensed scope.","Ask about location and lifestyle in a way that respects fair housing rules."],"lessons":[{"id":"I1-1","title":"11:14 PM, a couch in your client's area","minutes":6,"body":[{"t":"p","text":"Every lead in your client's CRM started as a person on a couch, in a car or at a kitchen table, scrolling a phone. Before you learn any script, you need to see that person clearly. This lesson tells one story. Keep it in your head every time a new lead appears on your screen."},{"t":"callout","title":"The story","text":"It is 11:14 PM. The kids are asleep. Maria and Luis are on the couch, looking at homes on one phone. Their rent went up again this month. They have saved for three years, and they still do not know if it is enough. Then they see a three-bedroom house in your client's area. It is not fancy. It is just the first home that looks like theirs."},{"t":"p","text":"Maria taps **“Contact agent.”** Her heart beats fast. The house does not scare her. The numbers scare her: the down payment, the monthly payment, the words she does not understand. She is afraid someone will make her feel stupid, or push her into something she cannot afford."},{"t":"p","text":"The next morning, inside the calling window, a phone rings. It is you, calling on behalf of your client, the agent. What happens in the next two minutes decides whether Maria and Luis ever buy that home with your client, or with anyone."},{"t":"p","text":"**What Maria needs from you.** She does not need a lecture about interest rates. She does not need ten listings. And you must never give her loan, rate or price advice: that is not your job, and it needs a license you do not have. She needs three things:"},{"t":"list","items":["**To feel heard.** You ask what they are hoping for and you listen to the answer.","**To feel safe.** You are calm, kind and patient. No pressure, no judgment.","**A clear next step.** You offer a meeting with your client, the agent, who can walk them through the real process. You mean it, and you give two concrete times."]},{"t":"p","text":"Nobody has ever walked Maria and Luis through buying a home. You are often the very first person in the industry who talks to them. That is a big responsibility, and it is also why this job matters."},{"t":"script","title":"How the first call can sound","lines":[{"who":"You","text":"Hi, is this Maria? This is Ana with the Rivera Home Team. Just so you know, this call may be recorded. You asked about the three-bedroom on Oak Street last night. Do you have two minutes?"},{"who":"Lead","text":"Oh, yes. Hi. Honestly, we were just looking."},{"who":"You","text":"That's how almost everyone starts. What did you like about that house?"},{"who":"Lead","text":"The yard. And it's close to my husband's work. But we don't even know if we can afford it."},{"who":"You","text":"That's a really common worry, and it's exactly what Daniel, our agent, helps with. He can sit down with you and walk you through the whole process, no pressure. Would Thursday at 6 PM or Saturday at 10 AM be easier for you?"}]},{"t":"p","text":"Look at what the ISA did. She announced the recording. She asked an open question about what Maria liked. She did not argue with “just looking.” She did not answer the money question herself. She connected the worry to the agent and asked for an appointment with two times."},{"t":"p","text":"**A year later.** Maria and Luis have their keys. Their kids play in the yard. They send their cousin to your client. That is who is on the other end of every inquiry: a family, and every family they know."},{"t":"callout","title":"Every price point gets your best","text":"A $250,000 inquiry and a $900,000 inquiry get the same speed, the same care and the same follow-up. Never pre-judge a lead by the price of the home they clicked. Today's small deal is next year's referral. No lead left behind."},{"t":"dodont","do":["Picture the person before you dial: what are they worried about tonight?","Use their name, slow down, and let them finish their sentences.","Offer the agent's help as the answer to their worries."],"dont":["Treat the lead as a number on a list.","Judge the lead by price point, accent, name or how they write.","Answer money, rate or price questions yourself."]}]},{"id":"I1-2","title":"Five buyer truths","minutes":7,"body":[{"t":"p","text":"Thousands of buyer conversations teach the same lessons again and again. We call them the five buyer truths. Learn them well: they tell you what to say when you are not sure what to say."},{"t":"p","text":"**Truth 1: Overwhelmed, not unmotivated.** A buyer who goes quiet is usually confused, not uninterested. They saw a word they did not understand (“escrow,” “contingency,” “pre-approval”), they felt embarrassed, and they stopped answering. When a lead goes silent, do not decide they are “not serious.” Ask yourself: what question are they stuck on? Then make it easy to come back."},{"t":"script","title":"Re-opening a quiet lead (by text, to a lead who agreed to texts)","lines":[{"who":"You","text":"Hi Kevin, Ana here with the Rivera Home Team. A lot of buyers tell us the hardest part is knowing where to start. Would a 15-minute call with Daniel this week help? He can do Wednesday at 5 or Thursday at 7."}]},{"t":"p","text":"**Truth 2: Education over pressure.** Buyers do not need more listings. They need clarity: what is the next step, and what does it need? Pressure freezes people. If you push, they stop picking up. As an ISA you do not teach the legal or financial details, but you can explain the **process** in simple words: “The first step is a short meeting with the agent. He explains how buying works, what it costs to get started, and what to do first.”"},{"t":"p","text":"**Truth 3: Guidance, not sales.** Think of the agent as someone who holds the map. The agent explains the roads; the buyer chooses where to go. Your role is to bring the buyer to the person with the map. You do not choose for them, and you do not tell them which neighborhood is “better.” A clear “no, that is not for us” is useful too: it helps the agent narrow the search. Write it in the notes."},{"t":"p","text":"**Truth 4: Trust comes from clear facts.** Buyers trust people who separate what they know from what still needs checking. You never quote a monthly payment, a rate or a price opinion. Instead, you are honest about what you can and cannot answer: “I don't want to guess on that. Daniel will go through the real numbers with you at the meeting, in writing.” That sentence builds more trust than any guess."},{"t":"callout","title":"Your scope","text":"You are an unlicensed assistant. You do discovery, A.L.M. qualification and appointment scheduling. You never give pricing promises, opinions of value, loan or rate advice, or legal explanations. When a question needs a license, the answer is always the agent (or, when the agent says so, the client's preferred lender)."},{"t":"p","text":"**Truth 5: Every inquiry matters.** The first-time buyer with a small budget, the investor, the person who “is just looking”: each one gets the same speed and care. Small deals become referrals. Old leads become clients. Many buyers start looking six months or more before they buy, so today's casual browser is next season's client."},{"t":"table","head":["Truth","What you see","What you do"],"rows":[["Overwhelmed, not unmotivated","The lead stops replying after one call","Find the question they are stuck on; offer a short meeting"],["Education over pressure","“We're not ready yet”","Explain the next step in simple words; no push"],["Guidance, not sales","“Which area is better?”","Ask what matters to them; leave the advice to the agent"],["Trust comes from clear facts","“How much would my payment be?”","Do not guess; book the agent who will show real numbers"],["Every inquiry matters","A low price point or a vague message","Same speed, same care, same follow-up"]]},{"t":"p","text":"**Putting the truths together.** Imagine a lead named Sofia who writes: “We looked at a few homes online but we probably can't buy for a while.” A weak ISA hears “not a lead” and moves on. A strong ISA hears Truth 1 and Truth 5: Sofia is probably unsure, and she still matters. The reply asks what “a while” means, what is holding them back, and offers a no-pressure meeting."},{"t":"script","title":"Using the truths on a call","lines":[{"who":"Lead","text":"We probably can't buy for a while."},{"who":"You","text":"That's completely fine. Many of our clients start looking six months early. Can I ask what “a while” looks like for you, and what you'd want to have in place first?"},{"who":"Lead","text":"Maybe spring. We're not sure how much we need saved."},{"who":"You","text":"That's one of the most common questions, and Daniel can walk you through it so you have a plan for spring. Would a short call next Tuesday at 6 or Wednesday at 12 work?"}]},{"t":"dodont","do":["Treat silence as confusion, not rejection.","Explain the next step, not the whole process.","Say “I don't want to guess” when a question needs a license."],"dont":["Send ten listings to someone who has not met the agent.","Predict prices or rates (“prices will only go up”).","Decide a lead is “not serious” because of budget or timeline."]}]},{"id":"I1-3","title":"The A.L.M. engine","minutes":7,"body":[{"t":"p","text":"Every call you make has a structure, and the structure has a name: **A.L.M.**, which stands for **Appointment, Location, Motivation**. Your client's whole team uses it, your QA scorecard measures it (A.L.M. Discovery is worth 30 of the 100 points), and every Follow Up Boss note you write is organized by it."},{"t":"p","text":"A.L.M. is not a form you read out loud. It is a checklist in your head. By the end of a good call, you know the answer to all three letters, or you know which one is still missing and you have a plan to get it."},{"t":"p","text":"**A is for Appointment.** Never end a call without pitching a date and time. For example, ask if they can meet the local specialist (your client, the agent) this Saturday at 11 AM or 2 PM. An appointment can be a home tour, a buyer consultation at the office or on video, a phone call with the agent, or a listing consultation for a seller. The appointment is the goal of every call, because the agent can only help people they actually meet."},{"t":"p","text":"**L is for Location.** Uncover target areas, preferred zip codes and commute tolerances. Where do they want to live? Which cities, neighborhoods or zip codes are they looking at? How far are they willing to drive to work? What kind of home: how many bedrooms and bathrooms, what price range, a yard, a garage, one story? Location also includes the property itself: which home did they ask about, and what did they like about it?"},{"t":"p","text":"**M is for Motivation.** Find the underlying “WHY”: relocating for work, growing family, downsizing, or lease expiring. Motivation tells you how urgent the move is and what really matters to the lead. It also includes the timeline: when do they want to be in the new home? A lead whose lease ends in 45 days is very different from a lead who wants to move “sometime next year.”"},{"t":"table","head":["Letter","What you need to learn","Example answer you log"],"rows":[["A: Appointment","A proposed or confirmed time with the agent","Proposed tour Sat 11:00 AM; lead will confirm by text tonight"],["L: Location","Areas, zip codes, commute, beds, baths, price range, the home they asked about","Westside, 4 bd / 2 ba, up to $550K, 30-minute commute max"],["M: Motivation","The reason for moving and the timeline","Job relocation, wants to move by March"]]},{"t":"p","text":"**Why this order of letters, if Appointment comes first?** A is first because it is the goal, not because it is the first question. In practice, you often ask a Location or Motivation question to open the conversation, and then you ask for the appointment early, sometimes in the first minute. When a lead asks about a specific home, you can ask for the tour right away: “Would you like to see it tomorrow at 4 or 6?” Then you learn L and M while you set it up."},{"t":"callout","title":"The golden rule of A.L.M.","text":"The agent should never have to ask the client the same question twice. If you learn it, you log it. Your A.L.M. note is the agent's preparation for the appointment."},{"t":"p","text":"**Good A.L.M. questions are open.** An open question cannot be answered with yes or no. “Are you looking in Westside?” gets a yes or a no. “Which areas are you looking at?” gets a real answer. Here are starter questions. Make them your own, and practice them out loud until they sound natural."},{"t":"list","items":["**A:** “Our agent can show it to you Thursday at 5 or Saturday at 10. Which is better?” / “Would a 15-minute call with the agent tomorrow at 12 or 6 help?”","**L:** “What made you click on that home?” / “Which areas are you looking at?” / “How far is too far from work?” / “What does the home need to have? What would be nice to have?”","**M:** “What has you thinking about moving now?” / “When would you ideally like to be in your new place?” / “What happens if you don't move by then?”"]},{"t":"p","text":"**Common mistakes.** The first mistake is the interrogation: ten questions in a row, like a survey. People hang up. Ask one question, listen, react to the answer, then ask the next one. The second mistake is forgetting the A: a friendly ten-minute chat that ends with “OK, call us when you're ready.” That call scores badly on QA, and worse, the lead never meets the agent. The third mistake is collecting the answers and not logging them."},{"t":"dodont","do":["Ask for an appointment on every call, with two specific times.","Ask open questions and follow up on the answer.","Log A, L and M in the note right after the call."],"dont":["Read the questions like a survey.","End with “let me know when you're ready.”","Ask about the lead's family, religion, origin or other personal traits; ask about the home they want."]}]},{"id":"I1-4","title":"A.L.M. in a real conversation","minutes":6,"body":[{"t":"p","text":"Knowing the three letters is easy. Using them in a real, friendly, two-to-five-minute conversation is a skill. This lesson walks through a full call, line by line, and shows you where each letter appears."},{"t":"p","text":"**The situation.** A lead named Marcus Hill filled out a form on a home portal 2 minutes ago, asking about a 4-bedroom house with a pool. The form includes his consent to calls and texts. His area code is in the same time zone as your client, and it is 10:20 AM on a Tuesday there: inside the calling window. You dial."},{"t":"script","title":"A full A.L.M. call","lines":[{"who":"You","text":"Hi, is this Marcus? This is Ana with the Rivera Home Team. This call may be recorded. You just asked about the 4-bedroom with the pool on Cedar Lane. Did I catch you at an OK time?"},{"who":"Lead","text":"Uh, sure. I just wanted to see more pictures of the kitchen."},{"who":"You","text":"Of course. What caught your eye about that one? (L)"},{"who":"Lead","text":"We need more space. We have two kids and our lease ends in about 45 days. (M)"},{"who":"You","text":"Forty-five days, so timing matters. Is Cedar Lane the area you want, or are you open to nearby areas too? (L)"},{"who":"Lead","text":"We like that side of town. My job is about 20 minutes from there."},{"who":"You","text":"Good to know. Besides four bedrooms, what does the home need to have? (L)"},{"who":"Lead","text":"A bigger backyard. And we'd like to stay under $500,000."},{"who":"You","text":"Got it. With your lease ending soon, the best next step is to see a few homes in person with Daniel, our agent. He could show you Cedar Lane and two or three similar homes this Saturday at 11 AM or 2 PM. Which works better? (A)"},{"who":"Lead","text":"Saturday at 11 could work."},{"who":"You","text":"Great. I'll put Saturday at 11 AM on Daniel's calendar and text you a confirmation with his contact details. Daniel will also send you a short agreement to review before the tour; he'll explain it. Anything else he should know before Saturday?"}]},{"t":"p","text":"**What happened in that call?** In under four minutes, the ISA learned Location (the side of town, a 20-minute commute, 4 bedrooms, a big backyard, under $500,000), Motivation (more space for the family, lease ends in 45 days) and set the Appointment (Saturday 11 AM). She never asked “What is your budget?” in a cold way; the lead offered it because the conversation felt safe."},{"t":"callout","title":"Notice the buyer agreement","text":"Since August 2024, an agent working with a buyer needs a written buyer agreement before any tour. The agent sends it and explains it. You never send, explain or sign it. You only mention that the agent will send it, and you flag it in the calendar invite."},{"t":"p","text":"**Listen for the letters the lead gives you for free.** Leads often answer questions you have not asked yet. When Marcus said “our lease ends in 45 days,” he gave you Motivation and timeline in one sentence. Do not ask “When do you want to move?” two minutes later: he already told you. Instead, use it: “Forty-five days, so timing matters.” That shows you listened."},{"t":"p","text":"**When you cannot get all three letters.** Some calls are short. The lead is driving, or busy, or nervous. In that case, prioritize like this:"},{"t":"steps","items":["Try for the appointment (A). Even a short phone call with the agent counts.","If they will not book, get one Location or Motivation answer so the next call is easier.","Agree on a next step: “Can I call you back tomorrow at 6?” and log it as a task.","Log what you learned and what is still missing, so the next person knows exactly where to start."]},{"t":"p","text":"**The A.L.M. note.** Right after the call, write your note in Follow Up Boss. Module I7 teaches the full note format; here is the short version so you can start practicing now:"},{"t":"table","head":["Field","Example"],"rows":[["Date/Time","Tue 10:24 AM"],["Disposition","Call, connected (4 min)"],["A","Confirmed tour Sat 11:00 AM with Daniel"],["L","Cedar Lane side of town, 4 bd, big backyard, under $500K, 20-min commute"],["M","Lease ends in ~45 days, needs more space"],["Next step","Calendar invite to Daniel with the lead link; buyer agreement flagged; reminders set"]]},{"t":"dodont","do":["Use what the lead tells you, out loud, to show you listened.","Ask for the appointment as soon as there is a reason to meet.","Write down what is still missing if the call was short."],"dont":["Ask a question the lead already answered.","Promise what the agent will do about price or terms.","Leave a call without a next step in the CRM."]}]},{"id":"I1-5","title":"Hard moments: money worries, “I already have an agent” and fair housing","minutes":6,"body":[{"t":"p","text":"Three situations come up again and again with buyers. Each one can go very well or very badly depending on your first sentence. Learn the right first sentence for each."},{"t":"p","text":"**1. “Can we even afford it?”** This is the most common worry, and it is a good sign: the lead is thinking seriously. The wrong answers are a brush-off (“Let me know when you're ready”), a guess (“Your payment would be about $2,400”), or a demand (“Are you pre-approved? We can't show homes without a letter”). The first is cold, the second is outside your scope, and the third freezes a first-time buyer."},{"t":"script","title":"Maria texts back (she agreed to texts)","lines":[{"who":"Lead","text":"Hi, we saw the 3 bedroom on Oak Street. Honestly not sure we can afford anything right now. Is it even worth talking?"},{"who":"You","text":"It's always worth talking, Maria. What are you hoping for, and what is making it feel hard right now? Daniel would love to meet and walk you through the real process, no pressure. Would Thursday at 6 or Saturday at 10 work?"},{"who":"Lead","text":"Oh wow, thank you. Nobody has explained any of this to us. Saturday at 10 is good."}]},{"t":"p","text":"The right reply does three things: it says “it is worth talking,” it asks what is making it feel hard (so you learn Motivation and worries), and it offers a meeting with two times. Financing questions go to the agent; the agent decides if and when to connect them with the client's preferred lender. Module I4 covers the financing conversation in depth."},{"t":"p","text":"**2. “I already have an agent.”** Never just hang up and delete the lead. Never pitch around another agent's agreement either. Ask one simple question first:"},{"t":"script","title":"The agent question","lines":[{"who":"Lead","text":"I already have an agent."},{"who":"You","text":"Understood, and I won't step on that. Have you signed an agreement with them?"},{"who":"Lead","text":"Yes, we signed last week."},{"who":"You","text":"Great, I'll make a note. If anything changes, we're here. Have a wonderful day."}]},{"t":"p","text":"If they **have** signed: respect it. Log it in Follow Up Boss (“Has agent, signed agreement, per lead on [date]”), tell your agent the same day, and follow whatever plan your client sets for that lead. Never try to talk them out of the agreement. If they **have not** signed: offer a second opinion from your local specialist and ask for a time."},{"t":"script","title":"When there is no signed agreement","lines":[{"who":"Lead","text":"No, we haven't signed anything. We've just been talking to someone."},{"who":"You","text":"Thanks for telling me. Many buyers like a second opinion before they decide who to work with. Daniel could do a quick 15-minute call tomorrow at 12 or 5. Would one of those work?"}]},{"t":"p","text":"**3. Fair housing questions.** Buyers often ask “Is it a safe area?”, “Are the schools good?”, “What kind of people live there?” or “Is it family-friendly?” These sound innocent, but answering them can be steering, which is illegal under the federal Fair Housing Act. You must treat every caller the same, never steer anyone toward or away from an area, and never describe a neighborhood by who lives there, its schools, crime or “safety,” religion, or how “family-friendly” it is."},{"t":"script","title":"The fair housing answer","lines":[{"who":"Lead","text":"Is that a safe neighborhood? We have kids."},{"who":"You","text":"Great question for your agent. I can also send you public sources. What else is important to you in the home itself, like the yard or the number of bedrooms?"}]},{"t":"callout","title":"Log the home, not the person","text":"Write what the lead wants in the home: bedrooms, yard, commute, price range, one story. Never write who they are: “young family,” “Hispanic couple,” “religious,” “retired.” Notes are business records, and the agent may share them."},{"t":"p","text":"**Why A.L.M. helps in every hard moment.** In all three situations, you return to the structure. Money worry? Ask what makes it hard (M), then book (A). Has an agent? Ask about the agreement, then respect it or book a second opinion (A). Fair housing question? Give the safe answer, then return to the home they want (L). The structure keeps you calm and keeps you inside your scope."},{"t":"dodont","do":["Answer “can we afford it?” with care, a question, and a meeting.","Ask whether they signed an agreement with the other agent.","Use the exact fair housing sentence, then move back to the home."],"dont":["Quote a payment, a rate or a price opinion.","Pitch around another agent's signed agreement.","Describe an area as safe, unsafe, good for kids, or by who lives there."]}]}],"practice":{"title":"Your A.L.M. call book and three recorded role-plays","minutes":90,"intro":"You will build your own A.L.M. question bank, then practice it out loud with a partner (another VA in training or your coach) in three recorded role-plays. Use only fictional leads. Record on your computer or phone with your partner's permission; never record a real lead for practice.","steps":["Write your A.L.M. call book (20 minutes): at least 5 questions for Location, 5 for Motivation and 3 ways to ask for the Appointment with two specific times. Write them in your own natural English. Add the exact fair housing sentence and the “Have you signed an agreement with them?” question.","Read your questions out loud three times. Mark any word that is hard to say clearly and replace it with a simpler one.","Role-play 1, the ready buyer (10 minutes): your partner plays a lead whose lease ends in 45 days and who asked about a specific home. Your goal: all three letters and a booked appointment in under 5 minutes. Remember to announce that the call may be recorded.","Role-play 2, the worried first-time buyer (10 minutes): your partner says “Honestly, I'm not sure we can afford anything.” Your goal: no guessing about money, learn what is making it hard, and offer a no-pressure meeting with two times.","Role-play 3, the hard moments (10 minutes): during one call your partner says “Is it a safe area?” and later “We've been talking to another agent.” Your goal: the fair housing answer, the agreement question, and the right next step for their answer.","Switch roles and play the lead for your partner, so you hear the calls from the buyer's side (20 minutes).","After each of your role-plays, write a practice A.L.M. note (Date/Time, Disposition, A, L, M, Next Step) exactly as you would in Follow Up Boss.","Listen to your three recordings. For each one, write two sentences: one thing you did well and one thing you will change."],"deliverable":"Send your coach (in your BeyondVAs training channel): your written A.L.M. call book, the three recordings, the three practice A.L.M. notes, and your six self-review sentences."},"quiz":[{"q":"What does the ALM qualification framework stand for in our ISA operations?","options":["Address, Listing price, Mortgage approval","Appointment, Location, Motivation","Action plans, Leads, Metrics","Agent, Lender, Marketing"],"answer":1,"explain":"ALM stands for Appointment, Location, and Motivation."},{"q":"A lead stopped replying after one short call. Based on the five buyer truths, what is most likely true?","options":["They were never serious and should be archived","They are overwhelmed and stuck on a question","They already bought a home","They want you to send many more listings"],"answer":1,"explain":"Overwhelmed, not unmotivated. A quiet buyer is usually confused. Find the question they are stuck on and make it easy to come back."},{"q":"A lead asks, “How much would my monthly payment be on that house?” What is the best answer?","options":["Give a quick estimate so they stay interested","Tell them to search for a mortgage calculator online and call back","“I don't want to guess on that. Our agent will go through the real numbers with you. Would Thursday at 5 or Saturday at 10 work?”","Ask for their credit score so you can estimate it"],"answer":2,"explain":"Payments, rates and loan advice are outside your unlicensed scope. Be honest that you will not guess, and book the agent, who shows real numbers."},{"q":"Which of these is the best Location question?","options":["“Which areas are you looking at, and how far is too far from work?”","“Are you looking in Westside?”","“Is the area important?”","“What kind of neighbors do you want?”"],"answer":0,"explain":"It is open (not yes or no) and it covers target areas and commute tolerance. Asking what kind of neighbors they want invites a fair housing problem."},{"q":"A buyer asks, “Is that a family-friendly, safe neighborhood?” What do you say?","options":["“Yes, lots of families with kids live there.”","“Great question for your agent. I can also send you public sources.”","“I would avoid that area if I were you.”","“The schools there are the best in town.”"],"answer":1,"explain":"Fair housing: never describe an area by who lives there, schools, crime or safety, or how family-friendly it is. Use the exact sentence, then return to the home they want."},{"q":"A lead says, “I already have an agent.” What is your first move?","options":["Hang up and delete the lead","Explain why your client is a better agent","Ask, “Have you signed an agreement with them?”","Offer a discount to switch"],"answer":2,"explain":"Ask about the agreement first. If they signed, respect it, note it and tell your agent. If not, offer a second opinion and ask for a time. Never pitch around a signed agreement."},{"q":"You had a friendly 8-minute call and learned the lead's areas and timeline, but the call ended with “Call us when you're ready.” What went wrong?","options":["Nothing; the lead will call back","The call was too short","You should have asked about their family","You never asked for the Appointment with specific times"],"answer":3,"explain":"A is the goal of every call. Never end a call without pitching a date and time, like Saturday at 11 AM or 2 PM."},{"q":"Which note follows the “log the home, not the person” rule?","options":["“Young Hispanic family, wants a quiet area”","“4 bd / 2 ba, big backyard, under $500K, max 20-min commute; lease ends in 45 days”","“Retired couple, very religious”","“Nice people, seem serious”"],"answer":1,"explain":"Log what the lead wants in the home and their timeline, never who they are. Notes are business records and may be shared."},{"q":"Two inquiries arrive at the same time: one for a $260,000 condo and one for a $950,000 house. How do you treat them?","options":["Same speed, same care, same follow-up for both","Call the expensive one first and the condo tomorrow","Send the condo lead to an email drip only","Ask the manager which one is worth your time"],"answer":0,"explain":"Every price point gets your best. Today's small deal is next year's referral."}]},{"id":"I2","part":"isa","title":"Speed to lead and the 3-strike cadence","summary":"Reach every new lead within minutes, inside the legal calling window, with a call, a text and a second call that follow one proven rhythm.","objectives":["Explain why the first five minutes decide most internet leads, and quote the research behind it.","Launch your first call within 180 seconds of a lead arriving, after a 30-second pre-dial check of consent, time zone and state.","Run the 3-strike cadence (call, text, second call) correctly, including when a lead arrives late in the day or after hours.","Leave a voicemail of 20 seconds or less and send a personalized first text that gets replies.","Handle missed inbound calls and lead texts fast, with a reply that always asks for the appointment."],"lessons":[{"id":"I2-1","title":"The 5-minute rule: why speed wins","minutes":6,"body":[{"t":"p","text":"Speed to lead is the time between the moment a lead arrives in your client's CRM and the moment you first try to reach them. For internet leads, it is the single biggest factor you control. Your client pays for leads from portals, ads and their website. Every minute you wait, that money loses value."},{"t":"p","text":"**The research.** Contacting an internet lead within **5 minutes** makes the odds of qualifying it about **21 times higher** than waiting 30 minutes. That number comes from the Lead Response Management Study by Dr. James Oldroyd (MIT) with InsideSales.com, 2007. Many real estate teams report the same pattern: the first helpful agent to call often wins the client."},{"t":"callout","title":"Your house rule","text":"Your dialer must launch within **180 seconds** (3 minutes) of lead arrival. All of this happens inside the calling window. A lead that arrives after hours gets its first call as soon as the window opens in the lead's time zone."},{"t":"table","head":["Time since the lead arrived","What it means"],"rows":[["0:00","Lead arrives in Follow Up Boss"],["3:00","Dialer launched, at the latest"],["5:00","Best window closes"],["30:00","Odds of qualifying about 21x lower"]]},{"t":"p","text":"**Why are the first minutes so powerful?** Think back to Maria on her couch (module I1). When she taps “Contact agent,” three things are true at the same moment:"},{"t":"list","items":["**Her attention is on the home.** The listing is still open on her phone. In 30 minutes she is cooking dinner, driving or asleep.","**Her question is fresh.** She remembers exactly what she wanted to ask. Tomorrow, she may not even remember which website she used.","**She is talking to several people.** Many portals and ads send inquiries to more than one agent, and many buyers contact several homes. The first friendly, helpful voice usually becomes “their agent.”"]},{"t":"p","text":"**The calling window always wins over speed.** Speed never means breaking the law. Your calling window is **9:00 AM to 8:00 PM in the lead's local time, Monday to Saturday, and noon to 8:00 PM on Sunday.** Texts follow the same window. If a lead arrives at 10:40 PM in their time zone, you do not call and you do not text. Their first call happens at 9:00 AM their time the next morning (or noon if it is Sunday), and it is the first call you make when the window opens. Some states are stricter; your manager gives you the rules for each state before you dial. Module F6 covers this in full."},{"t":"p","text":"**Speed is a habit, not a mood.** Most people answer leads fast when they are not busy and slowly when they are. Elite ISAs answer fast every time, because they set up their day around it. A new lead interrupts everything except a live conversation. If you are writing a note, you stop and dial. If you are on a call, you finish politely and dial right after."},{"t":"p","text":"**The lead lifecycle.** Speed is the first step in a pipeline that every lead moves through. Know the pipeline, because each step has an owner and a clear action:"},{"t":"steps","items":["**New Inbound Lead:** the lead arrives from a portal, ad, website or sign call. It lands on the New Speed-to-Lead Smart List.","**Speed Call:** you dial within 180 seconds, inside the window, and run the 3-strike cadence if they do not answer.","**A.L.M. Qualified:** you learn Appointment, Location and Motivation and set the appointment.","**Direct Agent Handoff:** you hand the lead to your client, the agent, with a clean note and a calendar invite.","**In-Escrow Tracker:** the agent works the deal; the lead is now in a transaction."]},{"t":"dodont","do":["Stop what you are doing when a new lead arrives (unless you are in a live conversation).","Check the lead's local time before every first call.","Make after-hours leads your first calls when the window opens."],"dont":["Wait until you have “a few leads” to call them together.","Call or text outside 9 AM to 8 PM (noon to 8 PM Sunday) in the lead's time zone.","Assume someone else on the team already called."]}]},{"id":"I2-2","title":"Your speed station and the 30-second pre-dial check","minutes":6,"body":[{"t":"p","text":"You cannot call within 180 seconds if you have to search for the lead, log in again or open five tabs. Fast ISAs prepare their workstation before the shift starts. We call it your speed station."},{"t":"p","text":"**Set up your speed station at the start of every shift:**"},{"t":"steps","items":["Clock in on Hubstaff. Your time and screenshots start now.","Open Follow Up Boss in your browser, with the **New Speed-to-Lead** Smart List (leads 0 to 15 minutes old) in its own tab.","Turn on new-lead notifications (sound and desktop) in Follow Up Boss. Test them once.","Check your headset, microphone and internet. Have your backup internet ready.","Open your text and voicemail templates so you can personalize them in seconds.","Check the time zones you will call today and any state rules your manager gave you."]},{"t":"p","text":"**The 30-second pre-dial check.** When a new lead appears, you have 180 seconds. Use the first 30 to read the lead profile. Never skip this: it keeps you legal and it makes your opener personal."},{"t":"table","head":["Check","Where to look","Why it matters"],"rows":[["Source","Lead source field (portal, ad, website, sign call)","Tells you which opener to use and how warm the lead is"],["What they asked about","The inquiry or property field and their message","Your opener names the home or the question"],["Local time and state","Area code, address or property location","Calling window and state rules; the lead's state counts"],["Consent to texts","Form consent, tags, or notes","Text only leads who agreed to texts"],["DNC and history","Tags, stage, previous notes","Never contact a lead tagged DNC; see who talked to them before"],["Assigned agent","Assigned-to field","The person whose calendar you book"]]},{"t":"p","text":"**Inbound versus other lists.** A new inbound lead asked to be contacted, so you may call them inside the window. Any other list (an old database, a farm list, a purchased list) may be called only if it was scrubbed against the National Do Not Call Registry and your client's own DNC list. Speed-to-lead is about inbound leads. Never treat an old list like a fresh inquiry."},{"t":"callout","title":"Dial by hand","text":"You dial every number by hand. Never use an autodialer or a prerecorded message unless your manager confirms written consent. Speed comes from preparation, not from machines."},{"t":"p","text":"**Time zones, the practical way.** You may work from Honduras or elsewhere in Latin America while your client is in Florida and the lead is in California. Always think in the **lead's** local time. Keep a small table on your desk with the current difference between your time and each US time zone. Remember that most of the US changes its clocks twice a year (daylight saving time, from March to November), while Honduras does not, so the difference changes. Arizona (most of it) and Hawaii do not change either. When you are unsure, check a world clock before you dial."},{"t":"table","head":["Example","Lead's local time","What you do"],"rows":[["Lead in California arrives Tuesday","8:15 AM Pacific","Wait. First call at 9:00 AM Pacific; set a task for 8:59"],["Lead in New York arrives Monday","7:50 PM Eastern","Call now. Text at 7:52 if they agreed to texts. Second call tomorrow at 9:00 AM"],["Lead in Texas arrives Sunday","10:05 AM Central","Wait. Sunday calls start at noon. First call at 12:00 PM Central"],["Lead in Florida arrives Saturday","8:20 PM Eastern","Too late. First call Sunday at 12:00 PM Eastern"]]},{"t":"p","text":"**Never batch your leads.** A common mistake is to “collect” new leads and call them all at the top of the hour. That turns a 2-minute response into a 40-minute response, and you lose most of the 21x advantage. One lead arrives, one lead gets dialed."},{"t":"p","text":"**When two leads arrive at once.** Call the first one. If they do not answer, leave the voicemail, send the text (if they agreed), then dial the second lead. A full Strike 1 and Strike 2 takes about two minutes, so both leads are still inside five minutes. If you are on a live conversation with lead 1, finish it well, then dial lead 2 immediately and tell your manager if leads are piling up."},{"t":"dodont","do":["Prepare your speed station before your first lead arrives.","Read source, inquiry, local time, state, consent and history before you dial.","Think in the lead's local time, every time."],"dont":["Text a lead who did not agree to texts.","Call a list that has not been scrubbed against the Do Not Call Registry.","Use an autodialer or recorded message without the manager's confirmation of written consent."]}]},{"id":"I2-3","title":"The 3-strike inbound cadence","minutes":6,"body":[{"t":"p","text":"Most new leads do not answer the first call. They are at work, driving, or they do not recognize the number. That is normal. What matters is what you do next. Every BeyondVAs ISA follows the same rhythm, called the **3-strike cadence**."},{"t":"table","head":["Strike","When","What you do"],"rows":[["Strike 1: Call","0 min","Outbound call. Let it ring 5 to 6 times. If no answer, leave a 20-second voicemail."],["Strike 2: Text","2 min","Immediate personalized text message referencing their property inquiry, to leads who agreed to texts."],["Strike 3: Call","About 4 hours later","Second phone call, before the 6:00 PM evening rush in the lead's time zone."]]},{"t":"callout","title":"Every strike stays inside the calling window","text":"Texts included. If Strike 3 would land after hours, make it first thing the next morning, when the window opens in the lead's time zone."},{"t":"p","text":"**Strike 1: the call.** Let the phone ring 5 to 6 times. Do not hang up after two rings: many people need a moment to find their phone. If voicemail answers, leave a short, friendly message of **20 seconds or less** (lesson I2-5 has scripts). Name yourself, your client's team, the home they asked about, and say you will text them. Then log the attempt."},{"t":"p","text":"**Strike 2: the text, at 2 minutes.** Many people do not answer unknown numbers but do read texts. The text must be personal: use their first name, name the home or question, and ask an easy question. Send it only if the lead agreed to texts (portal and ad forms usually include that consent; ask your manager if you are not sure)."},{"t":"script","title":"A Strike 2 text","lines":[{"who":"You","text":"Hi Jordan, this is Ana with the Rivera Home Team. I just tried calling about the 3-bed on Maple Ave you asked about. Would you like to see it this week? Daniel can show it Thursday at 5 or Saturday at 11."}]},{"t":"p","text":"**Strike 3: the second call, about 4 hours later.** Call again before 6:00 PM in the lead's time zone. After 6 PM, families are cooking, picking up kids and eating, and fewer people answer. Set a task in Follow Up Boss for Strike 3 as soon as you finish Strike 2, so you never rely on memory."},{"t":"p","text":"**Worked examples.** Practice the timing until it is automatic:"},{"t":"table","head":["Lead arrives (lead's time)","Strike 1","Strike 2","Strike 3"],"rows":[["Tuesday 10:12 AM","10:13 AM call + voicemail","10:15 AM text","About 2:15 PM call"],["Tuesday 2:40 PM","2:41 PM","2:43 PM","Before 6:00 PM (around 5:30 PM), or 9:00 AM Wednesday if you cannot"],["Tuesday 6:30 PM","6:31 PM","6:33 PM","Wednesday 9:00 AM (first thing in the window)"],["Saturday 7:55 PM","7:56 PM","7:58 PM","Sunday 12:00 PM (noon is when the Sunday window opens)"],["Wednesday 9:45 PM","Thursday 9:00 AM","Thursday 9:02 AM","About 1:00 PM Thursday"]]},{"t":"p","text":"**When the lead answers at any strike,** the cadence stops and the conversation begins: A.L.M., then the appointment. When the lead texts back, reply quickly (lesson I2-4). When the lead says “stop,” “remove me” or “don't call,” the cadence stops forever: tag them DNC, move the stage to Archived / DNC, pause every automation and log it. Never argue."},{"t":"p","text":"**After the third strike.** If the lead has not answered after three strikes, they are not lost. They move into your client's follow-up plan (often an action plan in Follow Up Boss) with scheduled calls and texts over the next days and weeks. Module I9 teaches long-term follow-up. Your job now is to log all three attempts cleanly so the plan starts on time."},{"t":"p","text":"**Logging each strike.** Every attempt gets logged in Follow Up Boss: the call with its disposition (no answer, voicemail left), the text, and the task for the next step. A lead with three logged attempts tells your client you did your job. A lead with no history looks like it was ignored, even if you called."},{"t":"dodont","do":["Ring 5 to 6 times before you give up on a call.","Send Strike 2 about 2 minutes after Strike 1, only to leads who agreed to texts.","Create the Strike 3 task immediately, timed before 6 PM in the lead's time zone."],"dont":["Wait 3 days before the second attempt.","Mark an unanswered lead as Trash or DNC.","Let Strike 3 fall outside the calling window."]}]},{"id":"I2-4","title":"Missed calls, inbound texts and the reply rule","minutes":5,"body":[{"t":"p","text":"Speed to lead is not only about new leads. It is also about leads who reach out to you: a missed call from a lead, a text reply, an email. A lead who contacts you is the warmest lead you will ever have. Treat their message like a brand-new lead."},{"t":"p","text":"**Missed a live call? Call back inside 60 seconds.** If a lead calls your client's line while you are on another call, call them back the moment you hang up, inside 60 seconds if you can. They just tried to reach you. In two minutes they may call the next agent on the list."},{"t":"p","text":"**The reply rule.** Every lead who texts or emails gets a reply fast, and the reply **asks for the appointment.** Your client sets the exact standard (many teams use two hours or less); during your shift, aim for minutes. If the message arrives outside the calling window, reply when the window opens in the lead's time zone. “Next week” is a lost client."},{"t":"script","title":"Dana has been waiting","lines":[{"who":"Lead","text":"Hi, it's Dana. I texted this morning about the house on 10th Street, did you get it? Is it still available?"},{"who":"You","text":"Hi Dana, yes, and sorry for the wait. I'll check with Daniel that 10th Street is still available and confirm right away. Would you like to see it tomorrow afternoon or Saturday morning?"},{"who":"Lead","text":"Saturday morning works. Thank you!"}]},{"t":"p","text":"Look at the reply. It apologizes once (not three times), it answers the question honestly (you confirm availability with the agent instead of guessing), and it offers two times. Compare it with weak replies:"},{"t":"table","head":["Reply","Why it fails"],"rows":[["“Got it. I'll get back to you next week with options.”","Too slow, and no appointment. A lost client."],["“Text me on my personal WhatsApp, it's faster.”","All client communication stays in the client's systems (Follow Up Boss). Never move a lead to a personal number."],["“So sorry, so sorry, we have been very busy, so sorry!”","One clear apology is enough. Too many sound unprofessional."],["“Yes it's available, the price is negotiable.”","You never discuss price or terms, and you never guess availability."]]},{"t":"p","text":"**Read the whole history before you reply.** Before you answer a text, open the lead's profile and read the last notes and messages. Maybe the agent already talked to Dana yesterday. Maybe she asked a different question last week. A reply that ignores the history (“Hi! Are you looking to buy?”) tells the lead nobody is paying attention."},{"t":"p","text":"**Questions you cannot answer.** Leads often text questions that need a license or information you do not have: “Will the seller take less?”, “What are the HOA fees?”, “Is the roof new?”, “Can I buy with no money down?” Do not guess. Use a bridge: acknowledge the question, say who will answer it, and ask for the appointment."},{"t":"script","title":"A bridge reply","lines":[{"who":"Lead","text":"Would the seller take $20K less?"},{"who":"You","text":"Great question for Daniel, our agent. He handles everything about price and offers. He can talk it through with you on a quick call today at 4 or tomorrow at 11. Which is better?"}]},{"t":"p","text":"**Emails.** Some leads prefer email. The same rule applies: reply quickly, personally, and ask for the appointment. Keep emails short: three to five sentences, the home they asked about, two times, and your name and the team's name. Never send attachments or documents the agent has not approved."},{"t":"callout","title":"Opt-out words end the conversation","text":"If any message says STOP, “remove me,” “unsubscribe,” or “don't call/text me,” do not reply with a sales message. Tag DNC, move the stage to Archived / DNC, pause all automations, and log the opt-out. Never delete the contact."},{"t":"dodont","do":["Call back a missed lead call inside 60 seconds.","Reply to texts fast, inside the window, and ask for the appointment with two times.","Read the history before every reply."],"dont":["Move a lead to your personal phone or WhatsApp.","Guess about price, availability, HOA fees or loans.","Send a sales reply to a STOP message."]}]},{"id":"I2-5","title":"Voicemails, texts and emails that get answered","minutes":6,"body":[{"t":"p","text":"Strike 1 often ends in voicemail, and Strike 2 is always a text. The words you choose decide whether the lead calls back. This lesson gives you templates and the rules behind them, so you can adapt them to any client."},{"t":"p","text":"**Voicemail: 20 seconds or less.** A long voicemail does not get heard. Speak a little slower than normal, smile while you talk (people can hear it), and say your name and callback number clearly. Follow this structure:"},{"t":"steps","items":["Their name and your name: “Hi Jordan, this is Ana with the Rivera Home Team.”","Why you are calling: “You asked about the 3-bed on Maple Avenue.”","An easy next step: “I'll send you a quick text. You can reply there or call me at 555-0142.”","Your number again, slowly: “That's 555-0142. Talk soon.”"]},{"t":"script","title":"Voicemail templates by source","lines":[{"who":"You","text":"Portal inquiry: “Hi Jordan, this is Ana with the Rivera Home Team. You asked about the 3-bed on Maple Avenue. I'm sending you a quick text with times to see it. You can also call me at 555-0142. That's 555-0142. Talk soon.”"},{"who":"You","text":"Ad lead: “Hi Priya, Ana here with the Rivera Home Team. You asked for the list of homes from our ad. I'll text you now so you can tell me which areas you like. My number is 555-0142. Again, 555-0142.”"},{"who":"You","text":"Website sign-up: “Hi Sam, this is Ana with the Rivera Home Team. Thanks for signing up on our website. I'll text you a quick question about what you're looking for. My number is 555-0142. Again, 555-0142. Thanks!”"}]},{"t":"p","text":"**Texts: short, personal, one question.** A good first text has four parts: their first name, who you are and which team, the home or request they asked about, and **one** easy question, usually two appointment times. Keep it under 300 characters. No links unless your client approves them. No ALL CAPS, no emojis in a first message, no pressure."},{"t":"table","head":["Situation","Template (fill in the brackets)"],"rows":[["Portal inquiry","Hi [first name], this is [you] with [client team]. You asked about [address]. Would you like to see it? [Agent] can show it [day/time] or [day/time]."],["Ad lead","Hi [first name], [you] here with [client team]. Thanks for asking about homes in [area]. What matters most to you in your next home?"],["Website sign-up","Hi [first name], it's [you] with [client team]. Thanks for registering. Are you looking to buy in the next few months, or just starting to look?"],["Open house sign-in","Hi [first name], [you] with [client team]. Thanks for visiting [address] on Saturday. What did you think of it?"]]},{"t":"callout","title":"Check your scope before you use any template","text":"Your client approves every template you send. In some states (Texas, for example), an unlicensed assistant may not call to find out whether someone wants to buy, sell or lease. Your manager sets your scope, and your client's approved scripts replace these examples where they differ."},{"t":"p","text":"**What makes a lead reply.** Leads answer questions that are easy and specific. “Let me know if you have any questions” gets silence: it puts all the work on the lead. “Would Thursday at 5 or Saturday at 11 work?” gets an answer, because it takes two seconds to choose. If they are not ready for an appointment, a question about the home works too: “What did you like most about it?”"},{"t":"p","text":"**Common mistakes.** Writing a paragraph instead of two sentences. Sending the same generic text to every lead (“Hi! Are you still looking?”). Forgetting to name the home. Writing in a way that sounds like a robot. Sending a text at 9:15 PM because “it is only a text” (texts follow the calling window). Promising anything about price, the seller or loans."},{"t":"p","text":"**The email touch.** Many clients also send an email within the first hour, either automatically through an action plan or written by you. If you write it, use the same structure as the text: name, home, two times, signature. Then log it."},{"t":"dodont","do":["Keep voicemails to 20 seconds and say your number twice.","Name the home or request in every first message.","End every message with one easy question."],"dont":["Leave a long voicemail with your life story.","Send generic texts like “Still looking?” to a brand-new lead.","Text outside the calling window or to leads who did not agree to texts."]}]}],"practice":{"title":"Speed drills, cadence timing and your message kit","minutes":90,"intro":"You will practice speed and the cadence until they are automatic. Use your Follow Up Boss practice account (or the practice leads your coach gives you), a timer, and fictional leads only. Never contact a real lead for practice.","steps":["Set up your speed station exactly as in lesson I2-2. Take a screenshot of your screen layout (with no real client data visible).","Time-zone worksheet (20 minutes): your coach gives you 10 lead arrival times in different states and days. For each one, write the lead's local time, the exact time of Strike 1, Strike 2 and Strike 3, and whether you text. Mark any lead you must not contact yet.","Speed drill (20 minutes): your coach (or a partner) adds 5 practice leads to your practice account at random times. For each one, do the 30-second pre-dial check, then “dial” (call your partner) and start the timer when the lead appears. Record your time from arrival to dial. Goal: every lead under 180 seconds.","Write your message kit (20 minutes): one voicemail and one first text for each source (portal, ad, website, open house), personalized with fictional names and 555 numbers. Every voicemail must read in 20 seconds or less: time yourself.","Record your four voicemails as audio. Listen back. Re-record any that are over 20 seconds or unclear.","Reply drill (15 minutes): answer these three fictional lead texts in writing: (a) “Is the house on Pine St still available?” (b) “Would the seller take less?” (c) “STOP texting me.” Use the reply rule, the bridge reply, and the opt-out procedure.","Log each practice lead's attempts in your practice account: call disposition, voicemail, text, and a Strike 3 task at the right time."],"deliverable":"Send your coach (in your BeyondVAs training channel): your completed time-zone worksheet, your speed-drill times for all 5 leads, your written message kit, the 4 voicemail recordings, your 3 written replies, and a screenshot of one practice lead's activity log showing all three strikes."},"quiz":[{"q":"What is the primary objective of an ISA during the first 3 minutes of an inbound lead arrival?","options":["Explain closing costs and draft a mock buyer representation contract.","Initiate contact immediately, execute ALM qualification, and secure an agent appointment.","Wait 30 minutes to make sure the client isn’t busy browsing properties.","Assign the lead to an automated email drip without calling."],"answer":1,"explain":"Speed-to-lead is king. The goal is rapid engagement and securing the appointment using ALM."},{"q":"If an inbound lead doesn’t answer your first call, what is the required next step?","options":["Wait 3 days before calling back.","Immediately send a targeted text message addressing their inquiry and schedule the afternoon follow-up call.","Mark the lead as Trash/DNC.","Assign the lead to an external call center."],"answer":1,"explain":"Immediate text follow-up captures leads who prefer texting over answering unfamiliar numbers."},{"q":"According to the Lead Response Management Study (Oldroyd, MIT, with InsideSales.com, 2007), contacting a lead within 5 minutes instead of 30 minutes makes the odds of qualifying it about:","options":["2 times higher","5 times higher","21 times higher","100 times higher"],"answer":2,"explain":"About 21 times higher. That is why your dialer must launch within 180 seconds of a lead arriving, inside the calling window."},{"q":"A portal lead in California arrives at 8:15 AM Pacific time on a Tuesday. What do you do?","options":["Call immediately; speed matters more than the window","Send a text now and call at 9:00 AM","Wait until the afternoon so you don't bother them","Wait and make your first call at 9:00 AM Pacific, the moment the window opens"],"answer":3,"explain":"The calling window (9 AM to 8 PM Monday to Saturday in the lead's local time) applies to calls and texts. The lead gets the first call as soon as the window opens."},{"q":"A new lead arrives at 6:30 PM their time on a Tuesday. When is Strike 3?","options":["At 10:30 PM, exactly 4 hours later","Wednesday at 9:00 AM their time, first thing in the window","Next Monday","Never; after 6 PM you skip Strike 3"],"answer":1,"explain":"Every strike stays inside the calling window. If Strike 3 would land after hours, make it first thing the next morning."},{"q":"How long should your Strike 1 voicemail be?","options":["20 seconds or less","About 1 minute, to explain everything","2 minutes, including the listing details","Do not leave voicemails"],"answer":0,"explain":"Let it ring 5 to 6 times; if no answer, leave a voicemail of 20 seconds or less with your name, the home, and your number said twice."},{"q":"A lead's form shows they did NOT agree to texts. They miss your first call. What do you do for Strike 2?","options":["Text anyway; it's only a text","Skip the text, log the call and voicemail, and set the Strike 3 call task","Text from your personal phone instead","Mark them DNC"],"answer":1,"explain":"Text only leads who agreed to texts. Without consent, you continue with calls inside the window and log everything."},{"q":"Dana texted three hours ago asking if the house on 10th Street is available. Which reply is best?","options":["“Got it. I'll get back to you next week with some options.”","“Text me on my personal cell instead, it's faster.”","“Hi Dana, sorry for the wait. I'll confirm with Daniel that it's available right away. Want to see it tomorrow afternoon or Saturday morning?”","“Yes, and the seller is flexible on price.”"],"answer":2,"explain":"Apologize once, answer honestly without guessing, and ask for the appointment with two times. Never move leads to a personal phone or discuss price."},{"q":"Which is the correct order of the lead lifecycle?","options":["Speed Call, New Inbound Lead, Direct Agent Handoff, A.L.M. Qualified, In-Escrow Tracker","New Inbound Lead, A.L.M. Qualified, Speed Call, In-Escrow Tracker, Direct Agent Handoff","New Inbound Lead, Direct Agent Handoff, Speed Call, A.L.M. Qualified, In-Escrow Tracker","New Inbound Lead, Speed Call, A.L.M. Qualified, Direct Agent Handoff, In-Escrow Tracker"],"answer":3,"explain":"A lead arrives, gets the speed call, is A.L.M.-qualified, is handed to the agent, and then is tracked in escrow."},{"q":"A lead called your client's line while you were on another call. What should you do when you hang up?","options":["Call them back inside 60 seconds","Wait for them to call again","Send an email tomorrow","Add them to the 30-60 day nurture list"],"answer":0,"explain":"A lead who calls you is the warmest lead you have. Call back inside 60 seconds, before they call the next agent."}]},{"id":"I3","part":"isa","title":"Conversations that set appointments","summary":"Turn dials into real two-minute conversations, open each call the right way for its lead source, ask discovery questions that open people up, and ask for the appointment on every call.","objectives":["Run a two-minute conversation with a clear structure: opener, discovery, appointment, confirmation.","Use the right opener for portal, ad, open-house, sign-call and past-client leads, including the recording notice.","Ask open discovery questions, follow up on answers and listen more than you talk.","Ask for the appointment on every call with two specific times, and confirm it so the lead shows up.","Plan a daily power hour that works your lists in the right order."],"lessons":[{"id":"I3-1","title":"Two-minute conversations beat ten dials","minutes":5,"body":[{"t":"p","text":"In this job you will hear two words all the time: **dials** and **conversations**. A dial is any call you make, answered or not. A conversation is a live talk with a lead that lasts **two minutes or more**. Dials are the input you control completely. Conversations are what actually move appointments. Ten dials with no conversation set nothing; one good two-minute conversation can set an appointment."},{"t":"p","text":"Your daily targets (module I11) are 80 to 100 dials, 12 to 15 live conversations and 2 to 3 A.L.M.-qualified appointments per shift. Look at the math: about one dial in seven becomes a conversation, and about one conversation in five becomes an appointment. That means every conversation is precious. When someone answers, you must be ready."},{"t":"p","text":"**Why two minutes?** Many people decide in the first 20 seconds whether to stay on the phone. If they are still talking after two minutes, they trust you enough to answer real questions. Short calls under 30 seconds are usually wrong numbers, “call me later,” or hang-ups. Your goal is to get past the first 20 seconds, then make the next 90 seconds useful for the lead."},{"t":"table","head":["Time","Stage","What you do"],"rows":[["0:00 to 0:20","Opener","Your name, your client's team, the recording notice, why you are calling, and permission (“Do you have two minutes?”)"],["0:20 to 1:20","Discovery","Two or three open questions about Location and Motivation; listen and react"],["1:20 to 1:50","Appointment","Give a reason to meet and offer two specific times"],["1:50 to 2:30","Confirmation","Repeat the day, time and place; say how you will confirm; ask if there is anything the agent should know"]]},{"t":"p","text":"This is a guide, not a stopwatch. Some leads book in 60 seconds; some need six minutes. But if you are at minute five and have not asked for the appointment, you are probably chatting, not working."},{"t":"p","text":"**Your voice is your whole presentation.** The lead cannot see you. They hear only your voice, your energy and your clarity. Phone English is a skill you can train:"},{"t":"list","items":["**Smile when you talk.** It changes the sound of your voice. People hear warmth.","**Slow down by about 10 percent,** especially for names, numbers and times. Nervous callers speak fast.","**Use the lead's name** two or three times in the call, not more.","**Repeat to confirm:** “So that's Saturday at 11 AM at 42 Cedar Lane, right?” This also protects you from misunderstandings.","**Short sentences.** One idea per sentence. It is easier to say and easier to understand.","**Stand up or sit up straight** for important calls. Your voice sounds more confident."]},{"t":"callout","title":"Your accent is not the problem","text":"Many clients choose bilingual ISAs on purpose: millions of people in the US speak Spanish at home, and many prefer it for big decisions. Clarity matters, not accent. If a lead says “Sorry?”, repeat more slowly with simpler words, never louder. If a lead prefers Spanish and your client approves, switch languages and log it."},{"t":"p","text":"**What a conversation is not.** A conversation is not a pitch. If you talk for 90 seconds straight about your client's team, the lead is gone. A good rule: the lead talks more than you do. Your job is to ask, listen, and guide toward the appointment."},{"t":"p","text":"**Count your conversations, not just your dials.** At the end of each hour, look at your numbers. If you made 25 dials and had zero conversations, check your timing (are you calling when people can answer?) and your list (are these numbers fresh?). If you had five conversations and zero appointments, the problem is the ask: listen to a recording and find where you stopped before asking."},{"t":"dodont","do":["Treat every answered call as a rare chance and be ready before you dial.","Follow the opener, discovery, appointment, confirmation structure.","Repeat names, times and addresses to confirm them."],"dont":["Talk more than the lead.","Speed up when you are nervous.","Measure your day only by dials."]}]},{"id":"I3-2","title":"Openers and scripts by lead source","minutes":7,"body":[{"t":"p","text":"The first 20 seconds depend on where the lead came from. A person who just asked about a home on a portal is in a very different mood from a person who clicked an ad last week or signed in at an open house. Use the opener that fits the source. Every opener includes the same four parts: **who you are, the recording notice, why you are calling, and permission.**"},{"t":"callout","title":"Always announce the recording","text":"Treat every state as requiring everyone's consent to record a call. Every opener includes a simple line like “Just so you know, this call may be recorded.” Never skip it, even on short calls."},{"t":"p","text":"**1. Portal inquiry (the lead asked about a specific home).** These are the warmest leads. They want information about one home, now. Name the home and ask for the showing early."},{"t":"script","title":"Portal inquiry","lines":[{"who":"You","text":"Hi, is this Jordan? This is Ana with the Rivera Home Team. Just so you know, this call may be recorded. You asked about the 3-bedroom on Maple Avenue a few minutes ago. Do you have two minutes?"},{"who":"Lead","text":"Yes, I wanted to know if it's still available."},{"who":"You","text":"I'll confirm that with Daniel, our agent, right now. Would you like to see it in person? He can do tomorrow at 4 or 6."},{"who":"Lead","text":"Maybe. What's it near?"},{"who":"You","text":"Good question. What's important for you to be close to, work or something else? (L)"}]},{"t":"p","text":"**2. Social media ad lead (the lead clicked an ad and filled a short form).** These leads are cooler. Many were scrolling and clicked out of curiosity. Lower the pressure, remind them what they asked for, and open with an easy question."},{"t":"script","title":"Ad lead","lines":[{"who":"You","text":"Hi Priya, this is Ana with the Rivera Home Team. This call may be recorded. You asked for our list of homes with pools on our social media ad. Is now an OK time?"},{"who":"Lead","text":"Oh, I just clicked to see pictures."},{"who":"You","text":"Completely get it! Most of our clients browse 6 months early. What neighborhood caught your eye?"}]},{"t":"p","text":"**3. Open-house sign-in (the lead visited a home and left their information).** They have already been inside a home with your client's team. Ask what they thought. Their opinion gives you Location and Motivation fast."},{"t":"script","title":"Open-house follow-up","lines":[{"who":"You","text":"Hi Sam, this is Ana with the Rivera Home Team. This call may be recorded. Thanks for stopping by the open house on Birch Street on Saturday. Do you have a minute?"},{"who":"Lead","text":"Sure."},{"who":"You","text":"What did you think of the home?"},{"who":"Lead","text":"Nice, but the kitchen was small."},{"who":"You","text":"That helps a lot. If Daniel found two or three homes like it with a bigger kitchen, would you want to see them this weekend? He has Saturday at 10 or 1."}]},{"t":"p","text":"**4. Sign call or inbound call (the lead calls your client's line).** The lead is calling you, so answer warmly and take control gently. Get their name and number first, in case the call drops."},{"t":"script","title":"Inbound call","lines":[{"who":"You","text":"Thank you for calling the Rivera Home Team, this is Ana. Just so you know, calls may be recorded. Who do I have the pleasure of speaking with?"},{"who":"Lead","text":"This is Luis. I'm calling about the sign on Elm Street."},{"who":"You","text":"Thanks, Luis. In case we get disconnected, what's the best number for you? ... Great. What made you call about Elm Street?"}]},{"t":"p","text":"**5. Past client or client's own database (people who already know your client).** These calls are about the relationship, not a hard ask. Only call these lists when your manager confirms they were scrubbed against the National Do Not Call Registry and your client's DNC list, and inside the window."},{"t":"script","title":"Past-client check-in","lines":[{"who":"You","text":"Hi Mrs. Carter, this is Ana, I work with Daniel at the Rivera Home Team. This call may be recorded. Daniel asked me to check in: how are you enjoying the house on Willow Court?"},{"who":"Lead","text":"We love it! Actually, my brother is thinking of moving here."},{"who":"You","text":"That's wonderful. Daniel would love to help him. Could you ask him if he would like a call from Daniel? If he says yes, send me his number and the best time to call."}]},{"t":"callout","title":"Your client's scripts and your state's rules come first","text":"These scripts are training examples. Your client may give you their own approved scripts; use those. Your scope depends on your client's state: in Texas, for example, an unlicensed assistant may not call to find out whether someone wants to buy, sell or lease. Your manager tells you exactly which calls you can make."},{"t":"dodont","do":["Name the home, ad or event the lead responded to in your first sentence.","Include the recording notice in every opener.","Get the caller's name and number early on inbound calls."],"dont":["Use a portal-lead opener on a cold ad lead.","Call a database or past-client list that has not been scrubbed.","Open with “How are you today?” and a long pause; get to the reason."]}]},{"id":"I3-3","title":"Discovery questions that open people up","minutes":6,"body":[{"t":"p","text":"Discovery is the part of the call where you learn about the lead: what they want, where, and why. Good discovery makes the lead feel understood. Bad discovery feels like a police interview. The difference is not the questions; it is how you ask and how you listen."},{"t":"p","text":"**Open questions, not closed ones.** A closed question gets yes or no. An open question gets a story, and stories carry information. Usually, open questions start with what, which, how, tell me, or walk me through."},{"t":"table","head":["Closed (weak)","Open (strong)"],"rows":[["“Do you like that house?”","“What did you like about that house?”"],["“Are you moving soon?”","“When would you ideally like to be in your new home?”"],["“Do you need 3 bedrooms?”","“How many bedrooms do you need, and how will you use them?”"],["“Is the commute important?”","“How far is too far from work for you?”"],["“Are you serious about buying?”","“What has you thinking about moving now?”"]]},{"t":"p","text":"**Go one level deeper.** The first answer is usually the surface. The real motivation is one or two questions below. Use short follow-ups: “Tell me more about that.” “What's driving that?” “What happens if you don't move by then?”"},{"t":"script","title":"Going deeper","lines":[{"who":"You","text":"What has you thinking about moving now?"},{"who":"Lead","text":"We just need more space."},{"who":"You","text":"Tell me more about that. What does “more space” look like for you?"},{"who":"Lead","text":"My mom is moving in with us in the spring, so we need a bedroom on the first floor."},{"who":"You","text":"That's really helpful. So a first-floor bedroom, and you'd want to be settled before spring. Is that right?"}]},{"t":"p","text":"Now you know the real need (a first-floor bedroom) and the real timeline (before spring). The agent can search much better with that. Note how you log it: “Needs a first-floor bedroom; wants to move before spring.” Log the home need, not the family details."},{"t":"p","text":"**Listen like it matters.** Listening is a skill you can practice:"},{"t":"list","items":["**Do not interrupt.** Let the lead finish, then wait one second before you speak. That small pause often makes them add something important.","**Reflect back.** Repeat the key point in a few words: “So the commute is the big one.” The lead feels heard, and you confirm you understood.","**React like a human.** “Oh, that's exciting.” “That sounds stressful.” Short and real.","**Take notes during the call,** in short words, so your Follow Up Boss note is accurate."]},{"t":"p","text":"**Your discovery question bank.** You do not need all of these on every call. Pick two or three, based on what the lead says."},{"t":"table","head":["Goal","Questions"],"rows":[["Location: area","“Which areas are you looking at?” “What do you like about that area?” “Are you open to nearby areas?”"],["Location: the home","“What does the home need to have?” “What would be nice to have?” “What would rule a home out?”"],["Location: budget","“What price range are you comfortable looking in?” (Never comment on whether it is enough.)"],["Motivation: why","“What has you thinking about moving now?” “What's not working in your current place?”"],["Motivation: timeline","“When would you ideally like to move?” “Is there a date you need to be out by, like a lease ending?”"],["Process","“Have you bought a home before?” “Have you seen any homes in person yet?” “Is anyone else part of the decision?”"]]},{"t":"callout","title":"Questions you never ask","text":"Never ask about race, color, national origin, religion, sex, disability or family status (for example: “Do you have kids?”, “Where are you from?”, “Are you married?”). If the lead shares these things, do not write them down. Ask about the home they need, not who they are. And do not ask detailed credit or income questions: module I4 teaches how financing is handled."},{"t":"p","text":"**Mistakes to avoid.** Asking leading questions that push an answer (“You'd want a bigger yard, right?”). Asking two questions at once (“What areas and what price and when?”): the lead answers only one. Filling every silence. And the biggest mistake: doing excellent discovery and then forgetting to ask for the appointment. Discovery is the road; the appointment is the destination."},{"t":"dodont","do":["Ask one open question at a time.","Follow up with “Tell me more about that.”","Reflect back the key point before you move on."],"dont":["Ask about family, origin, religion or other protected traits.","Comment on whether a budget is “enough.”","Stack three questions in one sentence."]}]},{"id":"I3-4","title":"Always ask for the appointment","minutes":6,"body":[{"t":"p","text":"This is the most important lesson in the module. **Ask for the appointment on every call.** Not only on the easy calls. Not only when the lead seems ready. Every call. Your QA score, your client's results and your own career depend on this one habit."},{"t":"p","text":"**Ask for the tour before you qualify.** Many new ISAs think they must learn everything before they can ask for a meeting. The opposite is true. When a lead asks about a specific home, ask for the showing in the first minute: “Would you like to see it tomorrow at 4 or 6?” Then you learn Location and Motivation while you set it up. If they say no, you have lost nothing; you continue discovery and ask again later."},{"t":"p","text":"**Two times, never “when works for you?”** An open question about time makes the lead think, and thinking often ends in “I'll let you know.” Two specific times make it easy: they just pick one. If neither works, they usually suggest a third. That is still a yes."},{"t":"table","head":["Weak ask","Strong ask"],"rows":[["“Let me know if you want to see it sometime.”","“Daniel can show it Thursday at 5 or Saturday at 11. Which is better?”"],["“When are you free?”","“Is a weekday evening or Saturday morning easier for you?” then “Saturday at 10 or 11?”"],["“Do you maybe want to talk to an agent?”","“The best next step is a 15-minute call with Daniel. Tomorrow at 12 or 6?”"]]},{"t":"p","text":"**Say the word “appointment.”** Use the word clearly so the lead knows it is a real plan, not a maybe: “I'll book that appointment with Daniel for Saturday at 11.” Leads show up to appointments; they forget “maybes.”"},{"t":"p","text":"**Give a reason to meet.** People agree to meetings that help them. Connect the appointment to what they told you in discovery: “Since your lease ends in 45 days, seeing a few homes this weekend gives you time to choose.” “Since the monthly cost is your big question, Daniel can walk you through it at the meeting.”"},{"t":"p","text":"**Types of appointments you can set** (your client tells you which ones they want):"},{"t":"list","items":["**Showing or tour:** the lead sees one or more homes with the agent.","**Buyer consultation:** a meeting at the office or on video to plan the search.","**Phone call with the agent:** 10 to 15 minutes, for leads not ready to meet.","**Listing consultation:** for sellers (module I6)."]},{"t":"p","text":"**When the lead says “Just send me info.”** Do not argue, and do not just send it and disappear. Say yes, then ask for the appointment in the same breath:"},{"t":"script","title":"“Just send me info”","lines":[{"who":"Lead","text":"Just send me info."},{"who":"You","text":"I will email that right now! While I pull it up, are you free Saturday morning or afternoon to take a quick look inside?"}]},{"t":"p","text":"The pattern for any soft “no” is: acknowledge, pivot to a Location or Motivation question, then ask for the appointment again. Module I5 teaches objections in depth. For now, remember: one soft no is not the end. Two clear no's mean you set a follow-up instead: “No problem. Can I check in on Thursday?”"},{"t":"p","text":"**Lock it in before you hang up.** When the lead says yes, confirm every detail out loud:"},{"t":"steps","items":["Repeat the day, date, time and place (and the time zone if the lead and agent are in different ones).","Confirm the best phone number for reminders and whether they agreed to texts.","Ask who is coming and if anything is important for the agent to know.","Tell them what happens next: “You'll get a text confirmation with Daniel's details now, and a reminder before the appointment.”","For any tour, mention that Daniel will send a short buyer agreement to review before the showing. He explains it; you never do."]},{"t":"callout","title":"Right after the call","text":"Put the appointment in the agent's calendar before you do anything else, with the Follow Up Boss lead link in the invite and the buyer-agreement flag for tours. Set 24-hour and 1-hour reminders and change the stage to Pending Handoff. Module I8 covers the calendar and the 3-way handoff in full."},{"t":"dodont","do":["Ask for the appointment on every call, early.","Offer two specific times and use the word “appointment.”","Confirm every detail out loud before you hang up."],"dont":["Ask “When works for you?”","Give up after one soft no.","End the call without a booked appointment or a scheduled follow-up."]}]},{"id":"I3-5","title":"The power hour and the night-before confirmation","minutes":5,"body":[{"t":"p","text":"Great conversations come from great habits. The best ISAs do not “call when they have time.” They protect focused calling blocks every day, at the same time, and they confirm every appointment so it actually happens. This lesson gives you both habits."},{"t":"p","text":"**The power hour.** A power hour is a protected block of focused calling: no email, no social media, no notes beyond quick call logs. Choose a time when leads in your client's time zones are most likely to answer, often late morning or late afternoon on weekdays, before the 6 PM evening rush. Same time every day, so it becomes automatic."},{"t":"p","text":"**Work your lists in this order, every shift:**"},{"t":"steps","items":["**New Speed-to-Lead** (0 to 15 minutes old). These always come first, even in the middle of a power hour.","**Hot Active** (ready within 30 days).","**Scheduled Follow-Up Tasks** (due today).","**30 to 60 Day Nurtures** (check-ins)."]},{"t":"p","text":"Work each list top to bottom. Do not skip a lead because the name or the notes look “hard.” The difficult-looking lead is often the one who books. A.L.M. on every call; an appointment asked for on every call."},{"t":"table","head":["Part of the power hour","Minutes","What you do"],"rows":[["Prepare","5","Open your Smart Lists, check time zones and state rules, have scripts and calendar ready"],["Call block","45","Dial top to bottom; log each call in a few words right away"],["Clean up","10","Complete notes, set tasks, send confirmation texts, update stages"]]},{"t":"p","text":"**Between calls: log fast, then dial.** After each call, write the disposition and the key A.L.M. facts in short form. Set the next task. Then dial the next lead. Full, polished notes happen in the clean-up minutes, but never leave a call with nothing logged: you will forget details within the hour."},{"t":"p","text":"**Confirm the night before.** Many no-shows are appointments that nobody confirmed. People forget, get busy, or feel shy about cancelling. A short confirmation the day before fixes many of them. Send it inside the calling window, to leads who agreed to texts, in addition to the automatic 24-hour and 1-hour reminders."},{"t":"script","title":"Night-before confirmation text","lines":[{"who":"You","text":"Hi Jordan, Ana with the Rivera Home Team. Just confirming your appointment with Daniel tomorrow, Saturday, at 11:00 AM at 42 Maple Ave. Here's a photo of the home. Reply YES to confirm, or let me know if you need a different time."},{"who":"Lead","text":"YES, see you then!"},{"who":"You","text":"Perfect, Daniel will see you there. His number is 555-0177 if you need him tomorrow."}]},{"t":"p","text":"Include the address and a photo of the home (your client approves which photos and links you can send). The photo reminds the lead why they said yes. Ask for a simple reply, like YES, so you know they saw it."},{"t":"p","text":"**When the lead wants to reschedule.** That is good news: they still want to meet. Offer two new times right away and update the calendar, the reminders and the note. Tell the agent in the client channel. Never let a reschedule become “I'll let you know.”"},{"t":"p","text":"**When there is no reply.** If the lead does not answer the confirmation by midday, call them inside the window. If you still cannot reach them, tell the agent before the appointment so they can decide whether to go. After a no-show, the lead goes back to your follow-up plan with a friendly message: “Sorry we missed you! Want to pick a new time?”"},{"t":"callout","title":"Opt-outs still apply","text":"If the lead replies STOP or asks not to be contacted, cancel the reminders, tag DNC, move the stage to Archived / DNC, pause all automations, log it, and tell the agent."},{"t":"dodont","do":["Protect a power hour at the same time every day.","Work Smart Lists in order, top to bottom.","Confirm every appointment the day before, with the address and a photo."],"dont":["Check email or social media during the power hour.","Skip leads that look difficult.","Assume the automatic reminders are enough."]}]}],"practice":{"title":"Scripted drills, four recorded source calls and a shadowed live call","minutes":120,"intro":"This practice turns the scripts into habits. You will drill openers out loud, record four full role-plays (one per lead source), plan a power hour, and shadow a live call with an experienced ISA or your coach. Use fictional leads for all role-plays; when shadowing, only listen and take notes, and keep everything you hear confidential.","steps":["Opener drill (15 minutes): say each of the five openers from lesson I3-2 out loud 5 times, with the recording notice, until you can say them without reading. Time each one: it should take 20 seconds or less.","Discovery drill (15 minutes): rewrite 10 closed questions from your coach into open questions. Then, with a partner, practice “going one level deeper” on three surface answers (“We need more space,” “We want a good area,” “We're just looking”).","Ask drill (10 minutes): your partner says 10 different things a lead might say. After each one, ask for the appointment with two specific times. Your partner counts how many asks used two times and the word “appointment.”","Recorded role-plays (40 minutes): record four full calls with a partner playing the lead: a portal inquiry, an ad lead who “just clicked to see pictures,” an open-house visitor, and an inbound sign call. Each must include the recording notice, at least two open discovery questions, an appointment ask with two times, and a full confirmation.","After each role-play, write the A.L.M. note you would log and the night-before confirmation text you would send.","Power hour plan (10 minutes): write your power hour for your future client's main time zone: the start time (in your time and the lead's time), the list order, and your preparation checklist.","Shadow a live call (30 minutes): sit in on at least 30 minutes of live calling by an experienced ISA or your coach. Write down three openers you liked, two questions that opened a lead up, and every appointment ask you heard (word for word if you can)."],"deliverable":"Send your coach (in your BeyondVAs training channel): the four role-play recordings with their A.L.M. notes and confirmation texts, your 10 rewritten open questions, your power hour plan, and your shadowing notes. Your coach scores each recording on the opener, discovery and the appointment ask."},"quiz":[{"q":"What counts as a “conversation” in your daily numbers?","options":["Any dial you make","A voicemail you leave","A live talk with a lead that lasts two minutes or more","A text reply from a lead"],"answer":2,"explain":"Dials are the input you control; a conversation is a live talk of two minutes or more, and conversations are what move appointments."},{"q":"Which four parts does every opener include?","options":["Who you are, the recording notice, why you are calling, and permission","The listing price, the agent's experience, your name and a joke","Your name, a question about their credit, the address and a goodbye","The team's awards, your name, a discount and the appointment"],"answer":0,"explain":"Name and team, “this call may be recorded,” the reason (the home, ad or event), and “Do you have two minutes?”"},{"q":"A lead from a social media ad says, “I just clicked to see pictures.” What is the best reply?","options":["“Then why did you give us your number?”","“OK, sorry to bother you,” and hang up","“Can my agent meet you there today at 4 PM?”","“Completely get it! Most of our clients browse 6 months early. What neighborhood caught your eye?”"],"answer":3,"explain":"Ad leads are cooler. Lower the pressure, acknowledge, and pivot to an easy Location question before you work toward the appointment."},{"q":"Which question is an open discovery question?","options":["“Do you need three bedrooms?”","“What has you thinking about moving now?”","“Are you serious about buying?”","“You'd want a bigger yard, right?”"],"answer":1,"explain":"Open questions cannot be answered with yes or no and invite a story. “You'd want a bigger yard, right?” is a leading question."},{"q":"A lead says, “We just need more space.” What is the best next step?","options":["“Tell me more about that. What does more space look like for you?”","“How many kids do you have?”","“OK. Next question: what's your credit score?”","Write “needs space” and end the call"],"answer":0,"explain":"Go one level deeper to find the real need. Never ask about family status; ask about the home they need."},{"q":"Which appointment ask is strongest?","options":["“When works for you?”","“Let me know if you ever want to see it.”","“Daniel can show it Thursday at 5 or Saturday at 11. Which is better?”","“Do you maybe want to talk to someone?”"],"answer":2,"explain":"Two specific times make it easy to say yes. Open questions about time usually end in “I'll let you know.”"},{"q":"A lead asks about a specific home on a portal. When should you first ask for the showing?","options":["Only after you have learned everything about their finances","Early, often in the first minute: ask for the tour before you qualify","Never; let the agent ask","At the end of a second call next week"],"answer":1,"explain":"Ask for the tour before you qualify. You learn Location and Motivation while you set it up, and a no costs nothing."},{"q":"In what order do you work your Smart Lists during a power hour?","options":["30-60 Day Nurtures, Hot Active, New Speed-to-Lead, Scheduled Follow-Up Tasks","Scheduled Follow-Up Tasks, New Speed-to-Lead, Hot Active, 30-60 Day Nurtures","Hot Active, 30-60 Day Nurtures, Scheduled Follow-Up Tasks, New Speed-to-Lead","New Speed-to-Lead, Hot Active, Scheduled Follow-Up Tasks, 30-60 Day Nurtures"],"answer":3,"explain":"New leads first (0-15 minutes old), then Hot Active (ready within 30 days), then tasks due today, then 30-60 day nurtures."},{"q":"Why do you send a confirmation text the day before an appointment?","options":["Because many no-shows are appointments nobody confirmed","To remind the lead about the price","To send the buyer agreement yourself","It is optional and rarely helps"],"answer":0,"explain":"A text the day before, with the address and a photo, inside the window and to leads who agreed to texts, prevents many no-shows."},{"q":"The lead agrees to a tour on Saturday at 11 AM. Which step is NOT part of locking it in?","options":["Repeat the day, time and address out loud","Tell them the agent will send a buyer agreement to review before the showing","Explain the buyer agreement's terms and ask them to sign it with you","Confirm the best number for reminders"],"answer":2,"explain":"The agent sends and explains the buyer agreement; you never do. You only mention it and flag it in the calendar invite."}]},{"id":"I4","part":"isa","title":"The financing conversation","summary":"Ask about financing in twenty seconds once the appointment is set, log what you learn, and leave every loan, rate and credit question to the lender and every lender choice to the buyer.","objectives":["Place the 20-second financing conversation at the right moment: after the appointment is set, never before you have shown you care.","Tell the difference between a pre-qualification and a verified pre-approval, and ask what was verified without judging the answer.","Answer money questions (rates, credit, down payment, “should we wait?”) with a clear, friendly redirect that gives no loan, rate or credit advice.","Offer a lender introduction only when your agent says so, always as one option, and say out loud that the buyer chooses their lender.","Log financing status in a Follow Up Boss note so the agent never has to ask the same money question twice."],"lessons":[{"id":"I4-1","title":"Why money comes up, and when to bring it up","minutes":7,"body":[{"t":"p","text":"Almost every buyer who calls your client has one quiet question in their head: **“Can we actually afford this?”** Some say it out loud. Most do not. They ask about a house, a neighborhood or a tour, but the money question sits behind every answer they give you. A good ISA knows this question is there, respects it, and makes sure the buyer gets to the right person to answer it. That person is never you. It is a lender."},{"t":"p","text":"Financing matters to your client for a simple reason. In many US markets, sellers do not take an offer seriously unless the buyer shows a letter from a lender, or proof that they have the cash. A buyer who falls in love with a home and has not talked to a lender can lose it to another buyer who is ready. That is painful for the buyer and wasted time for the agent. Your job is to make sure financing is **on the buyer’s radar early**, gently, so the agent can help them be ready."},{"t":"callout","title":"The order matters","text":"**Care first, appointment second, money third.** Ask for the appointment before you qualify. Bring up financing only after the appointment is set, in about twenty seconds, and never as a test the buyer must pass to get help."},{"t":"p","text":"Why this order? Think about the buyer from module I1: 11:14 PM, on the couch, rent just went up, three years of saving and still not sure it is enough. If the first thing they hear from you is “Are you pre-approved? We cannot show homes without a pre-approval letter,” they feel judged. Many first-time buyers freeze, say “we’re just looking,” and never answer the phone again. The same buyer, asked about a lender **after** they have a tour booked and feel looked after, usually answers openly."},{"t":"dodont","do":["Set the appointment first, then ask the financing question in one or two sentences.","Treat every buyer the same at every price point: same questions, same tone, same respect.","Explain why the agent cares: so the offer is ready the day they find the right home.","Write down exactly what they told you, in their words, in your note."],"dont":["Open the call with “Are you pre-approved?”","Make the tour depend on a pre-approval letter unless your agent has told you that rule for that client.","Guess what someone can afford from their job, accent, address, name or how they sound.","Say anything that sounds like a promise: “You’ll qualify, no problem.”"]},{"t":"p","text":"There is one exception to “appointment first.” Sometimes the buyer brings up money themselves in the first minute: “Honestly, I don’t know if we can even afford anything.” When that happens, do not ignore it and do not answer it. Acknowledge it, tell them that is exactly what a short meeting with the agent (and, if they want, a lender) is for, and then ask for the appointment. You will practice this in lesson I4-5."},{"t":"p","text":"Here is how the money question fits inside A.L.M., the framework you use on every call. **Appointment** is still the goal. **Location** is where they want to live. **Motivation** is why they are moving and when. Financing is not a fourth letter. It is a detail you log under Motivation and Next Step, because how ready they are with money tells the agent how close the timeline really is. A buyer who says “we want to move in 60 days” and “we haven’t talked to anyone about a loan yet” has a real goal and a real gap. The agent needs to know both."},{"t":"table","head":["What the buyer says","What it tells the agent","Where you log it"],"rows":[["“We already have a pre-approval from our credit union.”","They may be ready to write an offer soon.","M and Next Step: “Pre-approved with own lender (credit union), per lead. Agent to confirm details.”"],["“We talked to someone online, they said we’re good.”","Maybe a pre-qualification only. Needs checking by the agent.","M: “Spoke with an online lender, unsure if pre-approval or pre-qual.”"],["“Not yet, we wanted to see houses first.”","Normal. The agent will start the lender conversation.","Next Step: “No lender yet. Agent to discuss financing at the tour.”"],["“We’re paying cash.”","Seller will want proof of funds. The agent handles it.","M: “Says cash buyer. Agent to request proof of funds.”"]]},{"t":"p","text":"Notice the last column. You never write your opinion (“probably can’t afford it”), and you never write anything about credit you were not told. You write facts and the source of the fact (“per lead”). Your agent will read your note before the appointment and walk in prepared, which is the whole point of your work."},{"t":"callout","title":"Remember","text":"You are not the lender and not the agent. You are the person who makes sure the buyer feels cared for, the appointment is on the calendar, and the agent knows where the buyer stands with money before they meet."}]},{"id":"I4-2","title":"The 20-second financing conversation","minutes":7,"body":[{"t":"p","text":"The financing conversation is short on purpose. It takes about twenty seconds, it happens right after the appointment is set, and it ends with a note, not a lecture. Here is the core line, which you should know by heart:"},{"t":"callout","title":"The core line","text":"“Have you talked to a lender yet? Many buyers get a verified pre-approval before touring, so the offer is ready the day they find the one.”"},{"t":"p","text":"Look at what this sentence does. It asks a yes or no question that is easy to answer. It explains **why** in terms of the buyer’s own goal (getting the home they love). And it uses the word “many,” which tells the buyer this is normal, not a test. It does not ask about credit score, income, savings or debt. Those are lender questions."},{"t":"script","title":"A full example: Jasmine, a portal lead","lines":[{"who":"You","text":"Perfect, Jasmine. I have you down with Daniel, our agent, on Saturday at 11 AM at the house on Birchwood. I’ll text you the confirmation in a minute."},{"who":"Lead","text":"Great, thank you!"},{"who":"You","text":"One quick question so Daniel can prepare. Have you talked to a lender yet? Many buyers get a verified pre-approval before touring, so the offer is ready the day they find the one."},{"who":"Lead","text":"Not yet. We weren’t sure when to do that."},{"who":"You","text":"That’s very common, and it’s a great question for Daniel on Saturday. He can explain how it works and what your options are. Is there anything else you’d like him to know before you meet?"},{"who":"Lead","text":"Just that we want to stay under about $2,000 a month."},{"who":"You","text":"Got it, I’ll write that down for him. You’ll get my text in a minute, and a reminder the day before. See you Saturday!"}]},{"t":"p","text":"Look at what you did **not** do. You did not tell Jasmine what house price $2,000 a month buys. You did not tell her what rate to expect. You did not tell her which lender to use. You logged the number she gave you and passed the question to the agent. Monthly budget is useful for the agent to know, and it is her information to share, so it goes in the note in her words."},{"t":"p","text":"There are four common answers to the core line. Each one has a simple follow-up and a simple note."},{"t":"table","head":["Answer","Your follow-up (one line)","Note"],"rows":[["“Yes, we’re pre-approved.”","“Great. Who is it with, so Daniel knows?” (Name of the lender or bank only.)","“Pre-approved with [lender name], per lead.”"],["“We got pre-qualified online.”","“Good start. Daniel can tell you if anything else is needed before you make an offer.”","“Pre-qualified online with [name], per lead. Agent to review.”"],["“No, not yet.”","“Totally normal. Daniel will walk you through it on Saturday.”","“No lender yet. Agent to discuss financing.” (Plus a lender introduction only if the agent has told you to offer one: lesson I4-4.)"],["“We’re paying cash.”","“Perfect, Daniel will explain what the seller will want to see.”","“Cash buyer, per lead. Agent to request proof of funds.”"]]},{"t":"p","text":"Keep your follow-up to one line. If the buyer starts asking more questions about money, that is a sign they are worried, and worry needs a person who can actually answer. You say: “Those are exactly the right questions. Daniel and a lender can answer them properly. I’ll put them in my note so they’re covered on Saturday.” Then write the questions in the note."},{"t":"callout","title":"Text version","text":"When the appointment was set by text, the financing question can go by text too, the same day, inside the calling window, and only to a lead who agreed to texts: “Hi Jasmine, it’s Ana with Daniel’s team. You’re all set for Sat 11 AM. Quick question so Daniel can prepare: have you talked to a lender yet? No problem if not.”"},{"t":"p","text":"Here is a common mistake. A new ISA, nervous about “qualifying,” asks five money questions in a row: Are you pre-approved? How much do you have for a down payment? What’s your credit like? How much do you make? Do you have debt? The buyer feels interrogated, and you have collected information you are not trained to use, which creates privacy risk for your client. **One question, one follow-up, one note.** That is the whole conversation."},{"t":"p","text":"Another common mistake is skipping the question because it feels awkward. Then the agent arrives at the tour and learns the buyer has never talked to a lender. The tour still happens, but if the buyer loves the home, they are not ready to act. Twenty seconds on your call saves the agent days."},{"t":"steps","items":["Set the appointment and confirm the date, time and place.","Say the core line, word for word, in a calm voice.","Listen to the answer and give the one-line follow-up from the table.","If they ask more money questions, write them down and promise the agent will answer them.","Log the financing status in your A.L.M. note before you dial the next lead."]}]},{"id":"I4-3","title":"The words of financing, and what “verified” means","minutes":7,"body":[{"t":"p","text":"You do not give financing advice, but you need to understand financing words. Buyers and agents use them, and your notes must use them correctly. If a buyer says “pre-qual” and you write “pre-approved,” you just gave your agent wrong information. This lesson gives you the vocabulary and one important idea: **a pre-approval is a boarding pass, not takeoff.**"},{"t":"glossary","terms":[{"term":"Lender / loan officer","def":"The bank, credit union or mortgage company that lends the money, and the person there who works with the buyer. Only they can talk about rates, programs and whether someone qualifies."},{"term":"Mortgage","def":"The loan used to buy a home. The home is the guarantee for the loan."},{"term":"Pre-qualification (pre-qual)","def":"A quick estimate, often online, based on what the buyer says about income and debt. Usually nothing is checked. Useful as a first step, weak in front of a seller."},{"term":"Pre-approval","def":"A lender has reviewed documents (for example pay stubs, bank statements, tax returns) and usually checked credit, and says in a letter how much they are willing to lend, with conditions. Much stronger than a pre-qual."},{"term":"Pre-approval letter","def":"The document the buyer’s agent sends with an offer to show the seller the buyer can likely get the loan."},{"term":"Down payment","def":"The part of the price the buyer pays from their own money. The rest is the loan."},{"term":"Closing costs","def":"Fees paid when the purchase is completed (lender fees, title, taxes and more). Separate from the down payment."},{"term":"Interest rate","def":"The cost of borrowing, as a yearly percentage. It changes with the market and the buyer’s situation. You never quote or predict it."},{"term":"Monthly payment","def":"What the buyer pays each month: usually loan payment plus property taxes and insurance, sometimes HOA fees and mortgage insurance."},{"term":"Credit score","def":"A number that shows how a person has handled debt. Lenders use it. You never ask for it or comment on it."},{"term":"Debt-to-income (DTI)","def":"How much of a buyer’s monthly income goes to debt payments. A lender word, not an ISA word."},{"term":"Conventional, FHA, VA, USDA loans","def":"Common US loan types with different rules. Buyers may mention them. Log what they say; the lender explains which fits."},{"term":"Cash buyer","def":"A buyer who will not use a loan. The seller will usually ask for proof of funds."},{"term":"Proof of funds","def":"A bank statement or letter showing the buyer has the money for a cash purchase or down payment. The agent requests it. You never collect it."},{"term":"Underwriting","def":"The lender’s final, detailed review after an offer is accepted. A pre-approval can still fail here if something changes."}]},{"t":"p","text":"Now the boarding pass idea. A boarding pass lets you walk to the gate. It does not mean the plane has taken off. A pre-approval is the same. It is a strong sign, but the loan is not final until underwriting is done. If the buyer changes jobs, buys a car on credit, or the documents were never really checked, the loan can fall apart weeks later."},{"t":"callout","title":"Ask what was verified, without judging","text":"When a buyer says they are pre-approved, you can ask one neutral question: “Did the lender review your documents, or was it a quick online estimate?” Then write the answer. You are not checking if they are good enough. You are giving the agent accurate information."},{"t":"script","title":"Example: Marco says he is pre-approved","lines":[{"who":"You","text":"Have you talked to a lender yet? Many buyers get a verified pre-approval before touring, so the offer is ready the day they find the one."},{"who":"Lead","text":"Yeah, we’re pre-approved."},{"who":"You","text":"Great. Who is it with, so the agent knows?"},{"who":"Lead","text":"One of those apps. I filled it out last week."},{"who":"You","text":"Got it. Did they review your documents, like pay stubs or bank statements, or was it a quick online estimate?"},{"who":"Lead","text":"Just the form, I think. Nobody asked for documents."},{"who":"You","text":"Thanks, that helps. Rachel will look at it with you on Thursday and tell you if anything else is needed before an offer."}]},{"t":"p","text":"Your note says: “Lead says pre-approved through an online app last week. No documents reviewed, per lead. Likely a pre-qual. Agent to review.” That is accurate, it is not insulting, and it gives Rachel exactly what she needs. Notice the word “likely” and the phrase “per lead.” You report what you heard and you mark your guess as a guess."},{"t":"dodont","do":["Use “pre-qualified” and “pre-approved” exactly as the buyer described their situation.","Write the lender’s name if the buyer offers it.","Write “per lead” when the information comes only from the buyer.","Pass every “what does this word mean?” question to the agent or lender, with a short, simple definition if you are sure of it."],"dont":["Ask for a credit score, income, savings amount or Social Security number. Ever.","Accept documents (bank statements, letters) by text or email. If a buyer sends one, tell your manager and follow their data rules.","Explain loan programs, rates or which loan is “better.”","Say “You’re pre-approved, so you’re good to go.” Only the lender can say that."]},{"t":"p","text":"One more word: a buyer may mention an ITIN, being self-employed, receiving income in cash, or a recent job change. These are important details for a lender, and they are nobody’s business to judge. Log them only if the buyer brings them up, in neutral words, and let the agent decide what to do. Never suggest that someone “won’t qualify.” You do not know, and saying it could also be unfair treatment."}]},{"id":"I4-4","title":"Always the buyer’s choice of lender","minutes":7,"body":[{"t":"p","text":"Many US real estate teams work with one or more lenders they trust. Your client may call this their “preferred lender.” A good preferred lender answers fast, explains clearly and closes on time, which helps buyers. But there is one rule that never changes: **the buyer always chooses their own lender.** You say it out loud, every time a lender introduction comes up."},{"t":"callout","title":"House rule","text":"Never give loan, rate or credit advice. Connect a buyer with your client’s preferred lender **only when your agent says so**, and always as one option: “You can use any lender you like.”"},{"t":"p","text":"Why so strict? First, it is right: the buyer is borrowing a lot of money and deserves to compare. Second, it protects your client. US federal rules on real estate settlement services (often called RESPA) forbid paying or receiving anything of value for referring a buyer to a lender, and a buyer who feels forced into a lender can file a complaint against the agent. Your manager confirms exactly how these rules apply to your client. Third, it builds trust. Buyers who feel pressured stop answering. Buyers who feel free often choose the agent’s lender anyway, because the introduction was helpful and respectful."},{"t":"p","text":"Before you ever offer an introduction, you need three things from your agent or manager, in writing in the client channel: (1) whether you should offer a lender introduction at all, (2) the lender’s name and how to introduce them (a warm three-way call, a text introduction, or the agent does it), and (3) when (for example, “only after the appointment is set” or “only when the buyer asks for one”). If you do not have these instructions, you do not offer a lender. You log the status and the agent handles it."},{"t":"script","title":"Offering an introduction (only when your agent has told you to)","lines":[{"who":"You","text":"Have you talked to a lender yet? Many buyers get a verified pre-approval before touring, so the offer is ready the day they find the one."},{"who":"Lead","text":"No, we don’t really know where to start."},{"who":"You","text":"That’s very normal. Daniel works with a loan officer who explains things clearly, and I can set up a short call so you have an option to compare. You can use any lender you like. Would that be helpful?"},{"who":"Lead","text":"Sure, I guess that would help."},{"who":"You","text":"Great. Is tomorrow at 12 or at 5 better for a 15-minute call?"}]},{"t":"p","text":"Notice the words “an option to compare” and “You can use any lender you like.” Those two phrases are what make this an introduction and not pressure. Also notice that you did not describe rates, fees or programs. You offered a conversation with a person who can."},{"t":"script","title":"A common question: “Do we have to use your lender?”","lines":[{"who":"Lead","text":"My cousin said we HAVE to use your team’s lender?"},{"who":"You","text":"Great question, and no: you always choose your lender. If it helps, I can introduce you to a loan officer Daniel works with, so you have an option to compare with whoever you’re talking to now. The choice is completely yours."}]},{"t":"table","head":["Wrong reply","Why it is wrong"],"rows":[["“Yes, with us it’s our lender or nothing.”","Never true, and it creates legal risk for your client. Always the buyer’s choice."],["“Lenders are all the same, go with whoever.”","Not helpful, and it is advice. Lenders differ. Offer the option to compare (if your agent allows) and let them choose."],["“Our lender has the best rates.”","A rate claim you cannot know or promise. Rates are lender talk."]]},{"t":"p","text":"Some buyers already have a lender, often a family bank or a credit union. Celebrate that: “Great, that’s one less thing to do.” Log the name. Do not suggest switching. If your agent wants a comparison offered to buyers who already have a lender, they will tell you exactly how to say it."},{"t":"p","text":"Some buyers prefer to speak Spanish with their lender. You may be the first person on the team who notices. Do not promise a Spanish-speaking lender. Write it in the note (“Prefers Spanish for financing conversations”) and ask your agent in the client channel whether there is an option to offer. Never ask about immigration status, ever."},{"t":"steps","items":["Check the client channel for your agent’s written lender instructions. No instructions, no introduction.","Ask the core financing line after the appointment is set.","If the buyer has a lender, log the name and stop there.","If not, and your agent has asked you to, offer the introduction as one option and say “You can use any lender you like.”","Book the lender call with two time options, or do the warm transfer the way your agent described.","Log it: “Offered intro to [lender] as one option; lead chose to [accept/decline]. Buyer chooses lender.”"]},{"t":"callout","title":"Never","text":"Never tell a buyer they must use a lender to see homes, get a better deal or work with your client. Never mention money, gifts or credits from a lender. If a buyer asks “Do you get something for sending me there?”, say: “That’s a great question for Daniel. I just schedule the call, and the choice of lender is always yours.” Then tell your agent the buyer asked."}]},{"id":"I4-5","title":"Money questions you must not answer, and what to say instead","minutes":7,"body":[{"t":"p","text":"Buyers trust a friendly voice. Once you have helped them, they will ask you things like “What rate can I get?” or “Is 600 good enough credit?” It feels rude not to answer. But any answer you give is advice you are not licensed or trained to give, and a wrong answer can cost a family thousands of dollars or a home. The kind thing, and the professional thing, is a warm redirect: **acknowledge the question, explain who answers it, and keep the appointment moving.**"},{"t":"callout","title":"The redirect pattern","text":"1) “That’s a really good question.” 2) “The person who can answer it properly is [the agent / a lender], because it depends on your situation.” 3) “I’ll put it in my note so it’s covered at [the appointment / the lender call].”"},{"t":"table","head":["Buyer asks","Never say","Say instead"],"rows":[["“What interest rate can we get?”","“Around 6 percent right now.”","“Rates depend on each person’s situation and change often, so only a lender can quote one. I’ll note it so Daniel can talk about next steps on Saturday.”"],["“My credit is around 580. Can I still buy?”","“Yes, FHA allows that.” / “Probably not.”","“Thanks for telling me. A lender is the right person to look at that with you. I won’t write your score down, but I’ll let Daniel know you have financing questions.”"],["“How much should we put down?”","“Always 20 percent.”","“That really depends on your plans and the loan. A lender can show you the options side by side.”"],["“How much house can we afford with $85,000 a year?”","“About $350,000.”","“That’s exactly what a pre-approval tells you, based on your real numbers. Daniel can explain how to get one.”"],["“Should we wait for rates to drop?”","“Rates will drop soon.” / “Buy now, they’ll go up.”","“Nobody can promise what rates will do. What would help you decide? Daniel can show you real numbers on Saturday, and then you choose.”"],["“Is it better to rent or buy?”","“Buying is always better.”","“That’s a big personal decision. The meeting with Daniel is a good place to look at it with real numbers, no pressure.”"],["“What will closing costs be?”","“About 3 percent.”","“That depends on the home and the loan. Your agent and lender will give you real estimates.”"],["“Can I use my tax refund or a gift from my parents?”","“Sure, that works.”","“Good question for a lender. Every loan has its own rules about where money comes from.”"]]},{"t":"p","text":"Look at the “Should we wait for rates to drop?” row carefully. It is the most tempting one. A prediction about rates is not a fact, and it is pressure. Nobody knows what rates will do, even after reading the news. When you say “Prices will only go up, buy now,” you are pushing, and the buyer feels it. When you ask what would help them decide and offer real numbers from a qualified person, you are guiding."},{"t":"script","title":"When money worry comes first: Maria","lines":[{"who":"Lead","text":"Honestly, I’m not sure we can afford anything right now. Is it even worth talking?"},{"who":"You","text":"I’m really glad you said that, Maria. Can I ask what’s making it feel hard right now?"},{"who":"Lead","text":"Rent went up again. We’ve been saving, but every house we see online looks so expensive."},{"who":"You","text":"That makes sense. A lot of the families we talk to feel the same way at the start. That’s exactly what a short, no-pressure meeting with Daniel is for: he can look at what you’re hoping for and help you see what’s realistic, and if you want, connect you with someone who can check the numbers. Would Tuesday at 6 or Thursday at 6 be easier?"},{"who":"Lead","text":"Thursday, I think."},{"who":"You","text":"Perfect, Thursday at 6. I’ll text you the details."}]},{"t":"p","text":"You never told Maria she could afford a house, and you never told her she couldn’t. You asked what was making it hard (Motivation), you normalized her feeling, and you offered the appointment as the place to get real answers. Your note: “Lead worried about affordability; rent increased; has been saving. No lender yet. Wants guidance on what’s realistic. Appt Thu 6 PM.”"},{"t":"p","text":"Fair housing and fair lending rules also apply to money. Never assume someone can or cannot afford a home because of their job, their family, their accent, the language they prefer, where they live now or how they sound. Never treat a lower price range as less important. Every inquiry gets the same speed, the same questions and the same respect. A buyer looking at a $180,000 condo deserves the same 20-second financing conversation as a buyer looking at a $1.2 million house."},{"t":"dodont","do":["Say “I don’t know, but the right person does, and I’ll make sure they cover it.”","Write the buyer’s money questions in the note, so the agent answers every one.","Stay warm. A redirect is help, not a rejection."],"dont":["Search the internet for a rate and read it to the buyer.","Share your own experience with loans (“When I bought my house...”).","Repeat a buyer’s credit score or income in a text, email or group chat."]},{"t":"callout","title":"When a buyer pushes","text":"Some buyers will say “Just give me a ballpark.” Stay friendly and firm: “I wish I could, but I’d be guessing, and a guess could steer you wrong with something this important. A lender can give you a real number in one short call.” Then ask for the time."}]}],"practice":{"title":"Financing role-plays and A.L.M. notes","minutes":75,"intro":"You will practice the 20-second financing conversation and the money redirects out loud until they sound natural, then write the notes your agent would read before the appointment. Work with your practice partner or coach playing the buyer. Record every role-play with your partner’s permission (screen recording or phone audio is fine) and use only fictional names and 555 phone numbers.","steps":["Warm-up (10 min): read the core line out loud 10 times until you can say it without looking: “Have you talked to a lender yet? Many buyers get a verified pre-approval before touring, so the offer is ready the day they find the one.” Record your tenth try.","Role-play 1 (5 min): “Jasmine” has just booked a Saturday tour and is not pre-approved. Set the appointment confirmation, ask the core line, give the one-line follow-up and close.","Role-play 2 (5 min): “Marco” says he is pre-approved through an app. Ask who it is with and whether documents were reviewed, without judging.","Role-play 3 (5 min): your partner, as the buyer, asks three money questions in a row: rate, credit score of 590, and how much to put down. Redirect each one with the three-part pattern and keep the appointment.","Role-play 4 (5 min): “My cousin said we HAVE to use your team’s lender?” Answer it, offer an introduction as one option, and say “You can use any lender you like.”","Role-play 5 (5 min): “Maria” opens with “I’m not sure we can afford anything.” Ask what makes it feel hard, normalize, and ask for the appointment with two times.","Role-play 6 (5 min): a cash buyer who is flying in on Friday. Confirm the appointment and log the cash detail correctly.","Notes (20 min): write a complete Follow Up Boss style note for each of the six role-plays using Date/Time, Call/Text Disposition, A, L, M, Next Step. Use “per lead” where the fact came only from the buyer. Never include a credit score or income figure.","Self-review (15 min): listen to your recordings. For each one, write one sentence on what you did well and one on what you would change. Mark any moment you gave advice by accident."],"deliverable":"Send your coach, in your BeyondVAs training channel: the six role-play recordings, the six written notes, and your self-review. Your coach will score whether you asked the financing question after the appointment, gave zero loan, rate or credit advice, and said the buyer chooses their lender."},"quiz":[{"q":"When should you ask the financing question on a new buyer call?","options":["In the first sentence, so you do not waste time with unqualified buyers","After the appointment is set, in about twenty seconds","Only at the end of the second phone call","Never: financing is not part of an ISA call"],"answer":1,"explain":"Care first, appointment second, money third. Asking before you have helped them makes many first-time buyers freeze."},{"q":"A buyer says, “We filled out a form on an app and it said we’re approved.” What is the best note?","options":["“Pre-approved. Ready to make offers.”","“Not qualified.”","“Says approved through an online app; no documents reviewed, per lead. Likely a pre-qual. Agent to review.”","“Credit unknown, probably weak.”"],"answer":2,"explain":"Write what you heard, mark the source with “per lead,” and mark any guess as a guess. Never write opinions about their credit."},{"q":"Which statement about a pre-approval is true?","options":["It means the loan is final and cannot fail","It is the same as a pre-qualification","It is the lender’s rate guarantee","It is a boarding pass, not takeoff: strong, but the loan can still fail in underwriting"],"answer":3,"explain":"A pre-approval is a strong sign, usually based on reviewed documents, but things can change before the loan is final."},{"q":"A buyer asks, “What rate can we get right now?” What do you say?","options":["“Rates depend on each person’s situation and change often, so only a lender can quote one. I’ll note it so the agent covers it.”","“About 6 percent, but it might drop.”","“Rates are going up, so you should buy now.”","“I can check online and text you a number.”"],"answer":0,"explain":"Never quote or predict rates. Acknowledge, say who can answer, and log the question."},{"q":"When may you offer an introduction to your client’s preferred lender?","options":["On every call, to every buyer","Only when your agent has told you to, and always as one option the buyer is free to refuse","Only to buyers who sound unsure about money","Never, under any circumstances"],"answer":1,"explain":"House rule: connect a buyer with the preferred lender only when the agent says so, and say “You can use any lender you like.”"},{"q":"“My cousin said we HAVE to use your team’s lender?” Which reply is right?","options":["“Yes, with us it’s our lender or nothing.”","“Lenders are all the same, go with whoever.”","“Our lender has the lowest rates in town.”","“Great question, and no: you always choose your lender. I can introduce one so you have an option to compare.”"],"answer":3,"explain":"Always the buyer’s choice of lender. You can offer an option to compare (when your agent allows it) and leave the choice with them."},{"q":"A buyer volunteers, “My credit score is 590.” What do you do with that number?","options":["Do not write the score down; note that they have financing questions for the agent or lender","Write it in the note so the agent sees it","Text it to the agent right away","Tell them it is too low to buy"],"answer":0,"explain":"You never collect, repeat or comment on credit scores. Pass the need, not the number, to the agent."},{"q":"“Should we wait for rates to drop?” Why is “Rates will drop soon” a bad answer?","options":["Because rates always go up","Because the buyer will think you are too young","Because a prediction is not a fact, and it is pressure; ask what would help them decide instead","It is a good answer if you read it in the news"],"answer":2,"explain":"No one can promise what rates will do. Guide with a question and real numbers from a qualified person."},{"q":"Which of these is a money question you are allowed to ask a buyer?","options":["“What is your credit score?”","“How much do you earn a year?”","“How much do you have saved for a down payment?”","“Have you talked to a lender yet?”"],"answer":3,"explain":"One question, one follow-up, one note. Income, savings and credit are lender questions."}]},{"id":"I5","part":"isa","title":"Mastering objections","summary":"Hear an objection as a question in disguise, use ask-listen-mirror to find what is behind it, then acknowledge, pivot and ask for the appointment, with ready replies for the objections you will hear every day.","objectives":["Tell an objection apart from an opt-out, and honor every opt-out at once without arguing.","Use ask, listen, mirror to uncover the real concern behind a short objection before answering it.","Answer any objection with the acknowledge, pivot, appointment pattern in three sentences or fewer.","Reply naturally to at least 15 common buyer, trust and scope objections without leaving your unlicensed scope or breaking fair housing rules.","Practice objections out loud every week and log each one in Follow Up Boss so the agent knows what the lead is worried about."],"lessons":[{"id":"I5-1","title":"What an objection really is (and what it is not)","minutes":6,"body":[{"t":"p","text":"An objection is anything a lead says that slows the conversation down: “We’re just looking,” “Send me the info,” “Prices are too high,” “I already have an agent.” New ISAs hear these as a door closing. Experienced ISAs hear them as **a question in disguise.** “We’re just looking” often means “I’m not ready to be pushed.” “Send me the info” often means “I don’t know you yet.” “Prices are too high” can mean three completely different things. Your job is to find the real question and answer that one."},{"t":"p","text":"Remember the five buyer truths from module I1. The first one matters most here: buyers are **overwhelmed, not unmotivated.** Someone who filled out a form at 11 PM is interested. They are also nervous, busy, and maybe a little embarrassed about how much they don’t know. An objection is often that nervousness coming out. If you meet it with pressure, it gets stronger. If you meet it with curiosity and calm, it usually gets smaller."},{"t":"callout","title":"First rule: an opt-out is not an objection","text":"If a lead says **“STOP”**, **“Remove me”**, **“Don’t call”**, or anything that clearly means stop contacting me, you do not handle it. You honor it: tag DNC, move the stage to Archived / DNC, pause all automations, log it. Never delete the contact. Never argue, never ask why, never try “just one more question.”"},{"t":"table","head":["The lead says","Objection or opt-out?","What you do"],"rows":[["“I’m just browsing right now.”","Objection","Acknowledge, pivot, appointment."],["“Please don’t bombard me with sales pitches.”","Objection (a request for less pressure)","Lower the pressure, acknowledge, ask a Location or Motivation question."],["“Stop calling this number.”","Opt-out","Apologize, confirm you will not contact them again, end the call, DNC process."],["“Take me off your list.”","Opt-out","Same as above, same day, every channel."],["“Text me instead, I can’t talk at work.”","Neither: a channel preference","Log the preference. Your manager confirms whether a spoken request counts as text consent for your client; until then, follow up by phone."],["“Call me next month.”","Objection (a timing objection)","Ask one question about timing, then set a specific follow-up date."]]},{"t":"p","text":"When you are not sure, treat it as an opt-out. If a lead says “I’m not interested, don’t bother,” you may ask one gentle clarifying question only if your manager allows it: “Understood. Would you like me to stop calling completely?” If they say yes, it is an opt-out. In most cases, the safest and kindest choice is simply to say “I’m sorry for the bother, I’ll make sure you don’t get more calls from us,” and follow the DNC process."},{"t":"script","title":"Honoring an opt-out the right way (David, an old website lead)","lines":[{"who":"Lead","text":"Stop calling this number! Remove me from your database right now!"},{"who":"You","text":"I’m very sorry for the disturbance, David. I’ve opted your number out, and you won’t receive more calls or texts from us. Have a good day."}]},{"t":"p","text":"Then, before your next dial: tag DNC, stage Archived / DNC, pause every automation, and log “Call, connected. Verbal opt-out. Tagged DNC, automations paused. No further contact.” You **do not delete** David. The DNC record is what stops future calls and texts from anyone on the team. If you argue (“Why are you angry? Are you sure you don’t want to buy?”), you break calling rules and your client’s DNC policy, and you can expose your client to fines."},{"t":"p","text":"Now, the mindset for real objections. You are not trying to “win.” You are trying to help a person take one small, useful step: a meeting with an agent who can answer their questions. If the honest answer is that they are not ready, the win is a specific follow-up date and a good note. A lead who hangs up feeling respected will answer your call in two months. A lead who hangs up feeling pushed will not."},{"t":"dodont","do":["Slow down when you hear an objection. A calm voice lowers pressure.","Get curious: “Can I ask what makes you say that?”","Keep your answer short. Three sentences or fewer.","Always end with a next step: an appointment, or a specific follow-up date."],"dont":["Argue, correct the lead, or say “You’re wrong.”","Answer the objection you expected instead of the one they said.","Use pressure lines: “This house won’t last,” “Prices will only go up.”","Treat an opt-out as an objection to overcome."]},{"t":"p","text":"Objection handling is 20 of the 100 points on the QA rubric you will learn in module I11. Your coach listens for three things: you stayed calm, you found the real concern, and you still asked for the appointment. You will learn the two tools for this in the next two lessons: **ask, listen, mirror** and **acknowledge, pivot, appointment.**"}]},{"id":"I5-2","title":"Ask, listen, mirror","minutes":6,"body":[{"t":"p","text":"Most objections are too short to answer well. “Prices are too high” is five words. You cannot know what the lead means yet. So before you answer, you use three small skills, in order: **Ask** what is behind it. **Listen** without filling the silence. **Mirror** their words back so they know you heard them. Only then do you answer."},{"t":"p","text":"**Ask.** Use an open question, one that cannot be answered with yes or no. Good open questions start with “what” or “how,” or with “Can I ask...” or “Tell me more about...” Avoid “why” at the start, because in English “Why do you think that?” can sound like a challenge. Compare:"},{"t":"table","head":["Closed or challenging (avoid)","Open and curious (use)"],"rows":[["“Why do you think prices are too high?”","“Which part feels too high: the asking price, the monthly payment, or what you get for the money?”"],["“Do you not want to buy anymore?”","“What changed since you reached out?”"],["“Are you sure you want to wait?”","“What would need to happen for the timing to feel right?”"],["“Is your agent good?”","“How is the search going so far?”"]]},{"t":"p","text":"**Listen.** After you ask, stop talking. Count to three in your head. Silence feels long on the phone, especially in a second language, and many ISAs rush to fill it with another question or a sales line. Don’t. The lead is thinking. The next thing they say is usually the real reason. If you interrupt, you lose it. While you listen, type short notes: their exact words are gold for the agent."},{"t":"p","text":"**Mirror.** Repeat the last one to three important words they said, with a gentle questioning tone, or summarize their concern in one short sentence. This shows you heard them, and it often makes them explain more without you asking another question."},{"t":"script","title":"Mirroring in action: Marcus","lines":[{"who":"Lead","text":"Honestly prices are just too high right now. We’re going to wait."},{"who":"You","text":"I hear you. Which part feels too high: the asking price, the monthly payment, or what you get for the money?"},{"who":"Lead","text":"The monthly payment, mostly. Our rent is already a lot."},{"who":"You","text":"Your rent is already a lot..."},{"who":"Lead","text":"Yeah, $2,300, and it goes up every year. We just don’t want to pay more than that for a mortgage."},{"who":"You","text":"So the goal is a monthly cost you can live with, ideally around what you pay now. That’s exactly what our agent can look at with you, with real numbers. Would Thursday at 5 or Saturday at 10 work for a short meeting?"},{"who":"Lead","text":"If you can show us the real numbers, we would look. Saturday."}]},{"t":"p","text":"Look at what happened. “Prices are too high” turned into a clear Motivation (rent rising), a clear budget concern (monthly cost around $2,300), and an appointment. You did not argue that prices are fine. You did not predict rates. You did not give a number. You asked, listened, mirrored, and then offered the meeting where the real numbers can be shown."},{"t":"callout","title":"Mirroring tips for second-language speakers","text":"Mirroring is a gift if English is your second language: you use the lead’s own words, so you do not have to find new ones. Keep your tone soft and slightly rising, like a question. Do not mirror every sentence, or it sounds strange. Once or twice per objection is enough."},{"t":"p","text":"A related tool is **labeling**: naming the feeling you hear. “It sounds like this has been stressful.” “It sounds like you’ve had a bad experience with agents before.” Labels are powerful when you are right and harmless when you are a little wrong, because the lead will correct you, and the correction tells you more. Never label in a way that judges (“It sounds like you can’t afford it”)."},{"t":"dodont","do":["Ask one open question, then wait at least three seconds.","Write down the lead’s exact words for the agent.","Mirror once or twice with a soft, rising tone.","Summarize the concern in one sentence before you answer it."],"dont":["Ask two questions at once (“What price, and when are you moving?”).","Fill the silence with a pitch.","Mirror with a flat, robotic voice, or mirror everything.","Label something personal: money, family, health, background."]},{"t":"p","text":"Practice this with everyday conversations too. When a friend says “Work is crazy this week,” try “Crazy?” and wait. You will be surprised how much people share when they feel heard. That same skill, on a call with a nervous buyer, is the difference between “We’ll wait” and “Saturday works.”"}]},{"id":"I5-3","title":"Acknowledge, pivot, appointment","minutes":7,"body":[{"t":"p","text":"Ask, listen, mirror helps you find the real concern. **Acknowledge, pivot, appointment** is how you answer it. It is a three-step pattern you can use for almost any objection, and it keeps your reply short, warm and moving forward."},{"t":"steps","items":["**Acknowledge** the concern in one sentence. Show it is normal and that you are not going to fight it. “Completely get it.” “That makes sense.” “Understood, and I won’t step on that.”","**Pivot** to a Location or Motivation question. This moves the conversation from the objection back to what the lead wants. “What neighborhood caught your eye today?” “What has you thinking about moving?”","**Appointment.** Ask for a specific time, with two options. Never “When works for you?” Always “Saturday morning or afternoon?” or “Thursday at 5 or 6?”"]},{"t":"callout","title":"The pattern","text":"Acknowledge, pivot to a Location or Motivation question, then ask for the appointment."},{"t":"p","text":"Here are the three core turnarounds. Learn them word for word first. Later you will make them sound like you."},{"t":"table","head":["Objection","Your reply"],"rows":[["“Just looking online.”","“Completely get it! Most of our clients browse 6 months early. What neighborhood caught your eye today?”"],["“I already have an agent.”","“Understood, and I won’t step on that. Have you signed an agreement with them?” If they have: “Great, I’ll make a note. If anything changes, we’re here.” Log it in Follow Up Boss and tell your agent. Never pitch around another agent’s agreement. If they haven’t: offer a second opinion from your local specialist and ask for a time."],["“Just send me info.”","“I will email that right now! While I pull it up, are you free Saturday morning or afternoon to take a quick look inside?”"]]},{"t":"p","text":"Let’s look closely at why each one works. In “Just looking online,” the acknowledgment (“Completely get it!”) removes pressure, and “Most of our clients browse 6 months early” tells them browsing is normal. The pivot (“What neighborhood caught your eye today?”) is an easy, fun Location question. Once they answer, you have a reason to offer a look at homes in that area."},{"t":"p","text":"“I already have an agent” is different, because there may be a signed agreement. If there is one, you respect it completely: note it, tell your agent the same day, and never try to pull the lead away. That is an ethics rule, not a sales tactic. If there is no signed agreement, the lead is free to talk to more than one agent, and a second opinion is a fair offer. You will see this one again in lesson I5-5."},{"t":"p","text":"“Just send me info” is often a polite way to end the call. So you say yes (and you really do send it, right away), and in the same breath you ask a simple appointment question. Many leads say “Saturday afternoon, actually.” If they say “No, just the info,” send it, log it, and set a follow-up task for two days later to ask what they thought."},{"t":"script","title":"When the lead asks for less pressure: Marcus Vance (social media ad lead)","lines":[{"who":"Lead","text":"I just clicked to see pictures of the kitchen! Please don’t bombard me with sales pitches."},{"who":"You","text":"Completely understand, Marcus! The kitchen in that home is beautiful. Most of our clients look 6 months out. What specific area are you hoping to stay in when you do make a move?"},{"who":"Lead","text":"Somewhere on the east side, near 30th Street. Our lease ends in about 45 days, and the kids need a bigger backyard."},{"who":"You","text":"That’s really helpful. Would it be useful if I sent you three homes on the east side with big yards and kitchens like that one, and then checked in on Thursday?"}]},{"t":"p","text":"Marcus asked not to be pitched, so a hard push (“You gave us your number! Can my agent meet you there today at 4?”) would end the call. Instead you acknowledged, pivoted to Location, and learned his Motivation and timeline. In this case the “appointment” step is softened into a specific next step, because pressure was the problem. On the next call, with the homes in hand, you ask for the tour."},{"t":"dodont","do":["Keep each step to one sentence.","Offer two specific times every time you ask for the appointment.","Do what you promise: if you say you will send info, send it within minutes.","Log the objection and the lead’s exact words in your note."],"dont":["Skip the acknowledgment and jump straight to the pitch.","Pivot to a question about money, credit or family background.","Ask “When works for you?” It gives the lead an easy “I’ll let you know.”","Use the same pattern three times in a row if the lead clearly says no."]},{"t":"callout","title":"How many times?","text":"Use the pattern up to two times per call. If the lead objects a third time, stop. Thank them, set a specific follow-up date they agree to (or a nurture task if they won’t give one), and log it. Respect is part of the method."}]},{"id":"I5-4","title":"The objection library, part 1: timing, interest and price","minutes":7,"body":[{"t":"p","text":"This lesson and the next give you ready replies for the objections you will hear most often. Each reply follows acknowledge, pivot, appointment. Read them out loud. Then, in your practice, change the words until they sound like you, without changing the structure or leaving your scope."},{"t":"table","head":["Objection","What it may really mean","A reply that works"],"rows":[["1. “We’re just looking.”","I’m early. Don’t push me.","“Completely get it! Most of our clients browse 6 months early. What neighborhood caught your eye today?” Then: “Would it help to see one or two in person this weekend, Saturday morning or afternoon?”"],["2. “Just send me the info.”","I don’t know you yet. / I want to end the call.","“I will email that right now! While I pull it up, are you free Saturday morning or afternoon to take a quick look inside?”"],["3. “Prices are too high.”","The asking price, the monthly payment, or the value for the money.","“I hear you. Which part feels too high: the asking price, the monthly payment, or what you get for the money?” Then let the answer change your approach, and offer the meeting where real numbers can be shown."],["4. “We’ll wait for rates to drop.”","We’re worried about the monthly cost.","“That makes sense, nobody wants to overpay. Nobody can promise what rates will do, but our agent can show you what the numbers look like today so you can decide with real information. Thursday at 5 or Saturday at 10?”"],["5. “Call me in six months.”","Not now, but maybe. Or: I want you to go away.","“Happy to. Can I ask what happens in six months: a lease ending, a job, school year?” Log the reason. “Would it help to meet the agent before then, so you know your options when the time comes? If not, I’ll call you on [specific date].”"],["6. “We need to sell our house first.”","We are a buyer and a seller.","“That’s really common. Our agent helps people do both and plan the order. Can I ask where your current home is?” Log as a buyer and seller lead. Offer a meeting about both. (Module I6 covers seller leads.)"],["7. “I’m busy right now.”","Bad timing, not a no.","“No problem at all, I’ll be quick or I can call back. Is later today at 6 or tomorrow at noon better?” Call back exactly then, inside the calling window."],["8. “I need to talk to my husband / wife / partner.”","I can’t decide alone.","“Of course, it’s a decision for both of you. Would it be easier if you both met the agent together? Saturday at 10 or Sunday at 1?”"],["9. “We found the house ourselves online, we don’t need an agent.”","We don’t see the value yet.","“That’s great, you’ve done the hard part. Can I ask what you liked most about it?” Then: “Our agent can get you inside and help you understand the home before you decide anything. Want to see it Saturday at 11 or 2?”"],["10. “How did you get my number?”","I don’t remember asking. Am I being spammed?","Be honest and specific: “You asked about the home on Oak Street on [the website] yesterday, and I’m following up for Daniel’s team.” If they say they didn’t, apologize and offer to remove them. If they want removal, it is an opt-out."]]},{"t":"p","text":"Look at objection 3 again, because it is the most important one in a high-price market. **“Prices are too high” means three things.** If it is the asking price, the lead may be looking at the wrong homes or the wrong area; the agent can show other options. If it is the monthly payment, it is a financing question; the agent and a lender can show real numbers (module I4). If it is the value (“$400,000 for that?”), the lead may need to see homes in person to understand the market. **Ask first, then let the answer change your approach.**"},{"t":"callout","title":"Never use predictions as pressure","text":"“Rates will drop soon and prices will only go up. You should buy now before it gets worse.” This line is wrong twice. A prediction is not a fact, and it is pressure. It also gives financial advice you are not allowed to give. Ask which part feels too high instead."},{"t":"p","text":"Objection 5, “Call me in six months,” deserves special care. A real reason (a lease ending) is Motivation: log it and plan around it. No reason? Offer value, like new homes in their area once a week, and set a specific follow-up date. “Not now” is a date, not a no."},{"t":"script","title":"“Call me in six months” done well: Priya","lines":[{"who":"Lead","text":"Honestly, just call me in six months."},{"who":"You","text":"Happy to, Priya. Can I ask what happens in six months?"},{"who":"Lead","text":"Our lease is up in April, and my bonus comes in March."},{"who":"You","text":"That makes sense, so you’d want to be ready to move in April. A lot of people meet the agent two or three months before, so they know what to look for and what to prepare. Would a short call with Daniel in January be useful? If not, I’ll call you the first week of February."},{"who":"Lead","text":"January is fine, actually."}]},{"t":"p","text":"Note for Priya: “Timing objection. Lease ends April, per lead. Agreed to call with agent in January. Task: confirm date first week of January.”"}]},{"id":"I5-5","title":"The objection library, part 2: trust, agents and questions outside your scope","minutes":7,"body":[{"t":"p","text":"The second group of objections is about trust (“Who are you?”), other agents, and questions you are not allowed to answer. Honesty is the foundation: **never pretend to be the agent, never pretend to be somewhere you are not, and never answer a question outside your scope.**"},{"t":"table","head":["Objection","A reply that works","Why"],"rows":[["11. “I already have an agent.”","“Understood, and I won’t step on that. Have you signed an agreement with them?” If yes: “Great, I’ll make a note. If anything changes, we’re here.” If no: “Would a second opinion from our local specialist be helpful? Thursday at 5 or Saturday at 10?”","A signed agreement is respected completely. No agreement means the lead is free to compare."],["12. “My cousin / friend is an agent.”","“That’s great to have someone in the family. Are you working with them on this search, or is it more of an option?” If they are working together, ask: “Have you signed an agreement with them?” If yes, respect it completely. If the cousin is only an option, offer a second opinion.","Same rule as above, said kindly."],["13. “Are you the agent?”","“No, I’m Ana, an assistant on Daniel’s team. I help schedule time with Daniel, who is the licensed agent and will answer your questions about the home.”","Never act as an agent; clarity builds trust."],["14. “Where are you calling from? Are you a real person?”","“Yes, I’m a real person! I’m Ana, I work remotely as an assistant with Daniel’s team. Daniel is the licensed agent, based in [city].”","Never lie about who or where you are. Most people accept an honest remote assistant."],["15. “Is this a good / safe neighborhood? How are the schools?”","“Great question for your agent. I can also send you public sources.”","Fair housing: never describe areas by who lives there, schools, crime, safety, religion or “family-friendly.”"],["16. “What’s the lowest the seller will take?”","“That’s exactly the kind of thing Daniel can help you with. I don’t have that information, and I’m not able to discuss price. Want to see the home first, Saturday at 11 or 2?”","ISAs never negotiate or discuss price or terms."],["17. “What’s your commission? Do I have to pay you?”","“Good question, and Daniel will explain exactly how that works before you decide anything. I schedule the meeting so you can ask him directly.”","Commission and terms are outside your scope."],["18. “I don’t want to sign anything.”","“Totally fair. Daniel will explain any paperwork before you see homes, and you can ask him anything about it. Nothing gets signed on this call.”","Since August 2024, a written buyer agreement is needed before a tour. The agent sends and explains it; you never do."],["19. “I had a bad experience with an agent before.”","“I’m sorry, that sounds frustrating. Can I ask what happened?” Listen. “Thank you for telling me. I’ll make sure Daniel knows what matters to you.”","Labeling and listening. The note helps the agent avoid the same mistake."]]},{"t":"p","text":"“I already have an agent” is easy to get wrong in both directions. Giving up loses leads who only talked to an agent once. Pushing past a signed agreement is unethical and risky for your client. The right path is the question: **“Have you signed an agreement with them?”**"},{"t":"steps","items":["Ask: “Have you signed an agreement with them?”","If yes: “Great, I’ll make a note. If anything changes, we’re here.” Log it in Follow Up Boss, tell your agent the same day, and never pitch around the agreement.","If no: offer a second opinion from your local specialist and ask for a time with two options.","If they are not sure: “No problem. Daniel can go over that with you.” Log it and tell your agent."]},{"t":"script","title":"Staying in scope: Kevin asks about price and safety","lines":[{"who":"Lead","text":"Is that area safe? And do you think they’d take $20K less?"},{"who":"You","text":"Both great questions for Daniel. On the area, I can also send you public sources so you can look for yourself. On price, I’m not able to discuss that, but Daniel can walk you through it after you’ve seen the home. Would Saturday at 11 or Sunday at 2 work to see it?"},{"who":"Lead","text":"Sunday at 2."},{"who":"You","text":"Perfect. I’ll text you the confirmation now."}]},{"t":"p","text":"You did not say “It’s safe,” “It’s not safe,” “Lots of families live there,” or “They might take less.” Each would break a rule: fair housing or unlicensed scope. Your note: “Lead asked about area safety (offered public sources) and whether seller would accept $20K less (referred to agent). Appt Sun 2 PM.”"},{"t":"callout","title":"Honesty about who you are","text":"If a lead asks directly whether you are in the US, tell the truth: “No, I work remotely from [your country] as part of Daniel’s team.” If they are uncomfortable, respect it: “I understand. Would you prefer that Daniel calls you himself?” Then tell Daniel. Lying once destroys trust with the lead and with your client."},{"t":"dodont","do":["Say your name and your client’s team early in every call.","Use the fair housing line word for word: “Great question for your agent. I can also send you public sources.”","Pass every price, commission, contract and value question to the agent, and log it."],"dont":["Say “I’m the agent” or let the lead believe it.","Give your opinion about a neighborhood, school or safety.","Explain or send a buyer agreement yourself."]}]},{"id":"I5-6","title":"Practice like a pro: drills, logging and getting better every week","minutes":6,"body":[{"t":"p","text":"Reading objection replies does not make you good at them. Saying them out loud, many times, does. On a live call you have about two seconds to respond. If the reply is not already in your mouth, you will hesitate, say “um,” or reach for pressure. Professional ISAs practice objections the way athletes practice: short, frequent and recorded."},{"t":"steps","items":["**Pick one objection a week.** Write it at the top of your notebook or a sticky note on your screen.","**Say your reply out loud 20 times** over the week, until it sounds natural, not memorized.","**Role-play it with your practice partner or coach** at least twice. They should vary the objection (“just browsing,” “only looking,” “not ready yet”) so you learn to recognize it in different words.","**Record yourself** once a week and listen back. Check your speed, your tone and whether you asked for the appointment.","**Use it on real calls** and mark in your notes when it worked and when it didn’t."]},{"t":"p","text":"Here is a good rotation for your first ten weeks: “we’re just looking,” “send me the info,” “prices are too high,” “we’ll wait for rates to drop,” “I already have an agent,” “call me in six months,” “I need to talk to my partner,” “are you the agent?”, “is it a safe area?”, and “how did you get my number?” After ten weeks, start again. You will be surprised how much better the second round sounds."},{"t":"callout","title":"Tone beats words","text":"Leads remember how you made them feel more than what you said. Smile when you speak (people can hear it). Slow down by about 10 percent when you hear an objection. Lower your voice slightly at the end of a sentence; a rising tone on statements can sound unsure. Your English does not need to be perfect. It needs to be calm and kind."},{"t":"p","text":"**Log every objection.** Your note in Follow Up Boss should say what the objection was, in the lead’s words, and how it ended. This matters for three reasons. First, the agent walks into the appointment knowing what worries the lead. Second, the next person who calls the lead (maybe you, in two weeks) can start where you left off instead of repeating the same mistake. Third, your coach can review patterns: if you lose most leads at “send me the info,” that is your next practice objection."},{"t":"table","head":["Weak note","Strong note"],"rows":[["“Not interested.”","“Objection: ‘prices too high’ = monthly payment, per lead (rent $2,300, rising). Wants payment near current rent. Appt Sat 10 AM with Daniel. Next: confirm Fri evening.”"],["“Has agent.”","“Says working with another agent; signed agreement: YES, per lead. Respecting it. Told Daniel in client channel. No further outreach unless lead contacts us.”"],["“Call later.”","“Timing objection: lease ends April, per lead. Agreed to call with agent in January. Task set for Jan 6.”"]]},{"t":"p","text":"Your coach scores objection handling on the QA rubric (20 of 100 points). Here is what a strong score sounds like: you heard the objection without interrupting; you asked an open question to find the real concern; you mirrored or summarized; you acknowledged in one sentence; you pivoted to Location or Motivation; you asked for the appointment with two specific times; you stayed in scope and inside fair housing rules; and if the lead opted out, you honored it immediately. A weak score usually comes from one of three mistakes: arguing, giving up after the first “no,” or answering a question you should have passed to the agent."},{"t":"dodont","do":["Practice daily for five minutes rather than once a month for an hour.","Ask your coach for one specific thing to improve after each review.","Celebrate small wins: a “just looking” that became a Saturday tour is a big deal."],"dont":["Read replies from a screen word for word on live calls; it sounds robotic.","Take objections personally. The lead is reacting to the situation, not to you.","Keep pushing after the second objection on the same call."]},{"t":"callout","title":"Your objection mindset","text":"Every objection is a question in disguise. Ask, listen, mirror. Then acknowledge, pivot, appointment. And when they say stop, stop."}]}],"practice":{"title":"The objection gauntlet","minutes":100,"intro":"This practice turns the objection library into habits. You will record your replies to the 19 objections in this module, then do three full role-play calls with your practice partner or coach, who will throw objections at you without warning. Use only fictional names, 555 numbers and example.com emails.","steps":["Script sheet (20 min): in a document, write your own version of the reply to each of the 19 objections in lessons I5-4 and I5-5. Keep the acknowledge, pivot, appointment structure. Mark each reply with A, P and Ap where each step starts.","Solo recording (20 min): record yourself saying each reply out loud, as if a lead just said the objection. Do not read word for word; glance at your sheet, then speak. Redo any reply that takes more than 20 seconds.","Opt-out drill (5 min): your partner mixes 10 short lines, some objections and some opt-outs (“remove me”, “stop texting me”). For each one, say “objection” or “opt-out” and give the correct response. Aim for 10 out of 10.","Role-play A (10 min): “Marcus Vance”, a social media ad lead for a 4-bed pool home: “I just clicked to see pictures of the kitchen! Please don’t bombard me with sales pitches.” Lower the pressure, pivot to Location, learn the Motivation and set a specific next step.","Role-play B (10 min): a portal lead who says “prices are too high”, then “call me in six months”, then “I need to talk to my wife.” Use ask, listen, mirror on the first one. Get to an appointment or a specific follow-up date.","Role-play C (10 min): a lead who asks “Are you the agent?”, “Is the area safe?”, and “Would they take $15K less?” Stay honest, stay in scope, use the fair housing line, and ask for the appointment.","Notes (10 min): write a Follow Up Boss style A.L.M. note for each role-play, with the objection in the lead’s words and how it ended.","Self-score (15 min): listen to the three role-plays and score yourself out of 20 on objection handling using the checklist in lesson I5-6. Choose your first “objection of the week” from your weakest moment."],"deliverable":"Send your coach, in your BeyondVAs training channel: your script sheet, the 19 solo recordings, the three role-play recordings, the three notes, your self-scores and the objection you chose to practice this week. Your coach will reply with a score and one thing to improve."},"quiz":[{"q":"A lead says, “Stop calling this number!” What do you do?","options":["Use acknowledge, pivot, appointment, because every objection is a question in disguise","Apologize, confirm they will not be contacted, then tag DNC, stage Archived / DNC, pause automations and log it","Ask why they are upset so you can fix the problem","Delete the contact so nobody calls them again"],"answer":1,"explain":"An opt-out is not an objection. Honor it immediately and never delete the contact: the DNC record is what stops future contact."},{"q":"A buyer says, “Prices are too high.” What is the best first move?","options":["Show them cheaper homes right away","Tell them rates will drop soon","Ask which part feels too high: the asking price, the monthly payment, or what you get for the money","Agree and set a follow-up for next year"],"answer":2,"explain":"Same words, different problems. Ask first, then let the answer change your approach."},{"q":"What are the three steps of ask, listen, mirror?","options":["Ask an open question, stay silent while they answer, repeat their key words back","Ask for the appointment, listen to their excuse, mirror their tone of voice","Ask about money, listen to the number, mirror it in the note","Ask three questions, listen to the last answer, mirror the agent’s script"],"answer":0,"explain":"Ask what is behind the objection, listen without filling the silence, and mirror their words so they feel heard and explain more."},{"q":"Which reply to “Just send me info” follows the pattern you learned?","options":["“Okay, bye.”","“I can’t send anything until you meet the agent.”","“What’s your budget first?”","“I will email that right now! While I pull it up, are you free Saturday morning or afternoon to take a quick look inside?”"],"answer":3,"explain":"Say yes, really send it, and ask for the appointment in the same breath with two options."},{"q":"A lead says “I already have an agent” and confirms they signed an agreement. What do you do?","options":["Offer a second opinion and ask for a time","Say “Great, I’ll make a note. If anything changes, we’re here,” log it and tell your agent; never pitch around it","Ask how much the other agent charges and offer a better deal","Remove the lead from Follow Up Boss"],"answer":1,"explain":"A signed agreement is respected completely. The second-opinion offer is only for leads with no signed agreement."},{"q":"Which question is the best open question to use after an objection?","options":["“Why don’t you want to buy?”","“Are you sure about waiting?”","“What would need to happen for the timing to feel right?”","“Do you have a pre-approval?”"],"answer":2,"explain":"Open questions start with what or how and cannot be answered with yes or no. “Why” can sound like a challenge."},{"q":"A lead asks, “Is that neighborhood safe for kids?” What do you say?","options":["“Great question for your agent. I can also send you public sources.”","“Yes, lots of families live there.”","“I’d avoid it, honestly.”","“The schools there are excellent.”"],"answer":0,"explain":"Fair housing: never describe an area by safety, schools, who lives there or how family-friendly it is."},{"q":"“Are you the agent?” Which answer is right?","options":["“Yes, I can help with everything.”","“Kind of, I do most of the work.”","Avoid the question and ask for the appointment","“No, I’m an assistant on the team. I schedule time with the licensed agent, who will answer your questions about the home.”"],"answer":3,"explain":"Never act as an agent or let a lead believe you are one. Honesty builds trust."},{"q":"How many times should you use acknowledge, pivot, appointment on the same call before stopping?","options":["Once only","Up to two times; after a third objection, thank them and set a specific follow-up date","As many times as it takes to get the appointment","Never more than once a week per lead"],"answer":1,"explain":"Respect is part of the method. After the second try, end kindly with a specific next step and a good note."},{"q":"Which statement about “Rates will drop soon, so buy now before prices go up” is correct?","options":["It is a strong close you should use with nervous buyers","It is fine if the news says so","It is wrong twice: a prediction is not a fact, it is pressure, and it is advice you may not give","It is only allowed with buyers who are already pre-approved"],"answer":2,"explain":"Predictions used as pressure push buyers away and break your scope. Ask which part feels too high instead."}]},{"id":"I6","part":"isa","title":"Working with sellers","summary":"Qualify seller leads with A.L.M., handle “what is my home worth?” without ever giving a value, set the listing consultation with every decision maker, and follow up in a way that respects the seller’s timeline.","objectives":["Name the main reasons people sell and the main seller lead sources, and know which sources you may call and under what rules.","Run seller discovery with A.L.M.: the listing consultation, the property, and the reason and timeline.","Answer “What is my home worth?” and “The website says it’s worth $X” warmly, without giving any number, range or opinion of value.","Book a listing consultation with two time options, every decision maker present, and a calendar invite the agent can act on.","Handle the common seller objections and sensitive situations, and build a follow-up plan that matches the seller’s timeline."],"lessons":[{"id":"I6-1","title":"Who calls about selling, and why","minutes":6,"body":[{"t":"p","text":"For many real estate agents, sellers are the most valuable leads. A listing brings a commission, a sign in the yard, buyer calls from that sign, and often a second deal when the seller buys their next home. That is why your client may ask you to work seller leads with special care. A seller lead is also very different from a buyer lead. A buyer is looking forward to something new. A seller is often leaving something: a home full of memories, a marriage, a parent’s house, a job, a city. Your tone has to match that."},{"t":"p","text":"People sell for a reason, and **the reason sets the timeline.** Ask for it early, gently, and listen to the whole answer. Here are the most common reasons you will hear:"},{"t":"table","head":["Reason","What you might hear","Usual timeline and tone"],"rows":[["Moving for work","“I got a job offer in Denver, I start in March.”","Fixed date, often urgent. Practical tone."],["Need more space","“We have a baby coming and we’re out of room.”","Medium. Often a buy-and-sell at the same time."],["Downsizing","“The kids are gone, the house is too big for us now.”","Flexible. Emotional; take your time."],["Inheritance","“My mom passed last year and we have her house.”","Varies. Be gentle; there may be several family members and legal steps (probate)."],["Divorce or separation","“We’re splitting up and need to sell.”","Often time-sensitive and stressful. Stay neutral; both owners matter."],["Landlord done with renting","“I’m tired of tenants. The lease ends in June.”","Tied to the lease. Tenants have rights; the agent handles that."],["Financial pressure","“We’re behind on payments.”","Can be urgent. Never give legal or financial advice; tell the agent quickly."],["Curious","“Just curious what it’s worth.”","Unclear. Often a future seller; worth a careful follow-up plan."]]},{"t":"p","text":"Seller leads come from several sources. Each source tells you something about how warm the lead is, and each one has its own calling rules. This matters a lot: **you may only call a seller in the calling window, and only if the lead asked to be contacted or the number was scrubbed against the National Do Not Call Registry and your client’s own DNC list.**"},{"t":"table","head":["Source","What it is","May you call?"],"rows":[["Home value form","The owner typed their address on your client’s website to get an estimate.","Yes, inside the calling window. They asked to be contacted. Text only if they agreed to texts."],["Sign call or ad call","Someone called about a listing and mentions they also need to sell.","Yes, inside the calling window. They contacted your client."],["Past client or sphere","People your client already knows, in the CRM.","Yes, inside the window, if your client gives you the list and it is scrubbed against the National DNC Registry and their own DNC list."],["Old database leads","People who registered months or years ago.","Only numbers scrubbed against the National DNC Registry and your client’s DNC list."],["Expired listings and FSBO","Homes that did not sell with another agent, or owners selling by themselves (“For Sale By Owner”).","Only if your manager confirms your scope allows it and the list is DNC-scrubbed. Many states limit this for unlicensed assistants."]]},{"t":"callout","title":"Scope comes first","text":"Your manager sets your scope before you dial. In some states, Texas for example, an unlicensed assistant may not call to find out whether someone wants to buy, sell or lease. In those states, you may only follow up with sellers who already asked to be contacted, in the way your manager describes. When in doubt, ask in the client channel before you call, not after."},{"t":"p","text":"One more source deserves attention: **your buyer leads.** Many buyers own a home now. When a buyer says “We need to sell our house first,” you have just found a seller lead. Log it as both, tell your agent, and treat the home sale with the same care as the purchase."},{"t":"dodont","do":["Ask for the reason early: “Can I ask what has you thinking about selling?”","Match your tone to the reason: practical for a job move, gentle for an inheritance.","Check the source and the calling rules before every outbound seller call.","Log the reason and the timeline in the lead’s words."],"dont":["Treat a seller like a buyer with the same rushed script.","Call an expired or FSBO list unless your manager confirms scope and the list is scrubbed.","Ask about debts, divorce details or the will. If they share, listen and log only what the agent needs.","Assume “just curious” means “not a seller.”"]}]},{"id":"I6-2","title":"Seller discovery with A.L.M.","minutes":7,"body":[{"t":"p","text":"You already use A.L.M. on buyer calls. With sellers, the three letters stay the same, but each one means something slightly different."},{"t":"table","head":["Letter","For a buyer","For a seller"],"rows":[["A: Appointment","A tour or buyer consultation with the agent.","A **listing consultation**: the agent visits the home (or meets by video) to look at it and talk about selling."],["L: Location","Where they want to buy.","**The property**: address, type, size, condition, recent updates. Plus where they are going next."],["M: Motivation","Why they are moving and when.","**Why they are selling and by when**, and what happens if it does not sell."]]},{"t":"p","text":"Seller discovery is a conversation, not a form. You do not need every answer on the first call. You need enough for the agent to walk into the consultation prepared, and you need the appointment. A good rule: **ask four to six questions, then ask for the appointment.** If the seller is talkative, let them talk and take notes. If they are short, keep it brief and get to the appointment faster."},{"t":"list","items":["**Reason:** “Can I ask what has you thinking about selling?”","**Timeline:** “Do you have a date in mind for when you’d like to be moved?”","**Next home:** “Where are you planning to go next? Will you be buying another home?” (If yes, that is also a buyer lead.)","**The property:** “Tell me a little about the home. How many bedrooms and bathrooms?” “Have you done any updates over the years?”","**Occupancy:** “Are you living in the home now, or is it rented or empty?”","**Decision makers:** “Is anyone else on the title or part of the decision?”","**Experience:** “Have you sold a home before?” “Have you talked with any other agents yet?”"]},{"t":"p","text":"Notice what is **not** on the list: “How much do you owe?” “What’s your mortgage payment?” “What price do you want?” These questions are important for the agent, but they touch money, value and terms, which belong in the consultation with a licensed professional. If the seller volunteers a number (“We need at least $500,000 to make this work”), write it down in their words and pass it on. Do not react, agree or disagree."},{"t":"script","title":"A home value lead: Linda Carter","lines":[{"who":"You","text":"Hi, is this Linda? This is Sofia, I’m an assistant with Rachel Moore’s team. Just so you know, this call may be recorded. You requested a home value report for 418 Maple Court a few minutes ago, is that right?"},{"who":"Lead","text":"Yes, I was just curious."},{"who":"You","text":"Of course. Lots of homeowners check. Can I ask what has you thinking about it?"},{"who":"Lead","text":"Well, my husband got a job offer in Phoenix. We might need to move by summer."},{"who":"You","text":"Congratulations to him! So summer is the goal. Will you be buying in Phoenix?"},{"who":"Lead","text":"Probably, yes. Once we sell here."},{"who":"You","text":"That makes sense. Tell me a little about the Maple Court home. How many bedrooms and bathrooms?"},{"who":"Lead","text":"Four bedrooms, two and a half baths. We redid the kitchen two years ago."},{"who":"You","text":"That’s great. Is your husband part of the decision too?"},{"who":"Lead","text":"Yes, we both own it."},{"who":"You","text":"Perfect. The best way to know what the home would sell for today is for Rachel to walk through it, see the kitchen and the updates, and show you real numbers. It takes about an hour, with both of you there. Would Tuesday at 5 or Thursday at 6 be better?"}]},{"t":"p","text":"In about three minutes, you learned the reason (job move), the timeline (summer), the next step (buying in Phoenix: a referral opportunity your agent may want to handle), the property basics, the recent update, and the decision makers. And you asked for the appointment with two times. Your note:"},{"t":"callout","title":"Example note","text":"**Date/Time:** Tue 2:14 PM. **Disposition:** Call, connected (4 min). **A:** Listing consult proposed Tue 5 PM or Thu 6 PM, both owners. **L:** 418 Maple Court, 4 bd / 2.5 ba, kitchen remodeled 2 years ago, owner-occupied. **M:** Husband’s job offer in Phoenix; wants to move by summer; plans to buy in Phoenix after selling. **Next step:** Confirm time by text; Rachel to prepare for consult; flag Phoenix purchase for possible referral."},{"t":"dodont","do":["Say your name, your client’s team and the recording notice at the start.","Ask four to six questions, then ask for the appointment.","Find out who is on the title and make sure everyone can attend.","Log a buy-and-sell as two needs, and tell your agent."],"dont":["Ask what they owe, what they want for the house or what their payment is.","Comment on the home: “That kitchen will add a lot of value.”","Spend 20 minutes on discovery and forget to ask for the appointment.","Book a consultation with only one of two owners without asking about the other."]}]},{"id":"I6-3","title":"“What is my home worth?” without giving a value","minutes":7,"body":[{"t":"p","text":"This is the most important skill in this module, and the easiest place to make a serious mistake. Almost every seller asks some version of: “So what do you think it’s worth?” You must **never** give a number, a range, a guess, or an opinion like “I think it’s on the high side.” You are not licensed to give an opinion of value, and in real estate that is a serious line. A wrong number also hurts everyone: too high and the seller is disappointed by the agent’s real analysis; too low and they may not list at all."},{"t":"callout","title":"The core idea","text":"You do not quote a price; your client does, at the home. “The agent can walk the property and show you what it would sell for today. Tuesday at 5 or Thursday at 6?”"},{"t":"p","text":"Why is the agent’s visit the right answer, and not just a way to avoid the question? Because it is true. A home’s value depends on things no website and no assistant can see from a phone call: the condition, the updates, the layout, the view, the street, and what similar homes nearby sold for recently. The agent prepares a **comparative market analysis (CMA)**: a study of recent nearby sales, homes currently for sale, and homes that did not sell. Then they adjust for what makes this home different. That takes a licensed professional and, ideally, a walk through the house."},{"t":"table","head":["Seller says","Never say","Say instead"],"rows":[["“What do you think it’s worth?”","“Homes there go for about $450K.”","“That’s exactly what Rachel will show you. She looks at recent sales nearby and at your home itself, so the number is real. Would Tuesday at 5 or Thursday at 6 work?”"],["“Just give me a ballpark.”","“Somewhere between $400K and $500K.”","“I wish I could, but any number from me would be a guess, and with your biggest asset you deserve a real one. Rachel can give you that in one visit.”"],["“The website says it’s worth $520,000.”","“That sounds about right.” / “Those sites are always wrong.”","“Those online estimates are a helpful starting point. They can’t see inside your home or your new kitchen, though. Rachel can show you how your home compares. Tuesday at 5 or Thursday at 6?”"],["“My neighbor sold for $600K, so mine is worth that.”","“Then yours is probably similar.”","“That’s really useful to know, I’ll note it for Rachel. She’ll look at that sale and others and compare them to your home.”"],["“Can you just email me the report?”","Sends a value you made or found online.","“Of course, if Rachel has a report ready, I’ll ask her to send it. The most accurate number comes from her seeing the home. Can we find a time?” (Only send what your agent prepares and approves.)"]]},{"t":"p","text":"Look at the website row carefully. Do not attack online estimates and do not agree with them. Both are opinions of value. Online estimates are automated: a computer guesses from public records and nearby sales. They can be close or far off. Your neutral line (“a helpful starting point... they can’t see inside your home”) is true and respectful."},{"t":"script","title":"Holding the line with a persistent seller: Robert","lines":[{"who":"Lead","text":"Look, I don’t want a sales pitch. I just want a number. What’s my house worth?"},{"who":"You","text":"I completely understand, Robert. You want a real answer, not a pitch. Can I ask what you’d do with the number?"},{"who":"Lead","text":"If it’s enough, we’d sell next spring and move closer to our daughter."},{"who":"You","text":"That makes sense, being closer to family. Here’s the honest answer: I’m an assistant, and I’m not able to give values. Any number from me would be a guess. Rachel can look at the home and the recent sales and give you a real number, with no obligation to list. It takes about 45 minutes. Would Saturday at 10 or Monday at 5 be easier?"},{"who":"Lead","text":"No obligation?"},{"who":"You","text":"None. You’ll just know where you stand for spring."},{"who":"Lead","text":"Okay. Saturday at 10."}]},{"t":"p","text":"Robert’s “I just want a number” became a Motivation (move closer to his daughter), a timeline (next spring) and an appointment. You used ask, listen, mirror from module I5, you were honest about your role, and you never gave a value. “No obligation” is a phrase to use only if your agent agrees with it; check your client’s preferred wording."},{"t":"dodont","do":["Say clearly that you are an assistant and not able to give values.","Explain why the agent’s visit gives a real number: condition, updates and recent nearby sales.","Log any number the seller mentions, in their words, with the source (“per lead,” “online estimate”).","Send a value report only if your agent prepared it and asked you to send it."],"dont":["Give a number, a range, a price per square foot, or “about what the neighbor got.”","Say whether their number is high, low or reasonable.","Look up recent sales and read them to the seller.","Promise a selling price, a time on market or that the home “will sell fast.”"]},{"t":"callout","title":"Why this protects you","text":"An opinion of value from an unlicensed person can break your client’s state rules and create legal risk for your client. On the QA rubric, giving a value costs you Compliance points, and your coach treats it as a serious error. Saying “That’s exactly what the agent will show you” is never wrong."}]},{"id":"I6-4","title":"Setting the listing consultation","minutes":6,"body":[{"t":"p","text":"The listing consultation (also called the listing appointment or listing presentation) is where the agent meets the seller, sees the home, shares the market analysis, explains how they would sell it, and, if the seller is ready, signs a listing agreement. You never discuss or sign the listing agreement, and you never discuss commission. Your job is to get the right people, at the right time, in the right place, with the agent fully prepared."},{"t":"steps","items":["**Ask with two times.** “Would Tuesday at 5 or Thursday at 6 be better?” Never “When works for you?” Use the agent’s real availability from their calendar.","**Get every decision maker.** “Will everyone on the title be able to be there?” If one owner travels, offer a video option for that person if your agent allows it, or find a time that works for all.","**Confirm the place.** Usually the property itself. For a vacant home or a seller who lives far away, ask your agent whether a video meeting or a meeting at the office is acceptable.","**Set expectations.** “It usually takes about an hour. Rachel will walk through the home and then sit down with you to go over the numbers.”","**Confirm by text** right away, if they agreed to texts, with the date, time, address and the agent’s name.","**Create the calendar invite** and move the stage. Then write the note."]},{"t":"p","text":"Why do all decision makers matter so much? Because if one owner is missing, the seller cannot decide, and the agent often has to come back a second time. Worse, the missing person may meet another agent in the meantime. In divorce or inheritance situations, there may be two or more people with different opinions. Ask the question neutrally and let the agent handle the rest."},{"t":"script","title":"Booking the consultation with everyone: the Nguyens","lines":[{"who":"You","text":"Rachel can walk through the home and show you what it would sell for today. Would Tuesday at 5 or Thursday at 6 be better?"},{"who":"Lead","text":"Thursday, but my wife works late on Thursdays."},{"who":"You","text":"Thanks for telling me. Since you both own the home, it’s best if you’re both there, so all your questions get answered at once. Would Saturday at 10 or Saturday at 1 work for both of you?"},{"who":"Lead","text":"Saturday at 10 is good."},{"who":"You","text":"Perfect. Saturday at 10 at 27 Lakeview Drive, with Rachel Moore. It takes about an hour. I’ll text you the details now, and you’ll get a reminder the day before. Is there anything you’d like Rachel to know or bring?"},{"who":"Lead","text":"We want to know how long it usually takes to sell."},{"who":"You","text":"Great question. I’ll put it in my note so she’s ready to cover it."}]},{"t":"p","text":"The calendar invite is your handoff to the agent. Build it so the agent can prepare in five minutes without calling you:"},{"t":"table","head":["Invite field","What goes in it"],"rows":[["Title","Listing consult: Tran and Mai Nguyen, 27 Lakeview Dr"],["Time and place","Saturday 10:00 AM (lead’s time zone), at the property"],["Follow Up Boss link","Paste the lead profile link"],["Property","3 bd / 2 ba, single family, new roof 2023, owner-occupied (per lead)"],["Motivation and timeline","Want to move closer to family in Sacramento by fall; will buy there"],["Decision makers","Both owners attending"],["Seller questions","How long homes usually take to sell; they mentioned an online estimate of $610K"],["Reminders","24-hour and 1-hour text reminders to the seller (if they agreed to texts)"]]},{"t":"p","text":"Then move the Follow Up Boss stage to **Pending Handoff**, so the team knows an appointment is set and nurture automations stop (your manager confirms how your client’s Follow Up Boss is set up). Write your A.L.M. note with the same facts. And the night before, send a short confirmation text with the address and the agent’s name: many no-shows happen because nobody confirmed."},{"t":"callout","title":"If the seller wants the agent to call first","text":"Some sellers prefer to talk with the agent by phone before an in-person visit. That is fine. Book a phone appointment with two time options, and use a 3-way warm handoff if the agent is available right now (module I8). Never leave it as “the agent will call you sometime.”"},{"t":"dodont","do":["Check the agent’s calendar before offering times.","Repeat the date, time, address and agent’s name before you end the call.","Write the seller’s questions in the invite so the agent prepares.","Confirm the night before."],"dont":["Book over another appointment or outside the agent’s working hours.","Promise what the agent will say about price or commission.","Send listing agreements or explain them.","Forget the stage change: automations may keep emailing a seller who already booked."]}]},{"id":"I6-5","title":"Seller objections and sensitive situations","minutes":7,"body":[{"t":"p","text":"Sellers object for different reasons than buyers. They may be protecting their pride in the home, testing the market, hoping to save money by selling alone, or dealing with a painful life event. Use the same tools from module I5: **ask, listen, mirror,** then **acknowledge, pivot, appointment.** And remember: if they say stop, it is an opt-out, not an objection."},{"t":"table","head":["Seller objection","A reply that works"],"rows":[["“We’re just testing the market.”","“That’s smart, it’s good to know where you stand. The fastest way to really know is for the agent to walk through the home and show you what it would sell for today, with no pressure to list. Would Tuesday at 5 or Thursday at 6 work?”"],["“We’re not selling for six months.”","“That’s helpful to know, thank you. A lot of sellers meet the agent a few months early so they know what to fix and what to leave alone before listing. Would a visit next month be useful? If not, when should I check back?”"],["“We’ll sell it ourselves.” (FSBO)","“I understand wanting to keep control of it. Can I ask what’s most important to you in the sale?” Listen. “Rachel can share how she would market it, and you can decide what makes sense. Would a short meeting be useful?” (Only if your scope allows FSBO contact.)"],["“We already have an agent we like / a friend who is an agent.”","“That’s great. Have you signed a listing agreement with them?” If yes: respect it, note it, tell your agent, never pitch around it. If no: “Would a second opinion be helpful before you decide?”"],["“Your agent will just tell me a high price to get the listing.”","“That’s a fair worry. Rachel shows the recent sales she used, so you can see exactly where the number comes from. You can ask her anything.”"],["“What’s your commission?”","“Rachel will go through all of that with you at the consultation. I’m not able to discuss it, but she’ll answer every question.”"],["“We need to find our next home first.”","“That’s really common. Rachel helps people plan the order so they’re not stuck between homes. Where are you hoping to move next?” Log it as a buy-and-sell."],["“The house needs too much work to sell.”","“A lot of homes sell with work needed. Rachel can tell you what is worth fixing and what isn’t. Would you like her to take a look?”"]]},{"t":"p","text":"Some seller calls touch hard life events: a death in the family, a divorce, a job loss, being behind on payments. These calls need extra care. You are not a counselor, a lawyer or a financial advisor, and you do not need to be. You need to be kind, patient and clear about what happens next."},{"t":"list","items":["**Inheritance:** start with condolences. “I’m so sorry for your loss.” Do not ask about the will or probate. If they mention it, log it: “Estate sale; probate status unknown, per lead.” Ask who else in the family is part of the decision.","**Divorce:** stay neutral and never take sides. Ask if both owners can be part of the meeting, or whether the agent should contact each one separately. Log facts, not feelings.","**Behind on payments or facing foreclosure:** do not give legal or financial advice, do not promise a sale will fix it, and do not mention lenders or programs. Say: “Thank you for trusting me with that. Rachel can talk with you about your options, and the sooner the better. Can we set a time today or tomorrow?” Tell your agent the same day.","**Tenant-occupied homes:** do not tell the owner what they can do with tenants. Log “tenant-occupied, lease ends [date], per lead.” The agent handles tenant questions."]},{"t":"script","title":"Gentle and clear: an inherited home","lines":[{"who":"Lead","text":"It was my father’s house. He passed in the spring. My brother and I don’t really know what to do with it."},{"who":"You","text":"I’m so sorry about your father. Thank you for telling me. There’s no rush on this call. Can I ask what you and your brother are thinking so far?"},{"who":"Lead","text":"Neither of us lives nearby, so we probably have to sell. But we haven’t even cleaned it out."},{"who":"You","text":"That’s a lot to handle from far away. Rachel helps families in this situation often. She could walk through the house, by video if you’re not nearby, and explain the options and steps, with no pressure. Would it be possible for you and your brother to join together, maybe Wednesday at 6 or Saturday at 11?"}]},{"t":"callout","title":"Fair housing applies to sellers too","text":"If a seller says “I only want to sell to a certain kind of family” or asks about the buyers’ background, do not agree and do not argue. Say: “That’s something Rachel will talk with you about; the law requires treating every buyer the same.” Log it neutrally and tell your agent the same day."}]},{"id":"I6-6","title":"Follow-up that respects their timeline","minutes":5,"body":[{"t":"p","text":"Most seller leads are not ready to list this week. A homeowner who says “Maybe next year” is not a lost lead. **“Not for six months” is a date, not a no.** Put it in Follow Up Boss with a next action, send something useful in between, and never pressure a seller who already told you when. Sellers remember the team that stayed in touch respectfully, and they list with that team."},{"t":"table","head":["Seller timeline","Smart List / stage","Follow-up rhythm (inside the calling window)"],"rows":[["Ready now (0-30 days)","Hot Active","Book the consultation now. If not booked, call again within 24 hours."],["Soon (1-3 months)","Hot or warm nurture, per your client’s stages","Call or text every 1-2 weeks. Ask what has changed. Offer the consultation at every contact."],["Later (3-6 months)","30-60 Day Nurture","A useful touch every 2-4 weeks (a market update the agent approves, a note about recent sales the agent prepared). A call each month."],["Far out (6-12+ months)","Long-term nurture","A monthly touch, a call every 2-3 months, and a call 3-4 months before their stated date."],["Opted out","Archived / DNC","No contact. Ever. Never delete the record."]]},{"t":"p","text":"What counts as “useful”? Something that helps the seller make a decision, not something that only asks for one. Examples: a short market update your agent prepared, a reminder of their own timeline (“You mentioned wanting to move before the school year; many sellers start preparing about 90 days before”), or an offer the agent approves, like a list of simple things to fix before photos. Never send your own value opinion, and never send market numbers your agent has not approved."},{"t":"script","title":"A respectful follow-up call: Linda, four months later","lines":[{"who":"You","text":"Hi Linda, it’s Sofia with Rachel Moore’s team. This call may be recorded. In June you mentioned possibly moving to Phoenix by next summer. I’m just checking in. How are things going with the move?"},{"who":"Lead","text":"Actually, his start date moved up. Now it’s April."},{"who":"You","text":"Oh, that’s much sooner. Thank you for telling me. With April in mind, many sellers like to meet the agent three or four months before. Would it help to have Rachel walk through the home in early December, so you have a plan before the holidays? Maybe December 3 at 5 or December 5 at 6?"}]},{"t":"p","text":"You opened with **their** words and **their** timeline, not with “Are you ready to sell yet?” You learned the timeline changed. And you asked for the appointment with two dates. If Linda had said nothing changed, you would thank her, ask if anything new would be helpful, and set the next follow-up date out loud: “I’ll check in again in early January, is that okay?”"},{"t":"steps","items":["After every seller call, set a Follow Up Boss task with a specific date that matches their timeline.","Put the seller on the right Smart List and stage the same day.","Write the reason and the timeline in their words so the next caller starts where you left off.","Before each follow-up, read the last note. Open with something they told you.","When the timeline changes, update the stage, the task dates and the note immediately, and tell your agent if the seller is now ready."]},{"t":"dodont","do":["Respect a stated date. Call when you said you would, not every week.","Offer the consultation at every contact, gently.","Text only sellers who agreed to texts, inside the window.","Tell your agent the same day when a seller moves from “later” to “now.”"],"dont":["Ask “Are you ready to sell yet?” as your opening line.","Send generic “Just checking in!” messages with nothing useful.","Keep calling someone who said “Please stop.” That is an opt-out.","Let a nurture seller go more than 10 days past their scheduled follow-up."]},{"t":"callout","title":"The seller mindset","text":"Listen for the reason. Never give a value. Get everyone to the consultation. And when they say “later,” make “later” a date you keep."}]}],"practice":{"title":"Seller call lab: discovery, value questions and the consultation invite","minutes":90,"intro":"You will practice the full seller call, from the first question to a calendar invite the agent can act on, in a Follow Up Boss practice account (or a practice document if your account is not ready). Your practice partner or coach plays the seller. Record every role-play. Use only fictional names, addresses, 555 phone numbers and example.com emails. Never give a value, even in practice.","steps":["Prepare (10 min): write your own seller discovery question list (4-6 questions) and your one-sentence reply to “What is my home worth?”. Read both out loud five times.","Role-play 1 (10 min): a home value form lead, “Linda Carter”, 418 Maple Court, husband has a job offer in another state. Run discovery, find out who is on the title, and book a listing consultation with two time options.","Role-play 2 (8 min): “Robert”, who says “I don’t want a pitch, I just want a number” three times, then “The website says $520,000.” Hold the line, find the Motivation and ask for the appointment.","Role-play 3 (8 min): an inherited home owned by two siblings who live in different states. Use a gentle tone, offer condolences, and book a video consultation with both siblings.","Role-play 4 (8 min): a seller who says “We’re not selling for six months” and “We might just sell it ourselves.” Turn “six months” into a date and a follow-up plan.","Compliance check (6 min): your partner asks, in any order, “What’s your commission?”, “Would they pay $600K for it?”, “Are the buyers going to be families?” and “Stop calling me.” Answer each correctly.","CRM work (25 min): for role-plays 1 and 3, create the lead in the practice account (or document), write the full A.L.M. note, set the stage to Pending Handoff, and write the complete calendar invite using the fields from lesson I6-4. For role-play 4, write the note, choose the Smart List and stage, and set three dated follow-up tasks that match the seller’s timeline.","Self-review (15 min): listen to your recordings. Mark every place you were close to giving a value, an opinion of the home, or a price comment, and rewrite that sentence."],"deliverable":"Send your coach, in your BeyondVAs training channel: the four role-play recordings plus the compliance check recording, the three notes, the two calendar invites, the follow-up task plan, and your self-review with rewritten sentences. Your coach checks that you gave zero values or price opinions, got every decision maker, and offered two times."},"quiz":[{"q":"A seller asks, “What do you think my house is worth?” What do you say?","options":["Give a range based on recent sales nearby","Say the online estimate is probably right","“That’s exactly what the agent will show you, from your home and recent nearby sales. Would Tuesday at 5 or Thursday at 6 work?”","Tell them you will look up the neighbors’ sales and text them"],"answer":2,"explain":"You do not quote a price; your client does, at the home. Never give a number, range or opinion of value."},{"q":"A seller says, “The website says my home is worth $520,000.” Which reply is best?","options":["“Those online estimates are a helpful starting point, but they can’t see inside your home. The agent can show you how yours compares.”","“That sounds about right for your area.”","“Those websites are always wrong, ignore them.”","“You could probably get more than that.”"],"answer":0,"explain":"Agreeing or disagreeing are both opinions of value. Stay neutral and offer the agent’s visit."},{"q":"In seller A.L.M., what does the A stand for?","options":["Asking price","Agent agreement","Address","The appointment: a listing consultation with the agent"],"answer":3,"explain":"A is always the appointment. For sellers, that is the listing consultation."},{"q":"Which question should you leave for the agent instead of asking it yourself?","options":["“Can I ask what has you thinking about selling?”","“How much do you still owe on the mortgage?”","“Is anyone else on the title?”","“Where are you planning to go next?”"],"answer":1,"explain":"Money, value and terms belong in the consultation with a licensed professional. If the seller volunteers a number, log it in their words."},{"q":"A married couple owns the home, but only one spouse can meet on Thursday. What do you do?","options":["Book Thursday with one spouse; the other can hear about it later","Cancel and call back next month","Explain that it’s best if both owners attend, and offer two times that work for both","Ask the agent to go twice"],"answer":2,"explain":"Every decision maker should be there so the seller can decide and the agent does not need a second visit."},{"q":"After you book a listing consultation, which Follow Up Boss stage do you set?","options":["Pending Handoff","Archived / DNC","30-60 Day Nurture","New Lead"],"answer":0,"explain":"Pending Handoff tells the team an appointment is set, so the agent takes over and nurture messages should not continue."},{"q":"You want to call a list of expired listings for your client. What must be true first?","options":["Nothing: expired listings are public","Your manager confirms your scope allows it, and the list is scrubbed against the National DNC Registry and your client’s DNC list","The sellers must have a new agent","You must call before 9 AM, when sellers are home"],"answer":1,"explain":"Scope depends on your client’s state, and any non-inbound list must be DNC-scrubbed. Calls stay inside the calling window."},{"q":"A seller says, “We’re not selling for six months.” What is the best response?","options":["Mark the lead as not interested","Call every week until they change their mind","Tell them prices will drop if they wait","Thank them, ask what happens in six months, set a dated follow-up plan, and send useful updates your agent approves"],"answer":3,"explain":"“Not for six months” is a date, not a no. Respect the timeline and make follow-up useful."},{"q":"A seller who inherited a home starts telling you about the will and a family disagreement. What do you do?","options":["Listen kindly, do not give legal advice, log only what the agent needs, and ask who is part of the decision","Explain how probate works in their state","Tell them which sibling is right","End the call because it is too personal"],"answer":0,"explain":"Be kind and patient, stay neutral, give no legal advice, and pass the facts the agent needs."},{"q":"A seller asks, “What’s your commission?” What do you say?","options":["Quote the usual percentage in your area","Say commissions are negotiable and offer a discount","“The agent will go through all of that with you at the consultation. I’m not able to discuss it.”","Say there is no commission"],"answer":2,"explain":"Commission, price and terms are outside an ISA’s scope. Pass them to the agent and keep the appointment."}]},{"id":"I7","part":"isa","title":"Follow Up Boss discipline","summary":"Work your Smart Lists in the right order, keep every stage, tag, task and A.L.M. note correct the same day, and close each shift with a CRM that any agent can trust.","objectives":["Work the four Smart Lists in the required order and explain why a new lead interrupts everything else.","Move a lead to the correct stage the same day its situation changes, and apply tags the way your client defines them.","Write a complete A.L.M. note (Date/Time, Disposition, A, L, M, Next Step) for every call and text, so the agent never asks the lead the same question twice.","Give every active lead a dated next task, and pause or remove action plans at the right moments (reply, handoff, opt-out).","Close your day in 10 minutes: clean lists, no overdue tasks, tomorrow's first three calls written down."],"lessons":[{"id":"I7-1","title":"If it is not in the CRM, it did not happen","minutes":7,"body":[{"t":"p","text":"Follow Up Boss (people say “FUB”) is the CRM most of our clients use. A CRM is a customer relationship management system: one place that holds every lead, every call, every text, every note and every appointment. For an ISA, the CRM is not an extra task after the real work. **The CRM is the real work that other people can see.**"},{"t":"p","text":"Think about who reads what you write. Your client, the agent, opens a lead before a tour and wants to know in 30 seconds who this person is and what they want. The team leader looks at reports to decide which leads go to which agent. Your BeyondVAs coordinator checks your activity to coach you. The QA reviewer scores you, and **Follow Up Boss Hygiene is 15 of the 100 points** on your scorecard. None of these people heard your call. They only see what you logged."},{"t":"callout","title":"The rule","text":"**If it is not in the CRM, it did not happen.** A great call with no note is, for your client, a call that never happened. A booked tour that is not in the calendar is a tour the agent will miss."},{"t":"p","text":"**What counts as an update.** Every one of these is a CRM update, and every one should be logged on the lead's profile:"},{"t":"list","items":["A call you made, connected or not, with its outcome (Follow Up Boss calls this the call result or disposition).","A text you sent or received. If you text from inside Follow Up Boss, it logs itself. If your client uses another texting tool, you add a note.","An email, if it was personal and not part of an automatic plan.","A note: your A.L.M. note after any conversation, or a short factual note after a no-answer.","A stage change, a new tag, a new or completed task, an appointment created."]},{"t":"p","text":"**Five to eight updates per new connection.** A connection is a lead you have spoken with. Aim for five to eight updates on each new connection during its first month (your client may set a different number): calls, texts, notes, a stage change, tasks. This is not busywork. A lead with eight useful touches in a month has been worked; a lead with one has been forgotten."},{"t":"p","text":"**The 10-day rule.** Every lead you are responsible for gets a real update at least every 10 days. Many teams treat a lead with no update in 10 days as abandoned, and it can be reassigned to someone else. When you look at a lead and the last activity is 9 days old, that lead is your next call, not tomorrow's."},{"t":"p","text":"**A quick tour of a lead profile.** Every client sets up Follow Up Boss a little differently, but most lead profiles show the same parts. Learn where each one is in your client's account during your first day:"},{"t":"table","head":["Part of the profile","What you check or do there"],"rows":[["Name, phone, email","Correct spelling. Add a second phone if the lead gives one. Never type a guess."],["Source","Where the lead came from (a portal, a Facebook ad, the website, an open house). It tells you which script to use."],["Stage","Where the lead is in the pipeline. You move it the same day it changes (lesson 3)."],["Assigned to","The agent (and sometimes the ISA) who owns this lead. Do not change it unless your manager tells you."],["Tags","Short labels for things like timeline, language or DNC (lesson 3)."],["Timeline / activity","Every call, text, email and note in order. Read it before you dial."],["Tasks and appointments","What is due next and when. Every active lead needs one (lesson 5)."],["Action plans","Automatic sequences of emails, texts or tasks (lesson 5)."]]},{"t":"p","text":"**Read before you dial.** Before every call, take 15 seconds to read the last three timeline entries and the last note. Nothing sounds worse to a lead than “So, are you looking to buy?” when they told your colleague yesterday that they are buying a 3-bedroom in Mesa in April. Starting with “Hi Dana, last week you mentioned April and a 3-bedroom in Mesa. Is that still the plan?” shows you listen."},{"t":"dodont","do":["Log the call result right after you hang up, before the next dial.","Write in English, in full words, so any agent can read it.","Read the last note before every call.","Keep everything inside Follow Up Boss and your client's approved tools."],"dont":["Keep notes on paper, in a personal notebook or in your phone “to add later”.","Text or call leads from a personal phone or WhatsApp outside the client's system.","Export, download or copy client lead lists to your computer. Never.","Change the assigned agent, merge duplicate leads or delete anything without your manager's OK."]},{"t":"p","text":"A last point about trust. Your client cannot stand behind you while you work. Your Hubstaff screenshots show that you were working; your Follow Up Boss record shows **what** you did and **how well**. A clean CRM is the strongest proof of your value, and it is the first thing a happy client mentions when they renew."}]},{"id":"I7-2","title":"Smart Lists: the order you work every shift","minutes":7,"body":[{"t":"p","text":"A Smart List in Follow Up Boss is a saved filter. It shows every lead that matches some rules right now, for example “created in the last 15 minutes” or “has a task due today”. The list updates itself: when a lead stops matching, it leaves the list. Smart Lists turn a database of thousands of people into a short, ordered to-do list."},{"t":"p","text":"Your client or your manager builds the Smart Lists. Your job is to work them in the right order, every shift, without skipping."},{"t":"steps","items":["**New Speed-to-Lead** (0 to 15 minutes old). Brand-new leads. You call within 180 seconds of arrival, inside the calling window. This list always comes first.","**Hot Active** (ready within 30 days). Leads who want to buy or sell soon. They are close to an appointment or already have one coming.","**Scheduled Follow-Up Tasks** (due today). Every call or text you or the agent promised for today.","**30 to 60 Day Nurtures** (check-ins). Leads who are a month or two away. You keep the relationship warm until they are ready."]},{"t":"callout","title":"Why this order","text":"The order follows the money and the clock. A new lead is worth the most in the first 5 minutes: contacting it within 5 minutes makes the odds of qualifying about 21 times higher than at 30 minutes. A hot lead can choose another agent this week. A task due today is a promise. A nurture lead can wait an hour without harm."},{"t":"p","text":"**New leads interrupt everything.** Keep the New Speed-to-Lead list (or Follow Up Boss notifications) open in a tab the whole shift. If a new lead arrives while you are in the middle of your nurture calls, finish the current call politely, log it in one line, and dial the new lead. Come back to write the full note after. The only things that do not get interrupted are a live conversation that is going well and a 3-way handoff in progress."},{"t":"p","text":"**How to work one list.** Open the list and sort it the way your client asks (often by oldest task first, or by last activity). Then, for each lead:"},{"t":"steps","items":["Read the last note and the last three activities (15 seconds).","Check the lead's time zone and the calling window. If it is before 9:00 AM or after 8:00 PM where the lead lives (noon on Sunday), skip and come back.","Check the tags: DNC means no contact at all. No text consent means call only.","Call. Text only if the lead agreed to texts.","Log the result and an A.L.M. note, update the stage and tags, set the next task.","Move to the next lead. The lead you just finished should drop off the list if you did step 5 correctly."]},{"t":"p","text":"**Common mistake: cherry-picking.** It is tempting to call the leads that look friendly and skip the ones that look hard. Do not. The lead with three no-answers is exactly the lead who needs your fourth try. Work top to bottom. If you skip a lead on purpose (for example, the calling window has not opened in Hawaii yet), leave a short note or task so you come back."},{"t":"p","text":"**Common mistake: working from the full database.** The “All leads” view is not a work list. If you scroll through all leads and call whoever you see, you will call people twice, miss people completely and call leads who are already with an agent. Use the Smart Lists."},{"t":"p","text":"**When a list is empty.** An empty list is good news: it means that list is done. Move to the next one. When all four are done, ask your manager what comes next (often a reactivation list your client has DNC-scrubbed, covered in module I9). Never invent your own list by filtering old leads and dialing them; old lists need a Do Not Call scrub before any call."},{"t":"table","head":["Time in your shift","What you do"],"rows":[["First 10 minutes","Open Follow Up Boss, check the New Speed-to-Lead list and any leads that came in overnight (their first call goes out as soon as the window opens in their time zone)."],["Most of the shift","Hot Active, then Scheduled Follow-Up Tasks, then 30 to 60 Day Nurtures, with new leads interrupting as they arrive."],["Last 10 minutes","Close the day (lesson 6)."]]},{"t":"p","text":"**Time zones matter for list order.** You may work for a team in Arizona and have leads from Florida. The calling window is based on the lead's local time, not yours and not the agent's. At the start of your shift, East Coast leads may already be callable while Pacific leads are not. Work the callable ones first and come back. If you are not sure of a lead's time zone, look at the area code and the address, and ask your manager if they disagree."}]},{"id":"I7-3","title":"Stages and tags: the lead's status at a glance","minutes":7,"body":[{"t":"p","text":"A **stage** says where a lead is in the pipeline. Every lead has exactly one stage at a time. The stage drives Smart Lists, reports and often automations, so a wrong stage causes real problems: a lead who already has a tour keeps getting “are you still looking?” texts, or a hot buyer sits in a nurture list nobody calls."},{"t":"p","text":"Stage names are different in every client's Follow Up Boss. Your client may say “Prospect” where another says “Contacted”. In training we use the stages below. On your first day, ask your manager for your client's stage list and write down which of their stages matches each of these."},{"t":"table","head":["Training stage","Means","Move a lead here when"],"rows":[["New Lead","Nobody has spoken with this person yet.","It arrives. You do not move leads back to New Lead."],["Contacted","You reached them, but the timeline or need is not clear yet.","You had a first real conversation."],["Warm Nurture","Real interest, but not ready in the next 30 days.","A.L.M. shows a timeline of one month or more."],["Hot","Ready within 30 days, close to an appointment.","The lead wants to see homes, list or meet soon."],["Pending Handoff","An appointment with an agent is booked.","You book the appointment. Immediately."],["Archived / DNC","Do not contact.","The lead opts out (“STOP”, “remove me”, “don't call”)."]]},{"t":"callout","title":"Same day, every time","text":"Move the stage **the day it changes**, during or right after the call. Not on Friday. Not at the end of the week. A stage that is three days old is a wrong stage for three days."},{"t":"p","text":"**Pending Handoff after booking.** When you book an appointment, the stage goes to Pending Handoff right away. This tells the team the lead belongs to an agent now, and it stops the cold drip campaigns that would otherwise keep texting the lead. Module I8 covers the full booking routine."},{"t":"p","text":"**Archived / DNC after an opt-out.** When a lead says “STOP”, “remove me” or “don't call”, you tag them DNC, move them to Archived / DNC, pause every automation and log the opt-out in a note. **Never delete an opted-out contact.** The record with the DNC tag is what stops anyone on the team from calling or texting them again. A deleted contact can come back through a new portal inquiry or an old list and get called by mistake."},{"t":"p","text":"**Do not use Archived for “annoying” leads.** A lead who did not answer six times is not a DNC. A lead who said “not interested right now” is not a DNC either; they go to nurture with a longer cadence. Only a clear request to stop contact, or a manager's instruction, makes a lead DNC."},{"t":"p","text":"**Tags.** A tag is a short label you add to a lead. A lead can have many tags. Smart Lists and action plans often use tags, so spelling matters: “Spanish” and “spanish ” (with a space) can be two different tags. Use only the tags your client already has. If you think a new tag is needed, ask in the client channel; do not create your own."},{"t":"table","head":["Common tag","Use it when"],"rows":[["DNC","The lead opted out. Always with stage Archived / DNC."],["Spanish","The lead prefers Spanish. Your agent may want to assign a Spanish-speaking agent."],["Text OK / No Text","Whether the lead agreed to texts. Text only leads who agreed."],["Has Agent","The lead signed an agreement with another agent. You respect it and never pitch around it."],["Timeline tags (0-30, 30-60, 60-90, 6-12 mo)","How soon they plan to move. Many nurture Smart Lists are built on these."],["Seller / Buyer / Both","What the lead wants to do."],["Bad Number","The phone number is disconnected or belongs to someone else. Note what you tried."]]},{"t":"p","text":"**“I already have an agent.”** This answer changes tags and stage, so learn it well. Ask whether they signed an agreement with that agent. If yes, respect it: tag Has Agent (or your client's equivalent), write a note with the agent's name if they shared it, tell your agent in the client channel, and stop pitching. If they did not sign, they are still free to choose: offer a second opinion from your agent and ask for a time."},{"t":"dodont","do":["Check stage and tags as part of every call, not only when something big happens.","Use the exact tag names from your client's list.","Ask your manager before archiving anyone who did not opt out."],"dont":["Leave a booked lead in Hot or Warm Nurture.","Delete an opted-out contact, or remove a DNC tag to “try one more time”.","Tag people by who they are (age, religion, family status, where they come from). Tag what they want in a home, never who they are. That is a fair housing rule."]}]},{"id":"I7-4","title":"The A.L.M. note: write it so nobody asks twice","minutes":6,"body":[{"t":"p","text":"After every conversation you write a note in Follow Up Boss. Not “Good call!” and not a thumbs up. Every note follows the same structure, so any agent can read any note from any ISA and find the information in the same place."},{"t":"p","text":"Every note logged in Follow Up Boss must list:"},{"t":"list","items":["**Date/Time**: when the contact happened.","**Call/Text Disposition**: what kind of contact and what happened (for example: Call, connected, 4 min; Call, no answer, VM left; Text, replied).","**A** (Appointment): the proposed or confirmed tour or consultation time.","**L** (Location): target neighborhoods, beds, baths, price.","**M** (Motivation): the reason for moving and the timeline.","**Next Step**: the action item and the assigned agent."]},{"t":"script","title":"Example note","lines":[{"who":"Date/Time","text":"Tue 10:42 AM"},{"who":"Disposition","text":"Call, connected (4 min)"},{"who":"A","text":"Proposed tour Sat 11:00 AM"},{"who":"L","text":"Westside Lancaster, 4 bd / 2 ba, up to $550K"},{"who":"M","text":"Job relocation, wants to move by March"},{"who":"Next step","text":"Text 3 listings today; hand off to Agent Jordan"}]},{"t":"callout","title":"Why it matters","text":"Your agent should never have to ask the client the same question twice. When the agent calls and says “I see you are relocating for work and want a 4-bedroom on the Westside by March,” the lead feels the team is organized. When the agent asks “So, what are you looking for?” the lead feels nobody listened."},{"t":"p","text":"**Write facts, not feelings.** Write what the lead said, not your opinion of them. “Says pre-approved with a local credit union up to $480K; did not share the letter” is a fact. “Probably can't afford it” is an opinion, and it should never be in a note. Never write a personal opinion about anyone's credit, income or character. Never write anything you would not want the lead to read, because some day they might."},{"t":"p","text":"**Write what they want in the home, not who they are.** “Needs 4 bedrooms, single story, yard for a dog, under 30 minutes to downtown” is good. “Big family, older couple, from Mexico” is not; it describes the person, and it can lead to steering. Fair housing law protects people from being treated differently because of race, color, religion, sex, disability, familial status or national origin, and many states add more. Keep notes about the home and the plan."},{"t":"p","text":"**What if you did not learn everything?** Write what you know and mark the rest. “A: not scheduled yet, asked for Sat, lead will check with spouse. L: Gilbert or Chandler, 3 bd, price not shared. M: lease ends June 30.” Unknown is useful information too: it tells the next caller what to ask."},{"t":"p","text":"**Short notes for short contacts.** A no-answer still gets a note, but a short one: “Tue 3:10 PM. Call, no answer, 20-sec VM left. Text sent (Text OK). Next: call Wed 11 AM.” Short and factual is perfect."},{"t":"table","head":["Weak note","Strong note"],"rows":[["Talked to her, she's interested.","Wed 2:15 PM. Call, connected (6 min). A: proposed Sat 10 AM or 1 PM, lead will confirm by text tonight. L: Riverside, 3 bd / 2 ba, up to $450K. M: first-time buyer, lease ends Aug 31. Next: confirm time tonight; hand off to Agent Lee."],["No answer.","Thu 9:20 AM. Call, no answer, 20-sec VM. Text sent. Next: second call Thu 1 PM (before 6 PM her time)."],["Not a good lead, rude.","Fri 11:05 AM. Call, connected (1 min). Lead said he is busy at work, asked for a call after 5 PM. Next: call Fri 5:30 PM his time."]]},{"t":"p","text":"**Language and clarity.** Write in English, even if the call was in Spanish. Add “Call in Spanish; prefers Spanish” and tag Spanish so the agent knows. Avoid abbreviations your client does not use. “VM”, “bd”, “ba” and “K” for thousands are common and fine; private shorthand is not. Spell names, streets and cities correctly; copy them from the lead's profile or the listing."},{"t":"p","text":"**Timing.** Write the note right after the call, while you remember. A note written two hours later loses details. If a new lead arrives right after a call, write one line now (“Connected, Sat tour proposed, full note to follow”) and complete it within the hour."},{"t":"dodont","do":["Use the same six-part order every time.","Quote the lead's exact words when they matter (“We must be out by June 30”).","Write the next step with a day and time, and the name of the agent."],"dont":["Write opinions about credit, income, appearance or personality.","Leave A, L or M empty without saying “unknown” or “not shared”.","Copy a previous note and forget to change the date or details."]}]},{"id":"I7-5","title":"Tasks and action plans: every lead has a next step","minutes":6,"body":[{"t":"p","text":"A note records the past. A **task** schedules the future. In Follow Up Boss a task is a reminder attached to a lead: “Call Dana, Thursday 11:00 AM”. Tasks feed the Scheduled Follow-Up Tasks Smart List, which is how tomorrow's work appears in front of you without you having to remember it."},{"t":"callout","title":"The rule","text":"**Every active lead has one open task with a date.** If a lead is not DNC, not closed and not handed off, and it has no open task, it is falling through the cracks."},{"t":"p","text":"**How to write a good task.** A good task says what, when and why, in a few words:"},{"t":"list","items":["**Type**: call, text or email. Follow Up Boss lets you choose.","**Title**: “Call: confirm Sat tour time” or “Call: ask if lease renewal was decided”. Not just “Follow up”.","**Due date and time**: in a time that will be inside the lead's calling window. A task due at 8:00 AM Pacific time is a trap.","**Assigned to**: you, unless the task belongs to the agent. Do not assign tasks to an agent without the agent or manager agreeing to that system."]},{"t":"p","text":"**How far out?** The next task date depends on the lead's timeline and what you promised. If you said “I'll call you Thursday,” the task is Thursday. If the lead asked for a call after payday, the task is the day after payday. When there is no promise, use the cadence from your client or from module I9 (for example, every 2 to 3 days for hot leads, weekly for 1 to 3 months away)."},{"t":"p","text":"**Complete or reschedule. Never ignore.** When you do the task, mark it complete and create the next one. If you cannot do a task today, move it to a real new date with a reason in the note. Overdue tasks pile up quickly and hide the important ones; your goal is zero overdue tasks at the end of every shift."},{"t":"p","text":"**Action plans.** An action plan is an automatic sequence your client sets up: for example, an email on day 1, a text on day 3, a call task on day 5, an email on day 10. Clients use them so that new and nurture leads keep hearing from the team even on busy days. Your client decides which action plans exist and which leads go on them. You may be asked to start a plan on a lead, or to pause one."},{"t":"table","head":["Situation","What to do with automations"],"rows":[["The lead replies or you have a real conversation","Check whether the plan pauses by itself. Many clients set this up; if not, pause it so the lead does not get a “just checking in” text an hour after talking to you."],["You book an appointment","Stage to Pending Handoff, which stops cold drip campaigns. Check that no nurture plan is still running."],["The lead opts out","Pause every automation. Tag DNC, stage Archived / DNC, log it."],["The lead moves from hot to a 6-month timeline","Ask whether to start the client's long-term nurture plan, and set your own check-in task."],["A text in the plan says something wrong or outdated","Tell your manager and the client in the client channel. Do not edit the client's templates yourself."]]},{"t":"p","text":"**Automations do not replace you.** An automatic email on day 10 is nice, but it does not ask about the lead's motivation, handle an objection or book a tour. Action plans keep the door open. Your calls walk through it. Never assume “the plan will follow up” and skip your task."},{"t":"p","text":"**Never send mass messages yourself.** Do not select 200 leads and send them all the same text, even if Follow Up Boss lets you. Mass texting has strict consent rules, it is easy to hit DNC contacts, and one mistake can expose your client to fines. Mass messages are the client's decision, built by the client or your manager, sent only to leads who agreed to texts."},{"t":"p","text":"**Appointments are not tasks.** A tour or consultation goes in as an appointment (and on the agent's Google Calendar), not as a task that says “tour Saturday”. Module I8 covers this. Set a task for yourself to confirm the appointment the day before."},{"t":"dodont","do":["Create the next task before you leave the lead's profile.","Put the due time inside the lead's calling window.","Pause automations when the lead replies, books, or opts out."],"dont":["Write tasks that only say “Follow up”.","Leave overdue tasks at the end of a shift.","Edit the client's action plan templates or start mass texts without approval."]}]},{"id":"I7-6","title":"Close the day in 10 minutes","minutes":5,"body":[{"t":"p","text":"The last 10 minutes of your shift decide how your next shift starts. A clean close means tomorrow you open Follow Up Boss and know exactly what to do. A messy close means tomorrow starts with 30 minutes of guessing, and some lead waits too long."},{"t":"steps","items":["**Notes complete.** Every call and text today has its A.L.M. note or its short no-answer note. Finish any “full note to follow” lines.","**Stages and tags right.** Every lead you spoke with today is in the correct stage. Every booked appointment is Pending Handoff. Every opt-out is DNC and Archived / DNC with automations paused.","**Tasks.** No overdue tasks. Every active lead you touched has one open task with a date inside the calling window.","**Appointments.** Every appointment booked today is on the agent's calendar with the Follow Up Boss link, reminders and the buyer-agreement flag for tours (module I8).","**Tomorrow's first three calls.** Write them down at the top of your daily report or in a task: the three leads you will call first, and why.","**Daily report.** Post your end-of-shift report in the client channel (module I11 has the format)."]},{"t":"p","text":"**Short, factual notes.** At the end of a long day you may want to write “did follow ups”. Resist. Short is fine; vague is not. “Called, no answer, left VM, texted. Next: call Thu.” is short and complete."},{"t":"p","text":"**Why three calls?** Choosing tomorrow's first three calls tonight removes the slow start. Usually they are: a hot lead who asked for a morning call, a lead whose 10-day mark is tomorrow, and an appointment confirmation. When you clock in, the New Speed-to-Lead list comes first; then you go straight to those three."},{"t":"script","title":"Example: tomorrow's first three calls","lines":[{"who":"1","text":"Maria Lopez (Hot): asked for a call at 9:30 AM her time to confirm Saturday tour time."},{"who":"2","text":"Kevin Brooks (Warm Nurture): last update 9 days ago; ask about the lease decision."},{"who":"3","text":"Ana and Tom Reyes (Pending Handoff): confirm tomorrow's 4 PM listing consultation with Agent Patel."}]},{"t":"p","text":"**Data protection at the close.** Before you clock out, close Follow Up Boss and any client tabs. You never download or export lead lists, never take screenshots of lead data to share outside the client's tools, and never keep lead phone numbers on your own phone. Passwords live only in the password vault. If you used a shared computer by accident, tell your manager."},{"t":"p","text":"**Your Hubstaff time and your CRM tell the same story.** Your client can see your Hubstaff hours and screenshots. If your screenshots show four hours of work and Follow Up Boss shows 20 calls and clean notes, the story matches. If your screenshots show four hours and the CRM shows three calls and no notes, your client will ask why, and your manager will review it before billing. Log as you go and the two always agree."},{"t":"callout","title":"A quick self-check","text":"Open your client's Smart Lists one last time. New Speed-to-Lead: empty or only after-hours leads with a first-call task for the morning. Scheduled Follow-Up Tasks: nothing overdue. Any lead you touched today with no next task? Fix it. Then send your report and clock out."},{"t":"p","text":"**When something went wrong.** If you missed a callback, booked a time the agent cannot make, or forgot to tag an opt-out, do not hide it. Fix what you can now, write it in your daily report and tell your manager. Module I10 teaches how to own a mistake with a client. Hidden mistakes become client complaints; reported mistakes become coaching."}]}],"practice":{"title":"CRM hygiene drill in a practice account","minutes":75,"intro":"You will clean up and work a set of practice leads the way you would on a real shift. Use only the practice Follow Up Boss account or the practice sheet your coordinator gives you, never a real client account. The leads are fictional.","steps":["Ask your coordinator for practice access (a sandbox Follow Up Boss account or the practice workbook). Write down the stage names and tags in it and map them to the six training stages from lesson 3.","Open the practice account's four Smart Lists. Write in one sentence each what rule each list uses and in what order you will work them. (10 minutes)","Lead 1, Priya Shah, arrived 4 minutes ago from a portal, wants to tour Saturday, Phoenix, 3 bd up to $425K, moving for a new job in May, agreed to texts. Write her full A.L.M. note as if you called and proposed Saturday 10 AM, set the stage, the tags and her next task.","Lead 2, Robert King, has had no update in 11 days, stage Hot, last note says he wanted to tour “soon”. Write the call you would make (two sentences), then a short no-answer note, and the next task with a date and time inside his window (he lives in Florida).","Lead 3, Luisa Moreno, texted “please remove me”. Do every step of the opt-out in the practice account and write the note. Explain in one sentence why you do not delete her.","Lead 4, Jake Turner, said “I already signed with an agent from my church.” Write the note (what he wants in the home is not needed; the agent's name is not known; decide what you leave out of the note and why, using lesson 4), the tag and stage you use, and the one-line message you post for your agent in the client channel.","Lead 5, Hannah Cole, booked a listing consultation for Thursday 5 PM with Agent Patel. Set the stage, check that no nurture plan is running, and create your confirmation task for Wednesday.","Rewrite these three weak notes into strong A.L.M. notes, inventing realistic facts: “interested”, “no answer”, “nice family, wants good schools” (for the last one, explain what was wrong and what you write instead).","Do your 10-minute close on the practice account and write tomorrow's first three calls with a reason for each.","Take screenshots of the five lead profiles (practice account only) and put them with your notes in one document."],"deliverable":"One document (Google Doc in your BeyondVAs folder) with: your stage map, the Smart List order, the five lead notes and actions, the three rewritten notes, tomorrow's first three calls, and screenshots of the five practice lead profiles. Share it with your coach."},"quiz":[{"q":"When logging a call inside Follow Up Boss, what must always be included in the note?","options":["A personal opinion about the client’s credit score.","Standard ALM syntax covering Appointment, Location, Motivation, and Next Step.","Just a thumbs up emoji.","Only the phone number dialed."],"answer":1,"explain":"Structured ALM notes ensure field agents know exactly what was uncovered without asking the client the same questions twice."},{"q":"You are calling your 30 to 60 Day Nurture list when a lead from a portal arrives. What do you do?","options":["Finish the nurture list first; the new lead is on the Speed-to-Lead list and will wait.","Wrap up the current call politely, log one line, and dial the new lead within 180 seconds, inside the calling window.","Start an action plan on the new lead so the system contacts it for you.","Text the new lead that you will call tomorrow."],"answer":1,"explain":"New Speed-to-Lead always comes first. Contacting within 5 minutes makes the odds of qualifying about 21 times higher than at 30 minutes. You finish the full note after."},{"q":"What is the correct order for working Smart Lists every shift?","options":["Scheduled Follow-Up Tasks, Hot Active, New Speed-to-Lead, 30 to 60 Day Nurtures","Hot Active, New Speed-to-Lead, 30 to 60 Day Nurtures, Scheduled Follow-Up Tasks","30 to 60 Day Nurtures, Scheduled Follow-Up Tasks, Hot Active, New Speed-to-Lead","New Speed-to-Lead, Hot Active, Scheduled Follow-Up Tasks, 30 to 60 Day Nurtures"],"answer":3,"explain":"The order follows the clock and the money: brand-new leads (0 to 15 minutes), then leads ready within 30 days, then tasks due today, then 30 to 60 day check-ins."},{"q":"A lead has had no update in Follow Up Boss for 12 days. What is true?","options":["It broke the 10-day rule, can be reassigned, and should be your next call.","Nothing; leads only need an update once a month.","It should be moved to Archived / DNC because it is cold.","It should be deleted to keep the database clean."],"answer":0,"explain":"Every lead gets a real update at least every 10 days. Past that, many teams treat it as abandoned and can reassign it. Cold is not the same as DNC, and you never delete leads."},{"q":"A lead who opted out last month appears in your list again. What should have happened when she opted out?","options":["Her contact should have been deleted so nobody could see it.","She should have been moved to Warm Nurture with a 90-day task.","She should have been tagged DNC, moved to Archived / DNC, with all automations paused and the opt-out logged, and never deleted.","Her phone number should have been changed to a fake number."],"answer":2,"explain":"The DNC record is what stops future calls and texts. A deleted contact can come back through a new inquiry or an old list and get called by mistake."},{"q":"Which note follows the rules?","options":["“Nice older couple, probably can't afford much.”","“Mon 4:05 PM. Call, connected (5 min). A: proposed Sat 11 AM. L: Mesa, 3 bd / 2 ba, single story, up to $400K. M: downsizing, wants to move by May. Next: confirm Sat by text tonight; hand off to Agent Kim.”","“Good call! Will follow up.”","“Wants a safe, family-friendly area with good schools.”"],"answer":1,"explain":"It has all six parts, facts only, and describes the home and the plan. The others are opinions, vague, or describe people and neighborhoods in ways fair housing forbids."},{"q":"You finished a call and the lead is still on an action plan that will text “Are you still looking?” tomorrow. What do you do?","options":["Nothing; automations always know what to do.","Delete the action plan from the client's account.","Edit the template so it sounds better.","Check whether the plan paused on the reply; if not, pause it (or change the stage if you booked an appointment), and set your own next task."],"answer":3,"explain":"Automations keep the door open; they must not talk over a real conversation. Pause or stop them at the right moments, and never edit or delete the client's templates yourself."},{"q":"Which task is written correctly for a lead in Los Angeles?","options":["“Follow up”, no date.","“Call: confirm Sat tour time”, Friday 10:00 AM Pacific time, assigned to you.","“Call back”, Friday 7:30 AM Pacific time.","“Tour Saturday” as a task instead of an appointment."],"answer":1,"explain":"A good task has a clear title, a date and a time inside the lead's calling window. 7:30 AM is before the 9:00 AM window, and a tour belongs in an appointment and the agent's calendar, not a task."},{"q":"What belongs in your 10-minute close at the end of a shift?","options":["Exporting the day's leads to a spreadsheet on your computer as a backup.","Clocking out first and finishing notes tomorrow morning.","Completing notes, fixing stages and tags, clearing overdue tasks, writing tomorrow's first three calls, and posting the daily report.","Sending one mass text to all nurture leads."],"answer":2,"explain":"The close leaves a clean CRM and a clear start for tomorrow. Never export or download client data, and never send mass texts without the client's approval and consent rules."}]},{"id":"I8","part":"isa","title":"Appointments, calendars and the 3-way handoff","summary":"Book appointments the agent can actually keep, put them on the calendar with everything the agent needs, hand the lead to the agent warmly by phone or text, and protect the show rate with confirmations and follow-up.","objectives":["Ask for and book a buyer tour or listing consultation with two specific options that fit the agent's calendar rules and the lead's time zone.","Create a complete calendar invite: Follow Up Boss link, A.L.M. summary, 24-hour and 1-hour text reminders, and the buyer-agreement flag for any tour, then move the stage to Pending Handoff.","Run a 3-way warm phone handoff with the standard script, and use a warm text introduction when the agent cannot join live.","Confirm appointments the day before, handle reschedules and no-shows calmly, and log the outcome after every appointment."],"lessons":[{"id":"I8-1","title":"What a good appointment is","minutes":6,"body":[{"t":"p","text":"Your main result as an ISA is an **A.L.M.-qualified appointment**: a real meeting between a lead and one of your client's agents, at a set time and place, with the Location and Motivation already known. Your daily target is 2 to 3 of them per shift. This module is about the moment you get a “yes” and everything that happens after it, until the agent and the lead are sitting together."},{"t":"p","text":"An appointment that never happens is worth nothing. An appointment where the agent arrives unprepared is worth very little. So a good appointment is not only booked; it is **kept**, and the agent walks in knowing the lead."},{"t":"table","head":["Appointment type","Who it is for","What usually happens"],"rows":[["Buyer consultation","A buyer who wants to start the search","The agent meets the buyer (in person, by phone or video) to plan the search and explain how they work together."],["Home tour (showing)","A buyer who wants to see one or more homes","The agent opens the homes and walks through them with the buyer."],["Listing consultation","A homeowner thinking about selling","The agent visits the home, looks at it, and later explains a price strategy and a marketing plan."],["Phone or video call with the agent","A lead who is not ready to meet but wants advice","A 15 to 30-minute call; often the step before a consultation."]]},{"t":"p","text":"Your client tells you which types you may book, for which agents, and how long each one lasts. Ask on your first day: “Which appointment types can I book, how long are they, and how much travel time do you need between them?” Write the answers in your notes."},{"t":"p","text":"**Always ask for the appointment.** The “A” in A.L.M. comes first for a reason. Never end a call without proposing a date and time. The strongest way to ask is with **two specific options**, not an open question:"},{"t":"script","title":"Two-option ask","lines":[{"who":"You","text":"The best way to know if this home works for you is to walk through it with our local specialist. Would Saturday at 11 AM or 2 PM work better for you?"},{"who":"Lead","text":"Saturday is hard. Maybe Sunday?"},{"who":"You","text":"Sure. Sunday I have 1 PM or 3:30 PM. Which one is easier?"},{"who":"Lead","text":"1 PM."},{"who":"You","text":"Perfect. Sunday at 1 PM. Let me confirm a few details so your agent is ready."}]},{"t":"p","text":"“When are you free?” gives the lead a big decision and often ends with “I'll let you know.” Two options give a small decision. If neither works, offer two more. If the lead truly cannot commit, propose a tentative time and a task to confirm: “Let me hold Sunday at 1 PM, and I'll text you tomorrow at 10 to confirm. Does that work?”"},{"t":"p","text":"**Only offer times the agent can keep.** Before your first call of the day, open the agent's calendar (your client will give you view or edit access to Google Calendar). Look for open blocks, the agent's personal blocks and travel time. If the agent shows homes on the other side of town at 1 PM, a 1:30 PM tour 40 minutes away is not really available. Some clients give you fixed “bookable” windows, for example Saturday 10 AM to 4 PM and weekdays after 5 PM; that makes this easy."},{"t":"callout","title":"Never book what you cannot confirm","text":"If you are not sure the agent is free, do not promise the time. Say: “Let me lock that with your agent and text you a confirmation within 15 minutes.” Then actually do it in 15 minutes."},{"t":"p","text":"**Check the basics before you hang up.** For every appointment you need: full name, best phone, email, the exact address (for a tour or a listing consultation), the date and time **in the lead's time zone**, who will attend (for example, a spouse who also decides), and permission to text a confirmation if they have not agreed to texts yet. Repeat the date and time back to the lead. “So that's Sunday the 8th at 1 PM, at 2214 Palm Drive. Correct?”"},{"t":"p","text":"**Stay inside your scope.** When booking, leads often ask questions that belong to the agent: “Do you think they'll take less?”, “What is my house worth?”, “Is this a good area for kids?” You never discuss price, terms or value, never negotiate, never describe areas by who lives there, schools, crime or “safety”. Use your bridge: “Great question for your agent. I can also send you public sources.” Then return to the booking."}]},{"id":"I8-2","title":"Booking it right: calendar, reminders and stage","minutes":7,"body":[{"t":"p","text":"The moment the lead says yes, you follow the same routine every time. Do it during the call or within two minutes after it. Memory has no alarm button: **every appointment goes in the calendar before the call ends** or right after you hang up, never “later”."},{"t":"p","text":"These are the calendar booking rules:"},{"t":"list","items":["Paste the Follow Up Boss lead profile link directly into the Google Calendar invite.","Set 24-hour and 1-hour text reminders.","Change the Follow Up Boss Stage immediately to “Pending Handoff”.","For any tour, note in the invite that the agent needs a signed buyer agreement before the showing. The agent sends and explains it; you never do."]},{"t":"p","text":"**Why each rule exists.** The Follow Up Boss link lets the agent open the full history in one tap from their phone, in the car, five minutes before the meeting. The reminders cut no-shows; people forget. Pending Handoff tells the team the lead now belongs to an agent and stops the automated cold drip campaigns, so the lead does not get a “are you still looking?” text the day before their tour. The buyer-agreement flag protects the agent and the lead, as you will see in lesson 4."},{"t":"script","title":"Calendar invite template","lines":[{"who":"Title","text":"Tour: Priya Shah, 2214 Palm Dr, Phoenix"},{"who":"When","text":"Sun Nov 8, 1:00 to 1:45 PM (Arizona time)"},{"who":"Where","text":"2214 Palm Dr, Phoenix, AZ"},{"who":"Guests","text":"Agent Jordan Kim (plus the lead only if your client invites leads to calendar events)"},{"who":"Lead","text":"Priya Shah, (602) 555-0148, priya.shah@example.com, Text OK"},{"who":"FUB","text":"[Follow Up Boss profile link]"},{"who":"A.L.M.","text":"A: tour confirmed Sun 1 PM. L: Phoenix (Arcadia or Central), 3 bd / 2 ba, up to $425K. M: new job starts in May, lease ends April 30."},{"who":"Note","text":"BUYER AGREEMENT: agent to send and explain the written buyer agreement before the showing. Spouse Arjun will attend. Prefers text."}]},{"t":"p","text":"Your client may have its own template. Use theirs, but check that it has the same information. Also create the appointment in Follow Up Boss if your client uses Follow Up Boss appointments; many Follow Up Boss accounts sync with Google Calendar, so one entry may create the other. Ask your manager how your client's account is set up, and never create the same appointment twice."},{"t":"p","text":"**Reminders inside the calling window.** Texts follow the same calling window as calls: 9:00 AM to 8:00 PM in the lead's local time, Monday to Saturday, and noon to 8:00 PM on Sunday. A 1-hour reminder for a 9:30 AM Saturday tour would go out at 8:30 AM, which is outside the window. In that case, send the reminder the evening before, before 8:00 PM, and write in the note why. For a Sunday appointment before 1:00 PM, the 1-hour reminder would land before noon; do the same. Send reminders only to leads who agreed to texts. If they did not, confirm by phone inside the window."},{"t":"script","title":"Reminder texts (adapt to your client's wording)","lines":[{"who":"24 hours","text":"Hi Priya, this is Ana with the Kim Home Team. A reminder of your tour tomorrow, Sunday, at 1 PM at 2214 Palm Dr with Jordan Kim. Reply C to confirm or R if you need a new time. Reply STOP to opt out."},{"who":"1 hour","text":"Hi Priya, Jordan will meet you at 2214 Palm Dr at 1 PM today. His cell is (602) 555-0193 if you need him. See you soon!"}]},{"t":"p","text":"**Time zones: yours, the agent's, the lead's.** You work from Honduras or another Latin American country. Honduras uses Central Standard Time all year and does not change clocks for daylight saving time. (If you live in another country, check whether your clocks change and build your own table.) Most of the United States moves its clocks forward in March and back in November (Arizona and Hawaii do not). This means the difference between your clock and your client's clock **changes twice a year**. A client in California is 2 hours behind Honduras in winter but only 1 hour behind in summer. Always book in the lead's time zone, write the time zone in the invite, and double-check the week the US clocks change."},{"t":"table","head":["US time zone (example city)","Difference from Honduras in winter (Nov to Mar)","In summer (Mar to Nov)"],"rows":[["Eastern (Miami)","1 hour ahead","2 hours ahead"],["Central (Dallas)","Same time","1 hour ahead"],["Mountain (Denver)","1 hour behind","Same time"],["Arizona (Phoenix)","1 hour behind","1 hour behind"],["Pacific (Los Angeles)","2 hours behind","1 hour behind"]]},{"t":"dodont","do":["Book while the lead is on the phone, or within two minutes.","Repeat the date, time and address back to the lead.","Write the time zone in every invite.","Move the stage to Pending Handoff immediately."],"dont":["Keep an appointment “in your head” or on paper until later.","Book over the agent's personal blocks or without travel time.","Send reminder texts before 9 AM or after 8 PM in the lead's time zone (noon on Sunday).","Leave a booked lead in a nurture stage where drip campaigns keep running."]}]},{"id":"I8-3","title":"The 3-way warm phone handoff","minutes":6,"body":[{"t":"p","text":"A **warm handoff** means the lead meets the agent through you, not as a stranger. In a **3-way handoff** you, the lead and the agent are on the same call for a moment. Some clients want this for every hot lead (for example, a buyer who wants to tour today), some only when the agent is available, and some never. Ask your manager which leads get a live transfer."},{"t":"p","text":"The warm handoff works because the lead already trusts you after a good conversation. When you introduce the agent as “our local market expert” and repeat what the lead told you, that trust moves to the agent in 15 seconds."},{"t":"script","title":"The 3-way warm phone handoff script (you, to the agent, with the lead on the line)","lines":[{"who":"You","text":"“[Agent Name], I have [Lead Name] on the line. They are looking for a [4]-bed home in [Location] around [Budget] with a goal to move by [Timeline]. [Lead], meet [Agent], our local market expert!”"}]},{"t":"p","text":"**Step by step.** Your phone system decides the buttons (Follow Up Boss calling, a softphone or the client's dialer all have a way to add a caller or transfer). Practice the buttons in a test call before you ever do it live."},{"t":"steps","items":["**Ask permission.** “Jordan, our local specialist, is available right now. Can I bring him on the line for one minute so you can meet him?”","**Put the lead on a short hold.** “I'll put you on a short hold, about 30 seconds.”","**Brief the agent privately first.** Call the agent: “Hi Jordan, I have a hot buyer, Priya, on hold. 3-bed in Phoenix up to $425K, new job in May, wants to tour Sunday 1 PM. Can you take her now?” This takes 20 seconds and means the agent is ready.","**Merge the calls and introduce,** using the script above. Use the real numbers and words the lead used.","**Step back.** Let the agent speak. Stay on the line quietly for a few seconds in case of a technical problem, then say goodbye: “I'll leave you two. Priya, you'll get a text confirmation from me.”","**Log it.** Write the A.L.M. note with “Disposition: Call, connected, 3-way handoff to Agent Jordan”, book or confirm the appointment the agent agreed, stage Pending Handoff."]},{"t":"callout","title":"If the agent does not answer","text":"Never leave the lead on hold longer than 30 to 45 seconds. Go back: “Jordan is with another client right now. Let me book you a time with him so you have his full attention. Would Sunday at 1 or 3:30 be better?” Then book it and send a warm text introduction (lesson 4)."},{"t":"p","text":"**Common mistakes.** Merging without briefing the agent, so the agent says “Hi, who is this?”. Reading the script like a robot. Telling the lead things the agent will have to correct (“Jordan can get you that house for $400K”). Talking over the agent after the introduction. Forgetting the recording disclosure: if your calls are recorded, you announced it at the start of your call, and if a new person joins a recorded call, the agent or you should mention that the call may be recorded. We treat every state as requiring everyone's consent."},{"t":"p","text":"**When to transfer and when to book.** A 3-way handoff is best when the lead is hot and the agent can talk now: a buyer standing outside a home, an all-cash buyer flying in this week, a seller who wants to list this month. For most leads, a booked appointment with a warm text introduction is enough. Do not hold up a good call trying to reach an agent who is in a showing."},{"t":"script","title":"Example: live transfer that sounds natural","lines":[{"who":"You","text":"Elena, you mentioned you're flying in Friday and have proof of funds. Our specialist Jordan is free right now. Can I bring him on for a minute?"},{"who":"Lead","text":"Yes, please."},{"who":"You","text":"Great. One short hold, about 30 seconds."},{"who":"You","text":"(to the agent, privately) Jordan, Elena Rostova, relocating from the Bay Area, all cash, wants Saturday 11 AM on the Westside Lancaster home. She's on hold. Ready?"},{"who":"Agent","text":"Ready, bring her in."},{"who":"You","text":"Jordan, I have Elena on the line. She's looking for a 4-bed home in Westside Lancaster, paying cash, with a goal to move as soon as her job starts next month. Elena, meet Jordan, our local market expert!"}]}]},{"id":"I8-4","title":"Warm text introductions and the buyer agreement","minutes":6,"body":[{"t":"p","text":"Most of your appointments will not have a live transfer. The agent is showing homes, the lead booked for next week, or it is a listing consultation. In these cases you make a **warm text introduction**: a short message that tells the lead who the agent is and tells the agent everything about the lead."},{"t":"p","text":"**Two messages, two audiences.** Send the lead a confirmation that names the agent and gives the agent's contact card or number (only to leads who agreed to texts, inside the calling window). Send the agent the summary inside the client's systems: the calendar invite, the Follow Up Boss note and assignment, and a short message in the client channel if your client wants one. Never use private DMs or a personal phone to talk with your client or the lead."},{"t":"script","title":"Warm text introduction to the lead","lines":[{"who":"You","text":"Hi Marcus, it was great talking with you! You're confirmed for Saturday at 2 PM at 1840 Quartz Ln, Palmdale, with Sofia Ruiz, our local specialist. Sofia's number is (661) 555-0177. She'll text you before Saturday. Reply STOP to opt out."}]},{"t":"script","title":"Handoff message to the agent (client channel)","lines":[{"who":"You","text":"Sofia, new appointment booked for you: Marcus Vance, Sat 2 PM, 1840 Quartz Ln, Palmdale (tour). L: Palmdale or East Quartz Hill, 4 bd with a big yard, budget not shared. M: lease ends in 45 days. Prefers text. Buyer agreement needed before the showing. Invite and FUB link are on your calendar. Stage: Pending Handoff."}]},{"t":"p","text":"**The buyer agreement rule.** Since August 2024, an agent working with a buyer needs a **written buyer agreement** signed before touring any home with that buyer. This came from changes in the industry's rules that year, and most US agents follow it everywhere. The agreement explains what the agent will do for the buyer and how the agent is paid. It is a legal document. Forms and details can differ by state and brokerage, so your manager confirms how your client handles it."},{"t":"callout","title":"Your role is to flag it, never to handle it","text":"**The agent sends and explains the buyer agreement. You never do.** You do not send it, explain it, summarize it, discuss what it says about payment, or ask the lead to sign it. You only make sure the agent knows a tour is booked, by writing the buyer-agreement note in every tour invite."},{"t":"p","text":"Leads sometimes ask about it. Keep your answer short, true and inside your scope:"},{"t":"script","title":"When the lead asks about the agreement","lines":[{"who":"Lead","text":"Do I have to sign something before the tour? What does it say about commission?"},{"who":"You","text":"Great question for your agent. Before the tour, Sofia will send you the paperwork and walk you through every part of it, including how she's paid, so you can ask her anything. Would it help if she called you Thursday to go over it?"},{"who":"Lead","text":"Yes, Thursday evening."},{"who":"You","text":"I'll put Thursday at 6 PM on her calendar for a quick call."}]},{"t":"p","text":"Notice what you did not do: you did not say what the agreement contains, how much the commission is, whether it can be changed or how long it lasts. Price, terms and commission are always the agent's conversation."},{"t":"p","text":"**Listing consultations are different.** A seller does not sign a buyer agreement. The agent may bring a listing agreement to the consultation; again, the agent explains it, never you. In the invite for a listing consultation, write what the seller told you: why they want to sell, their timeline, the property type, beds and baths, any recent updates, and whether all owners will be at the meeting. Do not write or say any price; the agent gives the opinion of value at the home."},{"t":"p","text":"**Financing in the handoff.** If the lead mentions a loan, credit or rates, write what they said in the note (“Says pre-approved with a credit union”, “Has not talked to a lender yet”). Never give loan, rate or credit advice. Connect the lead with the client's preferred lender only when the agent says so. Many agents want the lender introduction after they meet the lead; follow your client's instructions."},{"t":"dodont","do":["Send the lead the agent's name and number in the confirmation.","Give the agent the A.L.M. summary in the invite and the note.","Flag the buyer agreement in every tour invite.","Answer agreement questions with “Great question for your agent” and book a time."],"dont":["Send, explain or summarize the buyer agreement or a listing agreement.","Talk about commission, price or terms.","Introduce a lender unless the agent asks you to.","Message the agent or the lead outside the client's channel and tools."]}]},{"id":"I8-5","title":"Confirmations, no-shows and the after-appointment loop","minutes":7,"body":[{"t":"p","text":"Booking is half the job. The other half is the **show rate**: the share of booked appointments that actually happen. A team that books 20 appointments a month and keeps 10 has a 50% show rate; the same team at 80% gets six more meetings with the same effort. Most clients expect you to confirm every appointment the day before and to follow up after it. Here is how."},{"t":"p","text":"**The day-before confirmation.** The automatic 24-hour text is a reminder; your confirmation is a personal touch. The day before, inside the lead's calling window, call the lead (or text, if they prefer texts and agreed to them)."},{"t":"script","title":"Day-before confirmation call","lines":[{"who":"You","text":"Hi Hannah, this is Ana with the Patel Team. Just a reminder that this call may be recorded. I'm calling to confirm your meeting with Raj Patel tomorrow at 5 PM at your home on Birch Street. Is that still a good time?"},{"who":"Lead","text":"Yes, but my husband gets home at 5:30."},{"who":"You","text":"Thank you for telling me. Since you both decide, would 5:45 work better? I'll check with Raj and text you in 10 minutes."},{"who":"Lead","text":"That's better, yes."}]},{"t":"p","text":"Then check the agent's calendar, ask the agent in the client channel if needed, move the invite and the reminders, update the note, and confirm with the lead as promised. A small change made the day before saves a failed meeting."},{"t":"p","text":"**Reschedules.** When a lead needs a new time, never make them feel bad. Offer two new options right away, update the invite (do not create a second one), move the reminders and tell the agent. Write the reason in the note: “Rescheduled from Sat 11 AM to Sun 1 PM; lead's flight changed.” If the agent asks to move a time, you call the lead, apologize for the change and offer two options."},{"t":"p","text":"**Cancellations.** If a lead cancels without a new time, ask one gentle question to understand why (“Of course. May I ask what changed?”). The answer tells you the next step: they found a home with another agent (note it, tag if they signed with that agent), their plans moved (set a nurture timeline), or they were nervous about something (offer a short phone call with the agent instead). Move the stage back from Pending Handoff to the right nurture or hot stage, and set the next task."},{"t":"p","text":"**No-shows.** The agent tells you the lead did not come. Within 10 to 15 minutes of the start time, call the lead (inside the window). Assume the best: people get lost, stuck at work or confused about the time."},{"t":"script","title":"No-show call","lines":[{"who":"You","text":"Hi Marcus, it's Ana with the Ruiz Team. Sofia is at 1840 Quartz Lane for your 2 PM tour. I wanted to make sure you're okay. Are you on your way?"},{"who":"Lead","text":"Oh no, I thought it was tomorrow. I'm so sorry."},{"who":"You","text":"No problem at all, it happens. Sofia has tomorrow at 11 AM or 3 PM. Which one works?"}]},{"t":"p","text":"If the lead does not answer, leave a friendly 20-second voicemail, send a text if they agreed to texts, and set a task to call again the next day. Log the no-show in the note. Never send angry or guilt texts like “The agent waited for you for 30 minutes.”"},{"t":"p","text":"**After the appointment: close the loop.** The same day or the next morning, find out what happened. Ask the agent in the client channel, or read the agent's note in Follow Up Boss. Every appointment ends in one of a few outcomes, and you update the stage and next task to match:"},{"t":"table","head":["Outcome","What you do"],"rows":[["Another appointment (more tours, a second meeting)","Book it like the first one, or confirm the agent booked it. Keep the stage the client uses for active clients."],["A lender call booked or the lead now works with the agent","The agent owns the lead now. Follow your client's stage (often an active client stage). Ask if they want you to keep confirming tours."],["An offer or a listing agreement","Celebrate in the client channel. The agent handles everything; update the stage only if your client wants you to."],["Not ready yet","Move to the right nurture stage with a timeline tag and a dated task. Module I9 covers the follow-up."],["No-show, not reached","Keep trying with the no-show steps; after the client's limit, move to nurture."]]},{"t":"p","text":"**Track your own numbers.** In your daily report, list appointments booked, appointments that happened, no-shows and reschedules. Over time you will see patterns: maybe morning appointments have more no-shows, or leads who get a personal confirmation call show up more. Share these patterns with your manager; that is how good ISAs become great ones."},{"t":"callout","title":"Remember","text":"**Booked is not done. Kept is done.** The confirmation call, the calm reschedule and the after-appointment follow-up are what turn your bookings into meetings for your client."}]}],"practice":{"title":"Book, hand off and confirm: recorded role-plays and calendar build","minutes":90,"intro":"You will practice the full appointment routine with a partner (another trainee or your coach) playing the lead and the agent. Record the calls with your partner's agreement (Zoom or a phone voice recorder) and build the calendar entries in a practice Google Calendar, never your client's calendar.","steps":["Create a practice Google Calendar named “ISA practice” in your BeyondVAs account. Block these agent times as busy: Saturday 12 to 1 PM (lunch) and Sunday 10 AM to 12 PM (personal).","Role-play 1 (record it): Priya Shah, a buyer in Phoenix (Arizona time), wants to see 2214 Palm Dr. Open with the recording disclosure, confirm A.L.M. briefly, and book with a two-option ask. Your partner should say no to the first two options once.","Build Priya's calendar invite with the template from lesson 2: title, time zone, address, lead details, a placeholder for the Follow Up Boss link, the A.L.M. summary, the buyer-agreement flag. Write the stage you would set.","Write the 24-hour and 1-hour reminder texts for Priya. Then the tour is moved to Saturday 9:30 AM: write when each reminder goes out and why.","Role-play 2 (record it): a 3-way warm handoff. Your partner plays the lead, then the agent. Do all six steps: permission, hold, private brief, merge and introduce with the script, step back, log. Do it twice: once when the agent answers, once when the agent does not answer and you book a time instead.","Write the warm text introduction to the lead and the handoff message to the agent for role-play 2.","Role-play 3 (record it): the lead asks “What is this buyer agreement and how much commission will I pay?” Answer within your scope and book a call with the agent.","Role-play 4 (record it): day-before confirmation for a listing consultation where the seller needs a different time, then a no-show call the next day.","Make a table of the time difference between your city and Miami, Dallas, Denver, Phoenix and Los Angeles today, and after the next US clock change.","Listen to your recordings once. Write three things you did well and two things you will change."],"deliverable":"Send your coach the four recordings, screenshots of the practice calendar invites, the reminder texts and handoff messages, the time-zone table, and your short self-review, in one shared folder."},"quiz":[{"q":"What is the proper stage in Follow Up Boss after booking an appointment with an agent?","options":["Archived / DNC","Cold Nurture","Pending Handoff / Appointment Scheduled","Unassigned"],"answer":2,"explain":"Updating the stage to Pending Handoff notifies the team and stops automated cold drip campaigns."},{"q":"Which way of asking for the appointment works best?","options":["“Would Saturday at 11 AM or 2 PM work better for you?”","“When would you like to meet someday?”","“Call us back when you are ready.”","“I'll send you some listings and you can tell me if you want to see any.”"],"answer":0,"explain":"Two specific options turn a big decision into a small one. Open questions often end with “I'll let you know.”"},{"q":"Which of these is NOT one of the calendar booking rules?","options":["Paste the Follow Up Boss lead profile link into the Google Calendar invite.","Set 24-hour and 1-hour text reminders.","Email the lead the buyer agreement and explain the commission section.","For any tour, note that the agent needs a signed buyer agreement before the showing."],"answer":2,"explain":"The agent sends and explains the buyer agreement; you never do. You only flag it in the invite."},{"q":"A tour is booked for Saturday at 9:30 AM. When should the 1-hour text reminder go out?","options":["At 8:30 AM Saturday, exactly one hour before.","At 6:00 AM Saturday so the lead wakes up to it.","No reminder at all; reminders are optional.","The evening before, before 8:00 PM in the lead's time zone, with a note explaining why."],"answer":3,"explain":"Texts follow the calling window, 9:00 AM to 8:00 PM Monday to Saturday in the lead's time. A reminder at 8:30 AM would break it, so send it the evening before."},{"q":"During a 3-way handoff, what should you do before merging the lead and the agent?","options":["Brief the agent privately in about 20 seconds: name, A.L.M., what the lead wants now.","Nothing; merge right away so the agent can introduce himself.","Tell the lead the agent can get the home for less than the asking price.","Hang up on the lead and ask the agent to call later."],"answer":0,"explain":"A private brief means the agent joins ready and the lead never has to repeat themselves. Price promises are never allowed."},{"q":"You put the lead on hold for the 3-way handoff, and the agent does not answer after 40 seconds. What now?","options":["Keep the lead on hold until the agent calls back.","Go back to the lead, explain the agent is with another client, book a specific time, and send a warm text introduction.","Give the lead the agent's personal email and end the call.","Tell the lead you will call back sometime next week."],"answer":1,"explain":"Never leave a lead on hold longer than 30 to 45 seconds. Turn the missed transfer into a booked appointment with a warm introduction."},{"q":"A lead asks: “How much commission will I pay under that buyer agreement?” What is the best reply?","options":["“Usually about 2.5%, but it depends.”","“You don't need to worry about it, just sign it.”","“Great question for your agent. She will walk you through every part of it before the tour. Would a quick call with her Thursday help?”","“I'll email you the agreement and you can read it tonight.”"],"answer":2,"explain":"Commission, price and terms are always the agent's conversation. You never send, explain or summarize the agreement; you book time with the agent."},{"q":"Your client is in Los Angeles. You work from Honduras, which does not use daylight saving time. What happens in March when US clocks move forward?","options":["Nothing changes; the difference is always 2 hours.","Los Angeles goes from 2 hours behind you to 1 hour behind you, so you must re-check every booking and your shift times.","Los Angeles becomes 3 hours behind you.","Honduras moves its clocks too, so nothing changes."],"answer":1,"explain":"Honduras stays on Central Standard Time all year. When most of the US moves clocks forward, the gap to Pacific time shrinks from 2 hours to 1. Always book in the lead's time zone and write the zone in the invite."},{"q":"The agent tells you the lead did not show up for a 2 PM tour. What is the best first step?","options":["Text the lead: “The agent waited 30 minutes for you.”","Move the lead to Archived / DNC.","Wait a week and then send a nurture email.","Within 10 to 15 minutes, call the lead in a friendly way, check they are okay, and offer two new times."],"answer":3,"explain":"Most no-shows are confusion or delays, not refusals. A calm, quick call often saves the appointment. Archived / DNC is only for opt-outs."}]},{"id":"I9","part":"isa","title":"Nurture and long-term follow-up","summary":"Keep leads who are not ready yet warm with a cadence that matches their timeline, send texts, emails and calls that are useful instead of pushy, and safely re-engage cold leads until they are ready to meet an agent.","objectives":["Explain why most leads are not ready today and treat “not now” as a date, not a no.","Set a follow-up cadence by timeline and mix calls, texts and emails inside the calling window and consent rules.","Write short, personal nurture texts and emails that add value and end with an easy question.","Run a compliant database reactivation: confirm the list is scrubbed, use approved scripts, and log every reply correctly.","Recognize when a nurture lead is warming up and move them to Hot with an appointment."],"lessons":[{"id":"I9-1","title":"Why nurture wins","minutes":6,"body":[{"t":"p","text":"Most people who fill out a form on a real estate website are not ready to buy or sell this week. Some are three months away, some a year, some are just dreaming. If the team only works the leads who are ready today, it throws away most of the money it spent on advertising. **Nurture** is the long, patient follow-up that keeps those people connected to your client until they are ready."},{"t":"p","text":"Here is the simple truth of this business: when a lead is finally ready, they usually call the agent who stayed in touch. Not the agent who called once and gave up, and not the one who sent 40 pushy texts. The one who was helpful, remembered their plans and was there at the right moment. You are that person for your client."},{"t":"callout","title":"“Not now” is a date, not a no","text":"When a lead says “We're not moving for six months,” they are not saying no. They are telling you **when**. Put it in the CRM with a timeline tag, a dated task and a reason. Six months from now, that lead is a hot lead, and you will be the one who remembers."},{"t":"p","text":"**Quiet does not mean uninterested.** A buyer who stops answering is usually overwhelmed or confused, not unmotivated. Buying a home is a big decision with many unknown steps: saving for the down payment, talking to a lender, a lease to end, a job change, a family conversation. Often they are stuck on one question. Your job in nurture is to find that question and connect them with the agent who can answer it. Pressure freezes people. Education and patience move them."},{"t":"p","text":"**What nurture is and what it is not.**"},{"t":"dodont","do":["Remember their plan and refer to it (“You mentioned your lease ends in June”).","Send something useful: new homes that match what they said, a price drop, an open house, a market update from the agent.","Ask short, easy questions that are simple to answer.","Respect the timeline they gave you and the cadence your client sets.","Honor every opt-out immediately."],"dont":["Send “Just checking in!” with nothing else, again and again.","Push for an appointment every single time with someone who told you next year.","Contact people outside the calling window, or text people who did not agree to texts.","Argue with “not interested” or “stop”.","Give opinions on prices, rates or neighborhoods."]},{"t":"p","text":"**Your place in the system.** Your client probably has automatic action plans that send emails and some texts to nurture leads. Automations are good at being regular; they are bad at listening. You add what software cannot: a real voice, a personal text that responds to what the lead said last time, and the judgment to notice when a lead is warming up. Think of it this way: the action plan keeps the door open, and your calls invite them in."},{"t":"p","text":"**The nurture Smart Lists.** In module I7 you learned the Smart List order: New Speed-to-Lead, Hot Active, Scheduled Follow-Up Tasks, then 30 to 60 Day Nurtures. Nurture work happens in the last two lists. It never comes before a new lead, but it is not optional. A shift with no nurture calls is a shift that steals from next month's appointments."},{"t":"p","text":"**A story to remember.** Carla filled out a form in January. She said she wanted to buy “maybe next year”. The ISA tagged her 6-12 months, set a monthly check-in and sent her new listings in her price range every few weeks. In May, Carla replied to a text: “My landlord is selling. Can we look this weekend?” The ISA booked a tour that afternoon. Carla bought in July. Nothing about that was luck. It was a tag, a task and five months of small, useful messages."},{"t":"p","text":"In this module you will learn how often to contact each type of lead, what to send, how to restart conversations with old leads safely, and how to spot the moment a nurture lead is ready to meet the agent."}]},{"id":"I9-2","title":"Cadence by timeline","minutes":7,"body":[{"t":"p","text":"**Cadence** means how often you contact a lead and by which channel. Too little and the lead forgets your client. Too much and the lead feels chased and opts out. The right cadence depends mostly on one thing: **how soon the lead plans to move**. That is the M (Motivation and timeline) in your A.L.M. note."},{"t":"p","text":"Your client may have its own cadence; if so, follow it. If not, this table is a good starting point. “Personal touch” means a call or a personal text from you, not an automatic email."},{"t":"table","head":["Timeline","Typical tag","Personal touch","Mix"],"rows":[["Ready within 30 days","0-30 / Hot","Every 1 to 3 days","Calls first; texts to confirm; new listings as they appear. Goal: an appointment."],["1 to 2 months","30-60","Weekly","One call a week; a useful text or listing between calls."],["2 to 3 months","60-90","Every 1 to 2 weeks","Alternate a call and a text; client's emails continue."],["3 to 6 months","3-6 mo","Every 2 to 3 weeks","A call about once a month; value texts between."],["6 to 12 months","6-12 mo","Monthly","A monthly call or text that refers to their plan."],["More than a year, or unknown","12+ / Long-term","Every 60 to 90 days","A short question text or call; mostly the client's emails."]]},{"t":"callout","title":"The 10-day rule still applies","text":"Many teams require some update on every assigned lead at least every 10 days, or the lead can be reassigned. For long-term leads, the client's automatic emails often keep the record active between your personal touches. Ask your manager exactly which leads the 10-day rule covers in your client's account, and never let a Hot or 30 to 60 day lead pass 10 days without a personal touch."},{"t":"p","text":"**Move the cadence when the timeline moves.** Timelines change all the time. A 6-month buyer whose landlord sells the building is now a 30-day buyer. A hot buyer who loses a job may now be a year away. Every time you talk to a lead, update the M in your note, change the timeline tag and set the next task at the new cadence. A wrong tag means a wrong cadence."},{"t":"p","text":"**Mix your channels.** Some people never answer the phone but reply to every text. Others ignore texts and pick up calls. Notice which channel works for each lead and write it in the note (“Prefers text; never answers calls before 5 PM”). Use it. But remember the rules:"},{"t":"list","items":["**Calls and texts** happen only inside the calling window: 9:00 AM to 8:00 PM in the lead's local time, Monday to Saturday, noon to 8:00 PM on Sunday. Some states are stricter; your manager gives you the rules for each state before you dial.","**Texts** go only to leads who agreed to texts. Check the tag or the lead source before every first text.","**Calls to an old database, farm or purchased list** happen only after your client scrubs it against the National Do Not Call Registry and their own DNC list (lesson 4).","**Emails** come from your client's templates or your client's email account, with the unsubscribe option your client's system adds. Honor every unsubscribe.","**Every opt-out** (“STOP”, “remove me”, “don't call”) ends all contact: tag DNC, stage Archived / DNC, pause all automations, log it."]},{"t":"p","text":"**Pick good times.** Inside the window, some times work better. Many people answer between 10 and 11 AM, around lunch, and between 4 and 7 PM after work. Saturday mornings after 9 AM can be good. Try different times for a lead who never answers, and write which time worked."},{"t":"p","text":"**Example: setting cadence after a call.** You speak with Kevin Brooks. He rents, his lease ends in five months, he wants a 3-bed in Tampa, and he is saving for the down payment. Your note: “M: lease ends end of March, saving for down payment, timeline 3-6 months.” You tag 3-6 mo and set a task for a value text in two weeks: new 3-bed homes in his price range. A call task in four weeks. At the end of each call you ask one question that tells you if the timeline changed: “Is the end of March still the plan?”"},{"t":"p","text":"**How many tries before you slow down?** For a lead who never answers, the client usually sets a limit, for example eight to ten attempts in the first month across calls and texts, then a slower long-term cadence. A lead who does not answer is still a lead. You slow down; you do not delete, and you do not archive unless they opted out or your manager says so."},{"t":"dodont","do":["Set the next task at the cadence that matches the timeline tag.","Ask “Is [timeline] still the plan?” on every nurture contact.","Write which channel and time works for each lead."],"dont":["Call a 12-month lead every two days.","Let a Hot lead wait a week between touches.","Forget to change the tag when the timeline changes."]}]},{"id":"I9-3","title":"Texts and emails that add value","minutes":7,"body":[{"t":"p","text":"A nurture message has one job: to be **useful or easy to answer**, ideally both. The best ones are short, personal, and end with a simple question. The worst ones are long, generic and ask for nothing."},{"t":"p","text":"**The anatomy of a good nurture text:**"},{"t":"steps","items":["**Their name and yours.** “Hi Kevin, it's Ana with the Rivera Team.” People ignore texts from unknown numbers; say who you are.","**One personal detail** from your notes: their area, their timeline, the home they asked about.","**One useful thing** or one question. Not five.","**An easy reply.** A yes or no question, or a choice of two.","**Short.** Two or three lines. If it does not fit on a phone screen without scrolling, it is too long."]},{"t":"table","head":["Weak text","Strong text"],"rows":[["Just checking in!! Let me know if you need anything!!","Hi Kevin, it's Ana with the Rivera Team. Two new 3-bed homes came up in Brandon under $350K this week. Want me to send them?"],["Are you ready to buy yet? We have great agents available today.","Hi Dana, you mentioned spring for your move. Is that still the plan, or did anything change?"],["Hello, I am following up regarding your inquiry on our website from several months ago to see if you are still interested in purchasing a property in the area.","Hi Luis, are you still thinking about buying in Orlando this year?"]]},{"t":"p","text":"That last strong example is a **short question text**: one simple question about their plan. It works surprisingly well with old, quiet leads because it is easy to answer and does not feel like a sales pitch. Use it for leads who have not replied in a long time, not for someone you talked with yesterday."},{"t":"p","text":"**Value ideas for nurture touches.** Your client decides what you may send. These are common:"},{"t":"list","items":["**New listings** that match what the lead told you (area, beds, baths, price), from the search your client set up. Send a link or two, not twenty.","**Price drops** on a home the lead looked at or saved.","**Open house invitations** for homes in their search area.","**A market update** the agent prepared, for example how many homes sold last month in their area. Send it as the agent's update; do not add your own opinion.","**Seasonal or personal notes**: a home anniversary for a past client, or a friendly holiday message if your client does this.","**An offer of the agent's time**: “Would a 15-minute call with Sofia to plan your search be helpful?”"]},{"t":"callout","title":"Stay inside your scope in writing too","text":"Everything you write can be screenshotted and shared. Never write prices you think a home is worth, predictions (“prices will go up soon, buy now”), interest rates or loan advice (“rates dropped, now is the time”), or comments on legal terms. Never describe an area by who lives there, its schools, crime, “safety”, religion or how “family-friendly” it is. If they ask, write: “Great question for your agent. I can also send you public sources.”"},{"t":"p","text":"**Emails.** Most nurture emails come from your client's action plans. Sometimes you will send a personal email, for example to a lead who prefers email or to send listings. Keep the same rules: a clear subject (“Three new homes in Brandon under $350K”), a short greeting with their name, one purpose, one question, and your name, team and phone. Use your client's email account and signature, never a personal email. Never attach client documents or lead lists."},{"t":"script","title":"Personal nurture email","lines":[{"who":"Subject","text":"Three new homes in Brandon under $350K"},{"who":"Body","text":"Hi Kevin, Sofia asked me to send you three new 3-bedroom homes in Brandon that match what you told us: [link 1], [link 2], [link 3]. Do any of these look worth a visit? If your timeline is still March, Sofia is happy to plan a first tour day with you. Best, Ana, Rivera Team, (813) 555-0110"}]},{"t":"p","text":"**Replies.** When a lead replies, answer quickly, inside the window. If it is after 8 PM their time, answer first thing the next day inside the window. Log the reply, update A.L.M., and if they are warming up, call them. A reply is a door opening; walk through it."},{"t":"p","text":"**Spanish-speaking leads.** If a lead writes in Spanish or tells you they prefer Spanish, write to them in Spanish and tag Spanish. Your client may want a Spanish-speaking agent for them. Notes in Follow Up Boss stay in English so every agent can read them."},{"t":"dodont","do":["Say who you are in the first line.","Use one personal detail from the notes.","End with one easy question.","Send only to leads who agreed to texts, inside the calling window."],"dont":["Use many exclamation marks, all caps or emojis your client does not use.","Copy the same text to many leads at once.","Write prices, rates, predictions or neighborhood opinions."]}]},{"id":"I9-4","title":"Re-engaging cold leads safely","minutes":7,"body":[{"t":"p","text":"Every team has a database full of old leads: website registrations from two years ago, open house sign-ins, past inquiries that went quiet. **Database reactivation** means contacting these people again to find the ones who are now ready. Done well, it produces appointments from leads the team already paid for. Done carelessly, it produces complaints and legal risk. So reactivation starts with compliance, not with dialing."},{"t":"callout","title":"Before you dial an old list","text":"Inbound leads who asked to be contacted may be called inside the calling window. **Any other list** (an old database, a farm list, a purchased list) is called **only if your client has scrubbed it against the National Do Not Call Registry and their own DNC list.** Text only leads who agreed to texts. Dial by hand: no autodialer or prerecorded messages unless your manager confirms written consent. If you are not sure a list was scrubbed, ask before the first dial."},{"t":"p","text":"Why so careful? A person who asked about a home two years ago may have joined the Do Not Call Registry since then, or may have forgotten your client completely. Calling a number on the Registry without permission can bring fines for your client. Your manager and the client prepare the list; your job is to work only the list you were given and stop if anything looks wrong (for example, a lead marked DNC in the notes but still on the list)."},{"t":"p","text":"**Read before you dial.** Old leads often have history: a note from an earlier agent, an old opt-out, a “bought with another agent”. Read the timeline first. If there is any opt-out in the history, do not contact, and tell your manager the lead is on the list by mistake."},{"t":"script","title":"Reactivation call","lines":[{"who":"You","text":"Hi, is this Luis? This is Ana with the Rivera Team in Orlando. This call may be recorded. A while back you looked at homes on our website, and I'm just calling to see if buying a home is still on your mind this year."},{"who":"Lead","text":"Oh, wow, that was a long time ago. We put it on hold."},{"who":"You","text":"That makes sense. May I ask what put it on hold?"},{"who":"Lead","text":"We were saving more for the down payment. Maybe next summer."},{"who":"You","text":"Next summer, great. Is it still the Orlando area, and still around three bedrooms?"},{"who":"Lead","text":"Yes, maybe four now. We had a baby."},{"who":"You","text":"Congratulations! So four bedrooms in Orlando, next summer. Would it help if Sofia, our local specialist, did a short call with you this month so you know the steps and the numbers to aim for?"}]},{"t":"p","text":"Notice the note you will write: L is Orlando, 4 bd. M is saving for the down payment, timeline next summer. You do not write “new baby” as a description of who they are; you write what they need in the home (4 bedrooms). Tag 6-12 mo, set the cadence, and if they said yes to the agent call, book it."},{"t":"p","text":"**Common answers and what to do.**"},{"t":"table","head":["The lead says","What you do"],"rows":[["“We already bought.”","Congratulate them. Ask, if your client wants it, whether they know anyone else thinking of moving. Note “Bought in [year]” and use the client's stage or tag for it. Do not keep nurturing them as a buyer."],["“I'm working with an agent.”","Ask whether they signed an agreement. If yes, respect it, note it, tell your agent, and stop. If not, offer a second opinion and ask for a time."],["“Wrong number.”","Apologize, ask nothing more, tag Bad Number (or your client's tag), and note it. Do not call it again."],["“Not interested.”","Thank them. Ask one soft question (“Is that for now, or for good?”). If for good, ask if they want to be removed. If they say remove, it is an opt-out."],["“Stop calling me.”","Opt-out. Apologize, confirm you will remove them, end the call. Tag DNC, stage Archived / DNC, pause automations, log it. Never argue."],["“Maybe in a few months.”","“Not now” is a date. Ask what would need to happen first. Tag the timeline, set the cadence, offer something useful."]]},{"t":"p","text":"**The “close the file” message.** For a lead who has not replied to anything in a long time, some clients use a polite last message that gives them an easy way to answer: “Hi Dana, I haven't heard back and don't want to bother you. Should I close your file, or would you like me to keep sending homes in Mesa?” Many people reply to this. Use it only if your client approves it, and never as a threat. If they say “close it”, ask your manager whether that means an opt-out in your client's system, and follow the opt-out steps if the lead asked to stop contact."},{"t":"p","text":"**Sellers in nurture.** Old seller leads are valuable: a homeowner who asked “what is my home worth?” a year ago may be ready to list now. Never give a value or a price range, even an old one. Ask about their plans and offer the agent's visit: “The agent can walk the property and show you what it would sell for today. Would Tuesday at 5 or Thursday at 6 work?”"}]},{"id":"I9-5","title":"Nurture calls that move a lead forward","minutes":6,"body":[{"t":"p","text":"A nurture call is not a fresh qualification call. You already know the lead. The goal is to **update** what you know, find out what changed, give something useful, and agree on a small next step. When the timeline has moved closer, the next step becomes an appointment."},{"t":"steps","items":["**Prepare (30 seconds).** Read the last note and the timeline tag. Pick one useful thing to offer (new homes, a price drop, the agent's time).","**Open with their plan.** Name, team, recording disclosure, then their detail: “Last time you said your lease ends in March.”","**Ask what changed.** “Is March still the plan, or did anything change?” Then listen. This is where the real information comes.","**Update A.L.M.** Location, beds, price, timeline, reason. One or two quick questions, not a full interview.","**Offer the useful thing.** “I have two new homes in Brandon in your range. Want me to text them?”","**Agree a next step.** If they are close: ask for the appointment with two options. If not: “I'll check in after the holidays. Is text better?”","**Log and set the task** at the right cadence."]},{"t":"p","text":"**Buying signals: when nurture becomes hot.** Listen for these. Each one means the lead may be ready for the agent now:"},{"t":"list","items":["They ask about a **specific home** (“Is the one on Elm Street still available?”).","A **date gets close or fixed**: a lease end, a job start, a school year, a move-out notice.","They mention a **lender, a pre-approval or savings reached**.","They ask **how the process works** or what the next step is.","**A new decision-maker** joins: “My husband wants to see some homes too.”","For sellers: they mention **repairs to prepare**, a **move date**, or ask what homes nearby sold for."]},{"t":"script","title":"From nurture to appointment","lines":[{"who":"You","text":"Hi Kevin, it's Ana with the Rivera Team. This call may be recorded. Last time you said your lease ends in March. Is that still the plan?"},{"who":"Lead","text":"Actually, my landlord wants to sell, so I may have to move in January."},{"who":"You","text":"That's a big change. So now it's January instead of March. Still a 3-bed in Brandon?"},{"who":"Lead","text":"Yes. And I talked to a lender last week."},{"who":"You","text":"Great, that helps. With January so close, the best next step is a first tour day with Sofia so you can see what's out there. Would Saturday at 10 AM or Sunday at 2 PM work better?"},{"who":"Lead","text":"Saturday at 10."},{"who":"You","text":"Perfect. I'll put that on Sofia's calendar and text you her details."}]},{"t":"p","text":"After this call you move Kevin to Hot, change the tag to 0-30, write the A.L.M. note (“M: landlord selling, must move by January; has talked to a lender”), book the tour following module I8 (calendar invite with Follow Up Boss link, reminders, buyer-agreement flag), and move the stage to Pending Handoff. Do not ask about loan amounts or rates; the lender and the agent handle that."},{"t":"p","text":"**When the lead is not warming.** Many nurture calls end with “nothing has changed.” That is fine. Thank them, offer something useful, set the next touch and keep the call short. Two minutes of real attention every few weeks builds more trust than one long pitch. Never invent urgency (“prices are about to jump”) to push them; it is outside your scope and it damages trust."},{"t":"p","text":"**When to stop.** You stop all contact when the lead opts out. You stop nurturing as a buyer or seller when they bought or sold with someone else, or signed an agreement with another agent (note and tag it). For leads who never answer, you slow to the long-term cadence and follow your client's limits. You never delete a lead, and you never archive a lead as DNC unless they asked or your manager decided it."},{"t":"callout","title":"Measure your nurture","text":"In your daily report, include how many nurture contacts you made, how many replied, and how many moved to Hot or booked. Your QA reviewer looks for A.L.M. updates on nurture leads too. A team that sees appointments coming from old leads knows exactly what its ISA is worth."},{"t":"dodont","do":["Start every nurture call with what they told you last time.","Listen for buying signals and ask for the appointment when you hear them.","Keep calls short when nothing has changed."],"dont":["Repeat a full qualification interview every time.","Create false urgency or predict prices.","Ask about income, credit or rates. Write what they share; the lender handles it."]}]}],"practice":{"title":"Nurture campaign build and reactivation role-plays","minutes":90,"intro":"You will plan the nurture for ten practice leads, write the messages, and record two reactivation calls with a partner. Use the practice Follow Up Boss account or the practice workbook from your coordinator. All leads are fictional; never use a real client's database for practice.","steps":["Take the ten practice leads your coordinator gives you (or use these: two Hot, two 30-60, two 3-6 month, two 6-12 month, one unknown timeline, one seller who said “maybe next year”). For each, write the timeline tag, the cadence and the date of the next personal touch.","For three of the leads, write a full four-touch plan for the next 30 to 60 days: which channel, which day, and the exact message or call goal.","Write five nurture texts that follow the anatomy from lesson 3 (name and team, one personal detail, one useful thing or question, an easy reply, short). Include one short question text for a lead who has not replied in a year.","Write one personal nurture email with a clear subject line, sending three new listings for a buyer.","Rewrite these weak messages into strong ones: “Just checking in!!”, “Rates are dropping, now is the time to buy!”, “This is a great safe neighborhood for families.” Explain in one line what was wrong with each.","Write the steps you take before dialing a reactivation list (what you confirm, who you ask, what you check on each lead).","Role-play 1 (record it): reactivation call to an old website lead who is now 6 months away. Update A.L.M., set the next step, and write the note.","Role-play 2 (record it): nurture call where the lead's timeline suddenly moves closer. Catch the buying signal and book an appointment with a two-option ask. Write the note and list what you change in Follow Up Boss.","Role-play 3 (record it, short): the lead says “Stop calling me”. Handle it correctly and list every CRM step.","Listen to your recordings and write two strengths and two improvements."],"deliverable":"One shared document with the ten-lead cadence table, the three four-touch plans, the five texts and the email, the rewrites, your pre-dial checklist, and the notes for each role-play, plus the three recordings. Send the folder link to your coach."},"quiz":[{"q":"A lead tells you, “We're not moving for six months.” What does that mean for you?","options":["It is a polite no; archive the lead.","It is a date: tag the timeline, set the cadence and a dated task, and keep in touch with useful messages.","Call every day so they do not forget your client.","Move them to Archived / DNC until they call back."],"answer":1,"explain":"“Not now” is a date, not a no. The ISA who stays in touch usefully is usually the one the lead calls when ready. Archived / DNC is only for opt-outs."},{"q":"What is a reasonable personal-touch cadence for a lead who plans to move in 6 to 12 months?","options":["Every day","Every 2 to 3 days","About once a month","Once a year"],"answer":2,"explain":"Six to twelve months out: a monthly call or text that refers to their plan, with the client's emails in between. Daily contact feels like chasing; once a year is too little."},{"q":"Which nurture text is best?","options":["“Hi Kevin, it's Ana with the Rivera Team. Two new 3-bed homes came up in Brandon under $350K this week. Want me to send them?”","“Just checking in!! Let me know if you need anything!!”","“Rates just dropped, you should buy now before prices go up.”","“Hello, I am following up regarding your inquiry on our website from several months ago to see if you are still interested in purchasing a property in the area.”"],"answer":0,"explain":"It says who you are, uses a personal detail, offers something useful and ends with an easy question. Rate advice and price predictions are outside your scope."},{"q":"Your manager gives you an old database from three years ago to call. What must be true before you dial?","options":["Nothing; they were leads once, so they gave permission forever.","The list must be loaded into an autodialer for speed.","You must text everyone first to ask if you can call.","Your client has scrubbed it against the National Do Not Call Registry and their own DNC list, and you dial by hand inside the calling window."],"answer":3,"explain":"Any list other than fresh inbound leads who asked to be contacted is called only after a DNC scrub. Dial by hand; no autodialer or prerecorded messages without confirmed written consent. Text only leads who agreed to texts."},{"q":"During a reactivation call, the lead says, “We already bought a house last year.” What do you do?","options":["Congratulate them, ask (if your client wants) whether they know anyone else thinking of moving, note it, and stop nurturing them as a buyer.","Ask what price they paid and whether they overpaid.","Keep them on the buyer nurture plan in case they buy again next month.","Delete their contact."],"answer":0,"explain":"They are no longer a buyer lead. Note it with the client's stage or tag. Never comment on price or value, and never delete contacts."},{"q":"Which of these is a buying signal that a nurture lead may be ready for the agent now?","options":["“Nothing has changed, still next year.”","“Please send emails instead of calls.”","“My landlord is selling, so I have to move by January, and I talked to a lender last week.”","“I'm busy right now, call me next month.”"],"answer":2,"explain":"A fixed, close date plus a lender conversation means the timeline moved. Move them to Hot, update A.L.M. and ask for the appointment with two options."},{"q":"A nurture lead asks by text, “Is this a safe area for kids?” What do you reply?","options":["“Yes, it's very safe and family-friendly.”","“Great question for your agent. I can also send you public sources.”","“The schools are excellent there.”","“No, I would look in a different area.”"],"answer":1,"explain":"Fair housing: never describe areas by safety, crime, schools, who lives there or how family-friendly they are, in calls or in writing. Use the bridge and offer public sources."},{"q":"A lead has not answered eight calls and texts this month. They never opted out. What is the right move?","options":["Tag them DNC so they stop appearing in your lists.","Delete them; they are a bad lead.","Call them three times a day until they answer.","Slow down to the long-term cadence the client sets, keep the record, and try different times and channels."],"answer":3,"explain":"No answer is not an opt-out. Slow down, keep useful touches going and follow the client's limits. Never delete, and DNC is only for opt-outs or a manager's decision."},{"q":"A lead replies “Yes, send them!” to your text at 9:15 PM their time. When do you answer?","options":["Right away; they texted first, so the calling window does not matter.","Next week, at your next scheduled touch.","First thing the next day inside the calling window, then log the reply and update A.L.M.","Never; replies after hours should be ignored."],"answer":2,"explain":"Texts follow the same calling window as calls (9:00 AM to 8:00 PM, noon on Sunday). Answer as soon as the window opens, because a reply is a door opening."}]},{"id":"I10","part":"isa","title":"When you mess up: win the client back","summary":"Every ISA makes mistakes; this module teaches you to own them fast, repair trust with leads and with your client, fix the process, and handle compliance slips the right way.","objectives":["Recover from a mistake with a lead in four steps: own it, name what it cost them, fix the process, give them a reason to respond.","Tell your client about a mistake in the client channel before they discover it, using a five-part message.","Recognize a compliance mistake and follow the stop, escalate, log sequence without deleting or hiding anything.","Choose a process fix that stops the same mistake from happening again, and show it in your work the next day."],"lessons":[{"id":"I10-1","title":"Mistakes happen: what matters is the next hour","minutes":7,"body":[{"t":"p","text":"You will make mistakes in this job. Every ISA does, including the best ones. You will call a lead back a day late. You will book an appointment at 2 PM when the lead said 2 PM **their** time, and you were thinking of yours. You will forget to move a stage, and an automatic text will go out to someone who already booked. This module is not about never making mistakes. It is about what you do in the **next hour** after you notice one."},{"t":"p","text":"Here is the surprising truth: a fast, honest recovery often builds **more** trust than a perfect record. A lead who was let down and then treated with real respect remembers it. A client who hears about a problem from you first, with the fix already in place, learns that you are someone they can rely on when things go wrong. That is the person they want on their team for years."},{"t":"p","text":"The opposite is also true. A small mistake that is hidden becomes a big problem when someone else finds it. And in this job, someone else **will** find it. Many of your calls are recorded. Every call, text, note and stage change has a time stamp in Follow Up Boss. Hubstaff takes screenshots about every two minutes while you are clocked in, and your client can see them. If you hide a mistake, the client does not think \"they made an error\". They think \"I cannot trust what they tell me\". That is a much harder problem to fix."},{"t":"callout","title":"Skill problem or trust problem?","text":"A mistake you report is a **skill problem**: your coordinator can train you, and you get better. A mistake you hide is a **trust problem**: no training fixes it. Always choose the skill problem."},{"t":"p","text":"**The most common ISA mistakes.** Learn to recognize them so you can catch them early:"},{"t":"table","head":["Mistake","Who it hurts","Your first move"],"rows":[["Missed callback or late follow-up","The lead, then your client","Call or text the lead now (inside the calling window), then log it"],["Appointment booked at the wrong time or time zone","The lead and the agent","Tell the agent in the client channel, then contact the lead to fix the time"],["Stage not changed, so automations kept sending","The lead","Fix the stage, pause the wrong automation, tell your agent"],["Wrong address or missing details in the calendar invite","The agent","Correct the invite and tell the agent before the appointment"],["Daily report late or missing","Your client","Send it now with one line explaining the delay"],["Call outside the window, text without consent, missed opt-out","Your client, legally","Stop and tell your manager at once (lesson I10-5)"]]},{"t":"p","text":"**Why mistakes happen.** Most ISA mistakes come from three places: **memory** (you planned to do it later and forgot), **time zones** (you calculated in your own time instead of the lead's), and **speed** (you rushed a note or a stage change to get to the next call). Notice that none of these is about intelligence or effort. They are about **process**. That is good news, because a process can be fixed. You will learn how in lesson I10-3."},{"t":"p","text":"**The recovery sequence.** Whatever the mistake, use the same order. Practice it until it feels automatic:"},{"t":"steps","items":["**Stop the damage.** Fix what you can fix right now: correct the stage, pause the automation, update the calendar invite.","**Tell the right people.** Your coordinator, and your client in the client channel if it affects them. Do it in the same hour if you can, and always before the end of your shift.","**Repair it with the person affected.** Use the four steps in the next two lessons: own it, name what it cost them, fix the process, give them a reason to respond.","**Log it.** Write a factual note in Follow Up Boss. Never delete or edit old notes to make the record look better.","**Change the process** so it does not happen again, and let people see the change in your work."]},{"t":"dodont","do":["Tell your coordinator as soon as you notice the mistake","Fix what you can first, then explain","Use simple, factual words: what happened, when, what you did","Expect to feel uncomfortable, and do it anyway"],"dont":["Wait and hope nobody notices","Delete notes, texts or call logs to hide it","Blame the lead, the CRM, the internet or another person","Keep apologizing for days: one clear recovery is enough"]},{"t":"p","text":"One more thing about feelings. When you make a mistake with a US client, you may feel embarrassed or afraid of losing the job. That is normal. But remember that your BeyondVAs coordinator is there to back you up. You are not alone with the problem. Bring it to them early, and you will solve it together."}]},{"id":"I10-2","title":"Own it, and name what it cost them","minutes":7,"body":[{"t":"p","text":"The recovery method in this module has four steps. It comes from field experience with real buyers and sellers, and it works in English with US clients because it is direct, short and honest:"},{"t":"steps","items":["**Own it.** Apologize promptly. No excuses, no gifts, no long story.","**Name what it cost them.** Say the impact on them out loud.","**Fix the process.** Name the correction you already made.","**Give them a reason to respond.** A useful next step, not a plea. Leave room for their answer."]},{"t":"p","text":"This lesson covers the first two steps. The next lesson covers the last two."},{"t":"p","text":"**Step 1: Own it.** Apologize **promptly**, which means as soon as you can, inside the calling window. Every hour you wait makes the apology weaker, because the person starts to wonder whether you even noticed."},{"t":"p","text":"**No excuses.** In US business culture, an explanation that comes before the apology sounds like an excuse, even when it is true. \"My internet went down, and then the CRM did not save, so...\" The lead hears: \"It is not my fault.\" Even if the internet really went down, the lead does not care. They care that they waited."},{"t":"p","text":"**No gifts.** You may want to make it up to them with something: a discount, a gift card, a promise that the agent will do something special. Do not do this. As an unlicensed assistant you cannot promise anything about price, terms or commission, and you cannot offer incentives your client has not approved. A gift also feels like you are buying forgiveness. What the person wants is respect and a fix."},{"t":"p","text":"**No long story.** A long story makes the conversation about you. Keep the apology to one or two sentences."},{"t":"p","text":"**Watch your English here.** Some phrases sound like apologies but are not, and US listeners notice:"},{"t":"table","head":["Weak (sounds like avoiding blame)","Strong (sounds like ownership)"],"rows":[["\"Sorry if there was any confusion.\"","\"I am sorry. I made a mistake with the time.\""],["\"The appointment was missed.\"","\"I missed your appointment.\""],["\"Sorry, but the system did not save it.\"","\"I did not double-check it, and that is on me.\""],["\"Mistakes happen.\"","\"I let you down, and I want to fix it.\""],["\"I apologize for any inconvenience.\"","\"I am sorry you drove there for nothing.\""]]},{"t":"p","text":"Notice the pattern. Strong apologies use **\"I\"** and an **active verb**: \"I missed\", \"I booked\", \"I forgot\". Weak apologies hide the person (\"the appointment was missed\") or put the problem on the listener (\"if there was confusion\"). \"Any inconvenience\" is a formal phrase that sounds like a company letter, not a person."},{"t":"p","text":"**Step 2: Name what it cost them.** After you own it, say out loud what the mistake did to them. They already know. They lived it. When **you** say it, it shows that you understand, and that changes the whole conversation. Most people relax the moment they feel understood."},{"t":"p","text":"Be specific. \"I wasted your time\" is fine. \"You took the afternoon off work and drove 40 minutes, and nobody was there to open the door\" is much better. Use the details you have in Follow Up Boss: their schedule, their motivation, their timeline."},{"t":"script","title":"Missed callback: the lead asked for a call on Tuesday, you called Thursday","lines":[{"who":"You","text":"Hi Rachel, this is Ana with the Hollis Home Team. Just so you know, this call may be recorded. I owe you an apology. You asked me to call you Tuesday afternoon, and I did not call until today. That was my mistake."},{"who":"Lead","text":"Yeah, I noticed. I kind of figured you guys were not interested."},{"who":"You","text":"I understand why you felt that way. You told me your lease ends in March and you need to move quickly, and I lost you two days. I am sorry."},{"who":"Lead","text":"Okay. Well, I appreciate you saying that."}]},{"t":"p","text":"Look at what Ana did **not** say. She did not explain why she was late. She did not offer anything. She did not talk for long. She owned it in two sentences, named the cost (two days lost when time matters), and stopped to let Rachel answer."},{"t":"callout","title":"Do not over-apologize","text":"One clear apology is strong. Five apologies in one call make the lead uncomfortable and make you sound unsure. After step 2, move forward."},{"t":"p","text":"**When the person is angry.** Sometimes the lead or the agent will be upset, and they may say it strongly. Do not defend yourself and do not argue. Let them finish. Then say: \"You are right to be frustrated.\" and continue with the steps. If the lead says \"stop calling me\" or \"remove me\", that is an **opt-out**: apologize, honor it immediately (tag DNC, stage Archived / DNC, pause automations, log it), and end the call. Do not try to win back someone who opted out."},{"t":"p","text":"**Practice the words out loud.** Apologizing in a second language is hard because emotions make our vocabulary smaller. Memorize three short sentences so they come out naturally: \"I am sorry. That was my mistake.\" / \"You waited, and that cost you [the impact].\" / \"Here is what I already changed.\""}]},{"id":"I10-3","title":"Fix the process, and give them a reason to respond","minutes":7,"body":[{"t":"p","text":"Steps 1 and 2 show the person that you understand what happened. Steps 3 and 4 show them that it will not happen again, and give them a simple way to move forward with you."},{"t":"p","text":"**Step 3: Fix the process.** Name the correction you **already made**. Use the past tense, or \"now\" with the present: \"I changed\", \"I added\", \"I set up\", \"I now put\". A promise about the future (\"I will be more careful\") is weak, because everybody says it. A change that is already done is strong, because it is a fact."},{"t":"callout","title":"Memory has no alarm button","text":"Most ISA mistakes come from trusting your memory. The fix is almost always the same idea: **put it in the system before the call ends**. Every appointment goes in the calendar before you hang up. Every callback becomes a Follow Up Boss task before you dial the next number."},{"t":"p","text":"Here are common mistakes and process fixes that really work:"},{"t":"table","head":["Mistake","Process fix you can name"],"rows":[["Forgot a callback","Every callback becomes a Follow Up Boss task with a due time before the call ends."],["Wrong time zone","Every appointment and task is written in the lead's time zone, with the zone in the note (for example \"2:00 PM Central\")."],["Drip kept sending after booking","I change the stage to Pending Handoff while the lead is still on the line."],["Wrong address in the invite","I read the address back to the lead and copy it from the Follow Up Boss profile, never type it from memory."],["Double-booked the agent","I check the agent's live calendar before I offer two times, every time."],["Missed details in the handoff","I fill all six fields of the A.L.M. note before I send the invite."]]},{"t":"p","text":"Keep the fix short when you say it to a lead. They do not need to know about Follow Up Boss tasks. \"I now put every callback on my calendar before I hang up\" is enough."},{"t":"p","text":"**Step 4: Give them a reason to respond.** Now offer a **useful next step**. Not a plea. A plea sounds like this: \"Please give us another chance.\" It puts the work on the lead and makes them feel responsible for your feelings. A useful next step gives them something of value: a time with the agent, homes that match what they told you, an answer to the question they asked."},{"t":"p","text":"Then **leave room for their answer**. Ask one question and stop talking. Silence is uncomfortable, especially on a phone call in a second language, but it is respectful. The lead needs a moment to decide."},{"t":"script","title":"Wrong time: you booked 2 PM Pacific, the lead meant 2 PM Mountain, and the agent showed up an hour late","lines":[{"who":"You","text":"Hi Marco, this is Ana with the Hollis Home Team, and this call may be recorded. I am calling to apologize. I booked Saturday's showing in the wrong time zone, and Agent Jordan arrived an hour after you did. That was my mistake."},{"who":"Lead","text":"We waited outside with the kids for almost an hour."},{"who":"You","text":"I am sorry. You gave up your Saturday and stood outside with your family, and that should not have happened."},{"who":"You","text":"I already changed how I book: every appointment is now written in your time zone, and I read it back to you before I hang up. Agent Jordan still has the house available. Would Wednesday at 5:30 PM or Thursday at 6:00 PM Mountain time work better for you?"},{"who":"Lead","text":"Thursday could work."},{"who":"You","text":"Thursday at 6:00 PM Mountain time. I will send the confirmation to your phone right now."}]},{"t":"p","text":"All four steps took less than a minute. Ana owned it, named the cost, named a fix that is already done, and offered two specific times. She ended with the time read back, which proves the fix is real."},{"t":"p","text":"**When you cannot reach them by phone.** Leave a short voicemail and, only if the lead agreed to texts, send a text inside the calling window. Keep both brief:"},{"t":"script","title":"Recovery text (lead agreed to texts)","lines":[{"who":"You","text":"Hi Rachel, this is Ana with the Hollis Home Team. I am sorry I did not call Tuesday like I promised; I know your March move date makes every day count. I now put every callback on my calendar before I hang up. Agent Jordan found two 2-bedroom homes near your work. Want me to send them?"}]},{"t":"p","text":"Notice the question at the end. It is easy to answer with one word: \"yes\". That is what \"a reason to respond\" means."},{"t":"p","text":"**If they do not respond.** Do not chase them every day. Go back to the normal follow-up cadence for their timeline, inside the calling window. One recovery attempt by phone and one by text (if they agreed to texts) is enough for the apology itself. After that, treat them like any other lead and keep giving value."},{"t":"dodont","do":["Name a fix that is already done, in the past tense","Offer two specific times or one specific useful thing","Ask one easy question and wait for the answer","Write the recovery in the Follow Up Boss note"],"dont":["Say \"it will never happen again\" without a real change","Beg: \"please give us another chance\"","Offer discounts, gifts or promises about price or commission","Send three texts in a row because they did not reply"]}]},{"id":"I10-4","title":"When the mistake affects your client","minutes":7,"body":[{"t":"p","text":"Your client is the US agent or team you work for. Many mistakes affect them directly: they drive to an appointment at the wrong time, they get surprised by an angry lead, or they see a gap in your work. The four steps still apply, but there is one extra rule: **tell your client first, before they discover it.**"},{"t":"p","text":"If your agent learns about your mistake from a lead, it hurts twice: once for the mistake, and once because you did not tell them. If they learn it from you, with the fix already moving, it becomes a normal work update."},{"t":"p","text":"**Where to tell them.** Always in the **client channel**, never in a private DM, text or personal email. Clients talk with VAs only inside the client channel; that keeps your BeyondVAs coordinator informed and keeps a clear record. If the problem is serious, also message your coordinator directly so they can support you."},{"t":"p","text":"**What to write.** Use five short parts. This structure is easy to read on a phone between showings, which is where most agents will see it:"},{"t":"steps","items":["**What happened**, in one sentence, with \"I\".","**The impact** on the client or the lead.","**What I already did** to fix it.","**What I changed** so it does not happen again.","**What I need from you**, if anything. If nothing, say so."]},{"t":"script","title":"Client channel message: the stage was not changed, so a drip text went out","lines":[{"who":"You","text":"Hi Jordan, I made a mistake I want you to know about. I booked Megan Ross for Thursday 5 PM but did not change her stage to Pending Handoff, so she got an automatic \"still looking?\" text this morning. I moved her to Pending Handoff and paused the drip at 10:12 AM, and I texted her to confirm Thursday; she replied \"see you then\". From now on I change the stage while the lead is still on the line. Nothing needed from you; the invite and her A.L.M. note are in the calendar."}]},{"t":"p","text":"That message is short, honest and complete. Jordan does not need to ask a single question. Compare it with \"Hi, small issue with Sofia, sorry!!\" which tells Jordan nothing and forces them to investigate."},{"t":"p","text":"**How to handle a frustrated client.** Your agent may reply with frustration: \"This is the second time this month.\" Do not defend yourself and do not write a long explanation. Reply once, briefly: \"You are right, and I understand. I set up a checklist with my coordinator today so this stops.\" Then make sure the next ten bookings are perfect. Clients judge recovery by what they see in the following days, not by the words."},{"t":"callout","title":"Your coordinator is your backup","text":"If a client is upset, or you are not sure how to word a message, ask your BeyondVAs coordinator before you send it. That is part of their job. They have seen these situations many times and can help you respond calmly."},{"t":"p","text":"**Mistakes in your hours and reports.** Some mistakes are about your time, not your calls. You forgot to clock out of Hubstaff during lunch. You stayed clocked in while your internet was down. Your daily report was late. Tell your manager the same day so the hours can be reviewed and corrected before billing. Unexplained idle time is flagged and reviewed by your manager before the client is billed, so it is always better to explain it yourself. Never try to hide or adjust time on your own."},{"t":"table","head":["Situation","What to write and where"],"rows":[["Forgot to clock out for a 30-minute break","Message your manager: \"I forgot to clock out from 1:00 to 1:30 PM. Please remove it before billing.\""],["Internet outage during the shift","Tell your manager at once, switch to backup internet or power, and mention the gap in your daily report."],["Daily report late","Send it now in the client channel with one line: \"Sorry this is late; here is today's report.\""],["Missed the first 20 minutes of the shift","Tell your manager and your client in the client channel with the time you started."]]},{"t":"p","text":"**Patterns matter more than single mistakes.** One mistake is normal. The same mistake three times is a pattern, and your client will notice. If you see a pattern in your own work, raise it with your coordinator before the client does: \"I have had two time zone errors this month. Can we build a check for it?\" That kind of honesty is exactly what US clients value."},{"t":"dodont","do":["Tell the client in the client channel before they find out","Use the five parts: happened, impact, did, changed, need","Copy your coordinator on anything serious","Report time-tracking errors to your manager the same day"],"dont":["Send a private DM or personal text to the client","Write \"small issue, sorry!!\" with no details","Argue when the client is frustrated","Adjust or hide hours yourself"]}]},{"id":"I10-5","title":"Compliance mistakes: stop, escalate, log","minutes":7,"body":[{"t":"p","text":"Some mistakes are not just service problems. They can create **legal risk** for your client, and fines for calling and texting violations can be large. These mistakes follow a different path. You do not try to fix them alone with the lead. You **stop, escalate and log**."},{"t":"p","text":"**What counts as a compliance mistake?**"},{"t":"list","items":["A call or text outside the calling window: before 9:00 AM or after 8:00 PM in the lead's local time Monday to Saturday, or outside noon to 8:00 PM on Sunday, or outside a stricter state window your manager gave you.","A text to a lead who did not agree to texts.","A call to a number from an old database, farm or purchased list that was not scrubbed against the National Do Not Call Registry and the client's own DNC list.","Missing an opt-out: you kept calling or texting after the lead said \"STOP\", \"remove me\" or \"don't call\".","Forgetting to announce that the call may be recorded.","Saying something outside your scope: an opinion of value, a rate, a credit opinion, a legal clause, a promise about price or commission.","A fair housing slip: describing an area by who lives there, its schools, crime, \"safety\", religion, or how \"family-friendly\" it is."]},{"t":"p","text":"**The sequence.** When you notice a compliance mistake, do these steps in order:"},{"t":"steps","items":["**Stop.** Do not make another call or send another text to that lead until your manager tells you what to do.","**If it involves an opt-out, honor it now:** tag the contact DNC, move the stage to Archived / DNC, pause every automation, and log the opt-out. Do not argue and do not explain.","**Escalate at once.** Message your BeyondVAs manager in the same hour with the facts.","**Log it factually** in Follow Up Boss: what happened, when, and what you did.","**Never delete anything.** Not the contact, not the note, not the text. The record protects your client. A deleted opted-out contact can be called again by mistake.","**Let your manager decide** what the client hears and whether anyone contacts the lead again."]},{"t":"callout","title":"Why you do not \"fix it\" with the lead yourself","text":"If you texted a lead at 6:40 AM their time, calling them to apologize can make it worse, especially if they are upset. Your manager knows the client's rules and decides the next step. Your job is to stop, report and keep the record clean."},{"t":"p","text":"**Writing the incident note.** Keep it short and factual. No feelings, no excuses, no guesses:"},{"t":"script","title":"Incident note to your manager","lines":[{"who":"You","text":"Compliance incident, Tue 9:55 AM Eastern. At 9:40 AM Eastern I sent a text to lead Kevin Marsh (Sacramento, CA). That was 6:40 AM in his time zone, before the 9:00 AM start. I used the wrong time zone for his area. No reply from the lead. I have stopped all contact with him and logged it in Follow Up Boss. Please advise next steps."}]},{"t":"p","text":"**Scope slips.** Sometimes the mistake is something you **said**. A seller pushed hard and you said, \"Probably around $450,000.\" Or a buyer asked about rates and you said, \"About 6.5 percent.\" These are outside your scope as an unlicensed assistant. Tell your agent and your coordinator the same day. With your agent's approval, you can correct it on the next contact:"},{"t":"script","title":"Correcting a value you should not have given (with the agent's approval)","lines":[{"who":"You","text":"Hi Linda, this is Ana with the Hollis Home Team, and this call may be recorded. Yesterday I mentioned a number for your home, and I should not have. I am not the right person to give a value, and I do not want you to rely on a guess. Agent Jordan can walk through the home and show you what it would sell for today. Would Tuesday at 5 or Thursday at 6 work better?"}]},{"t":"p","text":"**Fair housing slips** follow the same path. If you said \"that area is very safe\" or \"lots of young families live there\", tell your agent and coordinator, log it, and from now on use the standard answer: \"Great question for your agent. I can also send you public sources.\" Log what the lead wants in the home, not who they are."},{"t":"p","text":"**Recording announcement missed.** If you realize in the middle of a call that you forgot, say it as soon as you notice: \"Before we continue, I should mention this call may be recorded.\" Then tell your manager after the call. Treat every state as one that requires everyone's consent."},{"t":"callout","title":"Reporting is always the better choice","text":"A compliance mistake you report in the same hour can usually be contained. A compliance mistake someone finds later, after it was hidden or deleted, can cost your client money and cost you the placement. Report it, every time."},{"t":"dodont","do":["Stop all contact with the lead and report the same hour","Honor any opt-out immediately and completely","Keep every record, even the embarrassing ones","Use the standard fair housing answer from now on"],"dont":["Call the lead to explain a call made outside the window","Delete an opted-out contact \"to be safe\"","Edit an old note to change the time or what you said","Try to make up for a scope slip with another promise"]}]}],"practice":{"title":"Recovery drills: three repairs, one client message, one incident note","minutes":75,"intro":"In this practice you will run the full recovery method on realistic cases, out loud and in writing. Your coach will listen for ownership (the word \"I\" and an active verb), a specific cost, a fix that is already done, and one easy question at the end. Work in a practice account or a shared document; never use real lead data.","steps":["Read the three scenarios. A: you promised to call a buyer on Monday at 6 PM her time and called Wednesday. B: you booked a seller's listing consultation at 4 PM Eastern when the seller meant 4 PM Central, and the agent waited an hour. C: you forgot to change a buyer's stage to Pending Handoff, and he received two automatic \"still looking?\" texts after booking.","For each scenario, write a phone script of 4 to 6 lines that uses all four steps: own it, name what it cost them, fix the process (a change already made), give them a reason to respond. Include the recording announcement.","For scenario A, also write a recovery text of 60 words or fewer for a lead who agreed to texts, ending with one easy question.","Record two role-plays with a practice partner or your coach playing the lead: scenario B with a calm seller, and scenario A with an annoyed buyer who says \"I figured you weren't interested.\" Each call 2 to 3 minutes. Do not read from the page for the apology; say it from memory.","Write the client channel message for scenario C using the five parts: what happened, impact, what I already did, what I changed, what I need from you. Keep it under 90 words.","Write a compliance incident note for this case: you called a lead in Tampa, Florida at 8:15 PM Eastern because you confused his time zone with another one. Use the format from lesson I10-5.","Check every piece against this list: no excuses, no gifts or promises, no \"sorry if\", one apology per call, a fix that is already done, one question at the end, no deleted records. Fix anything that fails."],"deliverable":"Send your coach one document with the three phone scripts, the recovery text, the client channel message and the incident note, plus the two role-play recordings. Add two sentences on which step felt hardest to say in English and how you will practice it."},"quiz":[{"q":"You missed a lead's appointment. What comes first when you reach them?","options":["Explain what went wrong with your schedule so they understand","Offer a gift card so they feel valued","Own it: apologize promptly, name what it cost them and the correction you already made","Wait for them to call you, so you do not bother them"],"answer":2,"explain":"Apologize promptly, name the impact and the completed fix, skip excuses and gifts, and leave room for their answer."},{"q":"Which sentence is the strongest apology in US English?","options":["\"Sorry if there was any confusion about the time.\"","\"I booked the wrong time, and that was my mistake.\"","\"The appointment time was entered incorrectly.\"","\"We apologize for any inconvenience this may have caused.\""],"answer":1,"explain":"A strong apology uses \"I\" and an active verb. \"Sorry if\" puts the problem on the listener, the passive voice hides who did it, and \"any inconvenience\" sounds like a form letter."},{"q":"Which is the best way to name a fix in step 3?","options":["\"I now write every appointment in your time zone and read it back before I hang up.\"","\"I promise I will be much more careful from now on.\"","\"It will never happen again, I can guarantee that.\"","\"Our CRM had a problem, but IT is looking at it.\""],"answer":0,"explain":"Name a correction you already made, in the past or present tense. Promises about the future are weak, and blaming a system is an excuse."},{"q":"A lead you let down has not replied to your recovery call and text. What do you do?","options":["Text every day until they answer","Mark them Archived / DNC because they are clearly upset","Ask the agent to offer them a lower commission","Return to the normal follow-up cadence for their timeline, inside the calling window"],"answer":3,"explain":"One recovery attempt by phone and one by text (if they agreed to texts) is enough. Then treat them like any other lead. Only an opt-out moves them to Archived / DNC, and you never discuss commission."},{"q":"You forgot to change a lead's stage and a drip text went out after booking. How should your client hear about it?","options":["They do not need to know if the lead did not complain","From you, first, in the client channel, with what happened, the impact, what you did, what you changed and what you need","In a private text to the agent so it stays between you","In next week's report, so it does not interrupt their day"],"answer":1,"explain":"Tell the client before they discover it, inside the client channel (never a private DM), using the five parts so they do not need to investigate."},{"q":"You texted a lead at 6:40 AM in their time zone. What is the right first move?","options":["Call the lead right away to apologize for the early text","Delete the text from Follow Up Boss so the record is clean","Stop contact with that lead, tell your manager at once and log the facts","Do nothing, since the lead did not reply"],"answer":2,"explain":"Compliance mistakes follow stop, escalate, log. Your manager decides the next step. Never delete records, and do not contact the lead on your own."},{"q":"Why must you never delete an opted-out contact, even after a mistake?","options":["The DNC record is what stops future calls and texts to that person","Deleting contacts is slow in Follow Up Boss","The agent might want to call them later to change their mind","Deleted contacts still count in your daily KPIs"],"answer":0,"explain":"If the contact is deleted, nothing stops the number from being imported and called again. Keep the contact, tagged DNC, in Archived / DNC with every automation paused."},{"q":"During a call you told a seller their home is \"probably worth about $450,000.\" What should you do?","options":["Nothing; it was only an estimate","Call back and give a more accurate number","Offer the seller a discount if the number was wrong","Tell your agent and coordinator the same day and, with the agent's approval, correct it and offer the agent's walk-through"],"answer":3,"explain":"An opinion of value is outside an unlicensed assistant's scope. Report it, and with approval tell the seller they should not rely on your guess and set the listing consultation."},{"q":"You forgot to clock out of Hubstaff during a 30-minute lunch. What do you do?","options":["Work 30 minutes less tomorrow to balance it","Tell your manager the same day so the time is removed before billing","Leave it; the client will not notice","Edit the screenshots to remove the lunch period"],"answer":1,"explain":"Unexplained idle time is flagged and reviewed by your manager before billing. Explaining it yourself the same day keeps your hours accurate and your client's trust intact."}]},{"id":"I11","part":"isa","title":"QA scorecard, daily KPIs and your daily report","summary":"How your work is measured every shift: the three daily KPIs, the 100-point QA rubric and the 90+ standard, how to score your own calls, and how to close the day with an honest report and clean time records.","objectives":["State the daily KPIs per shift (80-100 dials, 12-15 live conversations, 2-3 A.L.M.-qualified appointments) and explain what counts toward each.","Plan a shift around the Smart List order so speed to lead and follow-ups never fall behind.","Score a recorded call on the 100-point QA rubric and name the one change that would raise the score most.","Write an end-of-shift daily report in the client channel that matches your Follow Up Boss activity and your Hubstaff hours."],"lessons":[{"id":"I11-1","title":"Your daily numbers: dials, conversations, appointments","minutes":7,"body":[{"t":"p","text":"Your client hired an ISA for one main reason: more appointments with real buyers and sellers. Appointments come from conversations, and conversations come from dials. That is why every shift is measured with three numbers. These are your **daily KPIs** (Key Performance Indicators):"},{"t":"table","head":["KPI per shift","Benchmark","What counts"],"rows":[["Outbound dials","**80-100**","Every call you place by hand to a lead, inside the calling window, to a number you are allowed to call"],["Live conversations","**12-15**","A real two-way conversation with the lead (not a voicemail, not a wrong number, not a 10-second \"call me later\")"],["A.L.M.-qualified appointments","**2-3**","An appointment set with your agent where you know the Appointment time, the Location and the Motivation, logged and handed off"]]},{"t":"p","text":"**Think of it as a funnel.** Of 100 dials, many will not answer. Some numbers are wrong. Some leads will say \"call me later\". Out of 100 dials you might reach 12 to 15 real conversations. Out of those conversations, 2 or 3 become appointments. If one number is low, look at the number before it:"},{"t":"list","items":["**Few dials?** Check your time. Were you writing notes too slowly, waiting between calls, or doing tasks that are not calling? Plan your shift better (lesson I11-2).","**Many dials but few conversations?** Check when you call. Calling at the wrong hours for the lead's time zone reaches fewer people. Check your lists, too: old leads answer less than new ones.","**Many conversations but few appointments?** Check your calls. Are you asking for the appointment on every call? Are you handling objections with acknowledge, pivot, appointment? This is where the QA rubric helps (lessons I11-3 and I11-4)."]},{"t":"callout","title":"Benchmarks, not promises","text":"These numbers are the program benchmark. Your client may adjust them: a team with few new leads may expect fewer dials, and a team with a big database may expect more. Targets are set with your client in your first week and reported every week. BeyondVAs does not guarantee appointments to clients, and nobody expects you to set appointments on day one. New ISAs usually need a few weeks to set their first appointments and longer to reach a steady pace; your manager confirms what is realistic for your client."},{"t":"p","text":"**Dials only count when they are allowed.** A number is not a reason to break a rule. A dial counts only when all of these are true:"},{"t":"list","items":["It is inside the calling window in the **lead's** local time: 9:00 AM to 8:00 PM Monday to Saturday, noon to 8:00 PM Sunday, or the stricter window your manager gave you for that state.","The lead is an inbound lead who asked to be contacted, or the number comes from a list your client scrubbed against the National Do Not Call Registry and their own DNC list.","You dialed by hand. No autodialer and no prerecorded messages unless your manager confirms written consent.","The contact is not tagged DNC or in Archived / DNC."]},{"t":"p","text":"If you reach 100 dials by calling people who opted out or by calling at 8:30 PM, your QA score and your placement are at risk. Your client would much rather see 70 clean dials than 100 risky ones."},{"t":"p","text":"**What a \"live conversation\" means.** A live conversation is a real exchange: you introduce yourself, announce the recording, and talk about what the lead wants. A good target is two minutes or more. You do not need to finish A.L.M. on every call, but you should always ask at least one Location or Motivation question and always ask for the appointment. A call where the lead says \"wrong number\" is a dial, not a conversation."},{"t":"p","text":"**What an \"A.L.M.-qualified appointment\" means.** It is not just a time on the calendar. It means you know:"},{"t":"list","items":["**A**: the confirmed date and time, in the lead's time zone, with the agent.","**L**: where they want to buy (neighborhoods, beds, baths, price) or which property they want to sell.","**M**: why they are moving and their timeline."]},{"t":"p","text":"And it means the handoff is complete: the calendar invite has the Follow Up Boss profile link, the 24-hour and 1-hour text reminders are set, the stage is **Pending Handoff**, and, for any tour, the invite notes that the agent needs a signed buyer agreement before the showing."},{"t":"p","text":"**Volume and quality together.** Some ISAs chase dials and forget quality. Others have beautiful conversations but make only 30 dials. Neither works. The best ISAs keep a steady rhythm: dial, talk, log the note, next dial. That rhythm is the real skill, and you will build it through practice and through the scorecard in the next lessons."},{"t":"dodont","do":["Track your three numbers during the shift, not only at the end","Look at the number before a low number to find the cause","Count only real, allowed dials and real conversations","Ask for the appointment on every live conversation"],"dont":["Call outside the window or call opted-out contacts to reach 100 dials","Count voicemails or wrong numbers as conversations","Count a time on the calendar without A.L.M. and a handoff as an appointment","Hide a slow day: report the real numbers and what you will change"]}]},{"id":"I11-2","title":"Plan a shift that hits the numbers","minutes":7,"body":[{"t":"p","text":"Hitting 80 to 100 dials, 12 to 15 conversations and 2 to 3 appointments does not happen by accident. It happens because you plan the shift. This lesson shows you how to organize a shift around the **Smart List order** and the **calling window**, so that you never miss a new lead and never fall behind on follow-ups."},{"t":"p","text":"**The Smart List order.** Work your lists in this order, every shift:"},{"t":"steps","items":["**New Speed-to-Lead** (0 to 15 minutes old)","**Hot Active** (ready within 30 days)","**Scheduled Follow-Up Tasks** (due today)","**30-60 Day Nurtures** (check-ins)"]},{"t":"p","text":"The first list always wins. When a new lead arrives, you stop what you are doing (finish your sentence politely if you are on a call) and dial within **180 seconds**, inside the window. If the new lead does not answer, run the 3-strike cadence: the call (ring 5 to 6 times, 20-second voicemail), a personalized text at 2 minutes to leads who agreed to texts, and a second call about 4 hours later, before 6:00 PM their time. Then go back to where you were."},{"t":"p","text":"**Two clocks, always.** You work in Honduras or elsewhere in Latin America, and your leads live in US time zones. Before every block of calls, check what time it is **for the lead**. A shift that starts at 8:00 AM your time may be 9:00 AM or 10:00 AM in New York, but it is still too early to call California. Plan your blocks so that each time zone gets called inside its window."},{"t":"table","head":["Lead's time zone","Window (Mon-Sat)","Tip"],"rows":[["Eastern (New York, Atlanta, Tampa)","9:00 AM - 8:00 PM Eastern","Earliest in the day for you; good for the first block"],["Central (Chicago, Dallas, Houston)","9:00 AM - 8:00 PM Central","Remember Texas: no solicitation calls before 9 AM, noon on Sunday"],["Mountain (Denver, Phoenix)","9:00 AM - 8:00 PM Mountain","Arizona does not change its clocks in summer"],["Pacific (Los Angeles, Seattle)","9:00 AM - 8:00 PM Pacific","Latest in the day; often your best evening calls"]]},{"t":"p","text":"Your manager will tell you the rules for your client's states before you dial. Some are stricter than the default window."},{"t":"p","text":"**A sample shift.** Here is how an 8-hour shift can look for an ISA whose client is in Arizona (Mountain time). Your real schedule will match your client's hours:"},{"t":"table","head":["Lead's time","Block","Goal"],"rows":[["8:30 - 9:00 AM","Prep: read yesterday's notes, today's tasks, check the agent's calendar for open times","Know the first 10 calls before the window opens"],["9:00 - 11:00 AM","Power block 1: Hot Active, then Scheduled Follow-Up Tasks","35-40 dials, 5-6 conversations"],["11:00 - 11:15 AM","Notes catch-up and appointment handoffs","Every call logged, invites sent"],["11:15 AM - 12:30 PM","Power block 2: remaining tasks, then 30-60 Day Nurtures","25-30 dials"],["12:30 - 1:00 PM","Break (clocked out)","Rest"],["1:00 - 3:30 PM","Power block 3: second strikes, nurtures, re-engagement","25-30 dials, 4-5 conversations"],["3:30 - 4:30 PM","Close: notes, tomorrow's first three calls, daily report","Clean CRM, report sent"]]},{"t":"p","text":"New Speed-to-Lead interrupts any block at any time. Your break and your close are planned, but speed to lead still comes first if you are clocked in."},{"t":"callout","title":"Log as you go","text":"Write each A.L.M. note right after the call, in under a minute, before the next dial. If you wait until the end of the shift, you will forget details and lose time. Every Follow Up Boss note has the same six parts: Date/Time, Call/Text Disposition, A, L, M, Next Step."},{"t":"p","text":"**Protect your rhythm.** The difference between 60 dials and 90 dials is usually not effort. It is small gaps: checking messages between calls, searching for a lead's profile, writing long notes, waiting for the agent to answer about a time. Some habits that help:"},{"t":"list","items":["Open the next lead's profile while the current call is ringing out.","Know the agent's open appointment times before the block, so you can offer two times without waiting.","Keep the client channel open but check it at set times (for example, every 30 minutes), unless the agent marks something urgent.","Use your note template so the six fields are already there."]},{"t":"p","text":"**Watch the numbers during the shift.** At the halfway point, check your three KPIs. If you have 30 dials at mid-shift, you know you need to speed up. If you have 50 dials but only 3 conversations, think about time zones and lists. Small corrections at noon are easier than a bad report at the end of the day."},{"t":"dodont","do":["Start every shift with prep before the window opens","Drop everything for a new lead and dial within 180 seconds","Plan blocks by the lead's time zone","Take your break clocked out"],"dont":["Start the shift by checking email for 30 minutes","Call Pacific leads at your morning time because you are ready","Leave all notes for the end of the shift","Skip the 3-strike cadence because the lead \"probably will not answer\""]}]},{"id":"I11-3","title":"The 100-point QA rubric","minutes":7,"body":[{"t":"p","text":"Your calls are reviewed with a **QA (Quality Assurance) rubric**. QA means checking the quality of the work, not just the quantity. Your coach or manager listens to a sample of your recorded calls and reads the matching Follow Up Boss notes. Each call gets a score out of 100. The standard is **90 or higher**."},{"t":"table","head":["Category","Points"],"rows":[["Speed to Lead","20"],["ALM Discovery","30"],["Objection Handling","20"],["Follow Up Boss Hygiene","15"],["Compliance","15"],["**Total**","**100**"]]},{"t":"p","text":"Learn what earns full points in each category, and what loses them."},{"t":"p","text":"**Speed to Lead (20 points).** For a new lead: did you dial within 180 seconds of arrival, inside the calling window? If they did not answer, did you run the 3-strike cadence: call with a 20-second voicemail, a personalized text at 2 minutes (to leads who agreed to texts), and a second call about 4 hours later before 6:00 PM their time? For follow-up calls, this category looks at whether you called on the day the task was due. Contacting within 5 minutes makes the odds of qualifying about 21 times higher than at 30 minutes, which is why this category is worth so much."},{"t":"p","text":"**ALM Discovery (30 points).** The biggest category. Did you learn the Location (areas, beds, baths, price) and the Motivation (why they are moving, and when)? Did you ask for the **Appointment** with two specific times? A call with great rapport and no appointment ask loses most of these points. Full marks need open questions, real listening, and a clear ask: \"Can you meet our local specialist this Saturday at 11 AM or 2 PM?\""},{"t":"p","text":"**Objection Handling (20 points).** When the lead pushed back (\"just looking\", \"send me info\", \"I already have an agent\"), did you acknowledge, pivot to a Location or Motivation question, then ask for the appointment? Did you stay calm and friendly? If the lead had a signed agreement with another agent, did you respect it and never pitch around it? If there was no objection, the reviewer looks at how naturally you kept the conversation moving."},{"t":"p","text":"**Follow Up Boss Hygiene (15 points).** Is the note complete with all six parts (Date/Time, Disposition, A, L, M, Next Step)? Is the stage correct, for example Pending Handoff after booking? Is there a task for the next step? Are tags right? Your agent should never have to ask the lead the same question twice."},{"t":"p","text":"**Compliance (15 points).** Did you announce that the call may be recorded? Was the call inside the window for the lead's state? Did you stay in scope: no values, no rates, no credit advice, no legal clauses, no talk of price, terms or commission? Did you follow fair housing (no comments on who lives in an area, schools, crime or \"safety\")? Did you honor any opt-out right away?"},{"t":"callout","title":"Some mistakes matter more than the total","text":"Ignoring an opt-out, calling a DNC contact or giving an opinion of value is not just \"minus a few points\". It is reported to your manager right away, whatever the total score, because it creates real risk for your client."},{"t":"table","head":["Category","Full points look like","Common deductions"],"rows":[["Speed to Lead","Dialed in 2 minutes; text at 2 minutes; second call scheduled","Dialed after 20 minutes; no text; no second strike"],["ALM Discovery","L and M uncovered; two times offered; appointment set or next step agreed","Only \"are you still looking?\"; no appointment ask"],["Objection Handling","Acknowledge, pivot, appointment; calm tone","Argued, gave up at the first \"no\", or pushed without acknowledging"],["Follow Up Boss Hygiene","Six-part note, correct stage, task created","Note says only \"talked to lead\"; stage unchanged"],["Compliance","Recording announced; in window; in scope","No recording announcement; guessed a rate; \"great schools there\""]]},{"t":"p","text":"**How reviews work.** In your first weeks, expect your coach to review many of your calls. As your scores stay at 90 or above, reviews become a regular sample. You will receive the score, the points lost in each category, and usually one or two comments. Read them the same day."},{"t":"p","text":"**Why 90?** A score of 90 means the call was fast, discovered A.L.M., handled pushback, was logged cleanly and broke no rules, with only small details to improve. Below 90, something important was missing: usually the appointment ask, a weak note, or a compliance step. The QA score and your daily KPIs together tell your client the full story: how much you did, and how well."},{"t":"p","text":"This is also the standard behind the practice simulator in this program: the QA Live Score rewards correct responses and takes points away for wrong ones, in the same five areas. To earn your certificate you pass every module quiz, complete every practice assignment and finish every simulator call."}]},{"id":"I11-4","title":"Score your own calls and improve one thing at a time","minutes":7,"body":[{"t":"p","text":"The fastest way to improve is to listen to your own calls. Most ISAs do not like hearing their own voice, especially in a second language. Do it anyway. In one week of self-review, you will notice habits that no coach could explain as clearly."},{"t":"p","text":"**The self-review routine.** Two or three times a week, choose two recorded calls: one that went well and one that did not. Then follow these steps:"},{"t":"steps","items":["Open the call recording and the matching Follow Up Boss note side by side.","Listen once without stopping, just to hear the call as the lead heard it.","Listen again and score each category on the rubric: Speed to Lead (20), ALM Discovery (30), Objection Handling (20), Follow Up Boss Hygiene (15), Compliance (15).","Write one sentence per category: what earned points and what lost them.","Choose **one** thing to improve this week. Only one.","When your coach scores the same call, compare. Where your score is higher than your coach's, you are missing something. Ask about it."]},{"t":"p","text":"**A worked example.** Here is a call with a buyer lead, Tessa, who requested information about a home in Mesa, Arizona. The lead arrived at 10:02 AM; the ISA called at 10:04 AM."},{"t":"script","title":"Call excerpt","lines":[{"who":"You","text":"Hi Tessa, this is Luis with the Hollis Home Team. You asked about the home on Juniper Street. This call may be recorded. Is now a good time?"},{"who":"Lead","text":"Sure, quickly. I was just looking at pictures."},{"who":"You","text":"Totally understand. What caught your eye about that one?"},{"who":"Lead","text":"The backyard. We have two dogs. Our lease ends in May."},{"who":"You","text":"That makes sense. Are you looking mostly in Mesa, or other areas too?"},{"who":"Lead","text":"Mesa or Gilbert. Under 450."},{"who":"You","text":"Great. I will send you some homes with big yards in Mesa and Gilbert. Talk soon!"}]},{"t":"p","text":"The note in Follow Up Boss said: \"Talked to Tessa. Wants Mesa. Send listings.\" The stage was not changed."},{"t":"table","head":["Category","Score","Why"],"rows":[["Speed to Lead","20 / 20","Called in 2 minutes, inside the window"],["ALM Discovery","15 / 30","Good L (Mesa or Gilbert, under $450K, yard) and M (lease ends in May, dogs). No appointment ask at all."],["Objection Handling","16 / 20","Handled \"just looking\" with acknowledge and pivot, but never reached the appointment step"],["Follow Up Boss Hygiene","6 / 15","Note missing Date/Time, Disposition, A and a clear Next Step; stage not updated; no task"],["Compliance","15 / 15","Recording announced, in window, in scope"],["**Total**","**72 / 100**","Below the 90 standard"]]},{"t":"p","text":"Luis did many things well: fast, friendly, good questions. But he lost 24 points in two places (15 in ALM Discovery and 9 in Follow Up Boss Hygiene) that are easy to fix. The **one thing** to improve this week: **always ask for the appointment with two times before ending the call**. For example: \"Since your lease ends in May, it helps to start seeing homes early. Could you see a couple of them with our specialist Saturday at 10 or Sunday at 1?\" That single sentence, plus a complete note, would move this call above 90."},{"t":"p","text":"With that appointment ask and a complete note, the call would be logged like this:"},{"t":"script","title":"Six-part note","lines":[{"who":"You","text":"Date/Time: Tue 10:04 AM MST. Disposition: Call, connected (3 min). A: Not set; asked for Sat 10 AM or Sun 1 PM, lead to confirm by text tonight. L: Mesa or Gilbert, under $450K, large backyard (two dogs). M: Lease ends in May. Next step: Text 3 homes with large yards today; follow-up task Wed 5 PM MST."}]},{"t":"callout","title":"One thing at a time","text":"If you try to fix five things at once, you will fix none of them. Pick the change that is worth the most points. For most new ISAs, it is the appointment ask (ALM Discovery, 30 points) or the six-part note (Follow Up Boss Hygiene, 15 points)."},{"t":"p","text":"**Listen for your English, too.** While you review, notice your speaking. Do you talk too fast when you are nervous? Do you say \"okay, okay\" many times? Do you use long sentences that the lead has to ask you to repeat? Write down two phrases you want to say more naturally, and practice them out loud before your next shift."},{"t":"p","text":"**Track your scores over time.** Keep a simple table of your QA scores by week, next to your three KPIs. When your scores rise, you will see your appointments rise too. When they drop, look at which category fell first. That is where to focus."},{"t":"dodont","do":["Review two calls a week: one good, one difficult","Score with the same five categories your coach uses","Pick one improvement per week and practice it out loud","Ask your coach when your score and theirs are different"],"dont":["Only listen to your best calls","Feel defeated by a low score: it shows you exactly where to work","Try to fix everything in one week","Argue with feedback before you listen to the call again"]}]},{"id":"I11-5","title":"Close the day: the daily report and your time records","minutes":7,"body":[{"t":"p","text":"At the end of every shift, you send a **daily report** in the client channel. It is the most visible thing you do all day. Many US agents spend the day in showings and only see your work through this report. A clear, honest report every day builds more trust than anything else you can do."},{"t":"p","text":"**Close the day in 10 minutes.** Before you write the report, close your work:"},{"t":"steps","items":["Check that every call and text today has a note in Follow Up Boss, with all six parts for live conversations.","Check that every stage is correct (Pending Handoff for every appointment booked; Archived / DNC for every opt-out).","Check that every next step has a task with a due date and time in the lead's time zone.","Write tomorrow's first three calls.","Then write and send the daily report."]},{"t":"p","text":"**The daily report template.** Keep it short and in the same order every day, so your client can read it in 30 seconds:"},{"t":"p","text":"**Example: daily report in the client channel**"},{"t":"list","items":["**Daily report, Tue Nov 3 (Luis), shift 9:00 AM - 5:00 PM MST**","**Dials:** 88 | **Live conversations:** 13 | **A.L.M. appointments:** 2","**Appointments:** (1) Tessa Lane, buyer, Sat 10:00 AM, Mesa, under $450K, lease ends in May. Invite sent with the Follow Up Boss link; buyer agreement flagged. (2) Ron Fisher, seller, listing consultation Thu 6:00 PM, Gilbert, relocating for work in March.","**Hot follow-ups:** Maria Okafor (wants Saturday times; agent to confirm by Wed noon).","**Opt-outs:** 1 (tagged DNC, Archived / DNC, automations paused).","**Needs from you:** confirm Thursday 6 PM works for Ron.","**Tomorrow:** first calls are Maria Okafor, Dev Sharma, Kim Alvarez."]},{"t":"p","text":"Notice what is there: the three KPIs at the top, each appointment with A.L.M. in one line, what needs the agent's attention, opt-outs, and tomorrow's plan. Notice what is not there: long stories, feelings, or excuses."},{"t":"callout","title":"Your numbers must match","text":"The numbers in your report must match your Follow Up Boss activity and your dialer log. If they do not match, the client stops trusting the report. Never round up. If you made 61 dials, write 61, and add one line about why and what you will change."},{"t":"p","text":"**A slow day, reported honestly.** Some days will be slow: few new leads, a list that does not answer, a long training call. Report it plainly: \"Dials: 54 | Conversations: 7 | Appointments: 0. Lower dials because of a 90-minute system outage (reported to my manager at 11:10 AM). Tomorrow I start with the 12 second strikes I could not reach today.\" That is a professional report."},{"t":"p","text":"**Your time records.** BeyondVAs uses Hubstaff to track your time. While you are clocked in, it takes random screenshots about every two minutes, and it scores activity from keyboard and mouse use. Your client can see your hours and your screenshots. Your manager reviews each week's hours, and unexplained idle time is flagged and reviewed before the client is billed. Clients are billed for tracked hours, so accurate records protect both you and them."},{"t":"p","text":"**Why calls can look idle.** When you are on a 20-minute call, you are not typing much, so the tracker may show low activity. That is normal, and your manager reads your activity next to your dialer's call log. You make that easy by logging every call in Follow Up Boss as it happens and by keeping your daily report accurate. Gaps that are not calls, such as a break you forgot to clock out for or an internet outage, must be explained to your manager the same day."},{"t":"table","head":["Situation","What you do"],"rows":[["Break or lunch","Clock out of Hubstaff, then clock back in"],["Long call with low activity","Nothing extra: the call is in the dialer log and the note is in Follow Up Boss"],["Internet or power outage","Tell your manager at once, switch to backup internet or power, and mention it in the daily report"],["Forgot to clock out","Tell your manager the same day so the time is removed before billing"],["Personal task during the shift","Clock out first. Never do personal tasks while clocked in"]]},{"t":"p","text":"**Keep it professional.** The daily report goes only in the client channel, never by private DM or personal text. Do not paste lead phone numbers or emails into the report; names and the Follow Up Boss record are enough. Never download or export client data to build a report."},{"t":"p","text":"**Why this matters for you.** Your daily reports, your KPIs and your QA scores are the record of your work. They are what your client sees, what your manager reviews, and what supports your hours, which are paid in USD twice a month, on the 5th and the 20th. An ISA with honest reports, 90+ QA scores and steady numbers is the ISA every team wants to keep."},{"t":"p","text":"The elite standard is simple to say and hard to do every day: fast speed to lead, appointments secured with A.L.M., and clean CRM data, closed with an honest report."},{"t":"dodont","do":["Close the CRM before you write the report","Put the three KPIs at the top, with real numbers","Clock out for every break","Explain any gap to your manager the same day"],"dont":["Send the report by private message","Round up dials or count voicemails as conversations","Paste lead phone numbers or emails into the channel","Stay clocked in while doing personal tasks"]}]}],"practice":{"title":"Mock shift: plan it, work it, score it, report it","minutes":120,"intro":"In this practice you run a two-hour mock shift in your Follow Up Boss practice account, then score your own calls and write the daily report exactly as you will for a real client. Your coach plays the leads. Use only practice data.","steps":["Your coach gives you a practice list of 20 leads in three US time zones (a mix of new, hot, follow-up and nurture), plus one \"new lead\" that arrives during the shift. Write your shift plan first: the order you will call them by Smart List, and the time in each lead's zone for each block. Confirm every call falls inside the calling window.","Run the mock shift for 60 minutes with your coach playing the leads. Announce the recording on every call, ask for the appointment on every live conversation, and dial the surprise new lead within 180 seconds of it appearing. Record every call.","Log every call in the practice account as you go: six-part note, correct stage, a task for every next step. Set at least one appointment with a calendar invite that includes the Follow Up Boss link, 24-hour and 1-hour reminders, Pending Handoff, and the buyer agreement flag.","Close the day in 10 minutes: check notes, stages and tasks, and write tomorrow's first three calls.","Write the daily report using the template from lesson I11-5. Your dials, conversations and appointments must match your call log exactly.","Choose two of your recorded calls (one strong, one weak) and score each on the 100-point rubric, with one sentence per category and a total.","Write your one improvement for next week and the exact sentence you will practice (for example, your appointment ask)."],"deliverable":"Send your coach the shift plan, the daily report, the two scored calls with recordings, your one improvement, and a screenshot of one complete six-part note and one calendar invite from the practice account. Your coach scores the same two calls and you compare them in your next session."},"quiz":[{"q":"What is the ultimate standard for an elite BeyondVAs ISA?","options":["Making 300 dials without taking any notes.","Executing fast speed-to-lead, securing appointments via ALM, and maintaining clean CRM data.","Sending generic bulk emails once a week.","Waiting for clients to call the office."],"answer":1,"explain":"Speed, qualification discipline, and CRM hygiene define high-performing real estate ISAs."},{"q":"What are the daily KPI benchmarks per shift?","options":["300 dials, 50 conversations, 10 appointments","20-30 dials, 2-3 conversations, 1 appointment a week","80-100 dials, 12-15 live conversations, 2-3 A.L.M.-qualified appointments","80-100 texts, 12-15 emails, 2-3 listings"],"answer":2,"explain":"The benchmark per shift is 80-100 outbound dials, 12-15 live conversations and 2-3 A.L.M.-qualified appointments. Your client may adjust targets in your first week."},{"q":"Which category is worth the most points on the 100-point QA rubric?","options":["ALM Discovery (30)","Speed to Lead (20)","Compliance (15)","Follow Up Boss Hygiene (15)"],"answer":0,"explain":"The rubric is Speed to Lead 20, ALM Discovery 30, Objection Handling 20, Follow Up Boss Hygiene 15 and Compliance 15. ALM Discovery is the largest because appointments come from it."},{"q":"You have 95 dials but only 4 live conversations at the end of a shift. What should you check first?","options":["Your appointment ask","Whether you called at good times for each lead's time zone, and which lists you dialed","Your daily report format","Whether you need more dials tomorrow"],"answer":1,"explain":"When dials are high but conversations are low, look at the step before: timing by the lead's time zone and list quality. The appointment ask matters when conversations are high and appointments are low."},{"q":"It is 8:20 PM for a lead in Dallas and you are 6 dials short of 80. What do you do?","options":["Call quickly; a few minutes late is fine","Send texts instead, since texts do not count","Stop calling that lead today and report the real number","Call from a different number"],"answer":2,"explain":"The calling window ends at 8:00 PM in the lead's local time, and texts follow the same window. Dials only count when they are allowed. Report the real number."},{"q":"A call scored 72. The ISA called in 2 minutes and was friendly, but never asked for an appointment and wrote \"Talked to lead.\" What is the one change worth the most?","options":["Call even faster next time","Talk less and listen more","Always ask for the appointment with two specific times before ending the call","Announce the recording twice"],"answer":2,"explain":"The appointment ask sits in ALM Discovery, the 30-point category. Pick the one improvement worth the most points; the six-part note would be the next."},{"q":"Where and when do you send your daily report?","options":["In the client channel at the end of every shift","By private text to the agent when you remember","By email once a week","Only on days you set an appointment"],"answer":0,"explain":"The daily report goes in the client channel at the end of every shift. Clients talk with VAs only inside the client channel, never by private DM."},{"q":"You were on a 25-minute call, and Hubstaff shows low activity for that time. What should you do?","options":["Move the mouse during calls so activity stays high","Nothing extra: the call is in the dialer log and the note is in Follow Up Boss, which your manager reads next to the activity","Clock out during long calls","Delete the screenshots from that period"],"answer":1,"explain":"Calls look idle to a tracker. Your manager reads activity next to the dialer's call log, so logging every call as it happens is what explains it. Faking activity or deleting screenshots breaks trust."},{"q":"Your dialer log shows 61 dials today. What do you write in the daily report?","options":["80, because that is the benchmark","70, to be close to the benchmark","Nothing about dials, only appointments","61, with one line on why and what you will change tomorrow"],"answer":3,"explain":"Report numbers must match your Follow Up Boss activity and dialer log. Never round up; explain a slow day plainly and name your plan."},{"q":"What is the QA standard for every call?","options":["60 or higher","75 or higher","90 or higher","100 on every call"],"answer":2,"explain":"The standard is 90+. A serious compliance error, such as ignoring an opt-out, is reported to your manager right away whatever the total."}]}],"sim":[{"id":"lucia","name":"Lucía Herrera","ini":"LH","color":"#9ED8B5","email":"lucia.herrera@example.com","phone":"(480) 555-2760","source":"Portal Inquiry - 3 Bed Home in Mesa","stage":"New Lead","list":"speed","listLabel":"Speed-to-Lead (0-15m)","created":"1 min ago","alm":{"a":"Not Scheduled","l":"Mesa (portal listing, 3 bed)","m":"Unknown"},"kind":"Spanish speaker","says":"Hola, sí, yo pedí información de la casa de tres recámaras en Mesa. ¿Me puede atender en español? Mi inglés no es muy bueno.","seed":[["1 min ago","system","Lead created","Portal inquiry: 3 bed home in Mesa, AZ. Form note: \"Prefiero español.\" Agreed to calls and texts. Added to the Speed-to-Lead Smart List."],["1 min ago","system","Assigned","Round-robin assigned to you (ISA)."]],"order":[1,0],"choices":[{"label":"Continue in Spanish & Run A.L.M.","text":"“¡Claro que sí, Lucía! Con mucho gusto seguimos en español. Le comento que esta llamada puede ser grabada. ¿Qué le gustó de esa casa, y busca solo en Mesa o también en otras zonas? ¿Y qué la motiva a mudarse ahora? Si le parece, nuestro agente le puede mostrar la casa este sábado a las 10 de la mañana o a las 2 de la tarde. ¿Cuál le queda mejor?”","correct":true,"pts":25,"effect":{"stage":"Pending Handoff","alm":{"a":"CONFIRMED: Saturday @ 10:00 AM w/ Agent Jordan (Spanish)","l":"Mesa or Chandler, 3 bed / 2 ba, up to $420K, big kitchen","m":"Lease ends in 60 days; current home is too small"}},"coach":"She asked for Spanish, so you stayed in Spanish: that is your advantage as a bilingual ISA. You announced the recording, asked Location and Motivation in natural Spanish, and closed with two specific times. The A.L.M. note stays in English so the agent can read it.","note":{"disp":"Call, connected (Spanish). Appointment set.","next":"Calendar invite to Agent Jordan with the Follow Up Boss link; flag that Lucía prefers Spanish and that the buyer agreement is needed before the tour. 24-hour and 1-hour reminders by text in Spanish."}},{"label":"Send Info & Hang Up","text":"“Claro, Lucía, sin problema. Le mando la información de la casa por correo y cualquier cosa usted me llama. ¡Que tenga buen día!”","correct":false,"pts":-20,"coach":"Speaking Spanish was right, but you ended a speed-to-lead call with no Location, no Motivation and no appointment ask. A Spanish-speaking buyer deserves the same full A.L.M. call as anyone else: keep going in Spanish and offer two times.","log":"Answered in Spanish but ended the call after offering to email information. No A.L.M. discovery, no appointment asked."}]},{"id":"gary","name":"Gary Whitfield","ini":"GW","color":"#F2A7A0","email":"gary.whitfield@example.com","phone":"(813) 555-6604","source":"Website Home Value Request","stage":"New Lead","list":"speed","listLabel":"Speed-to-Lead (0-15m)","created":"3 mins ago","alm":{"a":"Not Scheduled","l":"Owns 4 bed in Brandon, FL (seller)","m":"Unknown"},"kind":"Home value","says":"Yeah, I filled out that home value form. Just give me a number. What is my house worth? I am not ready to list or anything.","seed":[["3 mins ago","system","Lead created","Website home value request: 4 bed / 2 ba in Brandon, FL. Added to the Speed-to-Lead Smart List."],["3 mins ago","system","Assigned","Assigned to you (ISA)."]],"order":[0,1],"choices":[{"label":"Guess a Price Range","text":"“Sure, Gary! Homes on your street have been selling around $480K, so I would say yours is probably worth somewhere between $470K and $490K.”","correct":false,"pts":-25,"coach":"That is an opinion of value, and an unlicensed ISA never gives one. A guess also anchors the seller to a number nobody verified. Explain that a real answer needs the agent to see the home, ask what is behind the question, and offer two times for a listing consultation.","log":"Gave the seller a price range for his home (opinion of value). Out of scope."},{"label":"No Number, Set the Consultation","text":"“Great question, Gary, and it deserves a real answer, not a guess from me. Online estimates never see the inside of your home, so they can miss by a lot. Agent Jordan can walk through it and show you what it would sell for today, with no pressure to list. Can I ask what has you curious about the value right now? ... That makes sense. Would Tuesday at 5 or Thursday at 6 work for a quick walk-through?”","correct":true,"pts":25,"effect":{"stage":"Pending Handoff","alm":{"a":"CONFIRMED: Listing consultation Thu @ 6:00 PM w/ Agent Jordan","m":"Thinking of downsizing to a smaller home; maybe sell in spring"}},"coach":"You never gave a number: the value comes from your agent, at the home. You took his question seriously, found the motivation behind it, and turned it into a listing consultation with two specific times.","note":{"disp":"Call, connected. Listing consultation set.","next":"Calendar invite to Agent Jordan with the Follow Up Boss link and the property address; 24-hour and 1-hour reminders. Seller asked for no pressure to list."}}]},{"id":"jasmine","name":"Jasmine Cole","ini":"JC","color":"#C3A6F2","email":"jasmine.cole@example.com","phone":"(704) 555-3381","source":"Portal Inquiry - 2 Bed Townhome","stage":"Hot","list":"hot","listLabel":"Hot Active Leads","created":"2 days ago","alm":{"a":"Not Scheduled","l":"Charlotte / University area, townhome","m":"First-time buyer, wants to move within 30 days"},"kind":"Financing","says":"Before we go any further, what are interest rates right now? My credit score is like 640. Do you think I even qualify?","seed":[["2 days ago","system","Lead created","Portal inquiry: 2 bed townhome in Charlotte, NC. Added to the Speed-to-Lead Smart List."],["2 days ago","call","Call","Connected 3 min. First-time buyer, wants to move within 30 days. Moved to Hot Active. Callback today."]],"order":[1,0],"choices":[{"label":"No Advice, Keep A.L.M. Moving","text":"“That is such a smart question to ask early, Jasmine. I am not a lender, so I cannot speak to rates, credit or whether you qualify, and I do not want to guess about something this important. Agent Jordan works with a preferred lender and can set up that conversation if it helps. Meanwhile, are you still focused on the University area? And what is driving the 30-day timeline? Could you meet Agent Jordan Saturday at 11 or 2 to map out your plan?”","correct":true,"pts":25,"effect":{"stage":"Pending Handoff","alm":{"a":"CONFIRMED: Buyer consult Sat @ 11:00 AM w/ Agent Jordan","l":"Charlotte University area or NoDa, 2 bed townhome, up to $300K","m":"Lease ends next month; first-time buyer; asked about rates and credit"}},"coach":"You gave no rate, credit or qualification opinion. You offered the agent's preferred lender only as something the agent sets up, then went straight back to Location, Motivation and two appointment times.","note":{"disp":"Call, connected. Appointment set. Financing questions referred.","next":"Calendar invite to Agent Jordan with the Follow Up Boss link; buyer agreement before any tour; lead asked about rates and credit: Agent Jordan to decide on a lender introduction."}},{"label":"Quote Rates & Reassure","text":"“Rates are around 6.5% right now, and honestly with a 640 you should be fine for an FHA loan. Let me get you set up with our lender today.”","correct":false,"pts":-25,"coach":"You quoted a rate, gave a credit opinion and promised a loan type: all financing advice an ISA never gives. You also set up the lender yourself; the agent decides that. Say you are not a lender, mention that the agent can connect her, and keep A.L.M. moving.","log":"Quoted an interest rate and told the lead she would qualify for FHA with a 640 score. Financing advice, out of scope."}]},{"id":"brian","name":"Brian Okoye","ini":"BO","color":"#8FD3E8","email":"brian.okoye@example.com","phone":"(303) 555-9147","source":"Facebook Ad - New Construction Homes","stage":"New Lead","list":"speed","listLabel":"Speed-to-Lead (0-15m)","created":"4 mins ago","alm":{"a":"Not Scheduled","l":"Aurora, CO (new construction ad)","m":"Unknown"},"kind":"Has agent","says":"Oh, thanks for calling, but I already have an agent. We signed the buyer agreement with her last week. I just clicked on the ad out of curiosity.","seed":[["4 mins ago","system","Lead created","Facebook ad: new construction homes in Aurora, CO. Added to the Speed-to-Lead Smart List."],["4 mins ago","system","Assigned","Round-robin assigned to you (ISA)."]],"order":[0,1],"choices":[{"label":"Pitch a Second Look","text":"“Totally get it, Brian! But you can still tour these new builds with us. Our agent knows this builder really well. How about a quick look Saturday? No obligation.”","correct":false,"pts":-20,"coach":"He told you he signed an agreement with another agent. Offering a tour anyway is pitching around her agreement, which you never do. Thank him, respect it, note it, and tell your agent.","log":"Lead confirmed a signed buyer agreement with another agent; ISA pitched a tour anyway."},{"label":"Respect the Agreement","text":"“Thank you for telling me, Brian, and congratulations on getting started. I will respect that and make a note so our team does not reach out about buying. If anything ever changes, you are always welcome to call us. Have a great day!”","correct":true,"pts":20,"effect":{"stage":"Contacted","alm":{"a":"None. Respecting existing buyer agreement.","m":"Signed buyer agreement with another agent last week (per lead). Do not pitch."}},"coach":"He confirmed a signed agreement, so you respected it, ended warmly and left the door open without pitching. Log it and tell your agent the same day; the agent decides what happens to the contact.","note":{"disp":"Call, connected. Has a signed buyer agreement with another agent.","next":"Tagged Has Agent. Told Agent Jordan in the client channel; automations paused pending the agent's decision. No further outreach about buying."}}]},{"id":"dana","name":"Dana Brooks","ini":"DB","color":"#F5CF7A","email":"dana.brooks@example.com","phone":"(615) 555-4418","source":"Past Client - Purchase Closed Last Year","stage":"Warm Nurture","list":"nurture","listLabel":"30-60 Day Nurture","created":"14 months ago","alm":{"a":"None (past client)","l":"Bought 3 bed in Franklin, TN","m":"Annual check-in"},"kind":"Past client","says":"Hi! So nice of you to call. We love the house. Actually, my coworker Nina keeps asking who helped us. She wants to buy this spring, and she said I could give you her number.","seed":[["14 months ago","system","Lead created","Buyer lead from website. Closed on a 3 bed in Franklin 12 months ago with Agent Jordan."],["6 months ago","call","Call","Check-in call. Settled in, happy with the home. Next check-in in 6 months."]],"order":[1,0],"choices":[{"label":"Thank, Confirm Consent, Log","text":"“Dana, that is wonderful, thank you so much for thinking of us! Just to make sure: Nina is okay with Agent Jordan calling her? Great. What is the best number for her, and is there a time of day she prefers? I will let Jordan know the introduction came from you.”","correct":true,"pts":25,"effect":{"stage":"Warm Nurture","alm":{"m":"Happy in home. Referred coworker Nina Carter, who agreed to a call from Agent Jordan (prefers weekday evenings)."}},"coach":"You thanked her, confirmed that Nina agreed to be contacted and how she prefers it, and logged the referral on Dana's record. Nina gets a call from the agent, not a text, because she has not agreed to texts yet.","note":{"disp":"Call, connected. Past client check-in. Referral received.","a":"None (past client). Next check-in in 6 months.","next":"New lead for Nina Carter, source Past Client Referral (Dana Brooks); consent to a call confirmed through Dana; told Agent Jordan in the client channel. Thank-you note to Dana."}},{"label":"Grab the Number & Start Texting","text":"“Perfect! Send me her number and I will add her to our list and start texting her new listings today.”","correct":false,"pts":-15,"coach":"A referral is a gift, but Nina agreed only to be contacted, not to texts or a listing drip. Thank Dana first, confirm Nina is okay with a call and how she prefers it, and log the referral so the agent makes the first contact.","log":"Took the referral's number and planned to text listings without her consent to texts. Did not thank the past client or confirm consent."}]}]};
